window.VPQ_PHARM_NEXT={"course":"Veterinary Pharmacology · Exam 2 (Lectures 10–20)","status":"Exam 2 lecture pathways 10–20 available; + enrichment separated; instructor flags retained | Nov 17: lecture 15 BRD agent inventory and additional starred agents audited","lectures":[{"id":"Lecture 10","title":"Diabetes I · Fundamentals & Insulins","summary":"Insulin physiology, diabetic signs and species differences; insulin preparation and safe administration.","why":"Loss of insulin action means cells cannot take up/store glucose efficiently, while glucose spills into urine and pulls water with it: PU/PD, weight loss and altered fuel metabolism.","concepts":[["Beta-cell insulin release","Glucose metabolism raises ATP → ATP-sensitive K+ channels close → depolarization → calcium entry → exocytosis. Insulin signaling moves GLUT4 into membranes of muscle and adipose cells."],["Dogs versus cats","Dogs typically require ongoing insulin; feline diabetes often involves insulin resistance and beta-cell dysfunction, so remission may be possible but is not guaranteed."],["Insulin formulation","Regular insulin acts quickly; protamine and/or zinc delay release (NPH, lente, PZI); glargine precipitates in subcutaneous tissue; detemir associates with albumin."],["Safe administration","Check insulin-specific label and concentration (U-40 versus U-100), matching devices, storage and manufacturer mixing directions. Injections, food intake and monitoring matter."]],"recording":"Lecture 10 recording discusses insulin secretion, the purpose of excipients, pet-specific diabetes patterns and blood-glucose management.","sources":["(10&11)Diabetes.pdf pp. 1–33","Lecture 10 transcript, 2026-10-15"],"status":"Lecture-by-lecture expanded from main Exam 2 guide pp. 27–40; clinical verification flags separated","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The guide describes diabetic dogs as universally Type 1 and cats as obesity-driven Type 2. Those are lecture simplifications; clinical categories and etiologies vary.","The source suggests a speculative gut-bacterium molecular-mimicry mechanism and seasonal diagnosis pattern (p. 29). Treat as speculative context, not core testable mechanism.","Specific insulin product mixing, storage and route directions must be verified against the actual formulation—not generalized from 'all other insulins'.","The guide's statements about rapid insulin never being used SQ and preparation-dependent onset are tied to its DKA scenario, not a universal restriction on every formulation."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · Learn first — insulin secretion pathway","kind":"core","what":"Beta-cell GLUT2 → intracellular glucose metabolism → ATP rises → ATP-sensitive K+ channel closes → depolarization → voltage-gated Ca2+ entry → insulin granule release.","why":"Membrane excitation converts a nutrient signal into hormone secretion. Sympathetic α2 input and somatostatin suppress insulin release in the source guide.","check":"Explain why sulfonylureas need a living, responsive beta-cell to work.","source":"Main guide pp. 27–28"},{"title":"2 · Connect diabetes to clinical signs","kind":"core","what":"Loss of insulin production or reduced insulin response → hyperglycemia; glucosuria produces osmotic diuresis (PU/PD), insufficient nutrient use contributes to weight loss and polyphagia, and increased fat mobilization can lead to ketosis. Plantigrade stance in cats is a possible neuropathy sign.","why":"Cells still require glucose transport and coordinated fuel storage; urine water follows solute when glucose exceeds renal reclamation capacity.","check":"A polyphagic dog is losing weight and drinking excessively. What links those signs mechanistically?","source":"Main guide pp. 27–29"},{"title":"3 · Dog versus cat priorities","kind":"core","what":"The guide presents dogs as typically insulin-deficient and insulin-dependent, with symptom control and prevention of DKA/hypoglycemia the principal goals. Cats commonly have insulin resistance and variable beta-cell reserve; remission may occur, especially after early control and addressing contributors.","why":"The amount of residual insulin secretion influences whether restoring insulin sensitivity can achieve independence from injections.","check":"Why is feline remission possible but not guaranteed, and why is canine remission uncommon?","source":"Main guide pp. 28–30"},{"title":"4 · Insulin molecules versus preparation","kind":"core","what":"Regular insulin lacks absorption-delaying excipients; protamine and zinc alter aggregate/crystal release, while fatty-acid-modified analogs can bind albumin. Amorphous preparations enter circulation faster than coordinated crystalline deposits.","why":"Formulation changes the rate of availability rather than changing the fundamental insulin receptor target.","check":"Compare how protamine, zinc and albumin binding delay absorption.","source":"Main guide pp. 30–31"},{"title":"5 · Rapid, short, intermediate and long acting","kind":"core","what":"Lispro/aspart are rapid analogs with limited veterinary use; regular insulin is short acting and used in critical-care DKA protocols. Intermediate preparations include NPH and porcine lente; long-acting options include PZI and analogs in Lecture 11.","why":"A flatter or longer absorption profile can reduce hyperglycemic gaps, but every insulin can cause hypoglycemia. Do not assume human onset/duration applies unchanged to cats or dogs.","check":"Why is a prandial rapid-acting analog not a routine substitution for a dog's maintenance insulin?","source":"Main guide pp. 30–33"},{"title":"6 · Handling and delivery safety","kind":"core","what":"Match insulin concentration with its calibrated device (U-40 versus U-100). Review individual product storage and resuspension directions; the guide distinguishes vigorous resuspension of Vetsulin from gentler handling of other preparations.","why":"A formulation/delivery mismatch changes administered units, while inadequate resuspension changes dose consistency.","check":"How could the correct-looking syringe volume still deliver the wrong number of units?","source":"Main guide pp. 31–34"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Insulin formulation decision","kind":"core","what":"Start with patient species and insulin purpose (emergency DKA vs maintenance). Compare duration mechanism (zinc/protamine/precipitation/albumin), actual product concentration and device. Predict what would happen if mixed, shaken incorrectly or paired with the wrong syringe.","why":"Why it matters: the right molecule can still yield a dangerous dose or time profile when the preparation/device is misused.","check":"Patient check: explain U-40 vs U-100 mismatch using units/mL and volume.","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 11","title":"Diabetes II · Monitoring, DKA & New Therapies","summary":"Longer-acting insulin, patient monitoring, DKA reasoning, non-insulin options and metabolic disease across species.","why":"Insulin causes potassium to enter cells, which can expose or aggravate a total-body deficit in DKA. SGLT2 inhibition excretes glucose through kidneys without ensuring adequate insulin action, making euglycemic ketoacidosis possible.","concepts":[["Basal insulin selection","PZI, glargine and detemir are not equivalent; onset/duration depend on species and formulation. Detemir can be particularly potent in dogs."],["Monitoring","Clinical signs and body weight matter; home glucose monitoring/CGM can detect occult hypoglycemia. Fructosamine reflects average glycemia, not short episodes."],["DKA","Stabilize perfusion and assess K+, phosphate, ketones and acid-base status; initiate regular insulin under frequent monitoring with electrolyte replacement as needed. Follow local emergency protocols."],["SGLT2 drugs in cats","Bexagliflozin and velagliflozin are for carefully selected, newly diagnosed cats not previously treated with insulin; ketoacidosis can occur without high glucose. Blood beta-hydroxybutyrate monitoring is central."],["Large animal comparisons","Periparturient bovine ketosis, hyperinsulinemic equine metabolic syndrome and their distinct physiologic goals are covered in this lecture."]],"recording":"Lecture 11 recording adds discussion of insulin choices, limitations of spot glucose values, alternatives to injections, and feline SGLT2 risks.","sources":["(10&11)Diabetes.pdf pp. 34–90","Lecture 11 transcript, 2026-10-28","2026 AAHA Diabetes Management Guidelines for Cats"],"status":"Lecture-by-lecture expanded from main Exam 2 guide pp. 27–40; clinical verification flags separated","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The source portrays Somogyi rebound as a common explanation for multi-day poor control and suggests switching insulin at the same number of units. Avoid using that as an automatic clinical adjustment; confirm hypoglycemia and reassess the entire patient.","The guide says declining renal insulin clearance can 'help' extend NPH duration. This is a safety concern requiring glucose monitoring, not a treatment strategy.","The guide lists fixed DKA glucose-lowering rates, fluid discard volumes and schedules; these are not executable emergency protocols. Follow current monitored hospital guidance.","The guide's SGLT2 discussion notes strict patient selection but also implies treatment can be broadly combined with other drugs. Product-specific contraindications, including previous/current insulin exposure, are essential.","The guide's recommendations involving thyroid hormone for EMS and insulin for cattle ketosis should not be applied outside verified species-specific evidence and current prescribing rules.","The reported feline remission percentages and fatality forecasts for acromegaly are source-specific and should not be presented as universal outcomes."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · Long-acting insulin depot comparisons","kind":"core","what":"PZI uses zinc/protamine to delay absorption; glargine is acidic in its container and forms a precipitating subcutaneous depot near physiologic pH; detemir binds albumin through fatty-acid modification; degludec forms a prolonged-release depot.","why":"The drug acts at the same insulin receptor, but distribution out of the injection-site depot makes the time–glucose curve different.","check":"Why can changing the diluent alter glargine's release profile?","source":"Main guide pp. 32–34"},{"title":"2 · Species, monitoring and hypoglycemia","kind":"core","what":"Dog starting drugs in the guide: NPH or Vetsulin; feline long-acting selections: glargine, detemir or PZI. Monitor clinical signs, body weight and glucose trends; fructosamine reflects longer-term exposure rather than moment-to-moment fluctuations.","why":"Cats can show stress hyperglycemia during blood sampling, while silent hypoglycemia may still occur. A single glucose value cannot characterize a full action curve.","check":"What does a glucose curve reveal that fructosamine cannot, and vice versa?","source":"Main guide pp. 33–35"},{"title":"3 · Apparent poor control and misleading rebound","kind":"core","what":"The notes list inconsistent duration, administration errors, concurrent disease (hyperadrenocorticism, acromegaly, reproductive hormonal influences, renal disease), and a putative Somogyi rebound pattern.","why":"Growth hormone and other counter-regulatory hormones increase insulin resistance; renal impairment can reduce insulin clearance, potentially prolonging action and increasing risk of hypoglycemia.","check":"What evidence would distinguish insufficient insulin duration from an overly low glucose nadir?","source":"Main guide pp. 34–36"},{"title":"4 · DKA: why stabilize first","kind":"core","what":"DKA care in the guide begins with perfusion and electrolyte assessment/correction, followed by protocol-monitored regular insulin. Monitor glucose, potassium, phosphate, hydration, and ketone/acid-base trends in an inpatient setting.","why":"Insulin promotes glucose and potassium movement into cells while suppressing ketone formation; severely potassium-depleted patients can deteriorate without careful electrolyte treatment.","check":"Why is declining glucose not proof that a DKA patient is metabolically stabilized?","source":"Main guide p. 36"},{"title":"5 · Non-insulin antidiabetics and selection","kind":"core","what":"Sulfonylureas (glyburide, glipizide) close beta-cell KATP channels; metformin influences hepatic glucose production and sensitivity; SGLT2 inhibitors (bexagliflozin, velagliflozin) produce glucosuria; GLP-1 receptor agonists alter insulin/glucagon signaling and satiety.","why":"These classes act at different organs and generally do not replace insulin in an insulin-deficient DKA patient. SGLT2-associated euglycemic DKA can occur even when blood glucose is not strikingly elevated.","check":"Why can a cat on an SGLT2 inhibitor have significant ketosis despite a glucose measurement that appears improved?","source":"Main guide pp. 36–38"},{"title":"6 · Cattle and horses: not small-animal diabetes","kind":"core","what":"Source compares periparturient negative-energy ketosis, 'fat cow' ketosis, equine metabolic syndrome, and insulinoma therapy with streptozotocin. Cattle ketosis involves energy balance, hepatic gluconeogenesis and adipose lipid mobilization; EMS primarily involves dysregulated insulin dynamics.","why":"Species and syndrome determine whether the problem is insulin deficiency, resistance, altered energy supply, or excessive insulin production.","check":"Why isn't equine metabolic syndrome treated as if it were canine insulin-deficient diabetes?","source":"Main guide pp. 39–40"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Diabetes therapy safety gate","kind":"core","what":"Before suggesting feline SGLT2 treatment: is this a newly diagnosed, clinically healthy, eating cat? Has it ever received insulin? Are ketones, dehydration, renal/hepatic/pancreatic illness or insulin dependence concerns present? Treat anorexia/vomiting with ketonemia as an emergency even if glucose is not very high.","why":"Why it matters: glucosuria can improve glucose numbers while inadequate insulin action permits fat catabolism and ketones.","check":"Patient check: why does a glucose of 140–180 mg/dL NOT rule out ketoacidosis on an SGLT2 inhibitor?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 12","title":"Thyroid & Parathyroid Pharmacology","summary":"Thyroid regulation, medication effects on thyroid tests, hypo/hyperthyroid therapies and calcium management.","why":"TRH→TSH→thyroid follicular signaling regulates iodination and release of T4/T3. Methimazole reduces new synthesis, whereas levothyroxine replaces deficient hormone. Calcium treatment addresses failure of PTH-regulated calcium homeostasis.","concepts":[["Thyroid axis","TSH promotes iodide uptake via NIS and thyroid hormone formation; thyroid peroxidase catalyzes iodination/coupling; circulating T4 is converted to active intracellular T3."],["Hypothyroidism","Levothyroxine is the standard replacement drug in dogs with confirmed hypothyroidism. Illness or medications may lower T4 without primary hypothyroidism."],["Feline hyperthyroidism","Compare methimazole/carbimazole, iodine-restricted diets, I-131 and thyroidectomy. Monitor T4, renal status, blood cells and adverse reactions where appropriate."],["I-131 patient selection","Pretreatment with methimazole is an individualized consideration, especially for renal or systemic disease; it is not a universal prerequisite."],["Parathyroid/calcium","Iatrogenic hypoparathyroidism may require calcium and calcitriol; periparturient hypocalcemia in cattle is treated and monitored for cardiac risks."]],"recording":"Lecture 12 recording discusses drug-associated thyroid test changes, methimazole preparations, radioactive iodine and calcium management.","sources":["(12)ThyroidDrugs.pdf","Lecture 12 transcript, 2026-10-28"],"status":"Detailed pass completed from main Exam 2 guide pp. 40–50; disputed points in separate review flags.","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The guide says T4—but not T3—mediates negative feedback. This is oversimplified: local T3 derived from T4 mediates much hypothalamic/pituitary feedback. Preserve the wording as a source note, not a universal physiology rule.","The guide's hyperthyroid Y/D diet claims, remission percentages, cost and radioiodine pre-treatment/withdrawal timelines are context-dependent; review current protocols, patient eligibility and radiation regulations.","Do not prescribe levothyroxine solely on low total T4 during illness or breed-related low values; distinguish nonthyroidal illness and drug effects.","The guide mentions thyroid hormone for equine metabolic syndrome/weight loss; this is not an automatic current indication.","The guide suggests hypertonic saline or tripelennamine to get persistently recumbent hypocalcemic cattle to stand; this is not a standard stand-alone protocol."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · HPT axis: follow the signal","what":"Hypothalamic TRH → pituitary TSH → thyroid follicular cell production and release of T4 and T3. Thyroid hormone acts through intracellular transcriptional mechanisms and participates in negative feedback.","why":"An endocrine axis regulates how much thyroid hormone is produced. Thyroid effects on metabolism persist longer than a rapid membrane receptor response.","check":"Why might a patient with low T4 and concurrent systemic illness not have primary hypothyroidism?","source":"Main guide pp. 40–43","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · Synthesize thyroid hormone, step by step","what":"Iodide enters thyrocytes through NIS; pendrin supports apical iodide movement; TPO organifies iodine on thyroglobulin and couples MIT+DIT to form T3, or DIT+DIT to form T4. Proteolysis releases hormone. Intracellular deiodination converts T4 toward active T3.","why":"Each synthesis step is a pharmacologic target: iodide transport differs from TPO inhibition, thyroid hormone replacement, or radioiodine tissue destruction.","check":"Why do methimazole and perchlorate not have the same molecular target?","source":"Main guide p. 41","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · Species: hypothyroid dog versus hyperthyroid cat","what":"The notes emphasize canine acquired hypothyroidism (lethargy, alopecia, weight gain/cold intolerance) and older feline hyperthyroidism (weight loss despite appetite, tachycardia, polyuria/polydipsia). Interpret T4 with examination, TSH, illness, medications and species.","why":"Increased T4 drives metabolic demand, whereas decreased hormone often reduces metabolic rate; however illness and drugs can lower measured T4 without gland failure.","check":"An older cat loses weight and has tachycardia: why is levothyroxine not the presumptive treatment?","source":"Main guide pp. 41–43","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · Pseudohypothyroidism and drug interference","what":"The guide lists nonthyroidal illness, breed-dependent low T4 in sighthounds/northern breeds, glucocorticoids, phenobarbital and sulfonamides as low-T4 pitfalls; carprofen's role is explicitly marked unclear. Distinguish hormone concentration from a true glandular diagnosis.","why":"Metabolic stress, treatment and breed physiology can change hormone concentrations or feedback without equivalent thyroid pathology.","check":"A sick dog has a low T4 while taking phenobarbital: which extra findings help before prescribing replacement?","source":"Main guide pp. 42–43","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · Four feline hyperthyroid treatments","what":"Compare iodine-restricted diet (substrate restriction), methimazole/carbimazole (TPO inhibition), radioactive I-131 (thyroid tissue ablation) and thyroidectomy. Each has different monitoring, adherence, handling and complication requirements.","why":"Reducing hormone synthesis is different from removing thyroid tissue. Restoring normal thyroid status can unmask impaired renal function, so biochemical response and patient monitoring matter.","check":"Which option decreases thyroid hormone synthesis without permanently removing hyperactive tissue?","source":"Main guide pp. 43–45","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · Parathyroid, hypocalcemia and lactation","what":"PTH regulates calcium homeostasis; calcium supplementation with calcitriol is discussed for hypoparathyroidism. The guide contrasts this with bovine periparturient hypocalcemia treated using monitored calcium administration and dry-period mineral management.","why":"The thyroid and parathyroid glands sit near each other, so thyroidectomy can injure parathyroid tissue. Lactational calcium demand can rapidly exceed homeostatic adaptation in dairy cattle.","check":"Why should a post-thyroidectomy cat with tremors prompt a calcium assessment?","source":"Main guide pp. 45–46","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Thyroid management and radioiodine","kind":"core","what":"Compare methimazole (reversible TPO inhibition), iodine-restricted diet and I-131 (thyroid tissue destruction). Assess clinical stability, renal and cardiac function before radioiodine isolation; distinguish lecture no-touch/withdrawal dates from facility-specific radiation instructions.","why":"Why it matters: correction of hyperthyroidism can expose underlying CKD and isolation restricts hands-on care.","check":"Patient check: is azotemia alone a universal ban on I-131? Why not?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 13","title":"Autonomic Pharmacology","summary":"Cholinergic and adrenergic receptor functions with urinary, ocular and neuromuscular applications.","why":"Receptor selectivity predicts both the desired organ response and collateral effects: alpha-1 activation increases urethral tone and mydriasis; muscarinic activation drives bladder contraction and miosis; beta-1 stimulation increases cardiac activity.","concepts":[["Receptor map","Preganglionic neurons use acetylcholine/nicotinic receptors. Parasympathetic effectors commonly use muscarinic receptors; sympathetic effectors mainly use adrenergic alpha/beta receptors."],["Bladder medications","Phenylpropanolamine increases urethral closure pressure; oxybutynin reduces detrusor overactivity; prazosin and tamsulosin relax urethral smooth muscle; bethanechol helps detrusor contraction only when outflow obstruction is excluded."],["Ophthalmic medications","Tropicamide short-acting mydriasis for exam; atropine longer-acting cycloplegia useful for some uveitis cases (glaucoma and species cautions); phenylephrine is alpha-1 agonist; pilocarpine produces miosis."],["AChE and muscle","Organophosphate poisoning leads to cholinergic excess; succinylcholine is a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker that causes paralysis without providing sedation or analgesia."]],"recording":"Slide-based: no matching Lecture 13 audio transcript found in indexed project files.","sources":["(13)AutonomicPharmacology.pdf"],"status":"Detailed pass completed from main Exam 2 guide pp. 40–50; disputed points in separate review flags.","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The guide's β1 label for reduced bladder/gut contraction simplifies autonomic physiology; β3-mediated detrusor relaxation and β2-related GI effects also matter, varying by tissue/species.","The guide's statement 'miosis = activate muscarinic or block α1' is oversimplified for clinical ocular drug selection; pupil constrictor and dilator pathways are not interchangeable.","The guide presents a fixed 24-hour pralidoxime window. OP aging and oxime benefit depend on compound and timing; emergency toxicology advice is required.","The guide's discussion of historical succinylcholine use in euthanasia/castration is not a humane or acceptable substitute for anesthesia and analgesia.","Atropine eye-drop versus ointment issues in cats, glaucoma, KCS and equine systemic effects are patient- and product-specific; apply veterinary ophthalmology guidance."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · Wire the autonomic nervous system","what":"Both sympathetic and parasympathetic preganglionic neurons use ACh at nicotinic receptors. Most parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh at muscarinic effectors; most sympathetic postganglionic neurons release NE at adrenergic targets. Adrenal medulla chromaffin cells receive preganglionic cholinergic input.","why":"Knowing where the synapse is lets you predict whether a drug changes ganglionic transmission, an end-organ response or circulating catecholamine signaling.","check":"A drug blocks muscarinic receptors: does it block the nicotinic NMJ?","source":"Main guide p. 46","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · Match receptor to tissue: heart, airway and vessels","what":"The guide highlights β1-mediated cardiac effects, β2 bronchodilation, α1 vascular contraction and mydriasis, and muscarinic bradycardia/bronchoconstriction. Nicotinic receptors are ion channels; muscarinic and adrenergic receptors are GPCRs.","why":"Adrenergic and muscarinic signaling usually modify smooth muscle, glands or heart. Receptor subtype determines whether smooth muscle relaxes or contracts.","check":"Why can a nonselective beta-blocker treat tachycardia yet worsen bronchospasm?","source":"Main guide pp. 46–47","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · Storage versus emptying: bladder receptor reasoning","what":"In the lecture, phenylpropanolamine supports urethral closure via adrenergic signaling; oxybutynin reduces detrusor overactivity via muscarinic blockade. Prazosin and tamsulosin decrease urethral smooth-muscle tone, whereas bethanechol stimulates detrusor contraction when outflow is patent.","why":"Storage requires a relaxed detrusor and closed outlet. Emptying requires coordinated detrusor contraction plus an open outlet; increasing detrusor pressure against obstruction is unsafe.","check":"Why must urethral patency be assessed before prescribing bethanechol?","source":"Main guide pp. 47–48","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · Eye receptors and duration of dilation","what":"Tropicamide briefly blocks muscarinic contraction for eye exams. Atropine provides longer muscarinic cycloplegia/mydriasis for selected painful uveitis; phenylephrine stimulates α1 to dilate. Pilocarpine/demecarium produce muscarinic miosis in selected ophthalmic indications.","why":"The iris sphincter constricts through muscarinic signaling while the dilator uses α1 signaling; drugs can change pupil size by either direction of action.","check":"Why is atropine's prolonged action an issue in horses, and why does glaucoma change the choice?","source":"Main guide pp. 49–50","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · Neuromuscular blockade is NOT anesthesia","what":"Succinylcholine produces depolarizing nicotinic neuromuscular blockade, causing paralysis of skeletal muscle. It does not cause unconsciousness, amnesia, or analgesia; paralysis without appropriate anesthesia/analgesia is unacceptable.","why":"Paralyzing a motor endplate prevents movement but does not interrupt conscious pain perception. Airway/ventilation and anesthesia are separate requirements.","check":"Why is a motionless patient not proof that an NMJ blocker has relieved pain?","source":"Main guide p. 50","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · Cholinergic toxidrome and antidote roles","what":"Organophosphate AChE inhibition increases acetylcholine at muscarinic, nicotinic and CNS sites. Atropine blocks muscarinic manifestations; pralidoxime can reactivate inhibited AChE before aging in susceptible OP exposures. Source calls for combined treatment.","why":"One antagonist cannot resolve effects at every receptor. Pralidoxime affects the poisoned enzyme, while atropine blocks downstream muscarinic receptors; airway and respiratory support remain crucial.","check":"Why doesn't atropine reverse nicotinic neuromuscular weakness?","source":"Main guide p. 50","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Autonomic receptors at the bedside","kind":"core","what":"Given a case, identify receptor → tissue → second messenger → clinical consequence. Alpha-1: vascular/outflow tone; alpha-2: reduced NE release; beta-1: cardiac output/renin; beta-2: smooth muscle relaxation; M3: gland/detrusor contraction. Distinguish fixing an outflow problem from increasing detrusor force.","why":"Why it matters: drugs affecting the same organ can have opposing receptor actions and different safety consequences.","check":"Patient check: why can bethanechol be dangerous with unresolved urethral obstruction?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 14","title":"Cardiovascular Pharmacology","summary":"Positive inotropes, antiarrhythmics, blood pressure control, RAAS and pulmonary hypertension.","why":"Heart failure can lower perfusion while activating RAAS and fluid retention; improving forward flow without worsening congestion or arrhythmias requires matching the drug to the physiologic disturbance.","concepts":[["Inotropes and calcium","Digoxin inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase and alters Ca2+ handling; dobutamine stimulates beta-1; dopamine and amrinone affect cAMP signaling; pimobendan is a calcium sensitizer and PDE3 inhibitor. Know how contractility changes and why arrhythmia risk follows."],["Antiarrhythmic groups","Class I sodium-channel drugs include IV lidocaine (acute ventricular), mexiletine and procainamide; class II beta blockers include propranolol; class III amiodarone prolongs repolarization; class IV verapamil/diltiazem block calcium channels. Drug choice depends on rhythm, not only the drug class."],["Hypertension vs CHF","Amlodipine lowers vascular resistance in hypertensive cats; telmisartan blocks AT1; enalapril/benazepril inhibit ACE. Hydralazine and nitroprusside belong to urgent monitored settings, not routine substitutable home choices."],["Drug traps","Hypokalemia increases digoxin toxicity; beta blockade may cause bronchospasm; amiodarone has pulmonary, ocular, GI and bradycardia risks; sildenafil is a PDE5 inhibitor for pulmonary hypertension."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)"],"status":"Detailed source pass completed: pp. 51–57; clinical cautions separately flagged","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The guide calls digoxin the main CHF drug; this is source wording, not a complete current CHF treatment standard.","The source's simplified dopamine and Na+/Ca2+ exchanger mechanisms depend on dose and ionic gradients.","Sotalol has both class II and III activity, although the source lists it under beta blockers.","The guide assigns verapamil/diltiazem to PVCs. Cardiac calcium-channel blockers primarily affect AV nodal conduction; verify the rhythm indication.","The source's 'lidocaine IV only' refers to ventricular antiarrhythmic administration, not local anesthesia.","Do not use the source's resuscitation agent list as a clinical CPR protocol; current RECOVER guidance is separate."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · CHF and calcium handling","what":"Source compares cardiac Na+/K+ ATPase, Na+/Ca2+ exchange, L-type calcium channels and five inotropes: digoxin, dopamine, dobutamine, amrinone and pimobendan.","why":"Increasing calcium availability or contractile sensitivity can improve inotropy but also raise arrhythmia risk.","check":"Why can a positive inotrope increase both cardiac output and abnormal rhythm risk?","source":"Main guide pp. 51–53","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · Five inotropes: differentiate the mechanism","what":"Digoxin inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase; dopamine has dose-dependent catecholamine effects; dobutamine stimulates β1; amrinone inhibits PDE3; pimobendan sensitizes the contractile machinery to Ca2+ and inhibits PDE3.","why":"β1 activation increases cAMP, PDE3 inhibition slows cAMP degradation, and cardiac glycosides alter ionic gradients. These drugs are not interchangeable.","check":"Which agent is an inodilator, and how does its action differ from digoxin?","source":"Main guide pp. 52–53","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · Digoxin and potassium","what":"The lecture highlights GI signs, arrhythmias and AV conduction slowing with digoxin, particularly in a patient receiving potassium-wasting diuretics.","why":"Low K+ increases vulnerability to digoxin effects at Na+/K+ ATPase; renal function also contributes to exposure.","check":"Why should serum potassium and renal function matter in a patient receiving digoxin?","source":"Main guide p. 52","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · Match antiarrhythmic class to rhythm","what":"Class I Na+ blockers: lidocaine, mexiletine, procainamide, flecainide. Class II β blockers: propranolol and a component of sotalol. Class III prolong repolarization: amiodarone and sotalol. Class IV L-type Ca2+ blockers: verapamil and diltiazem.","why":"Myocardial and AV nodal electrophysiology differ, so ventricular and supraventricular rhythms may require different targets. Antiarrhythmics may be proarrhythmic.","check":"Why is identification of the arrhythmia more important than memorizing a drug-class number?","source":"Main guide pp. 53–55","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · Hypertension: vascular, RAAS and cGMP targets","what":"Amlodipine relaxes vascular smooth muscle; telmisartan blocks AT1; enalapril and benazepril block ACE; hydralazine and nitroprusside are monitored vasodilators; sildenafil inhibits PDE5 in pulmonary hypertension.","why":"Systemic vascular resistance, RAAS-mediated fluid retention and pulmonary vascular resistance are different therapeutic targets.","check":"Why do systemic hypertension and pulmonary hypertension not have identical drug choices?","source":"Main guide pp. 55–56","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · Hyperthyroid cat: arrhythmia versus blood pressure","what":"The lecture contrasts propranolol for selected hyperthyroid-associated tachyarrhythmias and amlodipine for systemic hypertension; monitor the patient, not just the thyroid diagnosis.","why":"β blockade reduces adrenergic cardiac stimulation while vascular Ca2+ blockade lowers arterial resistance.","check":"Which physiologic abnormality would distinguish a rhythm-control question from a blood-pressure-control question?","source":"Main guide pp. 55–57","kind":"core"},{"title":"7 · Resuscitation drug context","what":"The source lists epinephrine as the principal resuscitation drug and other drugs for selected circumstances; it emphasizes contraction, vascular tone and cerebral perfusion.","why":"Drugs cannot replace effective CPR, ventilation, and identifying reversible causes; exam-oriented lists are not clinical protocols.","check":"Why would bicarbonate not automatically be chosen for every arrest?","source":"Main guide p. 57","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Cardiac choice and interactions","kind":"core","what":"Choose based on target problem: contractility, vascular resistance, HR/rhythm, congestion or RAAS. Trace whether the selected drug affects heart muscle, nodal conduction, arteries or electrolytes; then account for furosemide/digoxin/K and ACEI/spironolactone/K interactions.","why":"Why it matters: an apparently helpful cardiovascular combination can precipitate arrhythmias when potassium or renal clearance changes.","check":"Patient check: explain VPCs in a hypokalemic dog taking digoxin.","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 15","title":"Respiratory Pharmacology","summary":"Bronchodilators, antitussives, airway regulation and respiratory infection drug selection.","why":"Parasympathetic M3 signaling promotes airway smooth-muscle contraction; beta-2 activation relaxes it. The appropriate intervention depends on bronchospasm versus mucus, cough physiology, or infectious disease.","concepts":[["Airway receptor map","Beta-2 agonists terbutaline, albuterol and equine clenbuterol cause bronchodilation; muscarinic blockers include ipratropium, glycopyrrolate and atropine. Compare CNS and systemic adverse effects."],["Methylxanthines","Aminophylline and theophylline inhibit PDE and antagonize adenosine; they can cause CNS excitation, tachycardia and GI signs. Compare with inhaled, receptor-selective therapy."],["Cough approaches","Central antitussives: codeine, hydrocodone, butorphanol and dextromethorphan. Expectorants/secretions: potassium iodide, guaifenesin and ambroxol. Suppressing a productive cough is not automatically desirable."],["Species and legal considerations","Clenbuterol is an equine airway drug with racing and food-production concerns. Zilpaterol is harmful to horses. Respiratory infection antibiotic choices depend on organism, patient, stewardship and label restrictions."],["Ventilation","Doxapram stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors; do not mistake it for a substitute for an airway, ventilation or reversing an underlying cause."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)"],"status":"Detailed source pass completed: pp. 57–63; clinical cautions separately flagged","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The source says beta agonists cause uterine contraction; beta-2 signaling usually relaxes uterine smooth muscle.","The source says dextromethorphan does not cross the BBB, despite its central antitussive mechanism; verify the wording.","Categorical claims about H1 antihistamine-induced bronchoconstriction in cats and sheep need confirmation.","Doxapram cannot replace airway/ventilatory support or appropriate opioid reversal.","Racing and food-animal regulations around clenbuterol, zilpaterol, ractopamine and lubabegron need current jurisdictional checks.","Respiratory antibiotic lists are teaching comparisons, not prescriptions for every animal with cough."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · Three airway regulatory inputs","what":"ACh on M3 contributes to bronchoconstriction; β2 activation causes bronchodilation; NANC signaling includes nitric-oxide-mediated relaxation.","why":"Airway tone reflects opposing contractile and relaxant signaling; β2 agonists and muscarinic antagonists work by different receptor actions.","check":"How can both albuterol and ipratropium relax an airway?","source":"Main guide pp. 57–58","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · Beta-2 agonists and species","what":"Terbutaline and albuterol are described for small-animal bronchospasm; clenbuterol has equine airway indications. Higher systemic β agonist exposure can produce tremors and tachycardia.","why":"β2 activation raises cAMP and can shift K+ into cells while off-target/systemic activity causes unwanted effects.","check":"Why is clenbuterol's clinical effect not limited to equine bronchi?","source":"Main guide pp. 58–59","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · Methylxanthines versus antimuscarinics","what":"Theophylline/aminophylline have PDE and adenosine-related effects; atropine, ipratropium and glycopyrrolate block muscarinic receptors. Inhaled ipratropium produces less systemic exposure.","why":"Different routes to bronchodilation cause distinct GI, CNS and cardiovascular risks.","check":"Why is a narrower therapeutic margin important with theophylline?","source":"Main guide pp. 60–61","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · Equine and food-animal beta agonists","what":"The source discusses equine clenbuterol, livestock repartitioning agents such as zilpaterol/ractopamine and lubabegron. Zilpaterol is particularly hazardous in horses.","why":"Species response and legal product labeling matter as much as receptor family; racing and food-animal rules need up-to-date verification.","check":"Why can a β-agonist approved for one animal group be inappropriate or prohibited in another?","source":"Main guide p. 61","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · Cough: protective reflex and drug groups","what":"The guide groups peripheral expectorants/secretion modifiers, bronchodilators and centrally acting antitussives including hydrocodone, codeine, butorphanol and dextromethorphan.","why":"Productive cough helps clear secretions; suppressing cough without evaluating cause can impair airway clearance.","check":"Why should a productive cough be evaluated differently from an exhausting dry cough?","source":"Main guide p. 62","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · Airway inflammation versus immediate relief","what":"The source lists equine inhaled ciclesonide, H1 antagonists and mast-cell stabilizers (cromolyn, nedocromil) alongside bronchodilators.","why":"Treating bronchospasm does not by itself control underlying airway inflammation.","check":"Why might a rescue bronchodilator not be sufficient long-term asthma treatment?","source":"Main guide pp. 61–62","kind":"core"},{"title":"7 · Respiratory stimulation is not ventilation","what":"Doxapram stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors and can affect respiratory drive in selected settings; the notes also discuss a preliminary thioredoxin concept.","why":"An increase in respiratory effort does not guarantee a patent airway, effective ventilation or oxygen delivery.","check":"Why is respiratory rate alone an insufficient endpoint after doxapram?","source":"Main guide p. 63","kind":"core"},{"title":"8 · Respiratory antibiotics, stewardship and restrictions","what":"The guide lists BRD metaphylaxis macrolides and selected BRD treatments, plus amoxicillin-clavulanate in a companion-animal context.","why":"Selection requires organism, species, label/extra-label restrictions, clinical presentation and antimicrobial stewardship.","check":"Why shouldn't a BRD medication automatically be selected for a pet with cough?","source":"Main guide p. 63","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Respiratory rescue versus control","kind":"core","what":"Separate bronchodilation (beta-2 agonists), control of airway inflammation (corticosteroids), oxygen delivery and effective ventilation. Assess whether the patient has bronchospasm, inflammatory disease, obstruction or hypoventilation before choosing a drug.","why":"Why it matters: a transient improvement in airway diameter does not resolve chronic inflammation or guarantee gas exchange.","check":"Patient check: why is doxapram insufficient if tidal volume remains ineffective?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 16","title":"Renal Pharmacology · Diuretics","summary":"Nephron sites, fluid balance, diuretic potency and electrolyte monitoring.","why":"Blocked renal transporters leave solute in the tubule so water follows; compensatory RAAS activation and downstream transport can limit response. The same mechanism can create hypovolemia or potassium loss.","concepts":[["Nephron map","Mannitol acts osmotically; acetazolamide inhibits proximal carbonic anhydrase; furosemide/torsemide block thick-ascending-limb NKCC2; thiazides block distal Na-Cl transport; spironolactone blocks mineralocorticoid receptors; amiloride/triamterene block ENaC."],["Loops and braking","Furosemide produces robust diuresis and can trigger RAAS as volume falls. Torsemide lasts longer and requires careful patient-specific dose selection."],["K+ handling","Increased distal sodium delivery promotes K+ secretion. Aldosterone antagonism spares potassium but risks hyperkalemia; spironolactone also addresses aldosterone-driven remodeling."],["Complications and surveillance","Trend hydration, weight, blood pressure, urine output, creatinine and electrolytes. Volume depletion can produce prerenal azotemia; loop and thiazide drugs may cause metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia."],["Species traps","Equine EIPH and cattle udder edema appear in the source; food-animal withdrawal and current labeling must be verified before clinical use."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)"],"status":"Detailed pass using main Study Guide 2 pp. 64–67, with bracketed/➕ material separately identified and clinical uncertainties flagged","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The source's nephron water-reabsorption percentages and segment labels simplify physiology: NKCC2 is in the thick ascending limb, which is water-impermeable; avoid teaching water absorption there.","Source-specific diuretic onset, milk withdrawal intervals, race restrictions and approvals require current legal/product verification.","The guide's assertion that a furosemide response proves cardiogenic pulmonary edema is not a validated standalone diagnostic test.","The source suggests certain diuretics for oliguric acute kidney injury: improved urine production does not necessarily improve renal survival or resolve the cause.","The source says spironolactone universally raises digoxin half-life and recommends dose reduction; interaction depends on patient and assays—monitor clinically.","The guide presents CKD anemia as complete failure to inhibit HIF-PH. This simplifies kidney EPO pathophysiology; verify individual product labeling and monitoring."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · When does diuresis help?","what":"The lecture frames diuretics as tools to reduce extracellular fluid (ECF) expansion in conditions including CHF-associated pulmonary edema, selected hepatic/renal edema, equine exercise-induced pulmonary hemorrhage and bovine udder edema.","why":"In CHF, reduced effective forward circulation can activate RAAS, increasing sodium and water retention even when the patient is fluid overloaded. Removing ECF can reduce congestion, but inadequate perfusion can worsen if volume depletion is excessive.","check":"Why can a patient with fluid in its lungs still have a physiology that stimulates sodium retention?","source":"Main guide p. 64","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · Nephron map: where diuretics act","what":"Proximal tubule: acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase and mannitol acts osmotically. Thick ascending limb: furosemide/torsemide inhibit NKCC2. Distal tubule: thiazides inhibit the Na-Cl cotransporter. Collecting duct: spironolactone blocks mineralocorticoid receptors, while amiloride/triamterene inhibit ENaC. The guide additionally lists vasopressin V2 antagonism as a human-focused concept.","why":"The fraction of filtered sodium handled at a nephron segment and compensatory transport downstream explain why loop drugs are usually stronger diuretics than collecting-duct agents.","check":"Why is furosemide usually more potent than spironolactone as a sole diuretic?","source":"Main guide pp. 64–65","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · Furosemide versus torsemide","what":"Both are loop diuretics targeting the NKCC2 cotransporter in the thick ascending limb. The source emphasizes furosemide's short duration and torsemide's greater potency and longer effect, with species/product-specific indications.","why":"Blocking NKCC2 interferes with sodium reabsorption and the medullary concentrating gradient. A stronger or longer blockade can improve diuresis but increases potential for hypovolemia, azotemia, chloride and potassium disturbances.","check":"Why can a 'stronger' loop diuretic be harder to adjust safely in a patient with marginal kidney perfusion?","source":"Main guide p. 65","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · RAAS, aldosterone breakthrough and K+","what":"The lecture pairs ACE inhibitors with spironolactone to address incomplete suppression of aldosterone signaling in CHF. Spironolactone is an aldosterone receptor antagonist; ENaC blockers are a different potassium-sparing group.","why":"Aldosterone increases distal sodium uptake and potassium secretion. More sodium reaching distal nephron after a loop/thiazide often increases K+ loss; inhibiting aldosterone/ENaC can retain potassium and risk hyperkalemia.","check":"Why can furosemide contribute to hypokalemia while spironolactone can contribute to hyperkalemia?","source":"Main guide pp. 65–66","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · Adverse effects: volume, electrolytes, acid–base","what":"The source lists hypovolemia, prerenal azotemia, sodium/chloride/potassium losses, magnesium disturbances and metabolic alkalosis as important diuretic complications; acetazolamide may instead cause metabolic acidosis.","why":"Reduced circulating volume can decrease renal blood flow; shifts in renal sodium, potassium and chloride handling change acid–base and cardiac excitability. Monitoring is part of treatment, not a separate optional step.","check":"A dog with pulmonary edema becomes weak with rising creatinine and low potassium after diuresis. Which physiologic problems do you assess first?","source":"Main guide pp. 64–66","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · Diuretic choices and species cautions","what":"Mannitol is described for selected intracranial/intraocular pressure emergencies, thiazides for selected adjunct edema, and potassium-sparing agents for targeted circumstances. The source includes equine EIPH and bovine udder-edema examples, which depend on current labeling and withdrawal restrictions.","why":"Indication and species matter as much as nephron site: a drug appropriate for CHF is not automatically effective for intrinsic oliguric kidney injury, and food-animal use requires legal review.","check":"Why is a diuretic not automatically recommended simply because a patient has low urine output?","source":"Main guide pp. 64–67","kind":"core"},{"title":"7 · Feline CKD anemia: HIF versus EPO receptor","what":"The lecture adds molidustat, a HIF-prolyl-hydroxylase inhibitor, and darbepoetin, an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent, as different routes to address selected CKD-associated anemia.","why":"The kidney participates in oxygen sensing and erythropoietin regulation; HIF stabilization promotes endogenous EPO pathways, whereas darbepoetin directly stimulates the EPO receptor. Both require clinical monitoring.","check":"Why does CKD sometimes cause a poorly regenerative anemia even if red-cell loss or destruction is not evident?","source":"Main guide p. 67","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Nephron map and monitoring","kind":"core","what":"Label loop NKCC2, distal NCC, collecting duct ENaC and mineralocorticoid receptor. Predict urinary Na/Cl/water and K changes at each site; when combining with ACE inhibitors, check potassium, perfusion and renal function.","why":"Why it matters: transporter location predicts natriuretic potency and electrolyte tradeoffs.","check":"Patient check: distinguish hypokalemia with loop diuresis from hyperkalemia with ACEI + spironolactone.","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 17","title":"Gastrointestinal & Hepatic Pharmacology","summary":"Emesis, prokinetics, colic/ulcer therapy, pancreatitis and liver support.","why":"GI pharmacology targets chemoreceptor and vagal signaling, enteric acetylcholine, acid secretion, mucosal protection and nitrogen handling; obstruction changes whether prokinetic therapy is safe.","concepts":[["Anti-emetic receptor targets","Metoclopramide blocks D2; maropitant blocks NK1; ondansetron blocks 5-HT3. Their indications and adverse-effect profiles differ, and none should be selected solely from receptor memorization."],["Prokinetic versus obstruction","Cisapride activates 5-HT4; erythromycin at sub-antimicrobial doses activates motilin receptors; metoclopramide and selected H2 antagonists can affect motility. Mechanical obstruction must be excluded when indicated."],["Ulcer drug hierarchy","PPIs inhibit H+/K+ ATPase; sucralfate protects injured mucosa; misoprostol is a PGE1 analog with pregnancy cautions; H2 blockers reversibly antagonize histamine. Consider onset, species and interactions."],["Large-animal GI","Psyllium, mineral oil, magnesium sulfate and butylscopolammonium have different uses in equine colic; recognize emergency referral and avoid treating all colic as one diagnosis."],["Liver and pancreas","Lactulose traps ammonia as NH4+ in the colon; rifaximin alters intestinal bacteria; acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. Fuzapladib inhibits LFA-1 in canine pancreatitis—its drug class and indication should not be described as a monoclonal antibody."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)"],"status":"Detailed pass using main Study Guide 2 pp. 67–81, with bracketed/➕ material separately identified and clinical uncertainties flagged","enrichment":[],"reviewFlags":["The notes' CTZ/vagal emesis pathway and several route/handling comments are explicitly marked ➕ and bracketed enrichment, not confirmed slide statements.","The guide calls ondansetron the 'most potent' antiemetic and metoclopramide preferred for chemotherapy vomiting; choice depends on cause, species and clinical evidence.","Inducing vomiting after toxin ingestion is a high-stakes intervention; do not use informal household peroxide protocols or anecdotes as an action guide.","The source's misoprostol mechanism and comments about IUD effects are oversimplified; the important clinical precaution is reproductive risk and appropriate handling.","The guide's specific claims of 48-hour famotidine tolerance and cimetidine being the 'best' H2 blocker are not universal clinical recommendations.","The lecture's recommended antimicrobial use in 'IBD' and hemorrhagic diarrhea is broader than stewardship guidance; treatment should be evidence- and patient-specific.","Fuzapladib is an LFA-1 inhibitor, not a monoclonal antibody. The lecturer's idea of preventing repeat pancreatitis bouts is not an approved preventive recommendation.","The claim that NAC prevents contrast nephropathy is not established as a veterinary standard-of-care."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · Emesis: choose a receptor and assess the patient","what":"The lecture lists antiemetic targets D2 (metoclopramide), NK1 (maropitant), and 5-HT3 (ondansetron), comparing dogs and cats; the guide's expanded CTZ-vagal pathway discussion is marked ➕ rather than slide material.","why":"Humoral and neural signals can converge on emetic pathways, but different causes engage receptors differently. Treating vomiting does not identify or eliminate obstruction, toxin exposure, pancreatitis or systemic disease.","check":"A vomiting dog has abdominal pain and suspected obstruction. Why is simply stopping emesis not sufficient?","source":"Main guide pp. 68–72","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · Anti-emetics versus pro-emetics","what":"Metoclopramide blocks D2 and can aid motility; maropitant blocks substance P at NK1; ondansetron blocks 5-HT3. The lecture lists ropinirole and apomorphine to induce emesis in selected dogs, with species-specific alternative agents for cats. Horses cannot vomit.","why":"Blocking emesis, increasing GI motility and deliberately inducing emesis are fundamentally different treatment goals. Corrosive or sharp ingestions and neurologic compromise change whether induction is safe.","check":"Why would ropinirole be inappropriate as a routine treatment for nausea?","source":"Main guide pp. 69–73","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · Prokinetics: only after considering obstruction","what":"The source identifies cisapride (5-HT4 agonism/enteric acetylcholine), metoclopramide (D2-related activity), erythromycin (motilin agonism at low doses), selected H2 blockers, and equine options as motility modifiers.","why":"A prokinetic increases contractile activity or transit. If the lumen is mechanically blocked, stronger contractions can worsen distension, discomfort and injury.","check":"What finding must be investigated before deciding that vomiting reflects treatable GI hypomotility?","source":"Main guide pp. 73–74","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · Equine colic drugs are syndrome-specific","what":"Magnesium sulfate is described as an osmotic laxative, mineral oil as a lubricant, psyllium as a bulk-forming intervention for sand, and butylscopolammonium as an antimuscarinic antispasmodic. None can substitute for determining whether a horse has surgical colic.","why":"A spasmodic episode, sand enteropathy, luminal impaction and strangulating lesion have different mechanisms and urgency. A drug's effect on transit does not resolve all obstruction or ischemia.","check":"Why is an antispasmodic not proof that a horse with colic can safely go home?","source":"Main guide p. 74","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · Ulcers: parietal pump versus protective prostaglandins","what":"The source compares PPIs (omeprazole, pantoprazole), sucralfate, misoprostol (PGE1 analog) and H2 blockers. Histamine, gastrin and acetylcholine promote acid secretion; endogenous prostaglandins support mucus and bicarbonate defenses.","why":"Blocking the H+/K+ ATPase reduces acid secretion downstream of several secretagogues. Sucralfate provides mucosal coverage, while misoprostol replaces aspects of prostaglandin-mediated protection; pregnancy is an important caution.","check":"Why is sucralfate different from omeprazole even though both can help an ulcer patient?","source":"Main guide pp. 75–76","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · Diarrhea: treat the cause, avoid indiscriminate antimicrobials","what":"The lecture distinguishes maldigestion, malabsorption, dysmotility and hypersecretion, listing dietary changes, pancreatic enzymes, selected antiparasitic/antimicrobial therapy, and chronic-enteropathy approaches. Cobalamin is discussed as an adjunct where deficiency exists.","why":"Diarrhea is a clinical sign, not one bacterial diagnosis. Unnecessary antibiotics can alter intestinal microbiota, while salicylate products are hazardous in cats and toxin adsorbents have situation-specific limitations.","check":"Why is metronidazole not automatically indicated for uncomplicated acute canine diarrhea?","source":"Main guide pp. 76–78","kind":"core"},{"title":"7 · Pancreatitis, hepatic injury and copper balance","what":"Fuzapladib inhibits LFA-1-mediated leukocyte adhesion in selected canine acute pancreatitis and is not an antibody. The guide lists SAMe, silybin combinations, acetylcysteine, D-penicillamine, zinc and ursodiol for different liver-related targets.","why":"Hepatic antioxidant support, copper chelation, reduced copper absorption and improved bile flow target different pathophysiology. An anecdote about repeat pancreatitis prevention is not an established indication.","check":"Why would zinc supplementation and D-penicillamine both appear in a copper-associated hepatopathy lesson despite different mechanisms?","source":"Main guide pp. 79–80","kind":"core"},{"title":"8 · Hepatic encephalopathy and intestinal ammonia","what":"Lactulose is metabolized by colonic bacteria, promoting acidification and NH3→NH4+ trapping. Rifaximin is described as a poorly absorbed antimicrobial adjunct that can reduce gut bacterial ammonia contributions.","why":"Ionized ammonium is less readily absorbed than ammonia; modifying gut microbial activity and transit can reduce ammonia reaching systemic circulation when hepatic clearance is impaired.","check":"Why can lactulose improve neurologic signs in a patient with a portosystemic shunt without directly acting on a brain receptor?","source":"Main guide p. 81","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · GI syndrome → correct target","kind":"core","what":"First identify nausea/vomiting versus reflux/ulceration versus impaired motility versus mechanical obstruction. Match maropitant to NK1, ondansetron to 5-HT3, omeprazole to H+/K+-ATPase and metoclopramide to antiemetic/prokinetic action. Confirm patency before giving prokinetics.","why":"Why it matters: suppressing a symptom is not the same as treating its cause, and promoting motility against a blockage may injure tissue.","check":"Patient check: which findings mean metoclopramide should not be the reflex choice?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 18","title":"Neuropharmacology I · Behavior and Benzodiazepines","summary":"MAO inhibitors, TCAs, SSRIs, serotonin receptor drugs and GABA-A modulators.","why":"Serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine signaling influence behavior; an animal's behavior must also be interpreted through learning, fear, pain and environment. Medication supports a diagnosis and behavior plan rather than replacing assessment.","concepts":[["Three monoamine drug targets","Selegiline inhibits MAO-B; clomipramine blocks serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake; fluoxetine and paroxetine inhibit serotonin reuptake; buspirone acts at 5-HT1A. Compare the site of action and delayed behavioral response."],["Behavior uses and species","Selegiline appears for canine cognitive dysfunction; clomipramine and fluoxetine for canine separation-related problems; mirtazapine is mainly an appetite medication in cats. Indications and licensing must be checked per formulation."],["Benzodiazepine physiology","Diazepam, midazolam and alprazolam positively modulate GABA-A chloride signaling; tolerance, dependence, sedation, ataxia and paradoxical disinhibition are potential problems. Flumazenil is a benzodiazepine-site antagonist."],["Drug interaction reasoning","MAOIs with other serotonergic agents can cause dangerous interactions; serotonin toxicity is a clinical emergency. Risk is combination-, dose-, and drug-specific; avoid treating every serotonergic combination as equally unsafe."],["Behavior interpretation","Fear-based aggression is not caused simply by higher serotonin, and SSRIs are sometimes clinically used in aggression-associated anxiety after assessment. This contrasts with a simplified statement in the uploaded notes; don't teach that statement as a universal rule."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)","Neuropharmacology Lectures 18–20 One-Page Summary"],"status":"Detailed source audit completed from main guide pp. 82–119; ➕ and bracketed material remains separately flagged.","enrichment":[["Behavior ≠ one neurotransmitter","Fear, pain, environment and learning history all affect behavior. Serotonin modulates neural circuits but does not diagnose aggression."],["Behavioral treatment foundations","Medical assessment, safety, environmental management and behavior modification remain central; medication may help when indicated."],["Serotonin toxicity","Learn characteristic neuromuscular, autonomic and mental-status signs, contributing drugs and why combinations require assessment."],["GABA-A and disinhibition","GABA-A modulation can cause sedation and sometimes paradoxical behavior changes; explain receptor physiology separately from specific drug selection."]],"reviewFlags":["Simplified claim that higher serotonin universally causes aggression / SSRIs are never used when aggression is present is not a reliable clinical rule.","Confirm the four highlighted veterinary-labeled behavior products with instructor and current labels.","Tyramine food warnings are especially tied to MAO inhibitors, not automatically all serotonergic drugs.","Course wording equates elevated serotonin with aggression and states SSRIs should not be used for aggression. That is not a universal veterinary behavior rule; the guide itself includes contrary clinical commentary.","The source groups carbamazepine among TCAs in behavior tables; its principal anticonvulsant mechanism includes voltage-gated sodium channel blockade and it is not a conventional TCA antidepressant.","Source claims TCAs are all prodrugs and automatically unsuitable for all geriatric/liver patients. Those are oversimplifications that require drug-specific review.","Source's neurotransmitter/autoreceptor shorthand is simplified, including claims that trazodone/mirtazapine broadly block serotonin autoreceptors.","Claims about tyramine food interactions, serotonin toxicity and medication washouts vary by drug and dose. Do not turn lecture memory aids into universal prescribing rules."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · A. Start with the synapse","what":"Source introductory review: nerve impulse → presynaptic voltage-gated calcium entry → vesicle fusion → neurotransmitter release → postsynaptic binding → signal termination by uptake and enzymes. Presynaptic autoreceptors regulate transmitter release.","why":"Behavior drugs act at different stages of one communication pathway. Enzyme inhibitors and reuptake inhibitors may both increase available monoamines without having identical downstream effects.","check":"Why does inhibiting MAO differ from blocking a serotonin reuptake transporter?","source":"Main guide pp. 82–88","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · A. Three monoamine targets","what":"MAO inhibitor: selegiline preferentially inhibits MAO-B and is associated with canine cognitive dysfunction. TCAs such as clomipramine affect serotonin and norepinephrine uptake. SSRIs such as fluoxetine/paroxetine selectively inhibit serotonin reuptake.","why":"Differences in off-target receptor binding explain much of the TCA versus SSRI adverse-effect contrast. Their behavioral benefits can emerge gradually as circuits adapt.","check":"Why might a TCA need additional cardiovascular consideration compared with an SSRI?","source":"Main guide pp. 86–90, 97–103","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · A. Drug names to indications","what":"The guide associates selegiline with canine cognitive dysfunction; clomipramine/fluoxetine with canine separation-related anxiety and selected compulsive behaviors; buspirone with selected chronic anxiety and feline urine spraying; mirtazapine mainly with feline appetite support; trazodone with selected situational distress.","why":"Knowing the molecule is insufficient: a patient with urine marking, pain-related agitation, seizure-like behavior or separation distress needs assessment and environmental/behavioral management.","check":"Why shouldn't any behavior medication be selected from the presenting behavior alone?","source":"Main guide pp. 87–91, 97–105","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · A. MAOI and serotonergic interactions","what":"The notes emphasize the risk of serotonin toxicity with potentially interacting serotonergic medicines, especially MAOI combinations. Signs to recognize include altered mentation, agitation, autonomic instability, tremors and other neuromuscular signs.","why":"Increasing monoamine exposure through multiple pharmacologic routes can cause toxic overstimulation. The risk depends on specific medicines, pharmacokinetics and patient factors; not every drug combination has the same risk.","check":"Why must the medication history include preventatives, supplements and other prescriptions when a patient takes selegiline?","source":"Main guide pp. 86–88, 102–111","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · A. GABA-A and benzodiazepine use","what":"Diazepam, midazolam and alprazolam are benzodiazepines acting as GABA-A positive allosteric modulators; they are used in selected anxiety, sedation and seizure contexts. Alfaxalone and barbiturates also influence GABA-A signaling but differ in potency, site and clinical use.","why":"GABA-A activation permits inhibitory chloride conductance. Increased inhibition can cause sedation and anticonvulsant effects, but disinhibition and paradoxical responses can occur.","check":"How is a benzodiazepine's GABA-A action distinct from a serotonin reuptake inhibitor?","source":"Main guide pp. 87, 105–109","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · A. Separate fear, learning and drug response","what":"The lecture says to rule out disease and use training/environmental changes; drugs may be adjuncts. The guide includes urine marking, separation distress, phobias, compulsive behavior and cognitive dysfunction.","why":"Behavior depends on pain, health, previous reinforcement, environment and neural state, not one isolated transmitter concentration. Sedation is not proof that an animal feels less fearful.","check":"A dog appears quiet after a sedative but still avoids handling when touched. What should the clinical team assess?","source":"Main guide pp. 87–93, 109–113","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Behavior: drug effect versus welfare","kind":"core","what":"Differentiate tranquilization, anxiolysis, analgesia and behavior modification. Contrast SSRI (SERT) with TCA (broader monoamine/receptor actions), MAO inhibitor and rescue agents; screen concurrent serotonergic medications and washouts.","why":"Why it matters: a sedated dog can still be fearful; some combinations cause potentially serious serotonergic toxicity.","check":"Patient check: why is selegiline + fluoxetine a high-risk prescription combination?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 19","title":"Neuropharmacology II · Anticonvulsants","summary":"GABA/glutamate balance, seizure prevention, emergencies and species-specific adverse effects.","why":"Seizures represent uncontrolled synchronized network excitation. Some drugs enhance inhibition; others reduce excitatory transmission. Maintenance and emergency seizure control have different pharmacokinetic priorities.","concepts":[["Neural signaling","Benzodiazepines enhance GABA-A activity; barbiturates also act on GABA-A; levetiracetam binds SV2A to modify vesicular neurotransmitter release. Zonisamide has multiple mechanisms, including ion channel effects."],["Maintenance comparisons","Phenobarbital requires therapeutic monitoring; bromide has long kinetics and chloride-dependent renal handling; levetiracetam can be an adjunct. Combination regimens require interaction and patient-specific review."],["Species traps","Bromide may cause lower-airway disease in cats; bromide combined with phenobarbital may be associated with pancreatitis in dogs. Abruptly stopping phenobarbital can precipitate seizures."],["Status epilepticus","The lecture notes present a diazepam → phenobarbital → anesthetic escalation sequence. Current emergency protocols vary; this is the instructor's historical exam sequence, NOT an executable clinical dosing algorithm. Stabilization and specialist emergency guidance take priority."],["Uncertainties","Your notes flag primidone recommendations, diazepam in cats, and a blanket no-same-MOA combination rule for clarification. Keep these as review flags rather than prescribing absolutes."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)","Neuropharmacology Lectures 18–20 One-Page Summary"],"status":"Detailed source audit completed from main guide pp. 119–128; ➕ and bracketed material remains separately flagged.","enrichment":[["Seizure circuit physiology","Balance inhibitory GABA signaling with glutamatergic excitation, ion channel activity and synchronization."],["Bromide and chloride","Dietary chloride alters renal bromide clearance, so diet changes can alter drug concentrations."],["Rescue versus prevention","Emergency seizure stabilization and chronic maintenance have different goals, kinetics and monitoring."]],"reviewFlags":["Status epilepticus sequence and repeat intervals in the notes are an instructor exam model, not a modern bedside protocol.","Primidone recommendations and approval status conflict by reference and require confirmation.","A blanket prohibition on combining medications with overlapping mechanisms is too absolute.","Do not infer abrupt bromide discontinuation is clinically safe for every patient.","The source's stepwise status epilepticus drug timing and dosing should not be used as a clinical protocol; rely on current supervised emergency guidance.","The guide's 'never combine two drugs with the same MOA' is overly categorical and not a universal anticonvulsant treatment principle.","Primidone is listed as the only approved canine epilepsy drug but also identified in the guide as older and no longer preferred in some references. Confirm current status.","The source says potassium bromide can be stopped abruptly; sudden antiepileptic changes need individual veterinary oversight.","Claims that every seizure causes irreversible brain injury and universal lifelong treatment are oversimplifications; decision-making depends on seizure history, severity and diagnosis.","Human-study findings and numeric dosing/targets in ➕ epilepsy notes are additional material, not instructor slide requirements."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · B. How a seizure starts and why treatments differ","what":"The lecture describes synchronized excessive neural activity and contrasts stopping an active seizure with preventing recurrent events. Key treatment targets include GABA-mediated inhibition, excitatory signaling and synaptic release/ion channels.","why":"Emergency rescue requires sufficiently rapid effects and stabilization, while chronic control requires sustained efficacy, acceptable sedation and monitoring.","check":"Why is a drug useful for daily prevention not automatically suitable to stop a prolonged active seizure?","source":"Main guide pp. 119–122","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · B. GABA, SV2A and ion channel mechanisms","what":"Phenobarbital enhances GABA-A inhibition; levetiracetam binds SV2A synaptic-vesicle protein; bromide affects neuronal excitability; zonisamide and selected additional medicines affect ion channels. The notes discuss emergency benzodiazepine, anesthetic and adjunct choices separately.","why":"Increasing inhibition or limiting excitatory neurotransmission lowers the probability of synchronized firing, but kinetics, interaction and species tolerability differ.","check":"Why can levetiracetam and phenobarbital both reduce seizures despite acting on different targets?","source":"Main guide pp. 120–128","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · B. Phenobarbital maintenance, monitoring and withdrawal","what":"The source emphasizes phenobarbital as a major dog/cat maintenance agent with sedation, ataxia, polyphagia/polydipsia, enzyme induction, hepatic effects and serum concentration monitoring. Abrupt withdrawal can provoke seizures.","why":"Changing hepatic enzyme activity affects other medication exposure and sometimes phenobarbital kinetics. Drug levels help investigate adverse effects, inadequate exposure or adherence.","check":"Why should dose changes or suspected treatment failure trigger a review of levels, clinical signs and other drugs?","source":"Main guide pp. 122–123","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · B. Bromide–chloride and species safety","what":"KBr/NaBr are discussed chiefly for canine prevention, as single or adjunct medications. Chloride competes with bromide during renal handling; the notes flag pancreatitis risk in dogs, especially with phenobarbital, and bronchial disease in cats.","why":"A diet change can change bromide clearance and therefore its blood concentration over a prolonged period. Long kinetics mean rapid response to changes should not be assumed.","check":"Why is a stable dietary chloride intake particularly important for a dog receiving potassium bromide?","source":"Main guide pp. 124–128","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · B. Recognize status epilepticus without turning the table into a protocol","what":"The instructor notes list an escalation sequence including benzodiazepines, barbiturates, anesthetic agents and additional options such as levetiracetam. The detailed dose intervals and sequence are source-based exam notes, not current executable bedside instructions.","why":"Prolonged seizure activity threatens oxygen delivery, temperature regulation and neuronal survival; protocols depend on stabilization, patient response and contemporary emergency guidance.","check":"Why do status epilepticus cases need immediate monitoring and team escalation rather than blindly repeating the study-guide sequence?","source":"Main guide pp. 120–122","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · B. Refractory seizures and false treatment failure","what":"The source distinguishes new or add-on therapy when seizures persist from changing medication because of adverse effects. The ➕ notes discuss diaries, drug levels, adherence, diagnosis and diet.","why":"Continued seizures do not necessarily mean the pharmacologic target is wrong; missed doses, changing metabolism or another disease can mimic resistance.","check":"What would you check before labeling a dog 'refractory' despite apparent maintenance treatment?","source":"Main guide pp. 122–128","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Seizure pathway and drug selection","kind":"core","what":"Separate emergency termination from long-term prevention. Diazepam/midazolam enhance GABA-A signaling, phenobarbital is a barbiturate, levetiracetam binds SV2A, bromide is chloride-sensitive and requires careful long-term monitoring. Consider species-specific cautions.","why":"Why it matters: receptor kinetics, elimination and drug interactions drive onset, monitoring and safe changes.","check":"Patient check: why can changing dietary chloride destabilize a bromide-controlled dog?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]},{"id":"Lecture 20","title":"Neuropharmacology III · Sedation and Opioids","summary":"Alpha-2 agonists, phenothiazines, opioid receptor actions and reversals.","why":"Alpha-2 agonists decrease presynaptic norepinephrine release and reduce sympathetic outflow, causing sedation with cardiovascular effects. Opioids modulate pain transmission through receptor and circuit-level mechanisms.","concepts":[["Alpha-2 agonists","Xylazine, dexmedetomidine, medetomidine and romifidine differ in species, indications and duration. Alpha-2 agonists can cause marked bradycardia and altered hemodynamics; reversals are antagonist- and patient-dependent."],["Reversal comparison","Atipamezole, yohimbine and other alpha-2 antagonists do not reverse every sedative; naloxone reverses opioid effects. Consider duration mismatch and recurrence of clinical effects."],["Phenothiazine cautions","Acepromazine is a D2/alpha-1 antagonist with sedative and antiemetic effects, but does not supply reliable analgesia. Hypotension and penile prolapse in horses are important cautions."],["Opioid compare","Butorphanol is a mixed kappa agonist/mu antagonist; buprenorphine is a partial mu agonist; tramadol has weak opioid and monoamine actions; hydrocodone is an antitussive. Effects and effectiveness vary by species."],["Pain sensitization","Wind-up is activity-dependent spinal sensitization with NMDA involvement. Multimodal and preemptive analgesia can limit amplification; link the deeper neural circuitry to optional WHY material."]],"recording":"Based on the uploaded Exam 2 study guides; lecture-slide statements, personal additions and uncertain details are not treated as equally verified.","sources":["Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026)","Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions (uploaded Nov 2026)","Neuropharmacology Lectures 18–20 One-Page Summary"],"status":"Detailed source audit completed from main guide pp. 129–140; ➕ and bracketed material remains separately flagged.","enrichment":[["Pain wind-up pathway","Repeated nociceptive signaling enhances NMDA-dependent central sensitization. Compare peripheral transduction with spinal/central amplification."],["Sedation versus analgesia","Alpha-2 norepinephrine suppression and opioid receptor signaling differ; tranquilization cannot be assumed to relieve pain."],["Comparative species response","Drug effect, analgesia and reversal can vary across dogs, cats, horses and production animals."]],"reviewFlags":["Alpha-2 effects in swine vary: the blanket statement that none work is not a safe universal rule.","Acepromazine seizure-threshold statements are source-dependent and need verification.","Opioid species/formulation differences make simple potency rankings misleading.","The source's universal claim that alpha-2 agonists do not work in swine is not established as a categorical pharmacology rule; species-dependent effects need verification.","The source overgeneralizes alpha-2 cardiovascular effects as uniformly lowering vascular tone and blood pressure; early peripheral vasoconstriction can occur.","Historical high-dose xylazine euthanasia and paralyzing-drug descriptions are not safe or humane general protocols and should not be implemented as instructions.","Claims about acepromazine seizure threshold and its behavior uses are contested; it has no reliable analgesic or direct anxiolytic effect.","The guide describes buprenorphine as 'more potent than morphine' and splits receptor action into simplistic mu1/mu2 claims; potency does not establish clinical efficacy.","Source claim that catnip is a direct mu/kappa agonist is not sufficiently supported for a definitive pharmacologic drug fact.","Opioid-associated cancer-proliferation claims and blanket contraindications are not established as universal restrictions; clinical assessment is individualized.","Ultra-potent wildlife opioid medications are regulated hazardous agents; study identification and safety rather than dose or administration protocols."],"deepSections":[{"title":"1 · C. Alpha-2 autoreceptor signaling","what":"The source describes xylazine, detomidine, medetomidine/dexmedetomidine, romifidine and clonidine as alpha-2 agonists that reduce presynaptic norepinephrine release. These can cause sedation and some analgesia, while cardiovascular effects vary over time.","why":"Alpha-2 activation decreases central sympathetic output, but peripheral alpha-2 vascular actions can initially increase resistance and produce reflex bradycardia. Sedation does not imply stable perfusion.","check":"Why can a sedated patient have marked bradycardia despite no obvious agitation?","source":"Main guide pp. 129–133","kind":"core"},{"title":"2 · C. Species, selection and reversal","what":"Xylazine is emphasized in ruminants; detomidine/romifidine in horses; dexmedetomidine in dogs and cats. The lecture names atipamezole, yohimbine and tolazoline as reversal agents with agent- and species-specific applications.","why":"The receptor family overlaps, but potency, cardiovascular susceptibility, label and administration route differ by species. Reversing sedation may also remove analgesia or reveal pain.","check":"Why can't every alpha-2 agent or reversal be treated as identical across horses, cattle, dogs and cats?","source":"Main guide pp. 130–134","kind":"core"},{"title":"3 · D. Phenothiazines: tranquilization is not analgesia","what":"Acepromazine is highlighted as a D2/alpha-1-related tranquilizer with hypotension and equine penile prolapse cautions. The source also lists trimeprazine, fluphenazine and azaperone as other related agents used in different clinical contexts.","why":"Acepromazine reduces observable activity but does not reliably relieve pain or fear. Alpha-1 blockade can decrease vascular tone and worsen hypotension.","check":"Why should a fearful patient not be assumed comfortable simply because acepromazine makes it quiet?","source":"Main guide pp. 134–137","kind":"core"},{"title":"4 · E. Opioid receptor families and drug classification","what":"The guide compares full mu agonists (e.g., morphine, fentanyl), a partial mu agonist (buprenorphine), mixed kappa agonist/mu antagonist (butorphanol), and antagonists (naloxone/naltrexone). Hydrocodone is noted for antitussive use and loperamide for GI effects.","why":"Opioid receptors influence transmission and modulation of nociceptive information. Affinity, intrinsic activity, distribution and species determine analgesia and adverse effects; potency alone does not equal clinical efficacy.","check":"Why does buprenorphine's partial agonism differ from a full mu opioid agonist despite both binding mu receptors?","source":"Main guide pp. 137–140","kind":"core"},{"title":"5 · E. Safety: tramadol, fentanyl, species and reversal","what":"The source flags tramadol's variable analgesic effectiveness in dogs, seizure/serotonergic concerns, fentanyl patch lag and safety, loperamide neurologic risk with ABCB1/P-glycoprotein variants, and the danger of ultra-potent wildlife opioids.","why":"Drug metabolism and receptor subtype responsiveness can differ among species. Naloxone may act for less time than an administered opioid, allowing recurrent sedation or respiratory depression.","check":"Why is a transient improvement after naloxone not proof that the patient cannot become sedated again?","source":"Main guide pp. 138–140","kind":"core"},{"title":"6 · E. Wind-up and multimodal analgesia","what":"The lecture introduces wind-up as central sensitization after repeated painful input, with NMDA involvement and a rationale for anticipatory and multimodal pain management.","why":"Sustained nociceptive signaling can amplify spinal excitability. Treating pain before and after procedures can reduce ongoing neural sensitization, while selecting treatments based on the patient and procedure.","check":"Why does a physically quiet patient still require an independent pain assessment?","source":"Main guide pp. 137–140","kind":"core"},{"title":"7 · E. Restricted wildlife drugs and ethical boundaries","what":"Carfentanil and etorphine appear only in specialist wildlife immobilization context. The notes also mention euthanasia and neuromuscular blockade. These are tightly controlled, high-risk topics and not routine analgesic tools.","why":"Sedation, paralysis, unconsciousness and analgesia are distinct endpoints. High-risk opioid or paralytic agents require authorized specialist protocols and safety controls; the game should teach safe recognition, not provide operational use instructions.","check":"Why is paralysis never proof that pain perception or consciousness has been removed?","source":"Main guide pp. 128–129, 137–140","kind":"core"},{"title":"Clinical bridge · Sedation, analgesia and reversal decision","kind":"core","what":"Before reversing, identify every administered drug and receptor target. Dexmedetomidine: alpha-2; opioid agonists: mu/kappa as applicable; benzodiazepine: GABA-A. Atipamezole, naloxone and flumazenil are not interchangeable; plan analgesia and watch for re-sedation.","why":"Why it matters: removing a sedative or opioid effect can unmask pain while coadministered drugs remain active.","check":"Patient check: what remains after selective alpha-2 reversal in an opioid-treated animal?","source":"Exam 2 guide Lectures 10–20; applied course synthesis"}]}],"drugs":[{"id":"insulin-regular","name":"Regular insulin (Humulin R / Novolin R)","brand":"Humulin R / Novolin R","class":"Insulin • short-acting","route":"Short-acting regular insulin is typically IV infusion or IM in hospitalized DKA protocols; SQ can be used in other suitable contexts, not in profound hypoperfusion.","uses":"Rapid glucose and ketone management in hospitalized DKA, with protocol-based IV infusion or IM schedules.","moa":"Insulin receptor activation increases glucose uptake and inhibits lipolysis/ketogenesis.","effects":"Hypoglycemia and hypokalemia; fluid, potassium and glucose monitoring are essential.","why":"Mechanism: Insulin receptor activation increases glucose uptake and inhibits lipolysis/ketogenesis. Clinically this matters for Rapid glucose and ketone management in hospitalized DKA, with protocol-based IV infusion or IM schedules. Specific risk: Hypoglycemia and hypokalemia; fluid, potassium and glucose monitoring are essential.","lecture":"Lecture 10","species":"Dogs/cats","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending","contraindications":"Track hydration/perfusion, hourly glucose as indicated, ketones, acid-base, potassium and phosphate; ensure potassium safety before initiation.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: soluble short-acting insulin without an absorption-delay depot is used for hospitalized DKA/hyperglycemic crisis under protocols for IV CRI or IM administration.","In DKA: assess perfusion, electrolytes (especially potassium and phosphate), acid-base status and ketones; insulin may further lower serum potassium as glucose uptake increases.","COURSE says not to rely on SQ dosing in profoundly dehydrated DKA patients; this is a context-dependent statement, not an intrinsic prohibition on all subcutaneous regular insulin."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A DKA dog is dehydrated and K+ is 2.8 mmol/L while an insulin CRI is being considered.","decision":"Address perfusion and potassium per DKA protocol before/during insulin initiation; monitor K frequently and ensure ICU supervision.","why":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells and can worsen pre-existing hypokalemia, causing weakness and arrhythmia.","whatChanges":"The lecture favors IV/IM regular insulin in hospitalized DKA; SQ absorption can be unreliable with severe hypoperfusion.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/endocrine-system/the-pancreas/diabetes-mellitus-in-dogs-and-cats"],"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"31,36","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star in insulin preparations / DKA","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"In hospitalized DKA select short-acting regular insulin with a controlled monitoring protocol, not a long depot formulation.","physiologyWhy":"Insulin reduces hepatic ketogenesis and stimulates glucose/potassium entry into cells; serum potassium can fall abruptly despite total-body potassium depletion.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike glargine or lente, regular insulin can be titrated promptly in IV infusion protocols; do not infer that SQ regular insulin is forbidden in every setting.","monitorAndAvoid":"Track hydration/perfusion, hourly glucose as indicated, ketones, acid-base, potassium and phosphate; ensure potassium safety before initiation.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"insulin-lente","name":"Porcine lente insulin (Vetsulin / Caninsulin)","brand":"Vetsulin / Caninsulin","class":"Insulin • intermediate-acting","route":"Subcutaneous suspension (commonly U-40 Vetsulin/Caninsulin); resuspend as product directs and match the U-40 delivery device.","uses":"Management of diabetes in dogs and cats as product-labeled.","moa":"Zinc suspension delays subcutaneous absorption of insulin.","effects":"Hypoglycemia, wrong syringe strength, inconsistent mixing; U-40 labeled product; follow shake instructions.","why":"Porcine lente; follow manufacturer mixing instruction, not generic roll advice.","lecture":"Lecture 10","species":"Dogs; formulation-specific use in cats","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · safety details require current clinical verification","contraindications":"Not intended IV; use matched U-40 device","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: Vetsulin/Caninsulin is porcine lente insulin, an intermediate-acting veterinary product. Match U-40 devices to U-40 concentration.","COURSE: the Vetsulin formulation requires vigorous resuspension as directed by its product instructions; do not generalize this handling to glargine or other analogs.","Monitor food intake, weight, PU/PD, glucose trends and hypoglycemia risk; formulation-specific duration and species response matter."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog receives U-40 Vetsulin and an owner buys U-100 syringes when the usual syringes are unavailable.","decision":"Stop and correct the device mismatch before administration; the marked units are NOT interchangeable across concentrations.","why":"U-40 contains 40 units/mL and U-100 contains 100 units/mL; syringe calibration changes the actual delivered volume.","whatChanges":"Demonstrate the manufacturer's mixing/resuspension method and have the owner show injection technique.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/endocrine-system/the-pancreas/diabetes-mellitus-in-dogs-and-cats"],"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"32","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star at Vetsulin row","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use as a maintenance insulin option in appropriate diabetic dogs; teach owner device and vial handling, not just a drug name.","physiologyWhy":"Porcine insulin combined with zinc forms amorphous/crystalline fractions that extend SQ absorption; poor mixing changes delivered dose fractions.","compareAndContrast":"Different from U-100 human NPH or glargine: common Vetsulin is U-40 and has label-specific resuspension instructions.","monitorAndAvoid":"Check correct U-40 pen/syringe, resuspend exactly per product instructions, monitor appetite, weight, PU/PD, curves/CGM and hypoglycemia.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"insulin-nph","name":"NPH insulin","brand":"","class":"Insulin • intermediate-acting","route":"Subcutaneous intermediate-acting protamine suspension; gently resuspend exactly per manufacturer; match insulin concentration and syringe.","uses":"Option in insulin-treated canine diabetes according to individual management plan.","moa":"Protamine (isophane) slows insulin uptake.","effects":"Hypoglycemia and variable duration; use correct product/device strength.","why":"Protamine delays absorption; product-specific mixing directions.","lecture":"Lecture 10","species":"Common dog option; generally not first choice in cats","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · safety details require current clinical verification","contraindications":"Subcutaneous use; check concentration/device","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: isophane/protamine formulation slows insulin release and is a traditional intermediate-acting choice for many dogs.","Use a matched insulin delivery device and manufacturer-specific resuspension instructions; monitor glucose trends and patient signs.","Not an interchangeable unit-for-unit replacement for any basal analog in all species."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38"},{"id":"insulin-pzi","name":"Protamine zinc insulin (ProZinc)","brand":"ProZinc","class":"Insulin • longer-acting","route":"Subcutaneous protamine-zinc suspension (common ProZinc formulation U-40); verify syringe/pen and species label.","uses":"Basal insulin for cats and dogs as product-labeled.","moa":"Protamine/zinc delays release from injection site.","effects":"Hypoglycemia; U-40 strength requires matching device and proper resuspension.","why":"Mechanism: Protamine/zinc delays release from injection site. Clinically this matters for Basal insulin for cats and dogs as product-labeled. Specific risk: Hypoglycemia; U-40 strength requires matching device and proper resuspension.","lecture":"Lecture 11","species":"Cats and dogs per current product label","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending","contraindications":"Use product-matched U-40 device when supplied at U-40; monitor hypoglycemia, feeding patterns, weight and home glucose.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: protamine-zinc insulin slows absorption, historically emphasized for cats and now available in certain canine indications depending on label.","Verify U-40 device compatibility and specific ProZinc formulation; do not automatically exchange its dose with glargine or detemir.","Follow individualized glucose monitoring and watch for hypoglycemia."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"32–34","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Prominent lecture insulin comparison","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A newly diagnosed diabetic cat is eating reliably, and the owner asks whether PZI can be administered with a U-100 syringe already at home.","decision":"Use an insulin-matched delivery device: common veterinary PZI/ProZinc preparations are U-40. Select an individualized feline insulin plan and teach administration rather than converting by syringe marks.","why":"Protamine and zinc delay insulin absorption. The U-40 syringe marks assume 40 U/mL, so U-100 calibration does not deliver the indicated units from a U-40 vial.","whatChanges":"Verify the exact ProZinc label and species indication; monitor appetite, body weight, home glucose patterns and hypoglycemia rather than choosing insulin only by duration.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider veterinary PZI when a longer basal profile suits a diabetic cat or appropriately indicated dog; verify product species label.","physiologyWhy":"Protamine and zinc slow dissolution of the SQ insulin depot; the insulin-receptor mechanism remains unchanged.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike glargine's acidic precipitation or detemir's albumin binding, PZI delay is driven by protamine/zinc formulation.","monitorAndAvoid":"Use product-matched U-40 device when supplied at U-40; monitor hypoglycemia, feeding patterns, weight and home glucose.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"insulin-glargine","name":"Insulin glargine","brand":"","class":"Insulin analogue • basal","route":"Subcutaneous basal insulin (formulations include U-100/U-300); do not mix or casually dilute acidic depot formulation.","uses":"Basal insulin for feline diabetes and selected canine patients under veterinary plan.","moa":"Acidic formulation precipitates after subcutaneous injection and releases insulin gradually.","effects":"Hypoglycemia; do not dilute or mix unless explicitly supported by product guidance.","why":"pH-dependent precipitation creates slow subcutaneous release.","lecture":"Lecture 11","species":"Common feline insulin; canine protocols differ","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · safety details require current clinical verification","contraindications":"Do not dilute/mix without product-specific authorization","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: glargine has an acidic solution and forms a subcutaneous precipitate at physiological pH, giving a prolonged-release profile.","Do not dilute or mix casually; dilution alters solubility and depot kinetics. Verify product-specific handling and concentration.","COURSE: a commonly discussed feline basal-insulin option. Monitor clinical signs, weight, glucose curves/CGM and hypoglycemia."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture says use only manufacturer diluent; for glargine the practical patient-safety principle is not to dilute or mix with other insulins unless a specific product instruction explicitly allows it.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A diabetic cat's owner asks to dilute glargine with sterile saline because the dose seems very small.","decision":"Do not dilute or mix glargine outside product directions. Use a matching delivery device and re-evaluate dosing precision.","why":"Glargine's solubility changes with pH; acid-to-neutral precipitation creates its depot effect and dilution can disrupt kinetics.","whatChanges":"Match insulin concentration to pen/syringe, monitor clinical signs and glucose trends rather than guessing from a single spot check.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/endocrine-system/the-pancreas/diabetes-mellitus-in-dogs-and-cats"],"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"33–34","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Emphasized first-choice insulin in treatment text","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider basal insulin in selected cats and dogs, tailoring formulation/concentration and patient monitoring.","physiologyWhy":"Glargine's acidic solution forms a microprecipitate at physiologic tissue pH, allowing slow release after SQ injection.","compareAndContrast":"Different from NPH protamine complex and detemir albumin binding; mixing or diluting can disrupt release kinetics.","monitorAndAvoid":"Do not mix/dilute casually; identify U-100 versus U-300 products and pen/syringe compatibility; monitor clinical trends and occult hypoglycemia.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"insulin-detemir","name":"Insulin detemir","brand":"","class":"Insulin analogue • basal","route":"Subcutaneous long-acting insulin analog; compare concentration and species sensitivity before selecting delivery device.","uses":"Longer-acting insulin for selected patients; especially low canine dose requirements.","moa":"Fatty-acid-modified insulin reversibly binds albumin to extend action.","effects":"Pronounced canine sensitivity and hypoglycemia; close monitoring and formulation-specific doses.","why":"Myristic acid promotes albumin binding and longer action.","lecture":"Lecture 11","species":"Dogs more sensitive to detemir dosing; feline use exists","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · safety details require current clinical verification","contraindications":"Use particular caution in dogs; high apparent potency","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: myristic-acid modification promotes albumin binding and delayed release.","COURSE: detemir may be relatively potent in dogs, so changes from another insulin require individualized veterinary dosing rather than unit-for-unit substitution.","Monitor for hypoglycemia; formulation concentration is not equivalent to a universal fourfold increase in biologic potency."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"33–34","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Prominent lecture insulin comparison","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A 6-kg dog with diabetes has poor regulation on an intermediate insulin. The owner proposes switching to the same number of units of detemir.","decision":"Do not switch unit-for-unit. Reassess the current glucose curve, administration technique and disease before choosing a cautious detemir plan.","why":"Detemir's myristic-acid group binds albumin to delay release. Dogs can be particularly sensitive, making equivalent-volume or equivalent-unit conversions unsafe.","whatChanges":"Monitor glucose nadir, hypoglycemia, renal status, food intake and other insulin resistance causes; explain why human-concentration labeling does not mean canine dose equivalence.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider a long-acting insulin option only with individualized species-specific dose planning, particularly cautious in dogs.","physiologyWhy":"Fatty-acid modification promotes albumin binding and prolonged distribution, while insulin receptor activation still drives cellular glucose and potassium uptake.","compareAndContrast":"Not interchangeable unit-for-unit with glargine or PZI; some dogs respond strongly to small amounts of detemir.","monitorAndAvoid":"Review concentration, delivery device, clinical signs, CGM/curves and hypoglycemia; do not attribute potency to a universal human-product concentration multiplier.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"bexagliflozin","name":"Bexagliflozin (Bexacat)","brand":"Bexacat","class":"SGLT2 inhibitor","route":"Oral feline SGLT2 inhibitor tablet; labeled only for carefully selected insulin-naive cats; no concurrent insulin.","uses":"US-labeled feline diabetes treatment for appropriately screened newly diagnosed, otherwise healthy cats never previously receiving insulin.","moa":"Blocks renal proximal-tubule SGLT2 glucose reuptake, causing glucosuria despite limited insulin activity.","effects":"FDA boxed-warning risk of life-threatening diabetic ketoacidosis, including euglycemic DKA. Anorexia, vomiting, lethargy, dehydration and diarrhea require immediate veterinary evaluation; hyperglycemia may be absent in eDKA.","why":"Renal glucose loss can make blood glucose appear improved while inadequate cellular insulin drives lipolysis and ketogenesis; always assess ketones and clinical condition, not glucose alone.","lecture":"Lecture 11","species":"Cats only; strictly selected newly diagnosed, insulin-naive patients.","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"FDA primary-label high-risk safety audit 2026-11-23.","contraindications":"Do NOT use with insulin, in previously insulin-treated cats or insulin-dependent diabetes. Screen for ketones, pancreatic/hepatic/renal disease and illness before prescribing. If anorexia, vomiting, lethargy or dehydration occur, STOP drug and urgently assess for DKA regardless of glucose.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + FDA: Bexacat is an oral SGLT2 inhibitor for carefully selected newly diagnosed cats NEVER previously given insulin. NOT an adjunct to insulin; do not stop prescribed insulin just to switch.","SGLT2 reduces renal glucose reabsorption but cannot replace insulin's suppression of ketogenesis. Life-threatening DKA can occur even when glucose is normal (euglycemic DKA).","Screen before initiation: clinical status, hydration, appetite, CBC/chemistry, renal/hepatic status and blood/urine ketones as indicated; follow the product's blood beta-hydroxybutyrate and early-monitoring plan.","If anorexia, vomiting, lethargy, dehydration, weight loss or weakness develop, stop the SGLT2 product and obtain urgent veterinary evaluation; suspected DKA requires emergency insulin-based care after discontinuing the SGLT2 drug."],"clinicalRefs":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/product-safety-information/dear-veterinarian-letter-advising-veterinarians-important-safety-conditions-associated-use-bexacat","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=f918583d-0337-40da-8da1-1e1320b8d027","https://www.aaha.org/resources/2026-aaha-diabetes-management-guidelines-for-cats/section-6-sglt2-inhibitor-treatment-and-monitoring/"],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Only carefully screened, insulin-naive feline diabetics who do not need insulin; NEVER combine with insulin or use after prior insulin therapy.","physiologyWhy":"Renal SGLT2 blockade creates glucosuria without replacing insulin's anti-ketogenic action.","compareAndContrast":"Differ from glargine/PZI, which provide insulin; normal glucose does NOT exclude ketoacidosis.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor clinical signs and blood ketones; vomiting, anorexia or lethargy require urgent review and stopping the SGLT2 inhibitor.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture pp. 36–38 discusses non-insulin drug combinations generally. That broad statement MUST NOT override the drug-specific FDA prohibition against Bexacat used with prior or current insulin.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A cat received one week of glargine, and the owner wants to switch to oral Bexacat.","decision":"Do not give Bexacat: previous insulin treatment itself is a labeled contraindication. Discuss safe continuation of diabetes care.","why":"Renal glucose loss can make BG look better without sufficient insulin to stop ketone production; DKA may be euglycemic.","whatChanges":"Screen eligibility and ketones before any SGLT2 initiation; appetite loss, lethargy or vomiting during therapy requires discontinuation and urgent evaluation.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"37","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred SGLT2 drug-class topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"velagliflozin","name":"Velagliflozin (Senvelgo)","brand":"Senvelgo","class":"SGLT2 inhibitor","route":"Oral feline SGLT2 inhibitor solution; carefully screened insulin-naive cats only; no concurrent insulin.","uses":"US-labeled treatment of diabetes in carefully screened cats not previously treated with insulin.","moa":"Inhibits proximal renal glucose reabsorption so glucose is excreted in urine.","effects":"FDA boxed-warning risk of potentially fatal DKA or euglycemic DKA; anorexia, dehydration, lethargy and weight loss may develop without marked hyperglycemia.","why":"SGLT2 inhibition lowers glucose through glucosuria but does not replenish insulin; ketogenesis can continue despite normal/mildly high blood glucose.","lecture":"Lecture 11","species":"Cats only; strict screening and ongoing monitoring.","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"FDA primary-label high-risk safety audit 2026-11-23.","contraindications":"Do NOT use with insulin or in previously insulin-treated/insulin-dependent cats. Assess ketones, hydration, appetite, and concurrent disease prior to therapy. FDA labeling requires ketonuria evaluation 2–3 days after initiation, around day 7 and with illness; STOP drug and promptly treat ketosis/DKA appropriately even if glucose is normal.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + FDA: Senvelgo is an oral SGLT2 inhibitor for selected insulin-naive, otherwise healthy cats. It must NOT be administered concurrently with insulin, and is not suitable for cats previously treated with insulin.","Mechanism: increased urinary glucose excretion without restoration of adequate insulin activity; euglycemic DKA may occur despite glucose levels that are not markedly elevated.","Check eligibility including appetite, hydration, liver/renal/pancreatic status and ketones. Monitor blood beta-hydroxybutyrate and clinical signs according to the current label, especially at initiation.","If signs suggest DKA/euglycemic DKA, discontinue Senvelgo and obtain urgent veterinary intervention; insulin may be necessary as emergency treatment AFTER the SGLT2 inhibitor has been stopped."],"clinicalRefs":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/product-safety-information/dear-veterinarian-letter-regarding-important-safety-conditions-associated-use-senvelgo-velagliflozin","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ec5ca1f-bded-459e-a813-24d931d50b9a","https://www.aaha.org/resources/2026-aaha-diabetes-management-guidelines-for-cats/section-6-sglt2-inhibitor-treatment-and-monitoring/"],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Same insulin-naive restriction as Bexacat; Senvelgo liquid formulation does not make it safe with prior or concurrent insulin.","physiologyWhy":"SGLT2 inhibition lowers plasma glucose via urinary loss, not restoration of pancreatic beta-cell function.","compareAndContrast":"Euglycemic DKA can occur; differentiate blood glucose monitoring from ketone assessment.","monitorAndAvoid":"Evaluate suitability, hydration, intake, renal/liver/pancreatic disease and ketones before and during therapy.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture pp. 36–38 discusses non-insulin drug combinations generally. That broad statement MUST NOT override the drug-specific FDA prohibition against Senvelgo used with prior or current insulin.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A newly diagnosed cat has a glucose of 190 mg/dL after Senvelgo but stops eating and begins vomiting.","decision":"Treat this as possible euglycemic DKA, not reassuring glycemic control. Stop Senvelgo and urgently assess blood ketones, acid-base and hydration.","why":"SGLT2 inhibition permits urinary glucose excretion even as insufficient insulin allows lipolysis and ketogenesis.","whatChanges":"Concurrent or prior insulin prohibits this drug under US labeling; monitoring glucose without BHB misses a potentially fatal complication.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"37","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred SGLT2 drug-class topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"levothyroxine","name":"Levothyroxine (T4)","brand":"T4","class":"Thyroid hormone replacement","route":"Oral T4 replacement, with consistent dosing relative to feeding and standard post-dose monitoring.","uses":"Confirmed canine hypothyroidism and specific replacement indications in other species.","moa":"Replaces T4 which tissues convert to active T3; feedback adjusts thyroid axis.","effects":"Iatrogenic hyperthyroidism with overdosing; assess clinical signs, weight, cardiac status and timing of T4 tests.","why":"Mechanism: Replaces T4 which tissues convert to active T3; feedback adjusts thyroid axis. Clinically this matters for Confirmed canine hypothyroidism and specific replacement indications in other species. Specific risk: Iatrogenic hyperthyroidism with overdosing; assess clinical signs, weight, cardiac status and timing of T4 tests.","lecture":"Lecture 12","species":"Dogs primarily","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending","contraindications":"Use consistent product and administration timing; recheck thyroid hormone at appropriate post-dose timing and monitor for iatrogenic thyrotoxicosis.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: oral T4 replacement for confirmed hypothyroidism; treatment monitoring uses clinical response and appropriately timed serum thyroid testing.","COURSE: administer consistently relative to meals, maintain a consistent brand/formulation, and check medication expiration and storage when response unexpectedly changes.","Do not treat low total T4 in sick euthyroid patients solely based on a screening value; review confounding drugs and disease."],"studyGuidePages":"41–45","professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 43 says T3 does not feed back to hypothalamus/pituitary; that is an oversimplification. Both T3 and T4 participate in negative feedback, and T4 is standard replacement for other pharmacokinetic/physiological reasons.","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"43","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred hypothyroidism treatment topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog has lethargy and a low total T4 during severe systemic illness but no documented thyroid-specific pattern.","decision":"Do not automatically prescribe T4 based on a single low total T4. Assess illness, medications, clinical signs, free T4 and TSH as appropriate.","why":"Nonthyroidal illness and certain drugs suppress measured T4 without primary hypothyroidism; thyroid supplementation treats hormone deficiency, not every low lab result.","whatChanges":"In a confirmed hypothyroid patient, maintain a consistent formulation and dosing relative to food; reassess clinical response and properly timed T4 levels.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority veterinary teaching example; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Treat confirmed hypothyroidism when clinical signs and appropriate thyroid testing support a diagnosis; avoid reflex treatment from isolated low total T4.","physiologyWhy":"T4 is converted to active T3 in tissues and supplies thyroid hormone needed for metabolism, cardiovascular function and skin/hair cycling.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike T3 administration, levothyroxine provides a physiologic circulating precursor; BOTH T3 and T4 participate in negative feedback.","monitorAndAvoid":"Use consistent product and administration timing; recheck thyroid hormone at appropriate post-dose timing and monitor for iatrogenic thyrotoxicosis.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"methimazole","name":"Methimazole","brand":"","class":"Antithyroid • thyroid peroxidase inhibitor","route":"Oral tablets/liquid or approved feline transdermal product where available; formulation-specific absorption and handling.","uses":"Medical control of feline hyperthyroidism; transdermal formulation may be used with appropriate veterinary oversight.","moa":"Inhibits thyroid peroxidase, blocking new thyroid-hormone synthesis, not release of stored hormone.","effects":"Vomiting, anorexia, facial excoriation/pruritus, hepatopathy and blood dyscrasias; underlying CKD may emerge.","why":"Correcting hyperthyroidism reduces increased GFR that may have masked CKD.","lecture":"Lecture 12","species":"Cats with hyperthyroidism","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Baseline and follow-up CBC, liver/renal chemistry and T4; urgently review jaundice, bleeding or severe skin signs.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: blocks thyroid peroxidase-mediated iodination/coupling of thyroid hormone precursors; does not directly destroy the thyroid gland and existing stored hormone may persist.","COURSE: monitor T4, CBC and platelets, renal values (BUN/creatinine) and liver enzymes; consider vomiting, anorexia, facial excoriation, cytopenias and hepatotoxicity.","COURSE I-131 WORKFLOW: often used first to stabilize the patient and assess cardiovascular/renal/metabolic response; stop 10–14 days before I-131 per lecture, but individual nuclear-medicine centers may alter this for patient safety."],"clinicalRefs":["https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/vmc/radiology/iodine-131-therapy/","https://www.merckvetmanual.com/endocrine-system/the-thyroid-gland/hyperthyroidism-in-animals"],"studyGuidePages":"41–45","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use for medical control of feline hyperthyroidism and sometimes to assess renal/cardiovascular response before definitive therapy.","physiologyWhy":"Blocks thyroid peroxidase: iodination/coupling fall; previously synthesized hormone persists, so effect is not instant.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike I-131, it does not ablate the abnormal tissue; compare oral tablets and properly compounded transdermal options.","monitorAndAvoid":"Follow T4, CBC/platelets, liver/renal values; vomiting, facial pruritus, cytopenia or hepatopathy can necessitate reassessment.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A hyperthyroid cat becomes less tachycardic after starting methimazole but creatinine rises.","decision":"Reassess hydration, thyroid level and renal status before escalating dose or scheduling definitive treatment.","why":"Lowering thyroid hormones reduces hyperdynamic circulation and GFR; previously masked CKD can become clinically apparent.","whatChanges":"Track T4, CBC/platelets, liver enzymes and renal markers, and recognize facial excoriation/vomiting as possible adverse drug reactions.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"43–45","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred feline hyperthyroidism treatment topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"carbimazole","name":"Carbimazole","brand":"","class":"Antithyroid • prodrug of methimazole","route":"Oral prodrug converted to methimazole; formulation and geographic availability vary; monitor thyroid/CBC/liver effects.","uses":"Hyperthyroidism in cats where an appropriate product is available.","moa":"Converted to methimazole to inhibit thyroid peroxidase.","effects":"Similar monitoring/contraindications to methimazole; formulation and approval differ by region.","why":"Mechanism: Converted to methimazole to inhibit thyroid peroxidase. Clinically this matters for Hyperthyroidism in cats where an appropriate product is available. Specific risk: Similar monitoring/contraindications to methimazole; formulation and approval differ by region.","lecture":"Lecture 12","species":"Cats (country-dependent)","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending","contraindications":"As a methimazole prodrug, may cause GI intolerance, facial pruritus, hematologic abnormalities and hepatotoxicity; monitor CBC/T4 and renal values as thyroid status changes.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: prodrug converted to methimazole; inhibits new thyroid hormone synthesis by inhibiting thyroid peroxidase.","Feline use depends on available local formulation; monitor thyroid hormone, CBC, renal and hepatic values and adverse effects much like methimazole.","Do not equate carbimazole's prodrug conversion with radioactive iodine tissue destruction."],"studyGuidePages":"41–45"},{"id":"calcitriol","name":"Calcitriol","brand":"","class":"Active vitamin D hormone","route":"Oral active vitamin D therapy in selected hypoparathyroidism; monitor ionized calcium, phosphorus and kidney function.","uses":"Long-term therapy alongside calcium for hypoparathyroidism in selected patients.","moa":"Active vitamin D receptor agonism enhances intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption.","effects":"Hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, mineralization and renal injury if overtreatment occurs.","why":"Vitamin D restores calcium balance, but excess calcium-phosphorus can mineralize tissues.","lecture":"Lecture 12","species":"Hypoparathyroidism or selected renal/mineral disorders","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor ionized calcium, phosphorus and renal function; avoid unmonitored use in hypercalcemia.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D promotes calcium absorption; post-thyroidectomy hypoparathyroidism may require oral calcium plus calcitriol. Monitor calcium and phosphate for hypercalcemia."]},{"id":"phenylpropanolamine","name":"Phenylpropanolamine","brand":"","class":"Adrenergic agonist • urethral sphincter","route":"Oral dog urethral sphincter-tone therapy; immediate/extended-release products are not interchangeable without product review.","uses":"Selected urethral sphincter mechanism incontinence in dogs.","moa":"Increases alpha-adrenergic urethral smooth muscle tone.","effects":"Hypertension, tachycardia, agitation; use caution in cardiac/hypertensive patients.","why":"Mechanism: Increases alpha-adrenergic urethral smooth muscle tone. Clinically this matters for Selected urethral sphincter mechanism incontinence in dogs. Specific risk: Hypertension, tachycardia, agitation; use caution in cardiac/hypertensive patients.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Dogs","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Assess blood pressure, cardiac disease, restlessness and urinary obstruction before use; track continence and adverse sympathomimetic effects.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha-adrenergic effects increase urethral closure pressure for urethral sphincter mechanism incompetence; monitor BP/heart rate; caution in cardiovascular disease."],"studyGuidePages":"13:47–48","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"47","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Prominent autonomic application table","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A spayed female dog leaks urine during sleep but can void normally when awake. Another dog cannot urinate because of an obstructing stone.","decision":"For proven urethral sphincter mechanism incompetence, phenylpropanolamine may increase outlet tone; it is inappropriate as treatment for obstruction.","why":"Alpha-adrenergic agonism increases internal urethral sphincter resistance, counteracting passive leakage but potentially worsening obstructed outflow.","whatChanges":"Check BP/heart disease and differentiate true incontinence from polyuria, dysuria or retained urine before prescribing.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider alpha-adrenergic urethral support for appropriately diagnosed urethral sphincter mechanism incompetence in dogs.","physiologyWhy":"Alpha-1 adrenergic stimulation increases smooth-muscle urethral closure tone; sympathetic stimulation can also alter blood pressure and heart rate.","compareAndContrast":"Different from bethanechol (M3 stimulation of detrusor contraction) and prazosin (alpha-1 blockade facilitating outflow).","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess blood pressure, cardiac disease, restlessness and urinary obstruction before use; track continence and adverse sympathomimetic effects.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"oxybutynin","name":"Oxybutynin","brand":"","class":"Antimuscarinic • bladder","route":"Oral antimuscarinic for selected overactive bladder states; not to relieve mechanical urinary obstruction.","uses":"Detrusor overactivity and urinary urgency in selected patients; not treatment of mechanical obstruction.","moa":"Muscarinic blockade relaxes detrusor smooth muscle.","effects":"Dry mouth, constipation, tachycardia, urinary retention; assess post-voiding and obstruction risk.","why":"Mechanism: Muscarinic blockade relaxes detrusor smooth muscle. Clinically this matters for Detrusor overactivity and urinary urgency in selected patients; not treatment of mechanical obstruction. Specific risk: Dry mouth, constipation, tachycardia, urinary retention; assess post-voiding and obstruction risk.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Dogs/cats selected","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Avoid treating mechanical urinary retention as overactive detrusor; M3 blockade can worsen retention, constipation, tachycardia and dry mucosa.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Antimuscarinic drug for selected detrusor overactivity; anticholinergic constipation, urinary retention, dry mouth and ileus are considerations. Not equivalent to urethral sphincter support."],"studyGuidePages":"13:48"},{"id":"prazosin","name":"Prazosin","brand":"","class":"Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist","route":"Oral alpha-1 blocker used for selected urethral smooth-muscle relaxation; monitor BP and urine outflow.","uses":"Urethral smooth muscle relaxation where indicated; not a substitute for mechanical obstruction relief.","moa":"Blocks alpha-1-mediated smooth muscle contraction.","effects":"Hypotension, weakness; evidence for preventing recurrent feline urethral obstruction is limited.","why":"Mechanism: Blocks alpha-1-mediated smooth muscle contraction. Clinically this matters for Urethral smooth muscle relaxation where indicated; not a substitute for mechanical obstruction relief. Specific risk: Hypotension, weakness; evidence for preventing recurrent feline urethral obstruction is limited.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Cats/dogs selected","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Can cause low blood pressure/weakness; does not relieve a physical urethral plug, and routine prophylaxis after feline urethral obstruction has limited supporting evidence.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha-1 blocker lowers urethral outflow resistance in functional urethral spasm; can cause hypotension; it does not correct weak detrusor contraction."],"studyGuidePages":"13:48"},{"id":"tamsulosin","name":"Tamsulosin","brand":"","class":"Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist • uroselective","route":"Oral alpha-1 blocker with relative uroselectivity; consider hypotension and unresolved mechanical obstruction.","uses":"Functional urethral smooth muscle relaxation; evidence and prescribing are condition-specific.","moa":"Preferentially antagonizes alpha-1 receptors in urinary outflow tract.","effects":"Hypotension and dizziness/weakness; evaluate urinary tract function.","why":"Mechanism: Preferentially antagonizes alpha-1 receptors in urinary outflow tract. Clinically this matters for Functional urethral smooth muscle relaxation; evidence and prescribing are condition-specific. Specific risk: Hypotension and dizziness/weakness; evaluate urinary tract function.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Selected veterinary patients","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"May cause hypotension, dizziness/weakness; confirm urethral patency and separate functional spasm from physical obstruction.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: More uroselective alpha-1 antagonist for selected urethral outflow problems; still consider hypotension and distinguish obstruction from bladder atony."],"studyGuidePages":"13:48"},{"id":"bethanechol","name":"Bethanechol","brand":"","class":"Muscarinic agonist","route":"Oral muscarinic agonist to stimulate detrusor only after outlet patency is established; avoid active obstruction.","uses":"Atonic bladder with preserved outflow patency after appropriate assessment.","moa":"M3 receptor activation promotes detrusor contraction.","effects":"Contraindicated with urinary obstruction; diarrhea, bradycardia, salivation and abdominal pain possible.","why":"Contracts detrusor by direct muscarinic agonism; confirm patent urethra.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Dog/cat bladder; selected equine GI under supervision","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · safety details require current clinical verification · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Do not use with urinary outflow obstruction or compromised bladder","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Muscarinic agonist promotes detrusor contraction only when the urethral outflow is patent; never substitute for relief of mechanical obstruction."],"studyGuidePages":"13:48"},{"id":"tropicamide","name":"Tropicamide","brand":"","class":"Ophthalmic antimuscarinic","route":"Topical ophthalmic drops for temporary mydriasis; screen for glaucoma/angle concerns.","uses":"Short-term pupil dilation for ophthalmic examinations.","moa":"Blocks iris sphincter muscarinic activation to allow mydriasis.","effects":"Transient stinging, photophobia, elevated IOP in susceptible eyes, and uncommon systemic antimuscarinic effects with excessive absorption.","why":"Blocking iris sphincter contraction causes diagnostic pupil dilation but may impair aqueous outflow in susceptible eyes.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Dogs/cats/horses","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Assess glaucoma/iridocorneal angle risk before mydriasis; use appropriate concentration and monitor ocular comfort.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Short-acting antimuscarinic mydriatic for ophthalmic examination; ocular pigment influences onset/duration; do not use without checking glaucoma/IOP concerns."],"studyGuidePages":"13:49","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"49","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Prominent autonomic eye drug table","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog needs a retinal fundic examination, while another has ocular pain and suspected glaucoma.","decision":"Tropicamide is useful for short-duration diagnostic dilation, but suspected glaucoma warrants IOP assessment and caution with mydriatics.","why":"Muscarinic antagonism relaxes iris sphincter, causing mydriasis. It does not directly treat the painful cause and may affect aqueous outflow.","whatChanges":"Compare longer-lasting atropine cycloplegia for uveitis against short diagnostic tropicamide for examination.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Choose short-acting ophthalmic mydriasis for examination when indicated and safe for the eye.","physiologyWhy":"Muscarinic receptor blockade relaxes iris sphincter and ciliary muscle, enlarging the pupil without addressing underlying glaucoma.","compareAndContrast":"Compared with atropine, tropicamide has shorter ocular duration; unlike phenylephrine, it is antimuscarinic, not alpha-1 agonist.","monitorAndAvoid":"Check intraocular pressure/glaucoma risk and ocular disease; monitor for systemic anticholinergic effects in susceptible patients.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"atropine-ophthalmic","name":"Atropine ophthalmic","brand":"","class":"Ophthalmic antimuscarinic","route":"Topical ophthalmic atropine causes long ocular mydriasis/cycloplegia; repeated use in horses can affect GI motility.","uses":"Cycloplegia and comfort in selected anterior uveitis patients; reduces synechiae risk.","moa":"Muscarinic blockade relaxes ciliary muscle and dilates pupil.","effects":"Can raise IOP; horse GI effects/colic possible from systemic absorption. Use caution with glaucoma, decreased tear production and species sensitivity.","why":"Longer acting than tropicamide; helps uveitis pain and synechiae.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Ophthalmic cats may salivate after drops; equine systemic effects","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · safety details require current clinical verification · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Contraindicated in glaucoma; caution with KCS and equine ileus/colic risk","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Longer-acting cycloplegic/mydriatic for selected painful anterior uveitis; monitor IOP and avoid when glaucoma is suspected."],"studyGuidePages":"13:49"},{"id":"phenylephrine-ophthalmic","name":"Phenylephrine ophthalmic","brand":"","class":"Ophthalmic alpha-1 agonist","route":"Topical ophthalmic alpha-1 agonist for mydriasis without substantial cycloplegia; check ocular and systemic vascular effects.","uses":"Adjunctive mydriatic and selected ophthalmic diagnostic uses.","moa":"Contracts iris dilator smooth muscle via alpha-1 stimulation.","effects":"Local stinging, reflex ocular discomfort and systemic hypertension or tachycardia with absorption, especially higher concentrations.","why":"Alpha-1 stimulation contracts the iris dilator and vascular smooth muscle; absorption can produce systemic pressor effects.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Dogs/cats/horses selected","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Use caution with severe hypertension, significant cardiovascular disease, small patients or concurrent sympathomimetics; verify concentration and indication.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Topical alpha-1 agonist causes mydriasis without cycloplegia; can be used for selected diagnostic objectives, with cardiovascular precautions in susceptible patients."],"studyGuidePages":"13:49"},{"id":"pilocarpine-ophthalmic","name":"Pilocarpine ophthalmic","brand":"","class":"Ophthalmic muscarinic agonist","route":"Topical ophthalmic muscarinic miotic; assess current glaucoma indication and ocular inflammation before selecting.","uses":"Miosis for certain glaucoma/lens-related indications after ophthalmic assessment.","moa":"M3-mediated sphincter contraction improves conventional aqueous outflow in suitable eyes.","effects":"Brow ache/uveitis, ocular irritation; lens luxation or inflammatory glaucoma may contraindicate.","why":"Mechanism: M3-mediated sphincter contraction improves conventional aqueous outflow in suitable eyes. Clinically this matters for Miosis for certain glaucoma/lens-related indications after ophthalmic assessment. Specific risk: Brow ache/uveitis, ocular irritation; lens luxation or inflammatory glaucoma may contraindicate.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Species/indication-dependent","source":"CVM731B new slides and transcripts; current label check required","reviewStatus":"New course foundation; individual clinical audit pending · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Miotic stimulation can worsen painful uveitis or inappropriate lens position; assess lens status, glaucoma subtype, irritation and ocular inflammation.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Direct muscarinic miotic can promote aqueous outflow in selected glaucoma cases; lens luxation or uveitis changes suitability."],"studyGuidePages":"13:50"},{"id":"digoxin","name":"Digoxin","brand":"Digoxin","class":"Cardiac glycoside","route":"Oral chronic cardiac glycoside formulations; IV use only in selected supervised settings; narrow therapeutic index.","uses":"Selected cardiac conditions; specialist-supervised","moa":"Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition increases intracellular calcium via reduced Na+/Ca2+ exchange; vagal action slows AV conduction.","effects":"Anorexia, vomiting, diarrhea and potentially life-threatening dysrhythmias; hypokalemia increases toxicity.","why":"Low K+ facilitates digoxin toxicity while raised intracellular Ca2+ increases contraction.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species- and condition-specific","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Narrow therapeutic index: monitor renal function, K+, ECG and serum concentrations when indicated; avoid significant AV block without specialist guidance.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Cardiac glycoside inhibits Na+/K+-ATPase to increase myocyte calcium; narrow therapeutic margin. Monitor rhythm, renal function, electrolytes and drug concentrations when indicated; hypokalemia raises toxicity risk."],"studyGuidePages":"14:52","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/multimedia/table/cardiac-medications-of-dogs-and-cats","https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Selected rhythm/contractility problems under cardiology supervision, not a universal mainstay for every heart failure patient.","physiologyWhy":"Na/K ATPase inhibition raises intracellular Na+, reduces forward Na/Ca exchange and increases contractile Ca; vagal effects slow AV conduction.","compareAndContrast":"Pimobendan improves contractility through calcium sensitization and PDE3 rather than Na/K ATPase inhibition.","monitorAndAvoid":"Hypokalemia, renal dysfunction and interactions magnify toxicity; watch ECG, GI signs, electrolytes and concentrations as appropriate.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog on digoxin and escalating loop diuretics develops anorexia, bradyarrhythmia and low potassium.","decision":"Investigate digoxin toxicity and the potassium deficit; obtain ECG, renal/electrolyte assessment and drug levels when appropriate.","why":"Hypokalemia favors digoxin binding to Na/K-ATPase and renal dysfunction can increase exposure.","whatChanges":"Do not interpret vomiting as an isolated GI illness or assume digoxin is the standard first-line drug for any CHF patient.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"52","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Prominent cardiac comparison table","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"dobutamine","name":"Dobutamine","brand":"Dobutamine","class":"Beta-1 agonist inotrope","route":"Monitored IV beta-1 inotrope infusion in acute low-output states, with ECG and perfusion/BP monitoring.","uses":"Monitored acute cardiovascular support","moa":"Predominant beta-1 stimulation raises cAMP and myocardial calcium availability, increasing contractility.","effects":"Dose-related tachycardia, arrhythmias, increased oxygen demand and variable blood pressure.","why":"Supports low-output cardiac states but may worsen ischemia or obstructive disease.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"IV infusion with ECG and BP monitoring; correct hypovolemia and assess tachyarrhythmia/outflow obstruction first.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Primarily beta-1 inotrope used as monitored IV infusion for selected acute low-output cardiac states; watch tachyarrhythmias and perfusion."],"studyGuidePages":"14:53","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"pimobendan","name":"Pimobendan (Vetmedin)","brand":"Pimobendan","class":"Inodilator","route":"Oral inodilator (dog product); label-specific timing relative to feeding; specialist indications outside dogs.","uses":"Dogs: product-dependent FDA indications include managing CHF due to MMVD or DCM; verify stage-specific product labeling. Not a general treatment for every murmur or cardiomyopathy.","moa":"Positive inotrope and inodilator: calcium sensitization increases myocardial contractile force while phosphodiesterase-3 inhibition contributes vasodilation.","effects":"GI upset (vomiting, diarrhea), decreased appetite, lethargy and potential cardiovascular changes including arrhythmias; some adverse events in CHF trials can also reflect underlying disease.","why":"In systolic dysfunction, inotropy may improve forward flow and vasodilation can decrease workload, but increased contractility is undesirable with fixed outflow obstruction or hypertrophic obstructive disease.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Dogs under specific US product indications; feline use is off-label and requires disease-specific cardiology guidance.","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety pass 2026-11-23: see linked primary label or Merck Veterinary Manual; source lecture retained.","contraindications":"Do NOT use in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, aortic stenosis, or conditions where increasing cardiac output is inappropriate. Confirm cardiac diagnosis/stage; monitor perfusion, clinical response and tolerance.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: PDE3 inhibition plus calcium sensitization yields inodilation in dogs with specified cardiac indications. Distinguish disease/stage-specific labeled use from universal CHF therapy; monitor symptoms and arrhythmias."],"studyGuidePages":"14:53","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/multimedia/table/cardiac-medications-of-dogs-and-cats","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=cbc95ffc-0c9d-489c-b95a-959206c0f1a3"],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Choose for specific canine MMVD/DCM/CHF disease stages and label indications; not automatically for every murmur or structural lesion.","physiologyWhy":"Calcium sensitization and PDE3 inhibition improve inotropy while vasodilation reduces afterload.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike digoxin, primarily an inodilator; unlike furosemide, not principally removing excess fluid.","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess disease stage, patient pressure, perfusion, symptoms and rhythm; note species/label differences.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 53 suggests pimobendan is the only CHF drug causing vasodilation. Other cardiovascular drugs also cause vasodilation; pimobendan is specifically an inodilator with calcium-sensitizing and PDE3 effects.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog has a new low-grade murmur without staging or evidence of CHF, and the owner asks for pimobendan immediately.","decision":"Confirm the cardiac diagnosis and stage first; indications depend on structural disease and stage, not the presence of any murmur.","why":"Inodilation combines calcium sensitization and PDE3 inhibition; this is different from removing pulmonary edema with furosemide.","whatChanges":"Evaluate echocardiography/stage and hemodynamic suitability rather than broad 'CHF medication' labeling.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"53","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"lidocaine-arrhythmia","name":"Lidocaine","brand":"Lidocaine","class":"Class Ib antiarrhythmic / local anesthetic","route":"Monitored IV lidocaine for selected ventricular arrhythmias; local anesthetic infiltration or nerve block is a separate use/route.","uses":"Monitored IV treatment of certain serious ventricular tachyarrhythmias, mainly in dogs; separately used for local/regional anesthesia. Not a routine drug for supraventricular tachyarrhythmias.","moa":"Class IB sodium-channel blocker decreases ventricular ectopic excitability by targeting fast Na+ current, particularly in depolarized/ischemic ventricular tissues; peripheral local anesthetic action also blocks neuronal Na+ channels.","effects":"IV systemic toxicity may cause neurologic signs (muscle tremors, seizures, CNS depression), hypotension and rhythm changes. Cats are especially sensitive to CNS adverse effects.","why":"Blocks fast Na+ channels: stabilizes selected ventricular rhythms but excessive conduction or CNS sodium-channel blockade can cause cardiovascular or neurologic toxicity.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Dogs predominantly for antiarrhythmic IV use; use in cats is uncommon and requires greater caution.","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety pass 2026-11-23: see linked primary label or Merck Veterinary Manual; source lecture retained.","contraindications":"Continuous ECG, blood pressure and CNS monitoring for IV antiarrhythmic use. Use extra caution or alternate approaches in cats, severe hepatic dysfunction, conduction disease or seizures.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Class Ib sodium-channel blocker for selected ventricular tachyarrhythmias, typically monitored IV in dogs; not a universal treatment for atrial fibrillation. Cats can be more sensitive to neurologic toxicity."],"studyGuidePages":"14:54","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"54","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A hospitalized dog with a fast wide-complex ventricular rhythm is hypotensive; another patient has atrial fibrillation.","decision":"Evaluate hemodynamic stability and ECG classification urgently. IV lidocaine can be appropriate for selected serious ventricular arrhythmias, not as a reflex treatment for atrial fibrillation.","why":"Lidocaine is class Ib, acting mainly on inactivated sodium channels in ventricular tissue. AV nodal or atrial rhythm control may call for a different drug strategy.","whatChanges":"Monitor ECG, perfusion, neurologic toxicity and species sensitivity—cats can be more prone to lidocaine adverse effects.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use ECG-guided IV treatment for selected ventricular tachyarrhythmias, often in dogs, when clinical status warrants.","physiologyWhy":"Class Ib use-dependent blockade of cardiac fast sodium channels affects depolarization in abnormal ventricular tissue.","compareAndContrast":"Do not mistake lidocaine for AV-node-directed rate control of atrial fibrillation; mexiletine is the related orally used Class Ib option.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor rhythm, perfusion and neurologic toxicity; cats may be more sensitive to lidocaine adverse reactions.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"mexiletine","name":"Mexiletine","brand":"Mexiletine","class":"Class Ib antiarrhythmic","route":"Oral Class Ib drug for selected chronic ventricular arrhythmias; consider GI tolerance.","uses":"Oral ventricular arrhythmia management","moa":"Oral class IB fast sodium-channel blocker decreases ventricular excitability.","effects":"GI intolerance, tremor/ataxia, conduction changes or worsening arrhythmia.","why":"Ventricular sodium-channel blockade can suppress ectopy but also impair normal electrical activity.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Review ECG, liver function, conduction disease and other antiarrhythmics.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Oral class Ib agent used for selected chronic ventricular arrhythmias, sometimes paired with sotalol; GI and CNS effects warrant monitoring."],"studyGuidePages":"14:54","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"54","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog needs longer-term management of ventricular ectopy after the acute arrhythmia has been stabilized.","decision":"Consider an oral class Ib agent such as mexiletine within a cardiology-guided regimen rather than relying on repeated IV lidocaine.","why":"Both modify cardiac sodium channels, but route and pharmacokinetics make lidocaine suitable for certain monitored acute situations and mexiletine an oral maintenance option.","whatChanges":"Evaluate Holter/ECG response, GI intolerance, hepatic disease and interacting antiarrhythmics; avoid assuming suppression of isolated VPCs always improves outcome.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider oral ventricular arrhythmia management after diagnostic workup, often when chronic control is needed.","physiologyWhy":"Oral Class Ib sodium-channel blockade reduces ventricular excitability through a mechanism related to lidocaine.","compareAndContrast":"Contrast IV lidocaine for acute, monitored intervention with oral mexiletine for selected longer-term arrhythmia control.","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess ECG response, gastrointestinal intolerance, underlying heart disease, hepatic function and concurrent antiarrhythmics.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"procainamide","name":"Procainamide","brand":"Procainamide","class":"Class Ia antiarrhythmic","route":"Oral or monitored IV depending rhythm urgency; ECG and renal function alter plan.","uses":"Selected ventricular arrhythmias","moa":"Class IA sodium-channel blockade slows conduction and prolongs refractoriness.","effects":"Hypotension, QRS/QT prolongation, GI effects, and possible proarrhythmia.","why":"Excessive slowing of conduction and repolarization can itself trigger arrhythmia.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor ECG and BP; caution with conduction block, renal impairment and QT-prolonging agents.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Class Ia sodium-channel antiarrhythmic for selected atrial/ventricular arrhythmias; monitor QRS/QT, BP and proarrhythmia."],"studyGuidePages":"14:54","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"54","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • lecture tables (not opening master tables)","rationale":"Explicit ★ appears with this medication or its exact highlighted combination in the lecture table","verifyBold":"PDF text extraction does not establish bold/yellow typography"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"A dog with recurring ventricular tachyarrhythmia is evaluated for a longer-term rhythm-control drug rather than emergency IV lidocaine alone.","physiologyWhy":"Class Ia sodium-channel blockade slows impulse conduction and prolongs action-potential refractoriness; impaired cardiac conduction can worsen with excessive blockade.","compareAndContrast":"Distinguish procainamide (Class Ia, renal considerations) from mexiletine (oral Class Ib) and amiodarone (multi-channel Class III).","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess ECG and conduction block, QRS/QT changes, renal function and hypotension; do not automatically halve doses without patient-specific assessment.","provenance":"Main Exam 2 lecture table ★, course-specific clinical synthesis; prescribing requires current source verification"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Patient scenario: A dog with recurring ventricular tachyarrhythmia is evaluated for a longer-term rhythm-control drug rather than emergency IV lidocaine alone.","decision":"A dog with recurring ventricular tachyarrhythmia is evaluated for a longer-term rhythm-control drug rather than emergency IV lidocaine alone.","why":"Class Ia sodium-channel blockade slows impulse conduction and prolongs action-potential refractoriness; impaired cardiac conduction can worsen with excessive blockade.","whatChanges":"Assess ECG and conduction block, QRS/QT changes, renal function and hypotension; do not automatically halve doses without patient-specific assessment.","sourceType":"Starred-lecture-priority teaching case"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"propranolol","name":"Propranolol","brand":"Propranolol","class":"Nonselective beta blocker","route":"Oral nonselective beta blocker in selected rate-control contexts; formulation and hemodynamic response vary.","uses":"Selected tachyarrhythmias / feline hyperthyroid-associated tachycardia","moa":"Blocks beta-1 and beta-2 receptors","effects":"Bronchospasm, bradycardia, hypotension","why":"Beta blockade reduces catecholamine-mediated cardiac stimulation; nonselective beta-2 block risks bronchospasm.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Cat hyperthyroid tachycardia is a lecture example","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · safety details require current clinical verification · source-audited mechanism clarification · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Caution asthma/bronchospasm and bradycardia","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Nonselective beta-blocker for selected tachyarrhythmias and sympathetic symptoms of feline hyperthyroidism; caution with bronchospasm, bradycardia or decompensated cardiac disease."],"studyGuidePages":"14:54–57","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"54,57","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A hyperthyroid cat has marked tachycardia and a documented supraventricular tachyarrhythmia; another hyperthyroid cat primarily has systemic hypertension.","decision":"For the rhythm-dominant patient, a beta blocker may be useful after full assessment; for persistent systemic hypertension, a calcium-channel blocker such as amlodipine is often more directly appropriate.","why":"Beta-1 blockade reduces cardiac sympathetic drive and rate; amlodipine relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle and reduces systemic vascular resistance.","whatChanges":"Assess BP, ECG, asthma/bronchospasm, bradycardia and underlying thyroid control before selecting or combining treatments.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider beta blockade when sympathetic-driven tachyarrhythmia or hyperthyroid-associated tachycardia warrants rate management.","physiologyWhy":"Nonselective beta-1/beta-2 antagonism decreases cardiac rate/inotropy and can oppose beta-2 bronchodilation.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike beta-1-selective agents, nonselective propranolol raises more bronchoconstriction concern in reactive-airway patients.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor heart rate, blood pressure, perfusion; consider bronchospasm, conduction disease and diabetic masking of hypoglycemia signs.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"amiodarone","name":"Amiodarone","brand":"Amiodarone","class":"Class III antiarrhythmic","route":"Oral chronic or monitored IV antiarrhythmic depending indication; extensive interaction and organ monitoring.","uses":"Selected severe arrhythmias","moa":"Primarily class III potassium-channel blockade, with sodium, calcium and beta-adrenergic effects.","effects":"Bradycardia, hypotension, hepatotoxicity, thyroid dysfunction and potential pulmonary effects; multiple interactions.","why":"Long tissue persistence and broad channel actions explain efficacy and multi-organ risk.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Specialist, patient-specific","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor ECG, thyroid/liver parameters and interactions; caution with bradycardia, AV block and QT-prolonging agents.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Predominantly class III antiarrhythmic used for refractory serious rhythm disturbances; monitor ECG, hepatic and thyroid function and adverse reactions. Pulmonary toxicity is prominent in human literature."],"studyGuidePages":"14:55","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"55","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog continues to have sustained clinically important ventricular tachyarrhythmias despite initial management.","decision":"A cardiologist may consider amiodarone for selected refractory rhythms, balancing benefit against substantial interaction and toxicity risks.","why":"Predominant class III action lengthens repolarization/refractoriness, with additional sodium, calcium and adrenergic actions that alter conduction.","whatChanges":"Follow ECG/QT, liver values, thyroid function, bradycardia and other medication interactions; do not treat as first-line for an incidental harmless premature beat.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Reserve this broad-spectrum antiarrhythmic for selected serious rhythm disturbances when benefits outweigh substantial monitoring burden.","physiologyWhy":"Predominantly Class III potassium-channel effects extend repolarization/refractoriness, with additional sodium, calcium and beta-blocking properties.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike lidocaine/mexiletine Class Ib, amiodarone affects multiple channel systems and both atrial/ventricular rhythms.","monitorAndAvoid":"Review ECG/QT, bradyarrhythmia, drug interactions and hepatic/thyroid-related adverse effects; follow specialist protocol.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"amlodipine","name":"Amlodipine","brand":"Amlodipine","class":"Dihydropyridine Ca2+ channel blocker","route":"Oral vascular calcium-channel blocker for feline hypertension; monitor serial blood pressures.","uses":"Feline systemic hypertension","moa":"Dihydropyridine L-type calcium-channel inhibition relaxes peripheral arterioles.","effects":"Hypotension, weakness, GI signs, occasional gingival hyperplasia and reflex tachycardia.","why":"Lower resistance protects target organs but overcorrection reduces tissue perfusion.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor BP, renal/perfusion status and other antihypertensives, particularly in dehydration.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Dihydropyridine arteriolar vasodilator generally first-line for feline systemic hypertension. BP and renal monitoring; not a primary antiarrhythmic."],"studyGuidePages":"14:56–57","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"56–57","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred hypertension clinical contrast","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A cat with hyperthyroidism has repeatedly elevated systolic BP and retinal hemorrhage but no documented tachyarrhythmia.","decision":"Treat systemic hypertension promptly after assessment; amlodipine is a standard feline antihypertensive choice while addressing thyroid disease.","why":"Dihydropyridine L-type calcium-channel blockade relaxes peripheral arterioles and lowers afterload; propranolol instead primarily blocks cardiac beta receptors.","whatChanges":"Recheck BP and target organ findings, look for hypotension and kidney changes, and avoid assuming thyroid control alone will immediately correct ocular damage.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority veterinary teaching example; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use vascular-focused calcium channel blockade for feline systemic hypertension after clinical assessment.","physiologyWhy":"Dihydropyridine L-type calcium-channel inhibition relaxes arterial smooth muscle, reducing systemic vascular resistance.","compareAndContrast":"Compare diltiazem/verapamil, with greater nodal/conduction effects, and telmisartan, an AT1 receptor blocker.","monitorAndAvoid":"Trend blood pressure, kidney function, proteinuria and clinical target-organ damage; watch hypotension.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"telmisartan","name":"Telmisartan","brand":"Telmisartan","class":"AT1 receptor blocker","route":"Oral angiotensin AT1 blocker in suitable cats/dogs; confirm species-specific product, BP and renal/proteinuria assessment.","uses":"Feline hypertension/proteinuria in appropriate indications","moa":"Angiotensin-II AT1 receptor blockade reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone signaling.","effects":"Hypotension, GI upset, hyperkalemia and potentially increased creatinine.","why":"Less angiotensin II lowers pathologic intraglomerular pressure, but can impair filtration in hypoperfusion.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Check BP, renal values and K+; caution with hypovolemia, AKI, ACE inhibitors and K+-sparing agents.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: AT1 blocker for feline hypertension and selected proteinuric kidney disease; monitor BP, renal values and potassium, particularly when combined with other RAAS-blocking agents."],"studyGuidePages":"14:56"},{"id":"enalapril","name":"Enalapril","brand":"Enalapril","class":"ACE inhibitor","route":"Oral ACE inhibitor for selected cardiac/renal indications; monitor kidney parameters, BP and potassium.","uses":"Selected CHF / hypertension adjunct","moa":"ACE inhibition lowers angiotensin-II formation and aldosterone production.","effects":"Hypotension, azotemia and hyperkalemia, especially in low-perfusion states.","why":"RAAS blockade helps maladaptive remodeling but removes compensatory efferent arteriole constriction.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Measure BP, renal function, K+ and hydration; caution with spironolactone, ARBs and potassium supplementation.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: ACE inhibitor reduces angiotensin II/aldosterone; selected cardiovascular/proteinuria adjunct. Monitor creatinine, potassium, hydration and BP; caution with diuretics and NSAIDs."],"studyGuidePages":"14:56"},{"id":"sildenafil","name":"Sildenafil","brand":"Sildenafil","class":"PDE5 inhibitor","route":"Oral PDE5 inhibitor for selected pulmonary hypertension; avoid problematic nitrate and hypotension interactions.","uses":"Pulmonary hypertension","moa":"Raises vascular cGMP","effects":"Blood pressure and concurrent vasodilators","why":"PDE5 inhibition preserves cGMP-mediated pulmonary vascular relaxation.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · source-audited mechanism clarification","contraindications":"Systemic hypotension can occur, especially with nitrates or other vasodilators; assess true pulmonary vascular disease and concurrent medication exposure.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: PDE5 inhibitor maintains cGMP-mediated pulmonary vascular relaxation; selected pulmonary hypertension cases, not a general systemic antihypertensive. Monitor hypotension and underlying cardiopulmonary disease."]},{"id":"terbutaline","name":"Terbutaline","brand":"Terbutaline","class":"Beta-2 agonist","route":"Oral or injectable beta-2 agonist depending acute bronchospasm and species; monitor HR/tremor and K.","uses":"Selected feline bronchoconstriction","moa":"Beta-2 adrenergic receptor agonism raises cAMP in bronchial smooth muscle, producing bronchodilation.","effects":"Tachycardia, tremor, restlessness, transient hypokalemia and possible arrhythmias.","why":"Beta-2 relaxation relieves bronchospasm, while systemic beta activity and intracellular K+ movement cause side effects.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Use caution in tachyarrhythmia, severe heart disease or significant hypokalemia; bronchodilator does not replace anti-inflammatory airway treatment.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Beta-2 agonist for selected bronchoconstriction; systemic routes may increase tachycardia/tremor; not an anti-inflammatory substitute."],"studyGuidePages":"15:58–59"},{"id":"albuterol","name":"Albuterol","brand":"Albuterol","class":"Beta-2 agonist","route":"Inhaled rescue bronchodilator through species-appropriate chamber/nebulized device; not the sole controller for asthma.","uses":"Acute relief of bronchospasm as indicated","moa":"Short-acting beta-2 agonist raises cAMP to relax bronchial smooth muscle rapidly.","effects":"Tachycardia, agitation, tremor, hypokalemia and rarely arrhythmia with excess use.","why":"It opens constricted airways but cannot treat the underlying inflammatory trigger.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Monitor rescue frequency and heart rate; escalating use signals uncontrolled underlying airway inflammation. Use approved inhaler delivery and species-appropriate protocols.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Short-acting beta-2 bronchodilator for acute bronchospasm, commonly inhaled; monitor tachycardia, tremor and potassium, and combine with anti-inflammatory disease control as appropriate."],"studyGuidePages":"15:58–59","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"An asthmatic cat responds rapidly to inhaled albuterol but is needing rescue treatment frequently.","decision":"Recognize inadequate underlying airway control; assess inhaled glucocorticoid therapy and triggers rather than escalating rescue-only medication.","why":"Beta-2 activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle promptly but does not adequately suppress chronic airway inflammation.","whatChanges":"Monitor tachycardia, tremor and frequency of rescue use; severe respiratory distress needs urgent evaluation.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"58–59","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred bronchodilator group","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use inhaled short-acting rescue bronchodilation for bronchospasm in suitable patients, including cats with asthma.","physiologyWhy":"Beta-2 receptor activation raises cAMP in bronchial smooth muscle, promoting relaxation quickly.","compareAndContrast":"Contrasts with inhaled corticosteroids, which control chronic airway inflammation; rescue-only use does not address disease control.","monitorAndAvoid":"Track rescue frequency, tachycardia, tremor and possible potassium shifts; escalating need warrants reassessment.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"clenbuterol","name":"Clenbuterol","brand":"Clenbuterol","class":"Beta-2 agonist","route":"Oral equine beta-2 agonist where regionally labeled; verify competition and food-animal restrictions.","uses":"Equine airway disease","moa":"Beta-2 receptor agonism relaxes equine bronchial smooth muscle.","effects":"Tachycardia, tremor, sweating/agitation and potentially clinically important hypokalemia; exercise/competition restrictions.","why":"Bronchodilation improves airflow but adrenergic activation can stress the cardiovascular system.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Check region-specific equine label, competition restrictions and food-producing status; caution with cardiovascular disease.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Equine beta-2 bronchodilator (Ventipulmin) with tachycardia, tremors, sweating and restlessness at excessive exposure; also impacts smooth muscle beyond airway."],"studyGuidePages":"15:59","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A horse with recurrent airway disease becomes sweaty and tachycardic after an increased clenbuterol dose.","decision":"Suspect systemic beta-adrenergic effects and reassess dose and underlying inflammatory airway management.","why":"Loss of receptor selectivity at higher exposure produces beta-1 cardiac stimulation in addition to beta-2 bronchodilation.","whatChanges":"Monitor HR, tremors, sweating and respiratory benefit; distinguish bronchodilation from treating airway inflammation.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"59–61","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred airway and beta-agonist comparison","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Recognize the lecture's equine bronchodilator context and check regional product/competition regulations before discussing use.","physiologyWhy":"Beta-2 adrenergic agonism relaxes smooth muscle, though airway inflammation still needs independent management.","compareAndContrast":"Compare short-acting albuterol rescue and long-term inflammatory control; beta agonists do not eradicate inflammatory airway disease.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor cardiovascular stimulation, agitation/tremor and legal restrictions in competition or food-producing animals.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"theophylline","name":"Theophylline","brand":"Theophylline","class":"Methylxanthine","route":"Oral methylxanthine bronchodilator, including extended-release products; narrow therapeutic margin and interactions.","uses":"Selected airway conditions","moa":"Methylxanthine with phosphodiesterase inhibition and adenosine-receptor antagonism, contributing bronchodilation.","effects":"Nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, arrhythmias, agitation and seizures in toxicity.","why":"Increasing cAMP promotes bronchodilation, but systemic CNS and cardiac stimulation limits safe dosing.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Narrow therapeutic margin and significant drug interactions; evaluate concurrent medications, hepatic disease and seizure/arrhythmia risk.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Methylxanthine bronchodilator with narrow therapeutic index: nausea, excitation, tachycardia and seizures with toxicity. Formulation and interactions require monitoring."],"studyGuidePages":"15:60"},{"id":"ipratropium","name":"Ipratropium","brand":"Ipratropium","class":"Muscarinic antagonist","route":"Inhaled muscarinic blocker for airway bronchodilation; appropriate delivery device and underlying disease matter.","uses":"Inhaled bronchodilation in suitable patients","moa":"Inhaled muscarinic receptor antagonist blocks acetylcholine-mediated bronchoconstriction.","effects":"Dry mouth/secretions, occasional paradoxical bronchospasm, ocular irritation or mydriasis if aerosol enters eyes.","why":"Blocking parasympathetic airway tone relaxes bronchi, but also reduces glandular secretions.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Prevent exposure of eyes, especially in glaucoma patients; does not treat airway inflammation or replace other indicated therapy.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Poorly systemically absorbed inhaled antimuscarinic bronchodilator; less systemic anticholinergic activity than atropine, but may be adjunct rather than sole asthma management."],"studyGuidePages":"15:61"},{"id":"hydrocodone","name":"Hydrocodone","brand":"Hydrocodone","class":"Mu opioid agonist / antitussive","route":"Oral opioid antitussive products; check combination ingredients, controlled-drug rules and cough appropriateness.","uses":"Selected nonproductive debilitating cough","moa":"Central cough suppression","effects":"Sedation, respiratory depression and controlled-drug rules","why":"Centrally mediated suppression of nonproductive cough, requiring evaluation of cause.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · source-audited mechanism clarification · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Assess airway disease, respiratory depression, other sedatives, controlled-drug restrictions and combination syrup ingredients.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Central mu-opioid antitussive for selected troublesome nonproductive cough; avoid indiscriminately suppressing productive cough; sedation, constipation and controlled-substance precautions."],"studyGuidePages":"15:62","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"62,140","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star in opioid table","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog has a nonproductive cough that prevents sleep; another dog has pneumonia and needs to clear airway secretions.","decision":"Consider targeted antitussive therapy only after evaluating cause and the usefulness of cough; avoid indiscriminately suppressing a productive protective cough.","why":"Mu opioid activity suppresses the central cough reflex, which can improve comfort but may impair clearance of infectious secretions.","whatChanges":"Review sedation, constipation, respiratory status, other CNS depressants, controlled-substance rules and whether combination syrup includes homatropine.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider opioid-mediated cough suppression only in carefully selected patients after establishing whether suppressing cough is appropriate.","physiologyWhy":"Mu opioid effects in central cough pathways reduce cough reflex; opioid adverse effects include sedation and constipation.","compareAndContrast":"Differentiate hydrocodone antitussive use from bronchodilators for bronchospasm and antibiotics for bacterial infection.","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess airway disease, respiratory depression, other sedatives, controlled-drug restrictions and combination syrup ingredients.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"doxapram","name":"Doxapram","brand":"Doxapram","class":"Respiratory stimulant","route":"Parenteral respiratory stimulant in monitored clinical circumstances; cannot replace intubation/ventilation.","uses":"Selected monitored respiratory stimulation","moa":"Stimulates peripheral carotid-body chemoreceptors and respiratory drive.","effects":"Agitation, tachycardia, hypertension, tremors and seizures; can increase respiratory effort without effective ventilation.","why":"Respiratory drive stimulation cannot overcome a physically obstructed airway or exhausted respiratory muscles.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Not a replacement for airway patency, oxygenation or mechanical ventilation. Avoid or use great caution in seizure-prone patients.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Stimulates peripheral carotid chemoreceptors/respiratory drive in selected monitored settings; does not replace ventilation or CPR."],"studyGuidePages":"15:63"},{"id":"furosemide","name":"Furosemide","brand":"Furosemide","class":"Loop diuretic","route":"Oral chronic or IV/IM acute diuresis depending patient/product; route and species restrictions vary.","uses":"CHF-associated edema; selected other labeled indications","moa":"Loop diuretic inhibits NKCC2 in thick ascending limb, increasing sodium/chloride and water loss.","effects":"Dehydration, azotemia, hypokalemia, hypochloremia, metabolic alkalosis and rare ototoxicity.","why":"Reduced circulating volume helps edema but can trigger prerenal compromise.","lecture":"Lecture 16","species":"Dog/cat, horse and cattle indications differ","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Assess volume, electrolytes, renal perfusion and congestion; avoid unnecessary diuresis in dehydration.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Loop NKCC2 inhibitor for CHF pulmonary edema and selected species-specific edema/EIPH indications; watch volume depletion, azotemia, hypokalemic/hypochloremic alkalosis and ototoxicity with rapid high IV doses/aminoglycosides."],"studyGuidePages":"16:64–66","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/diuretics-for-use-in-animals"],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Choose for fluid overload/pulmonary edema, not for all cough or undifferentiated low urine output.","physiologyWhy":"Loop NKCC2 blockade decreases sodium recovery and medullary gradient, producing potent diuresis.","compareAndContrast":"Torsemide has longer/potent action; spironolactone blocks aldosterone with weaker stand-alone natriuresis.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor respiratory rate/effort, weight, creatinine/BUN, potassium/chloride, hydration and blood pressure; overdiuresis can worsen perfusion.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 65 gives withdrawal-time numbers for food-producing species; never apply a generic 48-hour meat/milk withdrawal to every product/species. Verify specific current label and legal rules.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog with CHF is breathing easier after diuresis, but creatinine and BUN rise while hydration falls.","decision":"Reassess volume/perfusion and diuretic plan rather than reflexively increasing the dose because there is still cough.","why":"NKCC2 inhibition removes sodium/water; overdiuresis reduces effective circulating volume and renal perfusion.","whatChanges":"Track resting respiratory rate, effort, weight, BP, renal indices, potassium and chloride—not just the presence of urine.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"64–66","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred diuretic group and principles","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"torsemide","name":"Torsemide","brand":"Torsemide","class":"Loop diuretic","route":"Oral potent loop diuretic for selected chronic cardiac congestion, with electrolytes/renal monitoring.","uses":"Selected canine CHF-associated fluid overload","moa":"Longer acting, potent loop diuretic that inhibits NKCC2.","effects":"Overdiuresis, electrolyte depletion, azotemia and hypotension.","why":"Sustained diuresis improves congestion but excess fluid removal decreases perfusion.","lecture":"Lecture 16","species":"Dog CHF; current indication/label specific","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor weight, hydration, renal values and electrolytes; not interchangeable mg-for-mg with furosemide.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Longer-acting, more potent loop diuretic for selected canine CHF, often when control with furosemide is difficult. Not unit-equivalent to furosemide; check renal function and electrolytes about a week after initiation or change."],"studyGuidePages":"16:65–66","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/diuretics-for-use-in-animals"],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider for selected canine heart failure with fluid retention, often after reassessing furosemide response.","physiologyWhy":"Like furosemide blocks NKCC2, but pharmacokinetics/duration and potency differ; never convert unit-for-unit.","compareAndContrast":"Overdiuresis may worsen azotemia and electrolyte disorders despite clinical improvement in edema.","monitorAndAvoid":"Track resting respiration, weight, renal markers, blood pressure and electrolytes following initiation/titration.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"}},{"id":"spironolactone","name":"Spironolactone","brand":"Spironolactone","class":"Aldosterone antagonist","route":"Oral mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, usually adjunctive rather than sole emergency diuretic.","uses":"Selected CHF adjunct and K-sparing therapy","moa":"Mineralocorticoid receptor blockade opposes aldosterone-mediated sodium retention and potassium excretion.","effects":"Hyperkalemia, GI upset and worsened renal indices with poor perfusion.","why":"Blocks maladaptive aldosterone effects while conserving potassium.","lecture":"Lecture 16","species":"Primarily companion animals; verify food-animal restrictions","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Check K+ and renal function especially with ACE inhibitors, ARBs or K+ supplements.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Mineralocorticoid-receptor blocker, weak alone as diuretic but adjunct for RAAS/aldosterone effects in CHF; may cause hyperkalemia (especially ACE inhibitor combination) and feline facial dermatitis."],"studyGuidePages":"16:65–66","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/diuretics-for-use-in-animals"],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Adjunct for certain CHF/RAAS-mediated syndromes, not a strong solitary rescue diuretic for acute pulmonary edema.","physiologyWhy":"Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism reduces aldosterone-driven sodium retention, potassium excretion and tissue effects.","compareAndContrast":"ACE inhibition reduces angiotensin II but aldosterone breakthrough can occur; combined potassium retention raises risk.","monitorAndAvoid":"Watch potassium, renal function and blood pressure, especially with ACEi/ARB or renal compromise.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog with CHF is already taking enalapril and has borderline elevated potassium when spironolactone is considered.","decision":"Check renal function and potassium closely and reconsider potassium-retaining combinations if hyperkalemia develops.","why":"ACE inhibition decreases aldosterone production; spironolactone blocks residual aldosterone action, reducing K excretion.","whatChanges":"Weak stand-alone natriuresis means it will not substitute for loop diuresis in acute pulmonary edema.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"65–66","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicitly starred ACE-inhibitor comparison","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"hydrochlorothiazide","name":"Hydrochlorothiazide","brand":"Hydrochlorothiazide","class":"Thiazide diuretic","route":"Oral thiazide often used as an adjunct to loop diuresis; monitor electrolytes, hydration and renal perfusion.","uses":"Selected resistant edema/diuretic combinations","moa":"Inhibits distal Na-Cl transporter","effects":"Electrolyte depletion; species/product limitations","why":"Distal NaCl cotransporter blockade causes diuresis with electrolyte effects; not as potent as a loop agent in many edema settings.","lecture":"Lecture 16","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · detailed Lecture 16/17 mechanism comparison · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Risk hyponatremia, hypokalemia, dehydration and azotemia, especially with loop diuretics; monitor electrolytes/volume status.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Distal Na-Cl cotransporter blocker for selected refractory edema in combination; monitor K/Na, renal function, dehydration and acid-base shifts. Thiazides increase calcium retention."],"studyGuidePages":"16:65","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/diuretics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"mannitol","name":"Mannitol","brand":"Mannitol","class":"Osmotic agent","route":"Monitored IV osmotic agent for selected cerebral/intraocular indications; avoid indiscriminate use in volume-overloaded patients.","uses":"Selected intracranial/intraocular pressure emergencies","moa":"Retains osmotic water in renal tubular lumen","effects":"Volume overload, kidney function and monitored administration","why":"Mechanism: Retains osmotic water in renal tubular lumen Clinically this matters for Selected intracranial/intraocular pressure emergencies Specific risk: Volume overload, kidney function and monitored administration","lecture":"Lecture 16","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Rapid IV osmotic expansion may worsen pulmonary edema or heart failure; avoid unmonitored administration in anuric or volume-overloaded patients.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: IV osmotic agent for selected intracranial/intraocular pressure crises; can cause transient intravascular volume expansion and worsen pulmonary edema, particularly in CHF."],"studyGuidePages":"16:64"},{"id":"acetazolamide","name":"Acetazolamide","brand":"Acetazolamide","class":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","route":"Oral/injectable carbonic anhydrase inhibitor; monitor metabolic acidosis, potassium and volume status.","uses":"Selected glaucoma / acid-base conditions","moa":"Inhibits proximal tubular carbonic anhydrase","effects":"Metabolic acidosis, electrolyte changes","why":"Proximal carbonic-anhydrase inhibition increases bicarbonate excretion and can produce metabolic acidosis.","lecture":"Lecture 16","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · detailed Lecture 16/17 mechanism comparison · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Bicarbonate loss can cause metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia and alkaline urine/urolith risk; consider renal function and respiratory acidosis.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor decreases aqueous humor and bicarbonate reabsorption; consider glaucoma/selected metabolic alkalosis. Can cause metabolic acidosis and hypokalemia."],"studyGuidePages":"16:64"},{"id":"metoclopramide","name":"Metoclopramide","brand":"Metoclopramide","class":"D2 antagonist / prokinetic","route":"Oral/injectable or monitored infusion for selected antiemetic/prokinetic indications; confirm no obstruction.","uses":"Selected vomiting and gastric hypomotility","moa":"Dopamine D2 antagonism in the chemoreceptor trigger zone plus enteric prokinetic effects, partly via 5-HT4 activity.","effects":"Behavioral changes/extrapyramidal signs, agitation, sedation, diarrhea and potential drug interactions.","why":"Increases upper GI motility and blocks dopamine-linked emesis; forceful contraction against obstruction can injure the gut.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Selected dog/cat uses","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Avoid in mechanical GI obstruction, perforation or GI hemorrhage; use caution with seizure disorders and interacting dopamine antagonists.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Dopamine D2 antagonist and 5-HT4 prokinetic for selected upper GI hypomotility/antiemetic cases; do not use with mechanical GI obstruction; extrapyramidal effects and species differences matter."],"studyGuidePages":"17:68–71","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use for selected upper-GI hypomotility and emesis contexts only AFTER excluding mechanical obstruction.","physiologyWhy":"D2 antagonism has antiemetic effects; 5-HT4-related cholinergic prokinetic actions increase motility.","compareAndContrast":"Maropitant blocks NK1/substance P; ondansetron blocks 5-HT3; none excludes a surgical abdomen.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor neurologic/extrapyramidal signs, response and obstruction concerns; cats may respond differently at the CRTZ.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A vomiting dog has abdominal distension, painful palpation and radiographs concerning for intestinal obstruction.","decision":"Do not give a prokinetic simply to move contents along; exclude or address the mechanical obstruction first.","why":"Enhancing coordinated GI contraction against an obstruction can worsen distension or tissue injury.","whatChanges":"Compare maropitant (NK1 antiemetic) and ondansetron (5-HT3 antiemetic) without mistaking symptom control for definitive treatment.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"68–73","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred antiemetic and prokinetic topics","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"maropitant","name":"Maropitant","brand":"Maropitant","class":"NK1 antagonist","route":"Oral tablets or injectable veterinary antiemetic; verify age/species-specific labeling.","uses":"Vomiting prevention/treatment","moa":"Neurokinin-1 receptor antagonist blocks substance P signaling in central emetic pathways.","effects":"Injection discomfort, lethargy, diarrhea or decreased appetite; hepatic impairment may alter clearance.","why":"Suppressing the vomiting center is not equivalent to treating the trigger or abdominal pain.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Do not let symptom suppression delay diagnosis of obstruction, toxin exposure or serious abdominal pain; assess hepatic function and product-route restrictions.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: NK1/substance-P antagonist with broad antiemetic use in dogs and cats; vomiting control does not always resolve nausea, underlying disease or aspiration risk."],"studyGuidePages":"17:68–70","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Broad-spectrum antiemetic in many dog/cat vomiting contexts while investigating underlying disease.","physiologyWhy":"NK1 blockade prevents substance P signaling near the final common emetic pathway.","compareAndContrast":"Ondansetron preferentially affects 5-HT3-mediated pathways; metoclopramide adds prokinetic activity.","monitorAndAvoid":"An antiemetic does not prove pain, obstruction or underlying nausea has resolved; examine the patient.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog stops vomiting after maropitant, but abdominal pain and progressive distension remain.","decision":"Continue evaluation for obstructive/surgical disease; stopping emesis does not resolve the cause.","why":"NK1 blockade interrupts substance P signaling in the emetic pathway but does not restore intestinal patency.","whatChanges":"Repeat exam and appropriate imaging if obstruction is suspected rather than discharging solely based on no vomiting.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"68–70","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred antiemetic topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"ondansetron","name":"Ondansetron","brand":"Ondansetron","class":"5-HT3 antagonist","route":"Oral/injectable 5-HT3 antagonist for selected emetic etiologies; monitor QT risk and interactions where relevant.","uses":"Selected severe/refractory nausea/vomiting","moa":"5-HT3 receptor antagonist blocks serotonergic vagal/central emetic signaling.","effects":"Constipation or diarrhea, occasional sedation and potential QT-related rhythm concern in predisposed patients.","why":"Serotonin blockade decreases emesis but does not improve intestinal obstruction or restore hydration.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Review prolonged QT, severe electrolyte abnormalities, concurrent QT-prolonging agents and patient-specific dosing.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: 5-HT3 antagonist especially relevant to chemotherapy/uremia-associated nausea and vomiting; consider QT/interactions and does not address all motion-sickness pathways."],"studyGuidePages":"17:68–70","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Useful for selected severe nausea and chemotherapy-associated/uremic emesis; mechanism differs from maropitant.","physiologyWhy":"Blocks 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents and in central emetic pathways.","compareAndContrast":"Neither an NK1 inhibitor nor a D2 prokinetic; different causes of vomiting respond differently.","monitorAndAvoid":"Review drug interactions and QT/conduction issues in susceptible patients.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"}},{"id":"cisapride","name":"Cisapride","brand":"Cisapride","class":"5-HT4 agonist prokinetic","route":"Oral prokinetic for selected GI dysmotility; exclude obstruction and consider QT/interacting drugs.","uses":"Selected GI hypomotility","moa":"5-HT4 agonism increases acetylcholine release from myenteric neurons, promoting gastrointestinal motility.","effects":"Diarrhea or abdominal cramping; potentially important drug interactions including QT-prolongation risks.","why":"Increasing propulsive GI activity can worsen physical obstruction; altered cardiac repolarization creates interaction concerns.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Compounded veterinary use","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Do not use when mechanical obstruction, perforation or GI hemorrhage is suspected; assess interacting medications and compounded-product quality.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: 5-HT4 prokinetic for selected feline constipation/megacolon or GI hypomotility; compounded availability and cardiac/QT interacting drugs require attention; avoid mechanical obstruction."],"studyGuidePages":"17:70–73"},{"id":"omeprazole","name":"Omeprazole","brand":"Omeprazole","class":"Proton pump inhibitor","route":"Oral proton-pump inhibitor; acid suppression depends on appropriate formulation and timing of repeated doses.","uses":"Acid-mediated ulcers","moa":"Irreversibly inhibits parietal-cell gastric H+/K+-ATPase, strongly decreasing acid secretion.","effects":"GI signs (vomiting/diarrhea); chronic unnecessary suppression may alter gastric physiology and microbiome.","why":"Acid-pump inhibition supports ulcer healing but does not address every cause of vomiting.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Use for established or strongly suspected acid-related disease rather than routine antiemesis; review long-term therapy and discontinuation approach.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Proton pump inhibitor for appropriate ulcer/esophagitis/acid-suppression indications; not routine for every vomiting patient. Repeated dosing/appropriate timing affects acid suppression."],"studyGuidePages":"17:73–76"},{"id":"sucralfate","name":"Sucralfate","brand":"Sucralfate","class":"Mucosal protectant","route":"Oral mucosal protectant (suspension/tablets); separate from interacting oral drugs because adsorption reduces absorption.","uses":"Selected GI mucosal injury","moa":"In acidic environments forms a viscous protective complex that binds ulcerated gastrointestinal mucosa.","effects":"Constipation and impaired absorption of some oral drugs (e.g., selected antibiotics and thyroid medications).","why":"Local coating protects lesions but can physically bind other medications and lower their absorption.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Use may vary","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Separate timing from drugs with adsorption/chelation interactions; use caution with significant renal dysfunction because of aluminum content.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Mucosal protectant forming local barrier at ulcerated tissue; separate administration from interacting oral drugs to avoid impaired absorption; not a standalone treatment for all GI disease."],"studyGuidePages":"17:73–75"},{"id":"misoprostol","name":"Misoprostol","brand":"Misoprostol","class":"PGE1 analog","route":"Oral prostaglandin E1 analog for selected GI mucosal protection; pregnancy exposure hazard and diarrhea.","uses":"Selected NSAID-associated ulcer management","moa":"Prostaglandin E1 analog increases mucosal mucus/bicarbonate production and reduces acid secretion.","effects":"Diarrhea, abdominal cramping and uterine contractions; possible abortion.","why":"Replacement of prostaglandin activity supports NSAID-associated gastric defense but also stimulates uterine smooth muscle.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Use varies","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Contraindicated in pregnancy; appropriate human handler precautions are essential, especially for pregnant people.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: PGE1 analog used for prevention/treatment of selected NSAID-associated GI mucosal injury; diarrhea and pregnancy/uterotonic precautions are major contraindication concerns."],"studyGuidePages":"17:73–76"},{"id":"lactulose","name":"Lactulose","brand":"Lactulose","class":"Nonabsorbable disaccharide","route":"Oral or rectal route for hepatic encephalopathy/constipation depending patient; titrate to effect and monitor dehydration.","uses":"Hepatic encephalopathy; constipation","moa":"Nonabsorbable disaccharide fermented into colonic acids; lowers colonic pH, trapping ammonia as NH4+, and acts as osmotic laxative.","effects":"Flatulence, cramping, diarrhea, dehydration and electrolyte abnormalities with excessive treatment.","why":"Acidic colon shifts absorbable NH3 to less-absorbable NH4+, reducing ammonia entry into circulation.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","contraindications":"Titrate to appropriate soft-stool target and monitor hydration/electrolytes; evaluate hepatic encephalopathy triggers.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Nonabsorbable disaccharide acidifies colon and traps NH3 as NH4+ in hepatic encephalopathy; excessive doses cause diarrhea/dehydration and electrolyte changes."],"studyGuidePages":"17:76–79"},{"id":"fuzapladib","name":"Fuzapladib (Panoquell-CA1)","brand":"Fuzapladib (Panoquell-CA1)","class":"LFA-1 inhibitor","route":"IV only in dogs; label dosing is once daily for three consecutive days. Reconstitute only with supplied diluent; use alongside standard pancreatitis supportive care.","uses":"Management of clinical signs associated with acute-onset pancreatitis in dogs (FDA conditional approval; not prevention or a cure).","moa":"Selective inhibitor of LFA-1 activation, decreasing inflammatory-cell adhesion and migration into injured tissues.","effects":"Reported in studies: GI signs (including vomiting/regurgitation), respiratory signs and urine abnormalities; events also occurred in control dogs. Laboratory safety findings included injection-site swelling/bruising and hypertension. Foreign post-approval reports include facial/tongue swelling, collapse and seizures; causality is not established for every report.","why":"Blocking LFA-1 activation reduces leukocyte adhesion and migration; this targets inflammation associated with pancreatitis, but does not replace fluids, analgesia, antiemetics or nutritional support. It is not a monoclonal antibody.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Dogs only, IV, under the US conditional-approval label.","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · detailed Lecture 16/17 mechanism comparison · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Do not use in dogs with known hypersensitivity to fuzapladib. Safety has not been evaluated in dogs under six months, pregnant/lactating/breeding dogs, or dogs with cardiac disease, hepatic failure or renal impairment. Use only as directed under its conditional-approval label.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: LFA-1 inhibitor (Panoquell-CA1), IV and conditionally FDA-approved for management of clinical signs of acute-onset pancreatitis in dogs; not a monoclonal antibody or universal cure."],"studyGuidePages":"17:76–79","clinicalRefs":["https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/13134","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=e2e316bd-ff8b-4d14-a2e8-c518f8b153cb"],"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"79","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred pancreas/liver section","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog is hospitalized with acute-onset pancreatitis, vomiting and inflammatory clinical signs; the owner asks whether a new injection cures pancreatitis.","decision":"Consider fuzapladib only within its label-specific indication and alongside fluids, analgesia, nutrition and complication monitoring—not as replacement therapy.","why":"LFA-1 inhibition targets leukocyte activation/adhesion, modifying aspects of the inflammatory process rather than removing the pancreatic trigger.","whatChanges":"Verify current conditional approval and duration/eligibility; track pain, perfusion, appetite, emesis and systemic complications.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Discuss the role of canine pancreatitis treatment within the precise conditional approval/label indication, without treating it as a cure for all pancreatitis.","physiologyWhy":"Inhibition of leukocyte function-associated antigen-1 (LFA-1) reduces neutrophil recruitment/adhesion and inflammatory injury.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike maropitant/ondansetron antiemetics or IV fluids, this targets leukocyte inflammation rather than replacing supportive care.","monitorAndAvoid":"Confirm label and patient criteria, monitor GI signs, hydration, perfusion and clinical trajectory; the course's class claim is not universal across species.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"selegiline","name":"Selegiline (Anipryl)","brand":"Selegiline (Anipryl)","class":"MAO-B inhibitor","route":"Oral MAO-B inhibitor used for canine cognitive dysfunction; significant interaction and washout cautions.","uses":"Canine cognitive dysfunction syndrome","moa":"Irreversible MAO-B inhibition reduces dopamine breakdown at therapeutic doses; selectivity is dose-dependent.","effects":"GI upset, restlessness, agitation or other neurologic/behavioral effects; dangerous serotonergic interactions possible.","why":"Reduced monoamine degradation may support cognitive signs but increases risk of excessive monoamine activity with interacting drugs.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Dogs with cognitive dysfunction","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Avoid concomitant SSRIs, TCAs, other MAO inhibitors and serotonergic agents; follow drug-specific washout intervals and product labeling.","veterinaryRelevance":"Established veterinary indication","veterinaryContext":"MAO-B inhibitor used for canine cognitive dysfunction; serotonergic combinations demand caution.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): MAO-B inhibitor for canine cognitive dysfunction; screen all serotonergic drugs, especially fluoxetine, clomipramine and trazodone. Follow required washout rather than co-prescribing automatically."],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Canine cognitive dysfunction syndrome after ruling out pain, sensory loss, systemic and neurologic mimics.","physiologyWhy":"MAO-B inhibition slows monoamine degradation; clinical response requires more than increasing a transmitter.","compareAndContrast":"Fluoxetine/clomipramine block reuptake and can be hazardous with MAO inhibition; switching requires washout.","monitorAndAvoid":"Document sleep-wake and behavior changes; assess other prescriptions before adding serotonergic medications.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"An older dog with disorientation has been given trazodone and is now considered for selegiline.","decision":"Check serotonergic combinations and washout before starting selegiline; evaluate pain, sensory disease and systemic mimics of cognitive dysfunction.","why":"MAO-B inhibition alters monoamine degradation, while trazodone has serotonergic activity.","whatChanges":"Behavioral improvement needs clinical follow-up and environmental support; avoid treating every age-related behavior as CCD.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"88,96,115","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicitly highlighted canine CCD drug","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"clomipramine","name":"Clomipramine (Clomicalm)","brand":"Clomipramine (Clomicalm)","class":"Tricyclic antidepressant","route":"Oral tricyclic antidepressant; behavior modification and delayed onset are part of plan.","uses":"Canine separation-related anxiety with behavior modification","moa":"Inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake","effects":"Cardiac, anticholinergic, seizure and interaction cautions","why":"TCA reuptake inhibition with extra anticholinergic/cardiovascular cautions; combine with behavior modification.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Dogs for separation-related anxiety; check cat off-label use","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · safety details require current clinical verification · neuro lecture distinction checked · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","contraindications":"Cardiovascular/conduction concerns, glaucoma, MAOI/serotonergic interactions","veterinaryRelevance":"Established veterinary indication","veterinaryContext":"TCA used for canine separation-related problems as part of a behavior-modification plan.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): TCA for canine separation-related problems with a behavior plan. Anticholinergic and cardiovascular effects and serotonergic interactions matter; do not abruptly combine with SSRI/MAOI."],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Dog separation-related anxiety as part of planned environmental/behavior modification, not as a stand-alone cure.","physiologyWhy":"TCA inhibiting SERT and NET with additional anticholinergic and cardiac effects.","compareAndContrast":"Fluoxetine is an SSRI; drugs are not equivalent across adverse effects, washout and comorbidity.","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess appetite, urinary/GI effects, cardiac disease, concomitant serotonergics and treatment response over weeks.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"90–101","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred reuptake class topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog vocalizes and damages doors only when left alone. The owner asks whether one dose of clomipramine before departure will stop the behavior.","decision":"Use a structured separation-related behavior plan. Clomipramine is a long-term treatment option, not a single-dose departure tranquilizer.","why":"TCA inhibition of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake produces gradual adaptation. Its antimuscarinic and cardiac effects influence patient selection.","whatChanges":"Screen cardiac/urinary/GI issues and serotonergic combinations, track absences and behavior, and allow time for therapeutic effects.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"}},{"id":"fluoxetine","name":"Fluoxetine (Reconcile)","brand":"Fluoxetine (Reconcile)","class":"SSRI","route":"Oral SSRI formulation, with long washout and delayed onset; behavior plan essential.","uses":"Selected canine separation-related and other behavior disorders","moa":"Selective serotonin reuptake transporter inhibition increases synaptic serotonin; clinical behavior response is delayed.","effects":"GI upset, appetite changes, restlessness, sedation and possible serotonin toxicity with interacting medications.","why":"Transporter blockade changes serotonergic signaling over weeks, not immediately; overlapping serotonergic drugs can be dangerous.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Dogs; selected cats/horses off label","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Avoid MAOIs including selegiline; account for long-lasting active metabolite and extended washout. Combine with behavior modification, monitor seizures and appetite.","veterinaryRelevance":"Established veterinary indication","veterinaryContext":"SSRI with dog-specific labeling for separation-related disorders; other uses depend on species and indication.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): SSRI with dog-labeled separation-anxiety formulation; delayed onset and long active-metabolite elimination affect switching/washout. Monitor appetite, agitation, interactions and behavior response."],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Selected canine separation-related behavior disorder with behavior therapy; other species/uses can be extra-label.","physiologyWhy":"SSRI blocks serotonin reuptake; chronic adaptation delays behavioral effects.","compareAndContrast":"Selegiline is an MAOI, clomipramine a TCA, trazodone a serotonin antagonist/reuptake inhibitor—combining/switching must be deliberate.","monitorAndAvoid":"Track target behaviors and possible activation, GI changes, interaction effects and prolonged washout due to active metabolites.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog taking fluoxetine for separation anxiety is also prescribed selegiline for cognitive changes.","decision":"Do not combine without a proper plan and washout; assess serotonin-toxicity risk and choose alternatives.","why":"SSRI inhibition of serotonin reuptake plus MAO inhibition can raise monoamine signaling dangerously.","whatChanges":"Evaluate target behaviors, comorbid pain/cognitive disease and all medications before adjusting therapy.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"90,110","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star at Reconcile and SSRIs","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"paroxetine","name":"Paroxetine","brand":"Paroxetine","class":"SSRI","route":"Oral SSRI; chronic behavioral plan and careful withdrawal, serotonin-interaction and pregnancy considerations.","uses":"Selected off-label behavior indications","moa":"Inhibits serotonin reuptake","effects":"Drug interactions, tapering and reproductive considerations","why":"Mechanism: Inhibits serotonin reuptake Clinically this matters for Selected off-label behavior indications Specific risk: Drug interactions, tapering and reproductive considerations","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","contraindications":"Avoid MAO inhibitors and unsafe serotonergic combinations; watch discontinuation effects, GI adverse effects and pregnancy-related concerns.","veterinaryRelevance":"Selected extra-label veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"SSRI class member; distinguish from dog-labeled fluoxetine and consider clinical context.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): SSRI used extra-label in selected behavior patients; differs in pharmacokinetics/withdrawal from fluoxetine. Review comorbidity and drug interactions."]},{"id":"buspirone","name":"Buspirone","brand":"Buspirone","class":"5-HT1A partial agonist","route":"Oral serotonergic partial agonist for selected behavior disorders, not rapid procedural sedation.","uses":"Selected anxiety-related behaviors","moa":"Modulates serotonergic signaling","effects":"Not an immediate rescue sedative; assess interactions","why":"5-HT1A partial agonism is not equivalent to immediate sedation and requires context for anxiety use.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · neuro lecture distinction checked · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","contraindications":"Evaluate behavioral context, co-medications and serotonergic interaction risk; measure behavioral outcomes over follow-up.","veterinaryRelevance":"Selected extra-label veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"5-HT1A partial agonist; limited role in selected anxiety cases, not acute restraint or sedation.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): 5-HT1A partial agonist for selected chronic anxieties or feline social behavior problems; not a rapid tranquilizer. Monitor disinhibition and serotonergic combinations."],"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"91,103","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred 5-HT receptor class topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A cat persistently urine-marks during household social conflict but is otherwise medically well; the owner wants a drug to stop marking tonight.","decision":"First investigate medical and environmental causes; buspirone may be selected for certain chronic fear/social patterns, but is not a rapid rescue sedative.","why":"Partial agonism at 5-HT1A modifies serotonergic signaling over time and differs from acute GABA-A benzodiazepine effects.","whatChanges":"Watch changes in sociability, disinhibition or aggression and review concurrent serotonergic drugs; monitor the specific target behavior.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider for selected anxiety presentations when delayed-onset maintenance therapy may be appropriate; not a rapid rescue sedative.","physiologyWhy":"Partial agonism at 5-HT1A receptors modulates serotonergic signaling, with adaptation over time.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike benzodiazepines, buspirone is not a GABA-A positive allosteric modulator and does not give the same immediate sedation.","monitorAndAvoid":"Evaluate behavioral context, co-medications and serotonergic interaction risk; measure behavioral outcomes over follow-up.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"mirtazapine","name":"Mirtazapine (Mirataz)","brand":"Mirtazapine (Mirataz)","class":"Noradrenergic/serotonergic antidepressant","route":"Oral formulations or feline transdermal appetite stimulant formulation; dose/route and effects differ.","uses":"Feline appetite/weight support","moa":"Alpha-2 and 5-HT receptor antagonism","effects":"Not primarily a behavior medicine; monitor adverse effects","why":"Mechanism: Alpha-2 and 5-HT receptor antagonism Clinically this matters for Feline appetite/weight support Specific risk: Not primarily a behavior medicine; monitor adverse effects","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","contraindications":"Excess exposure may cause vocalization, agitation, tachycardia and serotonin toxicity with interactions; cat renal disease affects dosing intervals.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Noradrenergic/serotonergic drug; feline appetite/weight indication is formulation-specific (Mirataz transdermal). Check interactions, agitation/vocalization and renal disease."]},{"id":"trazodone","name":"Trazodone","brand":"Trazodone","class":"Serotonin antagonist/reuptake inhibitor","route":"Oral serotonin-modulating drug used for selected situational anxiety; monitor sedation and serotonergic combinations.","uses":"Selected situational anxieties","moa":"Serotonin 5-HT2A receptor antagonism with serotonin reuptake inhibition and alpha-1 blockade.","effects":"Sedation, ataxia, GI signs, hypotension, paradoxical agitation and risk of serotonin toxicity in combinations.","why":"Serotonin modulation supports anxiety management; alpha-1 blockade can lower vascular tone.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Common canine situational use; others depend on clinician","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Review SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs and other sedatives; caution with hypotension or significant cardiovascular disease.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Serotonin antagonist and reuptake inhibitor for situational anxiety; sedation is not equivalent to resolution of fear. Evaluate additive CNS depression and serotonergic effects with SSRIs/MAOIs."],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Selected situational distress and previsit anxiolysis, often as a component of multimodal behavior management.","physiologyWhy":"Serotonin antagonist/reuptake inhibitor with sedating actions; reduced movement alone does not prove fear relief.","compareAndContrast":"Distinct from continuous SSRI therapy; can contribute to serotonergic toxicity in certain combinations.","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess onset, sedation, ataxia, patient response and concurrent SSRI/MAOI or other sedatives.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"}},{"id":"diazepam","name":"Diazepam","brand":"Diazepam","class":"Benzodiazepine","route":"Injectable/rectal emergency routes and oral formulations exist, but oral use in cats raises rare fatal liver toxicity concern.","uses":"Seizures / selected anxiolysis","moa":"Positive allosteric GABA-A modulator","effects":"Tolerance, sedation; oral use can cause rare fatal hepatic injury in cats","why":"GABA-A modulator; do not confuse sedation and reliable anticonvulsant protection.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Short-term emergency use in cats differs from chronic oral behavior use","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · safety details require current clinical verification · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Long-term oral use in cats associated with rare severe hepatic necrosis; chronic benzodiazepines can cause dependence","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Benzodiazepine GABA-A positive allosteric modulator used to terminate acute seizures; protocols favor IV/rectal in selected contexts. Chronic maintenance is limited by tolerance and feline oral hepatotoxicity concerns."],"studyGuidePages":"18–19:120–122","professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 120 gives specific repeated IV dosing/route for status epilepticus. Do not use this note as a stand-alone emergency protocol; current seizure emergency care includes airway, rapid benzodiazepine treatment and escalation under hospital guidance.","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"106–108,120","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred benzodiazepines and emergency seizure rescue","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog is actively convulsing in repeated seizures while its owner asks whether oral maintenance phenobarbital should be given first.","decision":"Prioritize emergency seizure termination and airway/temperature/metabolic stabilization, using a protocol-approved benzodiazepine route such as IV or intranasal agents as appropriate.","why":"Benzodiazepines rapidly enhance GABA-A inhibition; phenobarbital loading and chronic maintenance decisions serve distinct time scales.","whatChanges":"Monitor seizure cessation and respiratory effects; oral benzodiazepines are not a substitute for emergency care, and repeated rescue dosing follows a monitored protocol.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority veterinary teaching example; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use in appropriate acute seizure rescue or procedural contexts after choosing route and patient-specific safety.","physiologyWhy":"Positive allosteric modulation at GABA-A increases chloride-channel opening frequency in the presence of GABA, enhancing inhibition.","compareAndContrast":"Different from phenobarbital's barbiturate actions and flumazenil reversal; oral diazepam carries important idiosyncratic hepatic risk in cats.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor sedation, respiratory compromise and seizure control; do not recommend repeated oral dosing in cats without specialist review.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"midazolam","name":"Midazolam","brand":"Midazolam","class":"Benzodiazepine","route":"Injectable or intranasal/oromucosal emergency formulations depending protocol; short acting benzodiazepine, not only injectable BDZ.","uses":"Emergency seizure and sedation protocols","moa":"Positive allosteric GABA-A modulator","effects":"Respiratory/cardiovascular monitoring and route selection","why":"Mechanism: Positive allosteric GABA-A modulator Clinically this matters for Emergency seizure and sedation protocols Specific risk: Respiratory/cardiovascular monitoring and route selection","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Respiratory depression/sedation with opioids and other CNS depressants; paradoxical excitement or dependence can occur; maintain airway support.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Benzodiazepine for acute seizures and anesthesia, including intranasal/IV applications; formulation differences permit CRI in monitored settings."],"studyGuidePages":"18–19:120–122"},{"id":"phenobarbital","name":"Phenobarbital","brand":"Phenobarbital","class":"Barbiturate anticonvulsant","route":"Oral chronic anticonvulsant; IV loading only in monitored emergency care protocols.","uses":"Chronic epilepsy control; emergency protocols","moa":"Enhances GABA-A-mediated inhibitory transmission, reducing seizure propagation.","effects":"Sedation, ataxia, PU/PD/polyphagia, hepatic enzyme induction and potential hepatotoxicity, especially in dogs.","why":"GABA enhancement prevents repeated neuronal firing; hepatic enzyme induction changes clearance of other drugs.","lecture":"Lecture 19","species":"Dogs/cats; monitor levels and laboratory values","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Do not abruptly withdraw. In dogs monitor concentrations, liver function, sedation and interacting medications; use caution with severe hepatic disease.","veterinaryRelevance":"Established veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Common maintenance antiseizure medication; therapeutic monitoring and hepatic adverse effects matter.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Barbiturate enhances GABA-A inhibition for maintenance seizures; monitor serum trough concentrations, CBC/chemistry and hepatic status. Lecture suggests checks ~2 weeks, 6 weeks, then every 6 months; never abruptly stop after chronic use."],"studyGuidePages":"19:120–123","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Common long-term canine/feline antiseizure option; distinguish ongoing prevention from emergency benzodiazepine rescue.","physiologyWhy":"Enhances GABA-A inhibition; hepatic enzyme induction and concentration-dependent adverse effects complicate combination therapies.","compareAndContrast":"Levetiracetam acts at SV2A; bromide has renal chloride-linked handling; primidone is a phenobarbital prodrug.","monitorAndAvoid":"Lecture monitoring: level ~2 and 6 weeks, then roughly every 6 months and after dose changes; assess CBC/chemistry and never abruptly stop chronic therapy.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture pp. 120–123 presents a specific escalation and fixed serum level-check schedule. These are exam notes, not a universal emergency seizure protocol or a substitute for current individualized therapeutic drug monitoring.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A seizure-controlled dog on phenobarbital develops ataxia and an elevated drug level after a second prescription is added.","decision":"Review the new drug for enzyme interactions and measure phenobarbital exposure plus hepatic status.","why":"Phenobarbital is hepatically metabolized and an enzyme inducer; interacting drugs can change its concentration or other drug exposures.","whatChanges":"Monitor level and CBC/chemistry after changes; never abruptly discontinue chronic therapy due to rebound seizures.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"120–123","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicitly starred feline drug of choice","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-nervous-system/anticonvulsants-for-treatment-of-animals"]},{"id":"potassium-bromide","name":"Potassium bromide","brand":"Potassium bromide","class":"Bromide anticonvulsant","route":"Oral bromide for selected dogs; long half-life, consistent dietary chloride and therapeutic monitoring.","uses":"Selected canine seizure prevention","moa":"Bromide enters through chloride-associated pathways and enhances neuronal inhibition, reducing seizure excitability.","effects":"Sedation, ataxia, GI irritation, bromism with chloride changes or renal impairment; serious bronchial disease in cats.","why":"Low chloride intake reduces bromide elimination and may drive neurotoxicity.","lecture":"Lecture 19","species":"Common dog adjunct; generally avoided in cats","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Primarily canine; generally avoid in cats. Keep dietary chloride stable and monitor bromide concentrations, renal function and neurologic signs.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Bromide competes with renal chloride clearance: changing dietary chloride changes levels. Long half-life and slow steady state; pancreatitis (especially combined phenobarbital), PU/PD and feline cough/bronchial disease concerns."],"studyGuidePages":"19:123–125","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Canine maintenance/adjunct antiseizure drug, NOT routine cats because respiratory adverse effects can be severe.","physiologyWhy":"Bromide and chloride compete in renal handling; increased dietary chloride may decrease bromide concentrations and seizure protection.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike phenobarbital, bromide needs months to equilibrate and is not mainly cleared by liver metabolism.","monitorAndAvoid":"Keep dietary chloride consistent; monitor levels, clinical seizure response, sedation/ataxia, GI signs and possible pancreatitis.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 124 says bromide can be stopped abruptly and suggests a mechanism mainly through opening chloride channels. Avoid blanket discontinuation advice; discuss seizure risk and specialist taper/monitoring. Exact bromide mechanisms are incompletely understood.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog has been well-controlled on potassium bromide, but the owner starts feeding a new high-chloride prescription diet.","decision":"Document the change, review seizure control and plan bromide level reassessment rather than automatically increasing dose.","why":"Chloride and bromide compete at renal handling; more chloride can lower circulating bromide over time.","whatChanges":"Avoid routine feline bromide because bronchial/respiratory disease can be severe. Pancreatitis risk matters, especially with phenobarbital.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"123–124","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicitly starred adverse effects","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-nervous-system/anticonvulsants-for-treatment-of-animals"]},{"id":"levetiracetam","name":"Levetiracetam","brand":"Levetiracetam","class":"SV2A anticonvulsant","route":"Oral immediate/extended-release preparations or injectable inpatient therapy; dosing intervals depend on formulation and species.","uses":"Seizure control / adjunct","moa":"Binds synaptic vesicle protein SV2A, modulating transmitter release and reducing seizure propagation.","effects":"Sedation, ataxia, possible behavioral/GI changes; accumulation possible with renal dysfunction.","why":"Reduces vesicular neurotransmission without acting as a direct GABA receptor agonist.","lecture":"Lecture 19","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Consider renal clearance and formulation-specific dosing intervals; monitor seizures, adherence and CNS effects.","veterinaryRelevance":"Common veterinary use / often extra-label","veterinaryContext":"SV2A antiseizure medicine used in dogs and cats; adjust treatment for patient and renal function.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: SV2A antiseizure medication with fewer hepatic interactions, often shorter administration intervals; adjust with renal disease and distinguish from GABA-A receptor agents."],"studyGuidePages":"19:121,127","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Common adjunct or selected primary antiseizure therapy; also used in some urgent hospital seizure protocols.","physiologyWhy":"Binds SV2A vesicle-associated protein, modulating transmitter release rather than directly activating GABA-A.","compareAndContrast":"Immediate release often requires frequent administration; XR preparations have separate formulation constraints.","monitorAndAvoid":"Review renal clearance, administration consistency and seizure logs; a good safety profile is not equivalent to no monitoring.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog has breakthrough seizures after the owner gives immediate-release levetiracetam only once daily.","decision":"Check missed doses and formulation schedule before declaring drug failure or adding phenobarbital.","why":"SV2A modulation is not the same as GABA-A enhancement; immediate-release exposure may fall before the next dose.","whatChanges":"Review whether extended-release tablets can be used safely for that patient and ensure an appropriate dosing interval.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"127","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-nervous-system/anticonvulsants-for-treatment-of-animals"]},{"id":"zonisamide","name":"Zonisamide","brand":"Zonisamide","class":"Anticonvulsant","route":"Oral anticonvulsant with species-specific pharmacokinetics; monitor liver/adverse effects and seizure control.","uses":"Seizure control","moa":"Reduces excitability through voltage-gated Na+ and T-type Ca2+ channels; also has sulfonamide properties.","effects":"Sedation, ataxia, reduced appetite and rare serious idiosyncratic hepatic or blood-cell disorders.","why":"Multi-channel suppression stabilizes firing, but idiosyncratic toxicity is not dose-predictable.","lecture":"Lecture 19","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Assess sulfonamide reactions, hepatic symptoms and interactions with enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Sulfonamide-related anticonvulsant acting on voltage-gated channels; monitor appetite, GI effects, hepatic concerns and possible idiosyncratic reactions; may interact with phenobarbital."],"studyGuidePages":"19:125–127","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-nervous-system/anticonvulsants-for-treatment-of-animals"]},{"id":"xylazine","name":"Xylazine","brand":"Xylazine","class":"Alpha-2 agonist","route":"Injectable alpha-2 agonist for species-specific sedation, not interchangeable mg/kg across ruminants and horses.","uses":"Sedation/analgesia in suitable species","moa":"Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist reduces central norepinephrine release, causing sedation and analgesia.","effects":"Marked bradycardia, initial hypertension then hypotension, reduced cardiac output, respiratory changes and species-specific effects.","why":"Central sympathetic suppression produces sedation, while peripheral alpha-2 vasoconstriction explains early hypertension and reflex bradycardia.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Ruminants highly sensitive; equine and cat use differs","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Evaluate cardiac/perfusion status and species/product label; avoid assuming identical effects across ruminants, horses and dogs.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha-2 agonist producing sedation/analgesia and bradycardia; ruminants are particularly sensitive. Can induce emesis in cats; watch cardiac, respiratory and GI effects."],"studyGuidePages":"20:129–131","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"129–131","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred ruminant and alpha-2 teaching","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A veterinarian needs standing sedation in a bovine patient and a horse. A student suggests copying the same mg/kg xylazine dose.","decision":"Do not transfer doses between species. Ruminants are much more sensitive to alpha-2 agonists, and dosing/handling must be species-specific.","why":"Presynaptic alpha-2 activation reduces norepinephrine release, causing sedation and analgesia but also bradycardia, decreased motility and altered perfusion.","whatChanges":"Monitor recumbency, regurgitation/aspiration, respiration, tympany and withdrawal legality in food animals.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Choose species-adjusted alpha-2 sedation only after weighing cardiovascular/airway risks, especially heightened ruminant sensitivity.","physiologyWhy":"Presynaptic alpha-2 agonism suppresses norepinephrine signaling, producing sedation/analgesia with bradycardia and altered motility.","compareAndContrast":"Not dose-equivalent across horses and ruminants; alpha-2 effects reverse with appropriate antagonists, not naloxone.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor respiration, heart rate/perfusion, recumbency, bloat/regurgitation, pregnancy concerns and food-animal legality.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"dexmedetomidine","name":"Dexmedetomidine","brand":"Dexmedetomidine","class":"Alpha-2 agonist","route":"Injectable alpha-2 agonist for labeled sedation contexts; oromucosal dog gel is a distinct noise-aversion product.","uses":"Sedation/analgesia in appropriate patients","moa":"Reduces noradrenergic signaling","effects":"Bradycardia; reversal agent atipamezole when appropriate","why":"Alpha-2 agonist with patient/species-specific sedation and reversal by atipamezole; sedation is not analgesia by itself.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · neuro lecture distinction checked · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Track HR, perfusion, respiratory effort, temperature, other sedatives and return of pain on reversal.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Selective alpha-2 agonist for sedation/analgesia; can be reversed with atipamezole. Monitor bradycardia, perfusion and temperature; special noise-aversion oromucosal labeling is formulation-specific."],"studyGuidePages":"20:132–133","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Short-term sedation/analgesia for appropriate patient and formulation; assess cardiovascular status first.","physiologyWhy":"Central presynaptic alpha-2 stimulation reduces NE release; bradycardia, vasoconstrictive phases and altered cardiac output can occur.","compareAndContrast":"Atipamezole reverses alpha-2 component, not concurrent opioid or benzodiazepine.","monitorAndAvoid":"Track HR, perfusion, respiratory effort, temperature, other sedatives and return of pain on reversal.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A sedated dog receives dexmedetomidine and hydromorphone, then develops significant bradycardia and hypoventilation.","decision":"Assess the complete anesthetic/pain protocol: reversing alpha-2 activity alone will not reverse the opioid.","why":"Presynaptic alpha-2 agonism reduces NE release; the opioid independently acts at mu receptors.","whatChanges":"Atipamezole targets the alpha-2 component; naloxone targets opioids but can remove analgesia. Continue monitoring.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"132","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred alpha-2 reversal / sedation","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"romifidine","name":"Romifidine","brand":"Romifidine","class":"Alpha-2 agonist","route":"Injectable equine alpha-2 agonist for selected standing sedation; monitor cardiovascular response.","uses":"Equine sedation","moa":"Presynaptic alpha-2 activation","effects":"CV effects; formulation and mixing compatibility","why":"Mechanism: Presynaptic alpha-2 activation Clinically this matters for Equine sedation Specific risk: CV effects; formulation and mixing compatibility","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Monitor pulse, mucous membranes, BP if available, respiration, GI motility, fall risk and co-sedatives; confirm mixing compatibility.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Equine alpha-2 sedation/colic adjunct with longer duration in lecture; do not mix in same syringe with incompatible drugs; monitoring and reversal are protocol dependent."],"studyGuidePages":"20:133","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"133","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • lecture tables (not opening master tables)","rationale":"Explicit ★ appears with this medication or its exact highlighted combination in the lecture table","verifyBold":"PDF text extraction does not establish bold/yellow typography"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider an equine alpha-2 sedative/analgesic when the desired standing sedation and procedure duration justify its risks.","physiologyWhy":"Central presynaptic alpha-2 activation reduces norepinephrine release; peripheral alpha-2 effects can produce an early rise in vascular resistance with reflex bradycardia.","compareAndContrast":"Contrast detomidine and xylazine; duration, head carriage, patient response and product indication matter more than claiming one is always longest.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor pulse, mucous membranes, BP if available, respiration, GI motility, fall risk and co-sedatives; confirm mixing compatibility.","provenance":"Main Exam 2 lecture table ★, course-specific clinical synthesis; prescribing requires current source verification"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Patient scenario: Consider an equine alpha-2 sedative/analgesic when the desired standing sedation and procedure duration justify its risks.","decision":"Consider an equine alpha-2 sedative/analgesic when the desired standing sedation and procedure duration justify its risks.","why":"Central presynaptic alpha-2 activation reduces norepinephrine release; peripheral alpha-2 effects can produce an early rise in vascular resistance with reflex bradycardia.","whatChanges":"Monitor pulse, mucous membranes, BP if available, respiration, GI motility, fall risk and co-sedatives; confirm mixing compatibility.","sourceType":"Starred-lecture-priority teaching case"}},{"id":"acepromazine","name":"Acepromazine","brand":"Acepromazine","class":"Phenothiazine tranquilizer","route":"Oral or injectable phenothiazine tranquilizer, depending product/species; NOT analgesia.","uses":"Tranquilization/antiemesis as indicated","moa":"D2 and alpha-1 antagonism","effects":"Hypotension, prolonged sedation; no analgesia","why":"Dopamine/alpha-1 blockade tranquilizes without reliable analgesia and can cause hypotension; never equate immobility with relief of fear.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Dogs/horses; marked individual variation","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · safety details require current clinical verification · neuro lecture distinction checked · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Hypotension; equine penile prolapse; no analgesia","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Phenothiazine D2 antagonist and alpha-1 blocker causing tranquilization and hypotension, but NO reliable analgesia; caution in hypotension and breeding stallions (penile prolapse)."],"studyGuidePages":"20:134–136","professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 135 claims seizure threshold lowering; evidence and patient context are more nuanced than a universal contraindication. Independently evaluate hypotension, fear versus immobility, analgesia, and equine penile risks.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A frightened dog is immobile after acepromazine yet continues to show autonomic fear signals during a procedure.","decision":"Do not interpret immobilization as appropriate anxiolysis or analgesia; address fear and pain with a suitable plan.","why":"D2 antagonism tranquilizes and alpha-1 antagonism can cause hypotension without reliable analgesia.","whatChanges":"Avoid as a stand-alone painful-procedure drug, especially with hypotension or unstable perfusion.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"135","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • lecture tables (not opening master tables)","rationale":"Explicit ★ appears with this medication or its exact highlighted combination in the lecture table","verifyBold":"PDF text extraction does not establish bold/yellow typography"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider tranquilization in selected patients only when it is acceptable that the drug does NOT independently provide analgesia or reliable fear relief.","physiologyWhy":"Dopamine D2 antagonism contributes to tranquilization; peripheral alpha-1 blockade can reduce vascular resistance and cause hypotension.","compareAndContrast":"Compare with alpha-2 agonists (sedation with some analgesia, reversible) and anxiolytics (target fear rather than mere immobility).","monitorAndAvoid":"Avoid reflex use in hypotensive/debilitated patients; consider equine penile prolapse, reduced seizure-threshold claims as nuanced, and persistent pain/fear.","provenance":"Main Exam 2 lecture table ★, course-specific clinical synthesis; prescribing requires current source verification"}},{"id":"butorphanol","name":"Butorphanol","brand":"Butorphanol","class":"Mixed opioid agonist-antagonist","route":"Injectable or oral opioid product depending species; short analgesic duration can limit suitability for painful surgery.","uses":"Selected analgesia, sedation combinations, cough suppression","moa":"Kappa opioid receptor agonism with mu receptor antagonism or partial agonist activity depending system.","effects":"Sedation, dysphoria and respiratory effects; limited duration and ceiling of analgesia in many species.","why":"Partial opioid activity can relieve some pain or cough but also reduce effects of full mu agonists.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Dogs, cats and horses differ","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Avoid assuming sufficient analgesia for major painful procedures; assess interference with full mu agonists and concurrent sedatives.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Kappa agonist and mu antagonist/partial agonist with ceiling limits on analgesia; useful for some equine visceral pain and antitussive indications, not equal to full mu agonists for severe pain."],"studyGuidePages":"20:138","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Selected equine visceral pain and antitussive roles; inadequate as sole choice for many severe somatic pain states.","physiologyWhy":"Mixed kappa agonist and mu antagonist/partial agonist with ceiling properties.","compareAndContrast":"Morphine/hydromorphone are full mu agonists; buprenorphine partial mu agonist.","monitorAndAvoid":"Judge analgesia from behavior/physiology, not sedation; review species and concurrent opioids.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"138","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"An adult horse has acute visceral pain associated with colic; a dog has severe traumatic orthopedic pain.","decision":"Butorphanol may help equine visceral discomfort under evaluation, but its ceiling effect makes it a poor sole analgesic for some severe somatic pain.","why":"Kappa agonism and mu antagonism/partial effects differ from full mu agonists such as morphine and hydromorphone.","whatChanges":"Repeat pain and perfusion evaluation; relief of colic signs cannot exclude a surgical lesion.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"}},{"id":"buprenorphine","name":"Buprenorphine","brand":"Buprenorphine","class":"Partial mu opioid agonist","route":"Injectable/transmucosal or veterinary feline products with route-specific pharmacokinetics; verify formulation.","uses":"Selected pain management","moa":"High-affinity partial mu opioid receptor agonist with prolonged receptor binding.","effects":"Sedation, dysphoria and respiratory depression; onset and duration vary by route/formulation.","why":"Strong receptor binding provides analgesia but can complicate reversal or addition of full mu agonists.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Feline formulations differ from other species","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor respiratory status; do not equate different injectable, oral transmucosal or extended-release products; assess interaction with other opioids.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Partial mu opioid agonist useful for feline analgesia; highly species/formulation dependent (transmucosal, injectable, prolonged-release). Distinguish analgesia from potency/ceiling concepts."],"studyGuidePages":"20:138–139","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Analgesic in cats and other species with formulation-specific routes/durations; not an automatic replacement for full mu analgesia in every case.","physiologyWhy":"High-affinity partial mu agonism creates receptor occupancy and distinct analgesia/reversal characteristics.","compareAndContrast":"Butorphanol is mixed kappa/mu; naloxone is antagonist; numeric potency does not imply greater clinical efficacy.","monitorAndAvoid":"Assess actual pain score, sedation and respiratory status; do not interchange extended-release/transmucosal products.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"139","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • lecture tables (not opening master tables)","rationale":"Explicit ★ appears with this medication or its exact highlighted combination in the lecture table","verifyBold":"PDF text extraction does not establish bold/yellow typography"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A cat has painful oral surgery and receives transmucosal buprenorphine. A student concludes its high potency guarantees more maximal analgesia than a full mu agonist.","decision":"Explain that high-affinity partial mu agonism can provide useful feline analgesia but maximal efficacy and duration depend on pain severity, route and formulation.","why":"Affinity/potency and intrinsic efficacy differ; partial agonism can limit maximal receptor effect while route changes bioavailability.","whatChanges":"Monitor pain score, sedation and respiration, reassess if analgesia inadequate, and verify product-specific feline labeling.","sourceType":"Exam 2 directly-starred lecture opioid case (p.139)"}},{"id":"tramadol","name":"Tramadol","brand":"Tramadol","class":"Weak opioid + monoamine modulator","route":"Oral analgesic adjunct with variable canine effect and serotonergic/seizure cautions.","uses":"Selected pain indications depending on species","moa":"Mu activity plus 5-HT/NE reuptake modulation","effects":"Poor/variable efficacy in dogs, serotonergic and seizure risks","why":"Weak opioid and monoamine actions have variable dog analgesic effectiveness; consider seizures and serotonergic interactions.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Clinical analgesic efficacy inconsistent in dogs","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Course note summary; verify clinical guidelines and current labeling · safety details require current clinical verification · neuro lecture distinction checked · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","contraindications":"Seizure risk and serotonergic interactions","veterinaryRelevance":"Species-dependent / limited analgesic benefit","veterinaryContext":"Oral analgesia is unreliable in many dogs; species-specific metabolism changes efficacy.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Weak mu and monoamine effects; variable metabolism and poor evidence for reliable oral analgesia in dogs, seizure/serotonergic interaction risk, and bitter oral formulation for cats/horses."],"studyGuidePages":"20:139–140","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Selected species/context-specific use; oral canine osteoarthritis evidence is poor compared with other analgesic strategies.","physiologyWhy":"Weak mu agonism with norepinephrine/serotonin reuptake effects; active-metabolite formation varies by species.","compareAndContrast":"Not equivalent to morphine or buprenorphine; serotonergic effects add interaction and seizure risks.","monitorAndAvoid":"Check analgesic response, concurrent SSRIs/MAOIs, seizure history and species-specific efficacy.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 139 lists tramadol as useful for chronic analgesia; veterinary efficacy is highly species-dependent, with poor evidence for oral tramadol in canine osteoarthritis.","clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog with chronic osteoarthritis is still painful despite oral tramadol.","decision":"Reassess with evidence-supported multimodal OA treatment instead of assuming increasing tramadol fixes the pain.","why":"Canine formation of the active mu-opioid metabolite is variable/limited and clinical oral OA efficacy has been poor in trials.","whatChanges":"Review seizure disorders and serotonergic medications, especially concurrent SSRIs or MAOIs.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"139","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"naloxone","name":"Naloxone","brand":"Naloxone","class":"Opioid antagonist","route":"Injectable opioid antagonist (IV/IM/SC depending emergency context); effect may wear off before agonist.","uses":"Opioid toxicity reversal","moa":"Competitive opioid receptor antagonist reverses mu-mediated respiratory/CNS depression.","effects":"Acute loss of analgesia, agitation or withdrawal; recurrence of sedation as antagonist effect wears off.","why":"Receptor displacement restores breathing but does not remove opioid from the body.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Species and indication dependent","source":"Updated Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (uploaded Nov 2026); drug-specific clinical validation recommended","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Continue ventilation and observation; repeat dosing/infusion may be needed when opioid lasts longer than naloxone.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Competitive opioid antagonist for respiratory/CNS effects; can reverse analgesia and may wear off before long-acting agonists, requiring continued monitoring."],"studyGuidePages":"20:137–140","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Emergency opioid adverse-effect reversal with respiratory/CNS depression as indicated.","physiologyWhy":"Competitive mu opioid receptor antagonism, with effects at other opioid receptor types; can also abolish analgesia.","compareAndContrast":"Does not reverse dexmedetomidine (atipamezole) or benzodiazepines (flumazenil).","monitorAndAvoid":"Continue observation: opioid effect may outlast naloxone and respiratory depression can recur.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"140","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog is rescued from opioid-related respiratory depression after receiving a long-acting mu agonist; breathing returns after naloxone.","decision":"Continue monitoring even after an initial response because naloxone can wear off before the opioid.","why":"Competitive opioid receptor blockade restores opioid-suppressed respiratory signaling but also removes analgesia.","whatChanges":"Reassess ventilation, mental status, pain and recurrence of sedation; plan repeat intervention when necessary.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"}},{"id":"insulin-lispro","name":"Lispro (Humalog)","brand":"Lispro (Humalog)","class":"Rapid-acting insulin analog","route":"Rapid-acting insulin analog used by injection in selected contexts; not a routine replacement for veterinary basal insulin.","uses":"Rare veterinary use; meal-associated rapid insulin","moa":"Insulin receptor agonism","effects":"Hypoglycemia","contraindications":"Rapid insulin onset creates early hypoglycemia risk; verify feeding, concentration and species-specific plan; not equivalent to basal insulin.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Concentration, route and formulation matter","lecture":"Lecture 10","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","veterinaryRelevance":"Human-medicine comparison / uncommon veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Rapid prandial insulin analog; not routine first-choice maintenance insulin for dogs or cats.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Rapid insulin analog mainly discussed as a human prandial comparator; SQ onset differs from regular insulin, and veterinary feeding unpredictability limits routine use."]},{"id":"insulin-aspart","name":"Aspart (NovoLog)","brand":"Aspart (NovoLog)","class":"Rapid-acting insulin analog","route":"Rapid-acting injectable insulin analog used in selected glucose-management contexts; not equivalent to an intermediate maintenance insulin.","uses":"Rare veterinary use","moa":"Insulin receptor agonism","effects":"Hypoglycemia","contraindications":"Rapid-action insulin with hypoglycemia risk; route, feeding and monitoring needs differ from long-acting maintenance preparations.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Rapid SQ absorption; source describes IV kinetics differently","lecture":"Lecture 10","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","veterinaryRelevance":"Human-medicine comparison / uncommon veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Rapid analog taught for insulin-class comparison; veterinary crisis protocols require close monitoring.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Rapid prandial human insulin analog, seldom used for routine veterinary outpatient diabetes care; avoid extrapolating human meal timing."]},{"id":"insulin-70-30","name":"Humulin 70/30","brand":"Humulin 70/30","class":"Premixed insulin","route":"Premixed subcutaneous NPH/regular insulin; fixed ratio makes individualized feline/canine titration difficult.","uses":"Diabetes where a premix is appropriate","moa":"70% NPH + 30% regular insulin","effects":"Hypoglycemia","contraindications":"Fixed short/intermediate ratio limits independent titration of basal versus prandial effects; hypoglycemia risk if intake/absorption changes.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Fixed ratio is less flexible than separate formulations","lecture":"Lecture 10","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Premixed insulin combines intermediate NPH plus short-acting regular insulin; fixed ratio limits independent titration and is not universally interchangeable with Vetsulin."]},{"id":"insulin-degludec","name":"Degludec","brand":"Degludec","class":"Ultra-long-acting insulin","route":"Ultra-long-acting subcutaneous human insulin analog; veterinary use requires careful individualized product assessment.","uses":"Potential feline long-acting option","moa":"Insulin receptor agonism; long-lasting depot","effects":"Hypoglycemia","contraindications":"Very long exposure raises potential for prolonged hypoglycemia; high-concentration pen/device errors require caution and feline data may be limited.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Pen formulations require correct device","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Ultra-long basal insulin analog forms multihexamer depot and binds albumin; human duration figures do not apply directly to every veterinary species; limited veterinary evidence."]},{"id":"glyburide","name":"Glyburide","brand":"Glyburide","class":"Sulfonylurea","route":"Oral sulfonylurea; requires functional beta cells and has hypoglycemia risk; veterinary use highly selected.","uses":"Selected cats with residual insulin secretion","moa":"Blocks beta-cell ATP-sensitive K+ channels","effects":"Hypoglycemia","contraindications":"Requires functioning beta cells; may trigger hypoglycemia and is unsuitable for insulin-dependent or ketotic patients.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Insulin-deficient patients cannot rely on this approach","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","veterinaryRelevance":"Limited veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Sulfonylurea needs functioning beta cells; not a substitute for insulin in insulin-deficient cats.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: sulfonylurea closes pancreatic beta-cell KATP channels, depolarizing beta cells and stimulating endogenous insulin secretion.","Requires functional beta cells and is not an alternative to insulin for DKA or insulin-deficient diabetes. Source discussed limited feline use when injections declined; efficacy and safety require clinical assessment."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38"},{"id":"glipizide","name":"Glipizide","brand":"Glipizide","class":"Sulfonylurea","route":"Oral sulfonylurea; functional beta cells needed; not a replacement for insulin in insulin-dependent cats.","uses":"Selected cats retaining beta-cell activity","moa":"Blocks beta-cell ATP-sensitive K+ channels","effects":"Hypoglycemia","contraindications":"Requires functioning beta cells; can cause hypoglycemia and GI effects; not a replacement for insulin when ketosis/insulin dependence exists.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not a substitute for DKA care","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","veterinaryRelevance":"Limited veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Inconsistent efficacy in cats; compare with insulin and appropriately selected feline therapies.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: sulfonylurea that closes beta-cell KATP channels to stimulate insulin release.","Not effective without viable beta cells; potential hypoglycemia and limited feline clinical utility compared with appropriate insulin therapy."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38"},{"id":"metformin","name":"Metformin","brand":"Metformin","class":"Biguanide","route":"Oral biguanide; GI effects, renal function and limited veterinary usefulness influence selection.","uses":"Selected equine metabolic syndrome and limited feline use","moa":"Reduces hepatic glucose output and affects insulin sensitivity","effects":"GI intolerance","contraindications":"Often causes GI intolerance and can be unsuitable with major renal compromise, hypoxia or severe illness; species-specific efficacy is variable.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not a substitute for appropriate insulin therapy","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","veterinaryRelevance":"Selected veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Human type 2 diabetes standard; veterinary application is different, including selected equine metabolic contexts.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: biguanide reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis and may improve insulin sensitivity, but is not interchangeable with insulin replacement.","Lecture includes selected equine metabolic-syndrome contexts; human type 2 diabetes benefits do not extrapolate directly to diabetic cats.","Course mentions a low response rate in cats; distinguish instructor claim from current clinical outcomes."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38","professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture suggests thyroid hormone might improve diabetic/EMS metabolic treatment; not a routine veterinary intervention to accelerate weight loss in a euthyroid equine patient."},{"id":"streptozotocin","name":"Streptozotocin","brand":"Streptozotocin","class":"Alkylating antineoplastic","route":"Specialist cytotoxic injectable drug targeting pancreatic beta cells; nephrotoxicity and antineoplastic precautions.","uses":"Selected canine insulinoma protocols","moa":"Enters pancreatic beta cells via GLUT2; damages DNA","effects":"Significant antineoplastic toxicity","contraindications":"Cytotoxic drug may cause severe kidney injury and beta-cell damage; use only under specialist oncology protocols with renal and glucose monitoring.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Specialist oncology protocols","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Beta-cell toxic nitrosourea antineoplastic (GLUT2 uptake) discussed for insulinoma; serious toxicity and specialist use; not an insulin substitute for diabetic patients."]},{"id":"propylthiouracil","name":"Propylthiouracil (PTU)","brand":"Propylthiouracil (PTU)","class":"Thionamide antithyroid","route":"Oral antithyroid thionamide; generally not favored in cats because of serious adverse-effect risk.","uses":"Antithyroid historical/selected use","moa":"Inhibits thyroid peroxidase and peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion","effects":"Greater risk of serious adverse effects","contraindications":"Potential serious hepatic/hematologic and immune-mediated adverse reactions; generally disfavored for feline hyperthyroidism compared with methimazole.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Methimazole preferred for most feline cases","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: thionamide inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis; also decreases peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion.","COURSE notes horse/historical contexts; serious adverse effects make PTU an inappropriate routine choice for feline hyperthyroidism."],"studyGuidePages":"41–45"},{"id":"iodine-131","name":"Radioiodine I-131","brand":"Radioiodine I-131","class":"Therapeutic radionuclide • radioactive sodium iodide I-131","route":"Specialist-administered radioactive sodium iodide, typically SQ or IV; licensed nuclear-medicine facility","uses":"Definitive treatment for feline hyperthyroidism, including functional ectopic thyroid tissue; generally high efficacy.","moa":"Thyroid follicular cells concentrate iodide via NIS. I-131 emits beta particles that damage hyperfunctioning tissue; surrounding normal tissue is comparatively spared but not guaranteed unaffected.","effects":"Radiation-exposure restrictions during isolation; subsequent hypothyroidism; improvement in hyperthyroidism may reveal underlying CKD or azotemia.","contraindications":"Medically unstable cats or those needing hands-on interventions during radiation isolation may be unsuitable until stabilized. Review renal, cardiovascular and other metabolic disease individually; these are not universal categorical exclusions.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Radiation safety and renal reassessment","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","veterinaryRelevance":"Established definitive feline hyperthyroidism treatment","veterinaryContext":"The course's three-day no-touch and additional two-week gloves instructions are lecture-specific, NOT a universal isolation/discharge schedule. Radiation-release rules and methimazole withdrawal are set by the treating facility.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE NOTE — PRIOR TO THERAPY: stabilize hyperthyroidism using methimazole where clinically appropriate, then assess metabolic, cardiac and renal function. Select a cat stable enough to tolerate a period of limited hands-on medical care.","COURSE NOTE — 10–14 days BEFORE I-131: withdraw methimazole and iodine-restricted y/d diet under the treating facility's instructions. Do not independently discontinue methimazole in an unstable cat; some centers retain it until stabilization is possible.","COURSE NOTE — first 3 days AFTER I-131: no touching/handling taught in Lecture 12; after day 3, the notes specify gloves for 2 additional weeks, avoiding children and pregnant people and careful litter/feces handling.","CURRENT PRACTICE: no fixed universal '3-day' or 'two-week gloves' rule. Hospitalize/isolate until the facility's radiation-release criteria are satisfied; home-contact and waste rules are set by local radiation-safety instructions.","FOLLOW-UP: reevaluate T4, weight, renal values and clinical signs; hypothyroidism may require levothyroxine and renal function can change after euthyroidism."],"clinicalRefs":["https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/vmc/radiology/iodine-131-therapy/","https://www.merckvetmanual.com/endocrine-system/the-thyroid-gland/hyperthyroidism-in-animals","https://www.vet.cornell.edu/hospitals/services/internal-medicine-0/feline-hyperthyroidism"],"studyGuidePages":"44–45","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Stabilize first; consider thyroid response, kidney function, heart disease and whether the cat can tolerate isolation without interventions.","physiologyWhy":"Methimazole suppresses new hormone synthesis but I-131 is taken up by functioning thyroid tissue and destroys it; ectopic thyroid may be treated.","compareAndContrast":"Methimazole/y/d 10–14 day withdrawal and 3-day no-touch plus 2-week glove instructions are LECTURE memory aids, not universal rules.","monitorAndAvoid":"Radiation facility determines actual withdrawal, hospitalization, contact and waste restrictions; follow T4/renal values afterward.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A hyperthyroid cat has tachycardia and creatinine that increases after a methimazole trial.","decision":"Do not rush to isolation-based therapy. Stabilize, investigate renal/cardiac tolerance and determine whether the cat can safely go without hands-on interventions during the radioactive period.","why":"As thyroid hormone decreases, the previously high GFR can fall and reveal CKD. Radioiodine treats thyroid tissue, not renal disease.","whatChanges":"Lecture: stop methimazole and y/d for 10–14 days and no touch for 3 days; real nuclear-medicine centers set withdrawal and radiation instructions differently.","sourceType":"Teaching case—illustrative, not a dose/order set"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"44–45","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred feline hyperthyroidism treatment topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"}},{"id":"calcium-borogluconate","name":"Calcium borogluconate","brand":"Calcium borogluconate","class":"Calcium replacement","route":"Slow monitored IV calcium replacement in a recumbent cow with confirmed milk fever; oral prevention is distinct.","uses":"Bovine hypocalcemia / milk fever","moa":"Rapidly provides available Ca2+","effects":"Arrhythmia if given improperly","contraindications":"Monitor heart rate/rhythm during administration and reassess animal ability to stand; prevent relapse through dietary transition management.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Monitor during veterinary-administered IV treatment","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: IV calcium replacement for bovine parturient hypocalcemia; monitor heart rhythm and avoid overly rapid infusion; prevent recurrent cases via periparturient nutritional management."],"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"45–46","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred parathyroid / milk fever topic","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A newly calved dairy cow is recumbent with clinical findings consistent with periparturient hypocalcemia.","decision":"Treat with appropriately administered calcium and monitor cardiac status; reassess when normocalcemia does not restore standing.","why":"Insufficient extracellular calcium reduces neuromuscular contractility; intravenous calcium can rapidly restore excitable tissue function but overly rapid administration can produce arrhythmias.","whatChanges":"Monitor heart rate/rhythm during therapy and look for trauma, metabolic disease, or muscle injury if recumbency persists.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use careful calcium replacement for appropriately diagnosed parturient hypocalcemia in cattle with monitoring.","physiologyWhy":"Extracellular calcium is required for neuromuscular excitation and myocardial contraction; restoring it improves recumbency and weakness caused by hypocalcemia.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike oral calcium supplements for maintenance/prevention, IV calcium acts rapidly and can be dangerous if administered too fast.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor heart rate/rhythm during administration and reassess animal ability to stand; prevent relapse through dietary transition management.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"molidustat","name":"Molidustat (Varenzin-CA1)","brand":"Varenzin-CA1","class":"HIF-prolyl-hydroxylase inhibitor","route":"Oral suspension for cats, up to 28 consecutive days per treatment cycle; a minimum 7-day treatment pause before repeat cycles; monitor HCT/PCV weekly starting about day 14.","uses":"FDA conditionally approved for control of nonregenerative anemia associated with chronic kidney disease in cats (not for all causes of anemia).","moa":"Reversibly inhibits HIF prolyl hydroxylase, stabilizing HIF and increasing endogenous erythropoietin production and red cell formation.","effects":"Vomiting; potential excessive erythrocytosis/polycythemia, hypertension, potassium changes and thromboembolic events; seizures were reported in field studies. Avoid interpreting adverse-event reports as proven causality in every case.","contraindications":"Do not use in pregnant/lactating/breeding cats or those hypersensitive to molidustat. Use caution with thromboembolic risk or seizure history. Monitor PCV/HCT; stop if above reference interval. Consider separating phosphate binders/iron supplements by at least one hour; concurrent EPO drugs not studied.","species":"Cats only; US conditional approval, product-specific labeling.","why":"CKD can impair endogenous EPO signaling, leading to nonregenerative anemia. HIF-PH inhibition raises endogenous EPO but overcorrection raises blood viscosity and thromboembolic risk: monitor PCV/HCT and BP.","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Drug-safety audit: primary US label reviewed 2026-11-23; check current label before prescribing.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: HIF-prolyl-hydroxylase inhibitor (Varenzin-CA1) conditionally approved for nonregenerative anemia associated with CKD in cats; monitor hematocrit/PCV and avoid excessive erythrocytosis."],"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Conditionally approved feline CKD nonregenerative anemia treatment, not a substitute for determining other anemia causes.","physiologyWhy":"Inhibits HIF-prolyl hydroxylase, stabilizing HIF and promoting endogenous EPO signaling.","compareAndContrast":"Darbepoetin is injected exogenous EPO-stimulating analog; distinct mechanism and risks.","monitorAndAvoid":"Track PCV/HCT and clinical response; avoid overshooting hematocrit and reassess comorbidities.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"clinicalRefs":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=78719718-4a45-4fa2-ae1a-b5270209968f"]},{"id":"darbepoetin","name":"Darbepoetin","brand":"Darbepoetin","class":"Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent","route":"Injectable erythropoiesis-stimulating analog for selected CKD anemia; monitor PCV/HCT, BP and antibody-associated anemia risk.","uses":"Selected CKD-associated anemia","moa":"Activates erythropoietin receptor","effects":"Hypertension, adverse immune responses","contraindications":"May cause hypertension, antibody-mediated pure red cell aplasia and excessive erythrocytosis; trend HCT, BP and iron availability.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Monitor PCV/reticulocytes, BP and iron status","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Erythropoiesis-stimulating analog for anemia of CKD; monitor PCV, BP, iron status and antibody-mediated pure red-cell aplasia risk."]},{"id":"phenoxybenzamine","name":"Phenoxybenzamine","brand":"Phenoxybenzamine","class":"Irreversible alpha adrenergic blocker","route":"Oral irreversible alpha antagonist for selected pheochromocytoma planning; careful BP and volume monitoring.","uses":"Pheochromocytoma-related hypertension; selected urethral uses","moa":"Irreversibly antagonizes alpha receptors","effects":"Hypotension","contraindications":"Irreversible alpha blockade may cause marked hypotension and reflex tachycardia; volume and BP assessment important before anesthesia.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Long onset / duration","lecture":"Lecture 13","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Irreversible alpha-receptor blocker occasionally used for selected urethral outflow resistance; prolonged effects and hypotension limit use."],"studyGuidePages":"13:48–50"},{"id":"pralidoxime","name":"Pralidoxime (2-PAM)","brand":"Pralidoxime (2-PAM)","class":"Oxime antidote","route":"Parenteral oxime in appropriate organophosphate poisoning protocols; time since exposure and antidote selection matter.","uses":"Organophosphate toxicosis adjunct to atropine","moa":"Reactivates organophosphate-inhibited acetylcholinesterase before aging","effects":"Rapid administration may cause transient tachycardia, hypertension, muscular weakness or GI symptoms; response varies by organophosphate and exposure timing.","contraindications":"Best used early before acetylcholinesterase-organophosphate complex ages. It is an adjunct, NOT a substitute for atropine, decontamination and airway/ventilation support; avoid assuming efficacy for every cholinesterase poison.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Restores acetylcholinesterase activity at neuromuscular and other sites if reactivation occurs before aging, while atropine blocks excess muscarinic signaling.","lecture":"Lecture 13","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Oxime reactivates phosphorylated acetylcholinesterase before aging in some organophosphate poisonings; pair with atropine as clinically indicated; efficacy depends on toxicant/timing."],"studyGuidePages":"13:50"},{"id":"succinylcholine","name":"Succinylcholine","brand":"Succinylcholine","class":"Depolarizing neuromuscular blocker","route":"Injected depolarizing neuromuscular blocker under anesthesia/ventilation support only; NO analgesia or unconsciousness.","uses":"Specialized paralysis during anesthesia","moa":"Depolarizing neuromuscular blocker: persistent activation of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor endplate prevents repolarization and subsequent muscle contractions.","effects":"Apnea and complete respiratory muscle paralysis; hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias in susceptible patients, bradyarrhythmia, muscle fasciculations and prolonged blockade with atypical/low plasma cholinesterase activity.","contraindications":"Paralysis WITHOUT sedation, unconsciousness or analgesia. Must provide full general anesthesia and ventilatory support. Avoid in patients at risk of hyperkalemia (e.g., severe burns or neuromuscular denervation) and evaluate anesthesia-specific malignant hyperthermia risk.","species":"Specialized anesthesia only; species and patient status determine suitability.","why":"A continuously depolarized neuromuscular junction cannot respond normally to repeated motor-neuron impulses; patient remains capable of awareness and pain unless separately anesthetized.","lecture":"Lecture 13","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety pass 2026-11-23: see linked primary label or Merck Veterinary Manual; source lecture retained.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Depolarizing NMJ blocker causes paralysis but NOT unconsciousness, analgesia or anesthesia; high risk of apnea. Historical wildlife/euthanasia discussion is not an acceptable stand-alone humane method."],"studyGuidePages":"13:50","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Historic/rare depolarizing paralytic for controlled airway management; NOT anesthesia or analgesia.","physiologyWhy":"Persistently stimulates nicotinic NMJ receptors causing depolarizing block and flaccid paralysis.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike centrally acting sedatives it cannot ensure unconsciousness; unprotected administration can cause conscious paralysis and apnea.","monitorAndAvoid":"Requires anesthesia/ventilation; watch hyperkalemia and other complications in susceptible patients.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecturer mentions historical wildlife restraint/euthanasia. Neuromuscular paralysis without unconsciousness/analgesia is not humane euthanasia or anesthesia.","coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"50","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Prominent nicotinic neuromuscular lecture example","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A student proposes succinylcholine alone to immobilize a painful conscious animal for a procedure.","decision":"Never use paralysis as a substitute for anesthesia or analgesia. Neuromuscular blockers require an airway and anesthetic plan.","why":"Persistent nicotinic NMJ activation produces flaccid paralysis, including respiratory muscles, without unconsciousness or pain relief.","whatChanges":"Monitor ventilation continuously; identify hyperkalemia and neuromuscular risk factors; the historical lecture example does not justify standalone use.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-musculoskeletal-system/neuromuscular-blocking-agents-for-animals"]},{"id":"dopamine","name":"Dopamine","brand":"Dopamine","class":"Catecholamine inotrope/vasopressor","route":"Monitored IV catecholamine infusion; effects depend on dose and patient, monitor perfusion and arrhythmias.","uses":"Selected critically ill cardiovascular patients","moa":"Dose-dependent dopaminergic and adrenergic receptor effects","effects":"Arrhythmias","contraindications":"Dose/patient-dependent tachyarrhythmias and tissue ischemia can occur; titrate IV to monitored perfusion rather than using to treat all hypotension.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Effects are dose- and patient-dependent","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Dose-dependent catecholamine receptor effects; used as monitored IV cardiovascular support in selected shock states, not an unmonitored oral chronic heart-failure drug."]},{"id":"amrinone","name":"Amrinone (inamrinone)","brand":"Amrinone (inamrinone)","class":"PDE3 inhibitor","route":"Monitored parenteral PDE3 inotrope where available; not routine outpatient CHF medication.","uses":"Historically selected low-output cardiac conditions","moa":"Increases cAMP; positive inotropy/vasodilation","effects":"Arrhythmias, hemodynamic adverse effects","contraindications":"PDE3 inotropy can cause tachyarrhythmias, hypotension and platelet concerns; hospital monitoring required, with limited contemporary use.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Less common than contemporary therapies","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Inamrinone inhibits PDE3, increasing cardiac cAMP and contractility with vasodilation; largely historic/rare veterinary inotrope compared with pimobendan or ICU support."]},{"id":"flecainide","name":"Flecainide","brand":"Flecainide","class":"Class Ic antiarrhythmic","route":"Oral or monitored IV Class Ic antiarrhythmic depending setting; ECG conduction/proarrhythmia risk.","uses":"Selected arrhythmias under cardiology direction","moa":"Potent Na+ channel blockade","effects":"Proarrhythmia","contraindications":"Structural heart disease and conduction caution","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not broadly appropriate for all arrhythmias","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Class Ic sodium-channel blocker for specialist rhythm control; marked QRS widening and proarrhythmia risk, especially structural cardiac disease."]},{"id":"sotalol","name":"Sotalol","brand":"Sotalol","class":"Class III antiarrhythmic with beta blockade","route":"Oral beta-blocking Class III antiarrhythmic; watch QT prolongation, bradycardia and renal elimination.","uses":"Selected ventricular arrhythmias in dogs","moa":"Nonselective beta blockade plus class III potassium-channel inhibition.","effects":"Bradycardia, hypotension, QT prolongation, torsades risk and bronchoconstriction.","contraindications":"Review ECG/QT, renal function, BP and potassium; caution in heart block, bronchospasm and QT-prolonging combinations.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Slows sympathetic drive and prolongs refractoriness; excessive QT creates proarrhythmia.","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Nonselective beta blocker with class III potassium-channel effects; selected ventricular rhythm disorders; monitor ECG/QT, bradycardia and renal status."],"clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/systemic-pharmacotherapeutics-of-the-cardiovascular-system/antiarrhythmics-for-use-in-animals"]},{"id":"verapamil","name":"Verapamil","brand":"Verapamil","class":"Non-dihydropyridine Ca2+ channel blocker","route":"Oral or monitored IV non-dihydropyridine CCB; assess nodal blockade, BP and heart failure.","uses":"Selected supraventricular tachyarrhythmias","moa":"Blocks cardiac L-type Ca2+ channels","effects":"Hypotension, AV block","contraindications":"May cause bradycardia, AV block and negative inotropy; avoid inappropriate use for wide-complex ventricular arrhythmia without ECG identification.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"L-type calcium channel block impacts AV nodal conduction; the guide's PVC indication is questionable.","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · source-audited mechanism clarification","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker affects AV nodal conduction; selected supraventricular arrhythmias and not a first-choice therapy for ventricular tachycardia. Can depress contractility."]},{"id":"diltiazem","name":"Diltiazem","brand":"Diltiazem","class":"Non-dihydropyridine Ca2+ channel blocker","route":"Oral nodal CCB for selected rhythm disorders; IV use requires monitored indication and cardiac assessment.","uses":"Selected supraventricular arrhythmias / rate control","moa":"Blocks cardiac L-type Ca2+ channels","effects":"Bradycardia, AV block","contraindications":"AV nodal blockade may cause bradycardia/hypotension and worsen certain conduction disorders; distinguish supraventricular from ventricular rhythms.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"L-type calcium channel block impacts AV nodal conduction; the guide's PVC indication is questionable.","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · source-audited mechanism clarification","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker often used for rate control of selected supraventricular tachyarrhythmias (including atrial fibrillation); monitor BP and AV block."]},{"id":"hydralazine","name":"Hydralazine","brand":"Hydralazine","class":"Arteriolar vasodilator","route":"Oral or carefully supervised parenteral arteriolar vasodilator; rapid hypotension/reflex tachycardia risk.","uses":"Selected severe hypertension under monitoring","moa":"Direct vascular smooth-muscle relaxation","effects":"Hypotension, reflex tachycardia","contraindications":"Unpredictable vasodilation can trigger hypotension, reflex tachycardia and impaired perfusion; use carefully with serial BP.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Clinical urgency and monitoring dictate use","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Direct arteriolar vasodilator for selected severe hypertension/afterload situations under careful BP monitoring; reflex tachycardia and hypotension are possible."],"studyGuidePages":"14:56"},{"id":"nitroprusside","name":"Sodium nitroprusside","brand":"Sodium nitroprusside","class":"Nitric oxide donor","route":"Monitored IV infusion for severe hypertensive crisis; intensive BP monitoring and toxicity precautions.","uses":"Monitored hypertensive emergencies","moa":"Releases NO; increases cGMP-mediated vasodilation","effects":"Severe hypotension, toxic metabolite accumulation","contraindications":"Profound rapid hypotension and cyanide/thiocyanate accumulation risk with prolonged or excessive infusion; continuous monitored setting only.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Hospital-only infusion","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Rapid IV nitric-oxide donor for carefully monitored hypertensive emergencies; requires infusion and continuous BP assessment; cyanide/thiocyanate accumulation risk."],"studyGuidePages":"14:56"},{"id":"benazepril","name":"Benazepril","brand":"Benazepril","class":"ACE inhibitor","route":"Oral ACE inhibitor; monitor BP, creatinine and potassium with cardiovascular/renal indications.","uses":"Selected CHF/renal or blood-pressure adjuncts","moa":"ACE inhibition decreases angiotensin-II and aldosterone signaling.","effects":"Hypotension, renal-value elevations, hyperkalemia and GI signs.","contraindications":"Check BP, kidney trends and potassium; avoid unmonitored stacking with other RAAS inhibitors.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Less aldosterone reduces retention but potassium conservation can become excessive.","lecture":"Lecture 14","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: ACE inhibitor for selected cardiac/renal conditions; monitor azotemia, potassium, BP and hydration, particularly with diuretics/NSAIDs."],"studyGuidePages":"14:56"},{"id":"aminophylline","name":"Aminophylline","brand":"Aminophylline","class":"Methylxanthine","route":"Parenteral or oral methylxanthine preparation; theophylline-related CNS/cardiac/GI adverse effects and interactions.","uses":"Selected bronchodilation","moa":"PDE inhibition and adenosine receptor antagonism","effects":"CNS excitation, tachycardia, vomiting","contraindications":"Methylxanthine adverse effects include tachyarrhythmias, agitation, vomiting and seizures; watch narrow margin and interacting drugs.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Monitoring and formulation matter","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Theophylline complex with systemic bronchodilator activity; narrow therapeutic margin and interactions, particularly in cardiac/neurologic disease."],"studyGuidePages":"15:60"},{"id":"glycopyrrolate","name":"Glycopyrrolate","brand":"Glycopyrrolate","class":"Muscarinic antagonist","route":"Injectable antimuscarinic for selected anesthetic indications; tachycardia and GI motility cautions.","uses":"Selected airway/anesthetic applications","moa":"Blocks muscarinic ACh effects","effects":"Reduced secretions, tachycardia","contraindications":"Antimuscarinic effects include tachycardia, reduced GI motility and thickened secretions; assess species and cardiovascular consequences.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Poor BBB penetration","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Peripheral antimuscarinic with limited BBB penetration, used for secretion reduction or selected vagal bradycardia; anticipate tachycardia, thicker secretions and decreased GI motility."],"studyGuidePages":"15:61"},{"id":"dextromethorphan","name":"Dextromethorphan","brand":"Dextromethorphan","class":"Centrally acting antitussive","route":"Oral centrally acting antitussive; avoid suppressing useful productive cough and review serotonergic interactions.","uses":"Selected nonproductive cough","moa":"Modulates central cough signaling","effects":"Sedation/drug interactions","contraindications":"Risk serotonergic interactions and sedation; suppressing cough may be harmful when productive secretions need clearing.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Serotonergic drug interactions possible","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Centrally acting antitussive with uncertain efficacy in dogs; some OTC combination products contain dangerous co-ingredients. Assess causes of cough."],"studyGuidePages":"15:62"},{"id":"potassium-iodide","name":"Potassium iodide","brand":"Potassium iodide","class":"Expectorant / iodine source","route":"Oral iodine/expectorant historical use; thyroid effects and poor modern clinical utility need scrutiny.","uses":"Historical expectorant","moa":"Alters respiratory secretions","effects":"Iodism and thyroid concerns","contraindications":"Iodism/thyroid dysfunction and GI intolerance limit routine use; cannot substitute for identifying cause of cough.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Current role is limited; verify use","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Expectorant that changes respiratory secretion characteristics; prolonged iodide exposure can affect thyroid function and GI tolerance."],"studyGuidePages":"15:62"},{"id":"ciclesonide","name":"Ciclesonide","brand":"Ciclesonide","class":"Inhaled corticosteroid","route":"Inhaled corticosteroid for equine airway inflammation in labeled context; delivery device and competition regulations vary.","uses":"Selected equine airway inflammation","moa":"Local glucocorticoid receptor signaling","effects":"Steroid-related adverse effects","contraindications":"Inhaled steroid may suppress inflammation but not rescue acute bronchospasm; check horse product/device, competition and infection risk.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Verify availability and equine labeling","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Inhaled corticosteroid used in equine asthma formulations in some jurisdictions; reduces airway inflammation but is not an immediate bronchodilator for acute distress."]},{"id":"triamterene","name":"Triamterene","brand":"Triamterene","class":"K-sparing diuretic","route":"Oral potassium-sparing ENaC blocker where used; monitor potassium/renal function.","uses":"Selected adjunct diuresis","moa":"Blocks ENaC in collecting duct","effects":"Hyperkalemia","contraindications":"Potassium-sparing ENaC blockade can cause hyperkalemia, especially with impaired renal function or ACEI/ARB/spironolactone.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Weak diuretic alone","lecture":"Lecture 16","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Direct ENaC inhibitor; potassium-sparing weak diuretic, uncommon veterinary choice. Watch hyperkalemia especially with RAAS blockade."],"studyGuidePages":"16:65"},{"id":"amiloride","name":"Amiloride","brand":"Amiloride","class":"K-sparing diuretic","route":"Oral ENaC blocker; hyperkalemia risk especially with ACEI/ARB or other K-sparing drugs.","uses":"Selected adjunct diuresis","moa":"Blocks ENaC in collecting duct","effects":"Hyperkalemia","contraindications":"Potassium retention risk rises with renal impairment and ACE inhibitors, ARBs or spironolactone; monitor electrolytes and hydration.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Assess renal function/potassium","lecture":"Lecture 16","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Collecting-duct ENaC inhibitor; potassium-sparing with limited sole-diuretic efficacy, and hyperkalemia risk."],"studyGuidePages":"16:65"},{"id":"ropinirole","name":"Ropinirole ophthalmic","brand":"Clevor","class":"Dopamine D2 agonist","route":"Topical eye drops administered by veterinary personnel to induce vomiting in dogs; ophthalmic administration is a ROUTE, not an eye-disease indication.","uses":"Induction of emesis in dogs using approved ophthalmic ropinirole (Clevor), NOT treatment of ophthalmic disease.","moa":"Systemically absorbed dopamine D2-like receptor agonist stimulates chemoreceptor trigger zone (area postrema), activating the vomiting center.","effects":"Ocular irritation/conjunctival redness, third-eyelid protrusion, lethargy, prolonged vomiting, salivation, tremor, transient tachycardia and reduced blood pressure.","contraindications":"Do not use with CNS depression or seizures; ingestion of sharp objects, corrosives, volatile substances or organic solvents; corneal ulcer, eye irritation or injury; or known hypersensitivity. Assess aspiration risk and cardiohepatic disease.","species":"Dogs only; prescription, veterinarian-administered emetic.","why":"Its eye-drop route does not make it an ophthalmic therapy. Dopamine D2 activation in the chemoreceptor trigger zone triggers vomiting; avoid use when vomiting may cause additional injury.","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Drug-safety audit: primary US label reviewed 2026-11-23; check current label before prescribing.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Ophthalmic D2 agonist labeled to induce emesis in dogs under supervision; distinguish from antiemetic D2 antagonist metoclopramide; not intended for cats."],"studyGuidePages":"17:68–70","clinicalRefs":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=b8cfb2c3-18a7-4e35-a807-2b9b13aa10c2"]},{"id":"apomorphine","name":"Apomorphine","brand":"Apomorphine","class":"Dopamine agonist","route":"Injectable or conjunctival emetic used selectively in dogs; monitor sedation/aspiration and caustic ingestion contraindications.","uses":"Emesis induction in dogs","moa":"Stimulates CTZ to induce emesis","effects":"Prolonged emesis, CNS effects","contraindications":"Avoid routine emesis after corrosives, hydrocarbon aspiration risk, obstruction, or depressed consciousness; monitor CNS effects and persistent vomiting.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not appropriate for all ingestions","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: D2 agonist produces vomiting reliably in many dogs but is less useful and may cause excitation in cats; avoid when emesis is contraindicated."],"studyGuidePages":"17:68–70"},{"id":"ranitidine","name":"Ranitidine","brand":"Ranitidine","class":"H2 receptor blocker","route":"Oral/injectable H2 antagonist historically used; current availability and appropriateness require checking.","uses":"Historical GI therapy","moa":"Inhibits acid secretion; some motility effects","effects":"Variable effects","contraindications":"Historical H2 blocker; availability/regulatory status varies by region. Assess renal function, interactions and whether an H2 blocker is appropriate.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Historical product availability/regulatory caution","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): H2 antagonist with some GI prokinetic effects historically; US product market withdrawals/availability limit use. Do not present as a routinely available first-line option."]},{"id":"nizatidine","name":"Nizatidine","brand":"Nizatidine","class":"H2 receptor blocker","route":"Oral H2 blocker with possible motility properties; not a first choice for all GI disease.","uses":"Selected acid control / motility","moa":"Inhibits acid secretion; some prokinetic effects","effects":"Potential GI/CNS effects","contraindications":"Consider renal clearance and CNS/GI effects; not a reliable substitute for evaluating mechanical obstruction or prescribing a prokinetic for every ileus.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Check current availability","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): H2 antagonist, used as historical acid-suppression/prokinetic comparison; availability and species evidence differ from famotidine."]},{"id":"erythromycin-prokinetic","name":"Erythromycin (prokinetic use)","brand":"Erythromycin (prokinetic use)","class":"Macrolide / motilin agonist","route":"Oral low-dose macrolide motilin agonism in selected GI motility disorders; separate from antimicrobial resistance considerations.","uses":"Selected GI hypomotility","moa":"Motilin receptor activation at subantimicrobial doses","effects":"GI adverse effects, antimicrobial resistance concern","contraindications":"Motilin effects are inappropriate with mechanical GI obstruction; antimicrobial selection pressures, GI intolerance and interactions matter even at low motility doses.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"No mechanical obstruction","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Low-dose motilin receptor agonism can promote upper GI motility, but it is also a macrolide antimicrobial with dysbiosis/resistance and drug-interaction implications."],"studyGuidePages":"17:70–73"},{"id":"neostigmine","name":"Neostigmine","brand":"Neostigmine","class":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor","route":"Monitored injectable cholinesterase inhibitor for selected motility/neuromuscular uses; bradycardia and obstruction risk.","uses":"Selected equine hypomotility under monitoring","moa":"Raises acetylcholine at cholinergic synapses","effects":"Cholinergic toxicity","contraindications":"GI/urinary obstruction","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Requires case-specific assessment","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor raises acetylcholine at muscarinic and nicotinic junctions; selected GI motility/neuromuscular indications; bradycardia, secretions and obstruction risk."]},{"id":"famotidine","name":"Famotidine","brand":"Famotidine","class":"H2 receptor antagonist","route":"Oral/injectable H2 blocker for selected acid-related GI indications; renal clearance and tachyphylaxis matter.","uses":"Selected gastric acid conditions","moa":"Blocks histamine H2-mediated acid secretion","effects":"Tolerance with continued use","contraindications":"Renal impairment can increase exposure; rapid tolerance/tachyphylaxis may limit repeated H2-blocker therapy and this is not a primary antiemetic.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not a prokinetic","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): H2-receptor antagonist for selected acid-related disease; generally less potent than PPIs for ulcer healing and develops tachyphylaxis with continued use."]},{"id":"cimetidine","name":"Cimetidine","brand":"Cimetidine","class":"H2 receptor antagonist","route":"Oral/injectable H2 blocker with numerous CYP drug interactions; rarely preferred when alternatives exist.","uses":"Selected gastric acid conditions","moa":"Blocks H2-mediated acid secretion","effects":"Drug metabolism interactions","contraindications":"Inhibits multiple drug-metabolizing enzymes and can alter exposure to concurrent drugs; monitor renal function and interactions rather than routine long-term use.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not a prokinetic","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): H2 antagonist with notable CYP-mediated interactions and endocrine effects; not interchangeable with famotidine in interaction burden."]},{"id":"cobalamin","name":"Cobalamin (B12)","brand":"Cobalamin (B12)","class":"Vitamin","route":"Oral or injectable vitamin B12 supplementation based on species/clinical cause; test deficiency and response.","uses":"Hypocobalaminemia in GI/pancreatic disease","moa":"Replenishes cobalamin","effects":"Usually well tolerated","contraindications":"B12 supplementation is usually safe but does not correct an unresolved foreign body, severe exocrine pancreatic disease or other primary cause of malabsorption.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Replacement helps correct low B12 in selected chronic intestinal or pancreatic disease.","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · detailed Lecture 16/17 mechanism comparison · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Vitamin B12 replacement for documented deficiency/malabsorption (often chronic enteropathy); oral vs parenteral regimens depend on clinical context. Monitor clinical response and B12."]},{"id":"acetylcysteine","name":"Acetylcysteine (NAC)","brand":"Acetylcysteine (NAC)","class":"Glutathione precursor / antidote","route":"Oral or IV N-acetylcysteine for selected toxicologic/hepatic indications; formulation/route and protocol matter.","uses":"Acetaminophen toxicosis; selected liver injury","moa":"Supplies cysteine for glutathione synthesis and directly participates in redox reactions; protects against toxic acetaminophen metabolites.","effects":"Oral nausea/vomiting; IV anaphylactoid-like reactions, particularly with faster administration.","contraindications":"Acetaminophen toxicosis requires time-sensitive treatment. Ensure correct route/protocol and monitor infusion reactions.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Glutathione detoxifies NAPQI; replenishment limits oxidative hepatocyte injury and in cats methemoglobin-related oxidative stress.","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Glutathione precursor for acetaminophen toxicity and selected liver support, not broad substitute for specific toxicology stabilization; vomiting can occur with oral use."],"studyGuidePages":"17:76–79"},{"id":"rifaximin","name":"Rifaximin","brand":"Rifaximin","class":"Poorly absorbed antimicrobial","route":"Oral minimally absorbed antimicrobial for selected gut-origin ammonia control, with antimicrobial stewardship.","uses":"Selected hepatic encephalopathy adjunct","moa":"Reduces intestinal ammonia-producing bacteria","effects":"GI effects","contraindications":"Avoid indiscriminate antimicrobial treatment for noninfectious encephalopathy; review species/regional product status, cost, GI effects and resistance risk.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Assess stewardship and formulation","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Poorly absorbed rifamycin antimicrobial for selected hepatic encephalopathy intestinal ammonia reduction; costly and not a first-line replacement for managing hepatic disease."]},{"id":"ursodiol","name":"Ursodeoxycholic acid","brand":"Ursodeoxycholic acid","class":"Hydrophilic bile acid","route":"Oral hydrophilic bile acid for selected cholestatic liver disease; avoid complete biliary obstruction.","uses":"Selected cholestasis","moa":"Promotes bile flow; reduces hydrophobic bile acid burden","effects":"GI intolerance","contraindications":"Avoid in complete biliary obstruction","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Hydrophilic bile acid used in selected cholestatic disease; do not assume appropriate in complete biliary obstruction.","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · detailed Lecture 16/17 mechanism comparison · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Hydrophilic bile acid for appropriate cholestatic hepatobiliary disease; avoid with complete bile-duct obstruction; monitor liver markers and underlying disease."],"studyGuidePages":"17:76–79"},{"id":"penicillamine","name":"D-penicillamine","brand":"D-penicillamine","class":"Copper chelator","route":"Oral copper chelator given with meal-timing/absorption consideration; GI effects and hematologic monitoring.","uses":"Selected copper-associated hepatopathy","moa":"Chelates excess copper for elimination","effects":"GI effects","contraindications":"Copper chelation can cause anorexia/vomiting and mineral imbalance; monitor copper/liver status and separate dosing from interfering oral products.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Copper chelation is not equivalent to suppressing dietary copper uptake.","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · detailed Lecture 16/17 mechanism comparison · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Copper chelator for select copper-associated hepatopathy with monitoring of CBC, urinalysis/proteinuria and GI adverse effects; dietary copper reduction may also matter."],"studyGuidePages":"17:76–79"},{"id":"zinc-gluconate","name":"Zinc gluconate","brand":"Zinc gluconate","class":"Copper absorption antagonist","route":"Oral zinc for copper-absorption reduction; monitor copper status and zinc intolerance.","uses":"Selected copper hepatopathy maintenance","moa":"Reduces intestinal copper uptake","effects":"GI upset","contraindications":"Excess zinc can cause GI irritation and copper deficiency; absorption is affected by coadministered food or chelators, so monitor copper and tolerance.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Avoid dosing conflicts with other drugs","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Reduces intestinal copper uptake in selected copper-associated disease; dosing, GI tolerance and zinc/copper monitoring matter."],"studyGuidePages":"17:76–79"},{"id":"metronidazole","name":"Metronidazole","brand":"Metronidazole","class":"Nitroimidazole antimicrobial","route":"Oral/injectable nitroimidazole antimicrobial; neurotoxicity with high exposure, avoid indiscriminate chronic GI use.","uses":"Selected infections, historical GI use","moa":"Damages susceptible anaerobes/protozoa","effects":"Dose-related neurotoxicity","contraindications":"Dose-related neurotoxicity (ataxia, seizures) and dysbiosis limit indiscriminate chronic enteropathy use; consider liver dysfunction and true antimicrobial need.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not routine first-line therapy for nonspecific chronic diarrhea","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Nitroimidazole targeting anaerobes and some protozoa; avoid reflex treatment of nonspecific diarrhea; CNS toxicity and microbiome disruption can occur."]},{"id":"tylosin","name":"Tylosin","brand":"Tylosin","class":"Macrolide antimicrobial","route":"Oral macrolide for selected veterinary GI use; approved indications differ from empiric chronic enteropathy prescribing.","uses":"Selected canine chronic enteropathy under review","moa":"Inhibits bacterial ribosomal protein synthesis","effects":"Dysbiosis/resistance","contraindications":"Prolonged empiric use can drive dysbiosis/resistance; not automatically appropriate for every chronic diarrhea case, particularly without diagnostic workup.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Stewardship before empiric use","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Macrolide antibacterial sometimes used in selected antibiotic-responsive enteropathies; stewardship and resistant organisms matter. Do not routinely treat all chronic diarrhea."]},{"id":"sulfasalazine","name":"Sulfasalazine","brand":"Sulfasalazine","class":"5-ASA / sulfonamide prodrug","route":"Oral colon-delivered 5-ASA sulfonamide prodrug; canine keratoconjunctivitis sicca/blood monitoring.","uses":"Selected colitis","moa":"Colonic bacteria cleave sulfasalazine, delivering locally active 5-aminosalicylic acid for mucosal anti-inflammatory action.","effects":"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca in dogs, anorexia/GI signs, sulfonamide hypersensitivity and possible blood dyscrasias.","contraindications":"Monitor tear production during extended canine use, CBC as appropriate, and stop for eye pain, severe pruritus or systemic illness.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Colon-targeted 5-ASA reduces mucosal inflammation; sulfonamide moiety contributes systemic adverse effects.","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"GI/respiratory mechanism and clinical caution audit 2026-11-23; verify individual label.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): 5-ASA carrier prodrug for selected large bowel inflammation; not primarily a systemic antibacterial despite sulfonamide component. Monitor keratoconjunctivitis sicca and adverse effects in dogs."]},{"id":"amitriptyline","name":"Amitriptyline","brand":"Amitriptyline","class":"TCA","route":"Oral TCA; anticholinergic, cardiac-conduction and serotonergic interaction cautions.","uses":"Selected behavioral indications","moa":"Inhibits serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake; antimuscarinic action","effects":"Anticholinergic, cardiac effects","contraindications":"TCA anticholinergic effects and cardiac conduction changes can worsen glaucoma, urine retention or arrhythmias; avoid unsafe MAOI combinations.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Consult veterinary behavior specialist","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): TCA with serotonergic/noradrenergic and antimuscarinic effects; limited compared with more selective options. Monitor cardiac conduction, urinary retention and interactions."]},{"id":"doxepin","name":"Doxepin","brand":"Doxepin","class":"TCA","route":"Oral TCA with antihistamine effects; sedation and cardiac/anticholinergic effects.","uses":"Selected behavior/itch contexts","moa":"Inhibits monoamine reuptake with antihistamine effects","effects":"Cardiac/anticholinergic effects","contraindications":"TCA antihistamine effect causes sedation, anticholinergic effects and potential conduction abnormalities; medication interactions and comorbid cardiac disease matter.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Off-label caveats","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): TCA with prominent antihistamine activity; selected dermatologic/behavior comparisons, not a routine first-line sedative. Caution anticholinergic/cardiovascular effects."]},{"id":"imipramine","name":"Imipramine","brand":"Imipramine","class":"TCA","route":"Oral TCA; anticholinergic effects, cardiovascular conduction and interactions.","uses":"Selected behavioral/urologic uses","moa":"Inhibits monoamine reuptake","effects":"Cardiac effects","contraindications":"TCA may cause hypotension, urinary retention, arrhythmias and serotonin interactions; assess cardiac disease and concurrent psychotropics.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Check species / current practice","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): TCA used for selected historical behavior/urinary comparisons; NE/5-HT reuptake blockade plus antimuscarinic effects. Not equivalent to clomipramine across indications."]},{"id":"cyproheptadine","name":"Cyproheptadine","brand":"Cyproheptadine","class":"Serotonin / histamine antagonist","route":"Oral serotonin/H1 antagonist used as adjunct in selected serotonin toxicity; urgent supportive care comes first.","uses":"Selected severe serotonin toxicity adjunct","moa":"Blocks 5-HT2 and H1 receptors","effects":"Sedation, anticholinergic effects","contraindications":"Monitor temperature, muscle activity, neurologic status and cardiovascular stability; treat as urgent toxidrome.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Emergency veterinary assessment necessary","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Serotonin receptor antagonist used in selected serotonin-toxicity management as an adjunct after stopping offending drugs and providing supportive care; not a standalone antidote to all causes."],"studyGuidePages":"18–20:137","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"105–106,137","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog receiving an SSRI plus another serotonergic medication develops hyperthermia, agitation, tremor and gastrointestinal signs.","decision":"Stop the suspected serotonergic agents and stabilize the patient urgently; cyproheptadine can be a selected adjunct but is not stand-alone treatment.","why":"Serotonin receptor antagonism may reduce serotonin-toxicity manifestations, while fluids, cooling and seizure control address the physiological crisis.","whatChanges":"Differentiate serotonin syndrome from pain, malignant hyperthermia, or neurotoxins; reassess interacting drugs before any restart.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Consider as an adjunct in suspected serotonin toxicity only after discontinuing interacting drugs and initiating supportive stabilization.","physiologyWhy":"Serotonin receptor antagonism can counter selected excess 5-HT effects; it does not reverse all causes of hyperthermia or tremor.","compareAndContrast":"Different from flumazenil (benzodiazepine-site antagonist) and naloxone (opioid antagonist).","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor temperature, muscle activity, neurologic status and cardiovascular stability; treat as urgent toxidrome.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"alprazolam","name":"Alprazolam","brand":"Alprazolam","class":"Benzodiazepine","route":"Oral short-acting benzodiazepine in selected behavior cases; monitor paradoxical excitation.","uses":"Selected situational anxiety","moa":"Positive allosteric GABA-A modulator","effects":"Sedation, paradoxical disinhibition","contraindications":"Watch sedation, ataxia, paradoxical disinhibition/aggression, dependence and interaction with other CNS depressants; assess species-specific handling.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Taper with repeated use","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Benzodiazepine used for selected panic/noise triggers; assess onset and paradoxical disinhibition, sedation and dependence/tolerance. Avoid unmonitored abrupt cessation after chronic dosing."],"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"106","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • lecture tables (not opening master tables)","rationale":"Explicit ★ appears with this medication or its exact highlighted combination in the lecture table","verifyBold":"PDF text extraction does not establish bold/yellow typography"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"For selected episodic anxiety/fear treatment in dogs, pair appropriately selected short-acting anxiolysis with behavioral and safety interventions.","physiologyWhy":"Benzodiazepine positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A increases inhibitory chloride flux when GABA is present; effects can occur much sooner than SSRIs.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike fluoxetine/clomipramine maintenance agents with delayed adaptation, alprazolam may be situational; unlike acepromazine, it directly modulates inhibitory anxiolytic pathways.","monitorAndAvoid":"Watch sedation, ataxia, paradoxical disinhibition/aggression, dependence and interaction with other CNS depressants; assess species-specific handling.","provenance":"Main Exam 2 lecture table ★, course-specific clinical synthesis; prescribing requires current source verification"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Patient scenario: For selected episodic anxiety/fear treatment in dogs, pair appropriately selected short-acting anxiolysis with behavioral and safety interventions.","decision":"For selected episodic anxiety/fear treatment in dogs, pair appropriately selected short-acting anxiolysis with behavioral and safety interventions.","why":"Benzodiazepine positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A increases inhibitory chloride flux when GABA is present; effects can occur much sooner than SSRIs.","whatChanges":"Watch sedation, ataxia, paradoxical disinhibition/aggression, dependence and interaction with other CNS depressants; assess species-specific handling.","sourceType":"Starred-lecture-priority teaching case"}},{"id":"flumazenil","name":"Flumazenil","brand":"Flumazenil","class":"Benzodiazepine-site antagonist","route":"Monitored IV benzodiazepine-site antagonist; may unmask seizures.","uses":"Selected benzodiazepine reversal","moa":"Competitively blocks BDZ binding site at GABA-A","effects":"Agitation, anxiety, rapid return of sedation after reversal wears off and precipitation of seizures/withdrawal in dependent or mixed-exposure patients.","contraindications":"Avoid indiscriminate reversal in chronic benzodiazepine dependence, seizure control with benzodiazepines, or mixed proconvulsant intoxication; monitor airway and resedation.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Competing at the benzodiazepine GABA-A site reverses sedation but can abruptly remove protection against seizures.","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Competitive benzodiazepine binding-site antagonist; does not reverse opioids or alpha-2 drugs. Can precipitate withdrawal seizures in benzodiazepine-dependent patients."],"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"108","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"After sedation, a dog is obtunded from a known benzodiazepine exposure. The same dog also received an opioid.","decision":"Flumazenil can reverse the benzodiazepine component but not the opioid. Assess airway and other sedatives first; chronic benzodiazepine dependence raises withdrawal-seizure concern.","why":"Flumazenil competitively occupies the benzodiazepine site on GABA-A, unlike naloxone at opioid receptors.","whatChanges":"Continue respiratory and neurologic monitoring for re-sedation, and consider analgesic consequences of reversing other drugs.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use selective benzodiazepine reversal when it is appropriate and the seizure risk from antagonism is acceptable.","physiologyWhy":"Competitive antagonism at the benzodiazepine binding site of GABA-A reduces benzodiazepine sedation.","compareAndContrast":"Unlike naloxone and atipamezole, it does not reverse opioids or alpha-2 agonists; it may reverse useful anticonvulsant effect.","monitorAndAvoid":"Consider seizure history, mixed intoxications, possible resedation and whether airway support is preferable.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"alfaxalone","name":"Alfaxalone","brand":"Alfaxalone","class":"Neurosteroid anesthetic","route":"Injectable anesthesia induction/maintenance drug with airway and ventilation monitoring; not an analgesic.","uses":"Induction / selected sedation","moa":"Positive allosteric GABA-A modulator","effects":"Dose-related respiratory depression","contraindications":"Monitor apnea, blood pressure, ventilation, recovery and delivery rate; do not mistake hypnosis for pain relief.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Not a substitute for complete pain control","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Neurosteroid anesthetic positive modulator of GABA-A; injectable induction/selected sedation protocols; does not provide dependable analgesia; monitor apnea and cardiorespiratory status."],"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"109","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A cat needs a short anesthetic procedure; a student proposes alfaxalone as both the induction agent and the entire analgesic plan.","decision":"Alfaxalone can support induction or anesthesia but does not reliably provide procedural analgesia; design an appropriate multimodal pain plan.","why":"The neurosteroid positively modulates GABA-A to produce unconsciousness, which is not the same as blocking nociceptive processing.","whatChanges":"Monitor airway, ventilation, hypotension, recovery quality, and analgesic needs according to the procedure.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Recognize a titratable injectable anesthetic induction drug, with planned airway and ventilation support.","physiologyWhy":"Neuroactive steroid positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A produces hypnosis/unconsciousness, not independent surgical analgesia.","compareAndContrast":"Different from opioids providing analgesia or alpha-2 sedatives; must combine components appropriately for painful procedures.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor apnea, blood pressure, ventilation, recovery and delivery rate; do not mistake hypnosis for pain relief.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"pentobarbital","name":"Pentobarbital","brand":"Pentobarbital","class":"Barbiturate","route":"Monitored IV barbiturate anesthetic/euthanasia drug; humane use requires unconsciousness and legal disposal safeguards.","uses":"Specialist anesthesia/euthanasia contexts","moa":"Potentiates GABA-A inhibition","effects":"Respiratory/cardiovascular depression","contraindications":"Profound respiratory/cardiovascular suppression; humane euthanasia use requires legal protocol, unconsciousness and carcass/scavenger protection.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Rendering and scavenger poisoning risk","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Barbiturate used in specialist anesthesia/refractory seizure contexts and euthanasia solutions; respiratory depression and hospital-only administration; do not conflate with maintenance phenobarbital."]},{"id":"propofol","name":"Propofol","brand":"Propofol","class":"IV anesthetic","route":"Monitored IV anesthetic induction/maintenance; apnea/hypotension and lack of surgical analgesia require planning.","uses":"Anesthesia / refractory status protocols","moa":"Enhances GABA-A signaling","effects":"Apnea, hypotension","contraindications":"Can cause apnea and hypotension, especially with rapid boluses; loss of consciousness is not analgesia, requiring airway and pain management.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Monitor ventilation","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): IV GABA-A-modulating anesthetic for controlled induction and refractory seizure protocols; dose-dependent apnea/hypotension. No analgesia and requires airway monitoring."]},{"id":"primidone","name":"Primidone","brand":"Primidone","class":"Anticonvulsant prodrug","route":"Oral historical anticonvulsant in dogs, metabolized to phenobarbital; hepatic monitoring burden.","uses":"Historical epilepsy medication","moa":"Metabolized in part to phenobarbital","effects":"Hepatic and other adverse effects","contraindications":"Monitor liver enzymes/function, serum anticonvulsant levels as appropriate and clinical seizure control; avoid reflex use in cats.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Metabolized to phenobarbital among other products; the guide flags its use against modern clinical recommendations.","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · neuro lecture distinction checked · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Hepatically metabolized to phenobarbital and other metabolites; older canine antiseizure drug usually not preferred due to hepatic adverse-effect risk despite lecture discussions of legacy approval."],"studyGuidePages":"19:128","coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"128","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Explicit star","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog with chronic seizures and increased liver enzymes is offered primidone because the lecture calls it an approved canine antiseizure medication.","decision":"Do not select primidone solely on a historical approval statement. Evaluate liver function and current recommended first-line anticonvulsants.","why":"Primidone is metabolized to phenobarbital and other active metabolites; the extra metabolic burden can increase hepatic concern.","whatChanges":"Compare actual current product approval with clinical guideline recommendations and discuss hepatic monitoring and safer alternatives.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority clinical teaching case; not a dosing order"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Understand its historical anticonvulsant use but recognize why phenobarbital is usually preferred in contemporary canine practice.","physiologyWhy":"Primidone is metabolized to phenobarbital and other metabolites contributing to seizure suppression; added hepatic burden matters.","compareAndContrast":"Do not treat primidone as interchangeable with levetiracetam SV2A action or bromide chloride-sensitive elimination.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor liver enzymes/function, serum anticonvulsant levels as appropriate and clinical seizure control; avoid reflex use in cats.","provenance":"Exam 2 lecture-priority clinical synthesis (Nov 17); clinical prescribing and product restrictions require current independent verification"}},{"id":"felbamate","name":"Felbamate","brand":"Felbamate","class":"Anticonvulsant","route":"Oral adjunctive anticonvulsant in selected refractory patients; blood/liver monitoring and specialist use.","uses":"Selected refractory epilepsy","moa":"NMDA and sodium-channel modulation","effects":"Serious hematologic/hepatic risks","contraindications":"Potential hepatic injury and hematologic adverse effects require specialist selection and liver/CBC monitoring; not routine first-line monotherapy.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Specialist use","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Anticonvulsant affecting excitatory transmission; refractory canine epilepsy adjunct with potentially serious hematologic/hepatic effects; specialist monitoring and limited evidence."]},{"id":"gabapentin","name":"Gabapentin","brand":"Gabapentin","class":"Gabapentinoid","route":"Oral gabapentinoid; sedation, renal adjustment and indications differ from true antiseizure efficacy in dogs.","uses":"Selected neuropathic pain / adjunct","moa":"Binds alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, lowering excitatory transmitter release.","effects":"Sedation and ataxia; greater/prolonged effects possible in renal impairment.","contraindications":"Dose/interval must account for renal clearance and other sedatives; human liquids can contain problematic excipients (e.g., xylitol in some products).","species":"Species- and formulation-dependent","why":"Despite its name, gabapentin does not directly activate GABA-A or GABA-B receptors.","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","veterinaryRelevance":"Common selected veterinary use / often extra-label","veterinaryContext":"Gabapentinoid used for selected pain/anxiety adjuncts; not interchangeable with an opioid.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha2-delta voltage-gated calcium-channel ligand, NOT a direct GABA-receptor agonist; used for selected neuropathic pain/situational anxieties and sometimes adjunct seizure applications."],"studyGuidePages":"19:126"},{"id":"pregabalin","name":"Pregabalin","brand":"Pregabalin","class":"Gabapentinoid","route":"Oral gabapentinoid; sedation/ataxia and renal clearance; species evidence differs.","uses":"Selected neuropathic pain / adjunct","moa":"Binds alpha2-delta calcium-channel subunit","effects":"Sedation, ataxia","contraindications":"Renal clearance may prolong sedation/ataxia; monitor concurrent CNS depressants and adjust to patient renal status.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Renal considerations","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha2-delta calcium-channel ligand; not interchangeable with direct GABA agonists and generally not paired routinely with gabapentin due to overlapping mechanism/effects."],"studyGuidePages":"19:126"},{"id":"detomidine","name":"Detomidine","brand":"Detomidine","class":"Alpha-2 agonist","route":"Injectable equine alpha-2 sedative or oromucosal equine gel product depending indication; not interchangeable routes.","uses":"Equine sedation / analgesia","moa":"Reduces norepinephrine release","effects":"Bradycardia, GI hypomotility","contraindications":"Alpha-2 effects can produce bradycardia, reduced cardiac output, sweating and gut hypomotility; assess perfusion and equine colic severity.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Species-specific product use","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Equine alpha-2 agonist (including oromucosal gel formulation) for sedation/analgesia; monitor cardiac, GI motility and ataxia; avoid assuming species/formulation doses interchange."],"studyGuidePages":"20:130–132"},{"id":"clonidine","name":"Clonidine","brand":"Clonidine","class":"Alpha-2 agonist","route":"Oral alpha-2 agonist in selected behavior contexts; monitor BP and bradycardia.","uses":"Selected fear-related physical arousal","moa":"Reduces sympathetic outflow","effects":"Hypotension, sedation","contraindications":"Alpha-2-mediated hypotension/bradycardia and sedation can complicate use with cardiovascular disease or other CNS depressants; fear itself still needs treatment.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Behavior-modification adjunct","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Oral alpha-2 agonist used in selected situational anxieties; lecture suggests giving 1.5–2 h before trigger; reduces autonomic arousal but does not itself guarantee reduced fear."],"studyGuidePages":"20:133"},{"id":"atipamezole","name":"Atipamezole","brand":"Atipamezole","class":"Alpha-2 antagonist","route":"Injectable alpha-2 antagonist with product-specific route; reversal may eliminate associated analgesia.","uses":"Selected sedation reversal","moa":"Selective alpha-2 adrenergic antagonist reverses medetomidine/dexmedetomidine sedation.","effects":"Rapid arousal, excitement/tremors, tachycardia, vomiting and unmasking of untreated pain.","contraindications":"Reversal does not treat effects of other sedatives or anesthetics; reassess analgesia, temperature and cardiovascular function.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Removing alpha-2 sedation and its analgesic contribution can abruptly reveal pain.","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha-2 antagonist used to reverse medetomidine/dexmedetomidine effects; does NOT reverse an opioid given concurrently. Assess ongoing pain and monitoring after reversal."],"studyGuidePages":"20:132–134","teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Reverses medetomidine/dexmedetomidine in appropriate species/formulation contexts.","physiologyWhy":"Alpha-2 antagonist blocks the agonist receptor mechanism and decreases sedation.","compareAndContrast":"Naloxone reverses opioid receptors; flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines—never treat them as interchangeable.","monitorAndAvoid":"Watch rebound arousal, pain, hemodynamics and persistent effects of other co-administered agents.","provenance":"Course-aligned synthesis; compare lecture-specific points and independent veterinary sources; prescribing requires full current labels"},"coursePriority":{"level":"starred-lecture-topic-or-key-table","guidePages":"132–134","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • main lecture sections (not initial summaries)","rationale":"Starred reversal relationship","verifyBold":"Bold weight could not be independently extracted from this PDF's text layer"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"A dog received dexmedetomidine plus a full mu opioid before a procedure; after atipamezole it becomes alert but still hypoventilates.","decision":"Reassess opioid and airway effects instead of repeating atipamezole for all sedation; decide whether opioid reversal is necessary while managing pain.","why":"Atipamezole competitively reverses alpha-2 effects, whereas naloxone antagonizes opioids; each acts at a different receptor.","whatChanges":"Continue ECG, ventilation, temperature and pain monitoring; return of arousal can reveal pain when the alpha-2 analgesia disappears.","sourceType":"Lecture-priority veterinary teaching example; not a dosing order"}},{"id":"yohimbine","name":"Yohimbine","brand":"Yohimbine","class":"Alpha-2 antagonist","route":"Injectable alpha-2 antagonist for appropriate xylazine reversal; species and product use matters.","uses":"Selected xylazine reversal","moa":"Blocks alpha-2 receptors","effects":"Tachycardia/excitement","contraindications":"May cause excitation, tachycardia and hypotension/hypertension fluctuations; reversal is alpha-2-specific and does not remove opioid or benzodiazepine effects.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Species-dependent","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Alpha-2 antagonist used with some xylazine/other alpha-2 protocols in species-specific settings; not universal receptor reversal for all sedatives."],"studyGuidePages":"20:130–134"},{"id":"azaperone","name":"Azaperone","brand":"Azaperone","class":"Butyrophenone tranquilizer","route":"Injectable butyrophenone tranquilizer with swine context; check species/product/region and no inherent analgesia.","uses":"Selected swine sedation / social aggression contexts","moa":"D2 antagonist","effects":"Hypotension / sedation","contraindications":"Species/product restrictions for swine apply; tranquilization can cause hypotension and is not analgesia or full anesthesia.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Current availability and food-animal label check","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Butyrophenone D2 antagonist historically used in swine handling/behavior contexts; availability and label/jurisdiction can limit use."],"studyGuidePages":"20:137"},{"id":"morphine","name":"Morphine","brand":"Morphine","class":"Full mu opioid agonist","route":"Injectable full mu agonist or other formulations depending setting; monitor respiratory effects, vomiting and analgesia.","uses":"Severe pain in appropriate patients","moa":"Full mu opioid receptor agonist reduces nociceptive transmission in brain and spinal cord.","effects":"Respiratory depression, vomiting, sedation, bradycardia and urinary retention; species/route-related excitation possible.","contraindications":"Titrate and monitor ventilation, blood pressure, temperature and other sedatives; avoid assuming analgesia equals safe anesthesia.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Mu activation reduces pain transmission but also depresses brainstem respiratory drive.","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Full mu agonist for severe acute pain in appropriate monitored patients; vomiting, respiratory depression, sedation and GI motility changes; adjust for patient status."],"studyGuidePages":"20:138"},{"id":"fentanyl-patch","name":"Fentanyl transdermal","brand":"Fentanyl transdermal","class":"Full mu opioid agonist","route":"Transdermal long-acting fentanyl with delayed onset/offset and variable veterinary absorption; avoid unmonitored acute-only reliance.","uses":"Selected analgesia","moa":"Continuous transdermal opioid exposure","effects":"Exposure hazard / variable absorption","contraindications":"Delayed absorption and variable veterinary kinetics make patches unreliable for immediate rescue; accidental human/animal exposure and patch disposal are serious safety concerns.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Patch disposal and human contact","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Full mu agonist transdermal analgesia with delayed onset and prolonged drug exposure; dose/heat/accidental ingestion and human diversion hazards; not an immediate analgesic substitute."]},{"id":"naltrexone","name":"Naltrexone","brand":"Naltrexone","class":"Opioid antagonist","route":"Oral/long-acting opioid antagonist for selected uses; not identical to emergency IV naloxone.","uses":"Selected long-acting reversal","moa":"Blocks opioid receptors","effects":"Reversal of analgesia","contraindications":"Prolonged opioid antagonism removes analgesia and can precipitate withdrawal in dependent subjects; not interchangeable with naloxone emergency duration.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Longer acting than naloxone","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Longer-acting opioid antagonist with specific controlled opioid/wildlife reversal contexts; not benzodiazepine or alpha-2 reversal."],"studyGuidePages":"20:137–140"},{"id":"loperamide","name":"Loperamide","brand":"Loperamide","class":"Peripheral mu opioid agonist","route":"Oral peripheral mu agonist antidiarrheal; avoid in ABCB1-risk dogs and selected infectious/toxic diarrhea.","uses":"Selected diarrhea under veterinary guidance","moa":"Slows intestinal motility","effects":"Neurologic toxicity in ABCB1 variants","contraindications":"ABCB1/P-glycoprotein defects permit dangerous CNS opioid exposure; avoid masking invasive infectious/toxin-associated diarrhea without diagnosis.","species":"See species-specific cautions and course notes","why":"Peripheral mu opioid activity depends on P-glycoprotein keeping the drug out of the CNS; ABCB1 variants are a safety consideration.","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Master table coverage entry · clinical verification required · neuro lecture distinction checked · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Peripheral mu agonist for selected diarrhea indications; avoid infectious/inflammatory contraindications and beware marked CNS toxicity in ABCB1/P-glycoprotein-deficient dogs."],"studyGuidePages":"17–20:138–140"},{"id":"glp1-liraglutide","name":"Liraglutide","class":"GLP-1 receptor agonist","brand":"Liraglutide","moa":"GLP-1 receptor agonism enhances glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppresses inappropriate glucagon and slows gastric emptying.","uses":"Human GLP-1 receptor agonist discussed as an endocrine comparator; not an FDA-approved routine feline or canine diabetes drug.","effects":"Nausea, vomiting, decreased appetite and delayed gastric emptying; clinical safety and efficacy for veterinary patients are not established.","contraindications":"Do not substitute for insulin in insulin-dependent dogs or cats. Treat veterinary use as investigational/specialist-directed; avoid extrapolating human labeling to animals.","species":"Human drug; veterinary research/comparator only.","route":"Human injectable GLP-1 agonist; veterinary endocrine use is investigational/specialist, not routine labeled feline diabetes replacement.","why":"GLP-1 signaling depends on functional beta cells and does not replace absent insulin.","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","veterinaryRelevance":"Investigational / comparison","veterinaryContext":"GLP-1 mechanism is useful to learn, but not routine established canine/feline diabetes therapy.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: GLP-1 receptor agonism increases glucose-dependent insulin signaling, reduces glucagon and slows gastric emptying.","Primarily included for mechanism and research/comparison rather than standard feline or canine diabetes therapy.","The lecture speculates about refractory cases; avoid labeling an experimental adjunct as established treatment."],"studyGuidePages":"30–38"},{"id":"glp1-exenatide","name":"Exenatide","class":"GLP-1 receptor agonist","brand":"Exenatide","moa":"Activates GLP-1 receptors, increasing glucose-dependent insulin release, suppressing glucagon and slowing gastric emptying.","uses":"Human GLP-1 analog used in research/comparative teaching; not routine FDA-approved canine or feline diabetes treatment.","effects":"GI upset, decreased appetite and delayed gastric emptying; veterinary safety and outcomes remain incompletely established.","contraindications":"Not a substitute for required insulin in dogs or insulin-dependent cats. Clinical veterinary dosing and long-term safety are not established.","species":"Human comparator / investigational veterinary use.","route":"Human injectable GLP-1 agonist comparator; veterinary species/formulation evidence and diabetic indication are limited.","why":"Glucose-dependent secretory effect cannot reliably compensate for severe insulin deficiency.","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 38 discusses long-acting exenatide and early studies in diabetic cats; presented as a possible adjunct, NOT an FDA-approved routine feline diabetes treatment.","Never confuse GLP-1 receptor agonists with approved feline SGLT2 inhibitors (Bexacat, Senvelgo), which carry specific contraindications involving prior and concurrent insulin."],"veterinaryRelevance":"Investigational veterinary use"},{"id":"propylene-glycol","name":"Propylene glycol","class":"Glucogenic precursor","brand":"Propylene glycol","moa":"Supports hepatic glucose precursor supply","uses":"Bovine ketosis","effects":"Potential adverse effects with misuse","contraindications":"Use production-species protocol","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral glucogenic precursor in periparturient cattle ketosis protocols; excess exposure can have adverse effects.","why":"Species and formulation critical; toxic to cats","lecture":"Lecture 11","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Glucogenic precursor used orally for selected bovine periparturient ketosis; converted to substrates for gluconeogenesis; monitor intake/clinical status and treat concurrent disease."]},{"id":"iodine-restricted-diet","name":"Restricted-iodine thyroid diet","class":"Nutritional management","brand":"Restricted-iodine thyroid diet","moa":"Restricts iodine substrate for thyroid hormone synthesis","uses":"Dietary management of feline hyperthyroidism when an iodine-restricted therapeutic diet is the exclusive food source.","effects":"Poor acceptance, weight loss if inadequate intake, loss of thyroid control with treats or other foods; periodic T4/renal monitoring is needed.","contraindications":"Only effective with exclusive feeding; evaluate concurrent disease and nutritional needs. Multi-cat households require separate feeding to prevent other food access.","species":"Cats; specialized prescription diet, not a medication.","route":"Strict iodine-restricted feline therapeutic diet exclusively fed to be effective; treats hormone synthesis substrate supply, not acute crises.","why":"Thyroid hormone synthesis requires iodine, so reducing iodine intake limits new T3/T4 production without removing abnormal thyroid tissue.","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE: a strictly iodine-restricted diet limits substrate needed for thyroid hormone synthesis; therapeutic effect depends on exclusive feeding, including avoiding treats and other diets.","COURSE I-131 WORKFLOW: the guide directs discontinuation of y/d for 10–14 days before I-131. The radiation-treatment facility determines its preparation instructions.","A restricted-iodine diet is not I-131 and is not interchangeable with radioactive ablation."],"studyGuidePages":"41–45","professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture p. 43 uses the word remission for y/d dietary control. Distinguish suppression/control on exclusive iodine restriction from permanent remission/cure; hyperthyroidism generally returns if dietary iodine is reintroduced."},{"id":"calcium-carbonate","name":"Calcium carbonate","class":"Oral calcium salt","brand":"Calcium carbonate","moa":"Provides elemental calcium for intestinal uptake; absorption depends on formulation, feeding and vitamin D status.","uses":"Oral calcium supplement for selected chronic hypocalcemia and hypoparathyroidism cases; separate therapeutic role from phosphate binding.","effects":"Constipation, GI upset, hypercalcemia and calcium-phosphate mineralization with overuse.","contraindications":"Monitor ionized calcium, phosphorus and kidney values, especially with calcitriol or other vitamin D agents. Avoid in hypercalcemia.","species":"Selected dogs/cats under monitored treatment.","route":"Oral calcium salt, with elemental-calcium-specific formulation; monitor hypercalcemia and phosphorus.","why":"Replacing calcium addresses neuromuscular excitability; overcorrection increases renal and soft tissue mineralization risk.","lecture":"Lecture 12","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Oral calcium salt as part of post-thyroidectomy/hypoparathyroidism management with calcitriol when indicated; absorption, calcium level, and renal status matter."]},{"id":"demecarium","name":"Demecarium bromide","class":"Cholinesterase inhibitor / miotic","brand":"Demecarium bromide","moa":"Long-acting cholinesterase inhibition increases synaptic acetylcholine, activating iris sphincter and ciliary body muscarinic receptors.","uses":"Historical/selected ophthalmic miotic for glaucoma where appropriate; not broadly interchangeable with modern glaucoma medications.","effects":"Miosis, ocular irritation and possible systemic cholinergic signs (salivation, diarrhea, bradycardia) from absorption.","contraindications":"Use only after ophthalmic evaluation; caution with uveitis, lens instability/luxation and conditions where induced miosis could worsen the eye.","species":"Specialist ophthalmic use; product availability varies.","route":"Topical ophthalmic long-acting cholinesterase inhibitor/miotic, now uncommon; glaucoma risk and product availability.","why":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibition raises acetylcholine and contracts the iris sphincter; this can be harmful if lens position or ocular inflammation is abnormal.","lecture":"Lecture 13","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Long-acting cholinesterase inhibitor causes miosis by increasing acetylcholine; historical ophthalmic glaucoma comparator with potentially substantial cholinergic adverse effects; not equivalent to direct pilocarpine."]},{"id":"guaifenesin","name":"Guaifenesin","class":"Expectorant","brand":"Guaifenesin","moa":"Aids secretion clearance","uses":"Selected cough/secretions","effects":"GI adverse effects","contraindications":"Oral expectorant and injectable equine muscle-relaxant formulations are different; avoid mixing route/indication and assess airway clearance.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral expectorant in selected contexts, or injectable equine muscle relaxant in a different formulation; never conflate uses.","why":"Guide lists under secretion modifiers","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Expectorant that increases hydration of airway secretions; selected cough support but does not replace disease treatment. Guaifenesin used as a muscle relaxant in equine anesthesia is a distinct clinical context."]},{"id":"ambroxol","name":"Ambroxol","class":"Mucolytic / secretion modifier","brand":"Ambroxol","moa":"Supports secretion clearance","uses":"Respiratory mucus management","effects":"GI effects","contraindications":"Availability and labeling vary geographically; consider GI intolerance and do not substitute a mucolytic for infection/inflammation workup.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral mucolytic in regions where available; clinical use varies and is not a universal U.S. veterinary drug.","why":"Guide lists under secretion modifiers","lecture":"Lecture 15","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Mucolytic/secretory modifier used in some jurisdictions for respiratory mucus management; limited US veterinary routine availability; airway disease cause still needs treatment."]},{"id":"chlorothiazide","name":"Chlorothiazide","class":"Thiazide diuretic","brand":"Chlorothiazide","moa":"Thiazide diuretic inhibits Na+/Cl− cotransporter in distal convoluted tubule, increasing sodium and water excretion.","uses":"Selected edema","effects":"Hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hypochloremia, dehydration, weakness and prerenal azotemia.","contraindications":"Monitor BP, creatinine and Na+/K+/Cl−, especially if combined with loop diuretics. Do not treat dehydration with additional diuresis.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral/injectable thiazide depending product; monitor Na/K and kidney function.","why":"Blocking distal salt uptake promotes diuresis but can intensify electrolyte deficits during sequential nephron blockade.","lecture":"Lecture 16","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Thiazide diuretic blocks distal Na-Cl transport, with electrolyte and renal risks; label and milk withdrawal in food animals are species/product dependent."],"studyGuidePages":"16:65"},{"id":"magnesium-sulfate","name":"Magnesium sulfate","class":"Osmotic laxative","brand":"Magnesium sulfate","moa":"Retains intestinal luminal water","uses":"Selected equine impaction","effects":"Hypermagnesemia/toxicity","contraindications":"Avoid with obstructive or compromised GI","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral osmotic cathartic for selected equine indications or injectable magnesium for other uses; route-specific purpose differs.","why":"Guide notes short duration","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Osmotic cathartic with electrolyte/distension risk; evaluate obstruction/dehydration and species-specific GI use."]},{"id":"mineral-oil","name":"Mineral oil","class":"Lubricant laxative","brand":"Mineral oil","moa":"Coats stool and facilitates passage","uses":"Selected equine impaction protocols","effects":"Aspiration pneumonia if misadministered","contraindications":"Aspiration risk; use clinician-guided administration","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral/enteral lubricant under veterinary supervision; aspiration can cause severe lipid pneumonia.","why":"Not for all forms of colic","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Lubricant laxative; aspiration can cause severe lipoid pneumonia, especially with oral force-administration in compromised patients."]},{"id":"psyllium-husk","name":"Psyllium husk","class":"Bulk-forming fiber","brand":"Psyllium husk","moa":"Holds water and increases bulk","uses":"Equine sand enteropathy / selected GI disease","effects":"Obstruction risk if inappropriate hydration","contraindications":"Bulk fiber needs adequate hydration; avoid inappropriate administration with suspected complete obstruction and monitor equine impaction risk.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral/enteral bulk-forming fiber with adequate hydration; not an obstruction treatment.","why":"Source specifies sand impaction","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Bulk-forming soluble fiber for selected colonic motility/stool consistency problems; requires adequate hydration; not appropriate with obstruction."]},{"id":"butylscopolammonium","name":"Butylscopolammonium (Buscopan)","class":"Antimuscarinic antispasmodic","brand":"Butylscopolammonium (Buscopan)","moa":"Reduces smooth muscle spasm","uses":"Selected spasmodic equine colic","effects":"Tachycardia, transient ileus","contraindications":"Antimuscarinic effect can cause tachycardia and transient reduced GI motility; symptomatic spasm relief does not rule out surgical colic.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Injectable antimuscarinic (Buscopan) for selected equine colic spasm; HR effects and surgical-colic assessment.","why":"Does not resolve surgical colic","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Peripheral antimuscarinic antispasmodic in equine colic contexts; monitor transient tachycardia and avoid masking surgical colic."]},{"id":"pantoprazole","name":"Pantoprazole","class":"Proton pump inhibitor","brand":"Pantoprazole","moa":"Irreversible H+/K+ ATPase inhibition","uses":"IV acid suppression","effects":"GI effects","contraindications":"IV acid suppression is not a replacement for treating GI hemorrhage/shock and may be unnecessary without acid pathology; adjust formulation/route.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral or IV PPI depending setting; don't equate all dosing/absorption with omeprazole.","why":"Source uses IV formulation comparison","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: IV/PO proton-pump inhibitor for selected hospitalized or oral-therapy-limited acid suppression, not automatically interchangeable on a mg basis with omeprazole."],"studyGuidePages":"17:73–76"},{"id":"bismuth-subsalicylate","name":"Bismuth subsalicylate","class":"Antidiarrheal / salicylate","brand":"Bismuth subsalicylate","moa":"Mucosal protection with salicylate component","uses":"Selected diarrhea","effects":"Salicylate toxicosis","contraindications":"Avoid cats; caution bleeding risk","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral antidiarrheal salicylate; avoid in cats and caution with GI bleeding/NSAIDs.","why":"Cats have limited salicylate clearance","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Salicylate-containing antidiarrheal; cats are sensitive to salicylates, and product combinations/bleeding risks matter."]},{"id":"activated-charcoal","name":"Activated charcoal","class":"GI adsorbent","brand":"Activated charcoal","moa":"Adsorbs some ingested toxicants","uses":"Selected toxicosis decontamination","effects":"Aspiration, constipation","contraindications":"Not useful for all toxicants / contraindications vary","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral/enteral adsorbent in selected ingestions after airway assessment; not all toxins bind and aspiration is dangerous.","why":"Use under toxicology consultation","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): GI adsorbent for select recent oral toxicants, not all poisons; aspiration risk and contraindications in unprotected airway/ileus. Not a substitute for toxin-specific antidotes."]},{"id":"kaolin-pectin","name":"Kaolin / pectin","class":"Adsorbent mixture","brand":"Kaolin / pectin","moa":"Binds selected intestinal materials","uses":"Historical diarrhea support","effects":"Variable efficacy","contraindications":"Limited evidence for meaningful antidiarrheal benefit; could alter oral drug absorption and must not replace fluid/electrolyte therapy.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral GI adsorbent mixture with limited evidence; do not replace hydration/etiology assessment.","why":"Guide flags questionable evidence","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Older intestinal adsorbent mixture with limited evidence for many diarrheal causes; formulation and concurrent medication absorption concerns."]},{"id":"same","name":"S-adenosylmethionine (SAMe)","class":"Hepatoprotective adjunct","brand":"S-adenosylmethionine (SAMe)","moa":"Supports glutathione metabolism","uses":"Selected hepatopathy","effects":"GI intolerance","contraindications":"Oral SAMe can cause GI effects and bioavailability depends on formulation; use as an adjunct, not proof of hepatic recovery.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral SAMe supplement with formulation-dependent bioavailability, adjunct only.","why":"Supplement quality and evidence vary","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): SAMe is a hepatoprotective adjunct, not a cure for hepatic disease; source gives comparisons with antioxidant/glutathione pathways."]},{"id":"denamarin","name":"Denamarin (SAMe + silybin)","class":"Hepatic nutraceutical","brand":"Denamarin (SAMe + silybin)","moa":"Provides SAMe and milk-thistle constituents","uses":"Selected liver support","effects":"GI intolerance","contraindications":"SAMe/silybin is a nutraceutical adjunct with GI tolerance/formulation considerations; cannot replace diagnosing and treating primary liver disease.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral SAMe/silybin nutraceutical, not a replacement for diagnosing liver disease.","why":"Not a substitute for treating cause","lecture":"Lecture 17","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Combination nutraceutical SAMe plus silybin for liver support; not a replacement for specific diagnosis and therapy."]},{"id":"oxazepam","name":"Oxazepam","class":"Benzodiazepine","brand":"Oxazepam","moa":"GABA-A positive allosteric modulation","uses":"Selected anxiolytic/appetite effects","effects":"Sedation and ataxia","contraindications":"Benzodiazepine sedation, ataxia, disinhibition and dependence may occur; monitor concurrent CNS depressants and liver concerns.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral benzodiazepine where used; species behavioral indications and sedation risks.","why":"Cat risks and product use differ","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Benzodiazepine, taught for GABA-A allosteric effects and comparative pharmacokinetics; not routine primary canine/feline anxiolytic. Sedation/disinhibition risks remain."]},{"id":"lorazepam","name":"Lorazepam","class":"Benzodiazepine","brand":"Lorazepam","moa":"GABA-A positive allosteric modulation","uses":"Selected anxiety/seizure contexts","effects":"Sedation, ataxia","contraindications":"Sedation/respiratory risk increases with other CNS depressants; route compatibility and formulation differ from diazepam/midazolam.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral/injectable benzodiazepine depending formulation; avoid blanket IV/plastic compatibility claims.","why":"Not automatically interchangeable with diazepam","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Benzodiazepine with GABA-A allosteric action, primarily a human comparison/selected specialist veterinary context. Monitor sedation, interactions and paradoxical response."]},{"id":"chlordiazepoxide","name":"Chlordiazepoxide","class":"Benzodiazepine","brand":"Chlordiazepoxide","moa":"GABA-A positive allosteric modulation","uses":"Selected behavioral indications","effects":"Sedation, dependence","contraindications":"Long-acting benzodiazepine/metabolites can accumulate with hepatic dysfunction; sedation, dependence and paradoxical excitation risk.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral benzodiazepine with long-acting metabolites; hepatic/behavioral considerations.","why":"Longer acting benzodiazepine","lecture":"Lecture 18","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Longer-acting benzodiazepine largely taught for human/comparative pharmacology; not a routine small-animal first-line behavioral drug."]},{"id":"imepitoin","name":"Imepitoin","class":"Low-affinity partial GABA-A agonist","brand":"Imepitoin","moa":"Modulates GABA-A signaling","uses":"Canine epilepsy and noise-related indications (jurisdiction-dependent)","effects":"Ataxia, sedation","contraindications":"Verify regional veterinary approval","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral low-affinity partial GABA-A agonist antiseizure medication in some markets; authorization depends on region.","why":"Not simply interchangeable with phenobarbital","lecture":"Lecture 19","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · lecture parameters extracted, individual label audit ongoing","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Low-affinity partial GABA-A agonist; labeled for noise aversion in US dogs; epilepsy use/approval differs by jurisdiction. Not supported as emergency status drug."],"studyGuidePages":"19:125"},{"id":"trimeprazine","name":"Trimeprazine","class":"Phenothiazine","brand":"Trimeprazine","moa":"Dopamine/histamine effects","uses":"Selected pruritus combinations","effects":"Sedation","contraindications":"Phenothiazine/H1 effects may cause sedation, hypotension and anticholinergic effects; combined glucocorticoid products add separate steroid risks.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Oral phenothiazine antihistamine/tranquilizer, sometimes paired with glucocorticoid; sedation does not equal analgesia.","why":"Often combined with steroid products","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Phenothiazine-derived antihistamine/tranquilizer in select historical veterinary formulations; confirm the precise combination product and adverse sedation/anticholinergic effects."]},{"id":"fluphenazine","name":"Fluphenazine","class":"Phenothiazine","brand":"Fluphenazine","moa":"D2 antagonism","uses":"Historical sedation/behavior use","effects":"Extrapyramidal effects","contraindications":"Potential extrapyramidal and long-lasting neurobehavioral adverse effects; not routine anxiolysis, and depot effects may be difficult to reverse.","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Long-acting injectable or oral phenothiazine antipsychotic in selected contexts; not routine veterinary tranquilizer.","why":"Long-acting effects; limited current use","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Long-acting phenothiazine D2 antagonist used historically in horses, with prolonged extrapyramidal/behavioral adverse effects and misuse concerns; not routine field sedation."]},{"id":"carfentanil","name":"Carfentanil","class":"Extremely potent mu opioid agonist","brand":"Carfentanil","moa":"Mu receptor agonism","uses":"Highly restricted wildlife immobilization context","effects":"Severe respiratory depression, apnea and potentially fatal human occupational exposure; profound effects in target animals.","contraindications":"Highly restricted controlled wildlife immobilization context; no routine veterinary use. Requires authorized specialist occupational safeguards and emergency readiness.","species":"Restricted specialist wildlife/immobilization context; never routine small-animal use.","route":"Extremely potent controlled immobilization opioid used only under specially regulated expert wildlife protocols; parenteral exposure poses grave human risk.","why":"Extreme mu-opioid potency leaves little margin for exposure errors and can suppress respiratory drive.","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Extremely potent full mu agonist in restricted wildlife immobilization settings; severe human exposure hazard and reversal/emergency plan required; not routine companion-animal analgesia."],"studyGuidePages":"20:140"},{"id":"etorphine","name":"Etorphine","class":"Extremely potent mu opioid agonist","brand":"Etorphine","moa":"Mu receptor agonism","uses":"Highly restricted wildlife immobilization","effects":"Profound respiratory depression, apnea and cardiovascular effects; accidental human exposure can be fatal.","contraindications":"Highly restricted wildlife immobilization agent used only by specially trained, authorized professionals with occupational exposure controls and emergency response procedures.","species":"Restricted specialist wildlife immobilization; not routine companion-animal therapy.","route":"Extremely potent controlled parenteral wildlife immobilization opioid under specialist regulation; severe occupational exposure hazard.","why":"Very high-potency mu receptor stimulation causes immobilization but also life-threatening respiratory depression.","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Ultra-potent opioid in restricted wildlife immobilization; high personnel-exposure risk and specialized antagonism/handling; not routine small-animal treatment."],"studyGuidePages":"20:140"},{"id":"liposomal-bupivacaine","name":"Liposomal bupivacaine","class":"Extended-release local anesthetic","brand":"Liposomal bupivacaine","moa":"Voltage-gated Na+ channel blockade","uses":"Selected regional local analgesia","effects":"Local anesthetic toxicity if misused","contraindications":"Formulation and route restrictions","species":"Consult species-specific notes","route":"Locally infiltrated extended-release bupivacaine for selected surgical pain under product-specific label; not IV.","why":"Longer local release than conventional formulation","lecture":"Lecture 20","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Drug Comparison pp. 11–22","reviewStatus":"Source-linked supplemental table; clinical verification recommended · targeted summary; page-level provenance check pending","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Extended-release local anesthetic for specific postoperative infiltration/nerve-block indications; distinguish local sodium-channel blockade from systemic opioids and avoid intravascular administration."]},{"id":"epinephrine","name":"Epinephrine (adrenaline)","brand":"","class":"Adrenergic agonist • alpha/beta","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Cardiopulmonary resuscitation and anaphylaxis; drug choice and route differ by emergency scenario.","moa":"Alpha-1 vasoconstriction; beta-1 cardiac stimulation; beta-2 bronchodilation.","effects":"Tachycardia, hypertension, dysrhythmias, increased myocardial oxygen demand and ischemia with excessive systemic exposure; expected effects must be weighed against life-threatening emergency.","why":"Alpha-1 action supports vascular tone, beta-1 increases contractility, and beta-2 helps relieve bronchospasm; excessive adrenergic stimulation can cause ischemia or arrhythmias.","lecture":"Lecture 14","species":"Multiple species; emergency setting","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables pp. 3–4","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"First-line emergency drug for anaphylaxis when indicated: do NOT delay for routine contraindication concerns. Route and formulation depend on anaphylaxis versus CPR. Avoid unmonitored/incorrect routes and concentrations.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Main catecholamine in veterinary CPR and anaphylaxis protocols; receptor effects depend on dose. Do not substitute other listed resuscitation medications indiscriminately."],"studyGuidePages":"14:57"},{"id":"ephedrine","name":"Ephedrine","brand":"","class":"Mixed-acting sympathomimetic","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Historical or selected peripheral bronchodilator / pressor; not a first-line chronic cough treatment.","moa":"Direct and indirect adrenergic stimulation increases sympathetic tone.","effects":"Tachycardia, hypertension, agitation, arrhythmia and reduced response with repeated doses (tachyphylaxis).","why":"Releasing endogenous norepinephrine plus direct adrenergic stimulation raises blood pressure, but available stores and repeat dosing alter effectiveness.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species-/formulation-dependent","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables p. 5","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Use cautiously in hypertension, tachyarrhythmia, hyperthyroidism or severe cardiac disease. Drug interactions and indirect catecholamine depletion affect response; not routine cough therapy.","veterinaryRelevance":"Historical / selected veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Mixed sympathomimetic; mainly useful for comparing adrenergic effects with more targeted alternatives.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Mixed direct/indirect sympathomimetic historically used as a bronchodilator or pressor; less receptor-selective than beta-2 inhalers and has cardiovascular stimulation risks."]},{"id":"codeine","name":"Codeine","brand":"","class":"Opioid antitussive • mu receptor activity","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Selected severe, nonproductive cough where suppressing the cough is appropriate; avoid indiscriminate treatment of productive cough.","moa":"Central mu-opioid pathway depresses cough reflex.","effects":"Sedation, constipation, respiratory depression and unpredictable antitussive response due to species-specific metabolism.","why":"Central opioid pathways can reduce coughing but may impair airway clearance and ventilation.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Non-food patients; verify controlled-drug status","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables pp. 5, 7","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Do not suppress protective productive cough without diagnostic assessment. Caution with respiratory compromise, other CNS depressants and liver dysfunction; controlled-substance and combination-product restrictions apply.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Opiate antitussive for selected nonproductive cough, with variable species-dependent metabolism and sedation/constipation; not routine treatment for infectious productive cough."],"studyGuidePages":"15:62"},{"id":"hydromorphone","name":"Hydromorphone","brand":"","class":"Full mu opioid receptor agonist","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Severe analgesia; may induce emesis in cats in certain clinical contexts, with variable response.","moa":"Full mu opioid receptor agonist provides potent analgesia.","effects":"Sedation, vomiting, respiratory depression, bradycardia and hyperthermia in some cats.","why":"Strong mu activation produces analgesia alongside central respiratory and thermoregulatory effects.","lecture":"Lecture 17","species":"Dogs/cats; monitor effects","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables p. 5","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Monitor respiratory rate and effort, temperature (especially cats), other sedatives and recovery.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Full mu agonist for moderate–severe perioperative pain; common dog emesis, sedation and respiratory effects; cats may show hyperthermia and dysphoria."]},{"id":"ractopamine","name":"Ractopamine","brand":"","class":"Beta-adrenergic agonist • repartitioning agent","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Beta-adrenergic feed additive for specifically labeled production-animal uses; not a treatment for illness and not interchangeable between cattle and swine products.","moa":"Beta-adrenergic agonism alters partitioning of energy toward lean tissue growth in target food animals.","effects":"May produce behavioral, muscle and cardiovascular effects; animal-welfare, handling, withdrawal and importing-market restrictions matter.","why":"Beta signaling affects muscle/fat deposition, but systemic adrenergic changes can affect behavior and handling.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Certain U.S. labeled cattle/swine feed products; market restrictions vary.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables p. 7","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"Only feed according to the current FDA product label, approved species/production class and feed directions; extra-label production use is not permitted. Verify export market requirements.","veterinaryRelevance":"Regulated production-animal use","veterinaryContext":"Feed-production agent, not a pet medication; approval and trade restrictions vary.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Beta-adrenergic feed repartitioning agent in eligible production species with market and jurisdiction-specific controls; not a companion-animal medication."],"professorVsClinicReview":"Course production-animal feed indications are highly market/label dependent; avoid treating the drug as approved in every region or acceptable for every export market.","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/growth-promotants-and-production-enhancers/use-of-beta-adrenergic-receptor-agonists-in-animals"]},{"id":"zilpaterol","name":"Zilpaterol","brand":"","class":"Beta-adrenergic agonist • repartitioning agent","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Historical U.S. feedlot cattle beta-agonist growth-promotant label; the named commercial product was voluntarily withdrawn from U.S. market. Not an ordinary clinical prescription.","moa":"Beta-adrenergic receptor activation shifts nutrient partitioning toward skeletal muscle.","effects":"Welfare/locomotion concerns and severe hazard if fed to horses or other non-target species; major market restrictions.","why":"Production beta-agonist signaling may alter muscle metabolism and locomotor tolerance; this is not treatment of animal disease.","lecture":"Lecture 13","species":"Historical cattle finishing use; not horses.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables p. 7; https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/growth-promotants-and-production-enhancers/use-of-beta-adrenergic-receptor-agonists-in-animals","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"Do not assume current availability merely from historical FDA approval. Never extrapolate cattle feed directions to horses; verify product marketing and importing-market restrictions.","veterinaryRelevance":"Historical / limited market availability","veterinaryContext":"Production-feed beta agonist with withdrawn commercial product; important equine exposure hazard.","lectureProtocol":["CLINICAL / LECTURE COMPARISON — individual prescribing and region-specific label verification required: Beta-adrenergic growth repartitioning compound with historical cattle-feed marketing; commercially withdrawn in the US and a significant equine exposure/toxicity concern."],"professorVsClinicReview":"Lecture references historical U.S. cattle feed use. Product commercial availability and markets have changed; distinguish mechanism and toxicity from current availability.","clinicalRefs":["https://www.merckvetmanual.com/pharmacology/growth-promotants-and-production-enhancers/use-of-beta-adrenergic-receptor-agonists-in-animals"]},{"id":"tolazoline","name":"Tolazoline","brand":"","class":"Alpha-adrenergic antagonist • alpha-2 sedative reversal","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Historically used for reversal of xylazine-associated sedation in selected species; current formulation/label must be confirmed.","moa":"Nonselective alpha-receptor antagonism counteracts alpha-2 agonist effects.","effects":"Tachycardia, hypotension, GI effects and rapid loss of alpha-2 analgesia/sedation; close recovery monitoring is necessary.","why":"Nonselective alpha receptor blockade opposes alpha-2 agonist activity but can also reduce vascular tone.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Species-specific and regional product guidance","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables p. 8","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Use only under species- and product-specific veterinary instructions. Reversal does not correct hypoxemia, persistent effects from other drugs or untreated pain; monitor cardiovascular status.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE-ALIGNED CLINICAL COMPARISON (additional independent verification needed): Nonselective alpha antagonist with historic/selected xylazine reversal uses; reversibility and approval are species/protocol specific."]},{"id":"vatinoxan","name":"Vatinoxan","brand":"","class":"Peripheral alpha-2 adrenergic antagonist","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Peripherally selective alpha-2 antagonist coformulated with medetomidine (Zenalpha), FDA-approved for sedation and analgesia in dogs during clinical examinations and certain procedures.","moa":"Peripherally acting alpha-2 antagonist limits peripheral vasoconstrictive/physiologic alpha-2 actions.","effects":"Transient tachycardia, blood pressure changes and other cardiorespiratory effects possible in combination sedation protocols; product-specific monitoring required.","why":"Limited CNS penetration counteracts peripheral vasoconstriction while preserving much central alpha-2 sedation.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Dog combination formulations; jurisdiction-specific","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables p. 8; https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-approves-new-combination-drug-sedation-dogs","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Peripheral alpha-2 antagonist is NOT a stand-alone full reversal of central medetomidine sedation. Use only in the labeled medetomidine-vatinoxan formulation/indication and monitor patient hemodynamics.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Peripherally restricted alpha-2 antagonist combined with medetomidine in Zenalpha to mitigate peripheral cardiovascular effects without functioning as full central reversal."],"studyGuidePages":"20:132–134"},{"id":"carbamazepine","name":"Carbamazepine","brand":"","class":"Anticonvulsant • voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Source lists seizures and seizure-associated aggression; not a routine first-line canine/feline seizure medication.","moa":"Use-dependent inhibition of voltage-gated sodium channels dampens repetitive neuronal firing.","effects":"Sedation, ataxia, GI signs, hepatic or hematologic adverse effects and clinically significant enzyme-mediated drug interactions; veterinary evidence is limited.","why":"Sodium-channel inactivation reduces neuronal firing, but rapid metabolism and interactions limit practical veterinary use.","lecture":"Lecture 19","species":"Specialist use; note source versus current evidence","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables pp. 7–8","reviewStatus":"2026-11-23 targeted safety correction; educational, verify individual species label before use.","contraindications":"Not routine first-line canine or feline anticonvulsant; specialist assessment required for hepatic disease, concurrent enzyme-inducing antiseizure drugs and blood dyscrasias.","veterinaryRelevance":"Rare / nonroutine veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"Sodium-channel antiseizure drug taught for mechanism comparison; phenobarbital and levetiracetam are more commonly considered for dog/cat epilepsy.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Voltage-gated Na-channel antiseizure drug primarily taught as human/class comparison; not routine first-line for dog/cat epilepsy."],"studyGuidePages":"19:128"},{"id":"ketamine","name":"Ketamine","brand":"","class":"Dissociative anesthetic • NMDA receptor antagonist","route":"Species, product and intended use determine route","uses":"Dissociative anesthesia and selected multimodal analgesia; source includes glutamate-targeting mechanisms.","moa":"Noncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonism disrupts excitatory glutamatergic transmission and central sensitization.","effects":"Dysphoria, increased secretions, muscle tone and variable cardiovascular responses; excessive depression possible in catecholamine-depleted patients.","why":"NMDA blockade prevents wind-up and causes dissociative anesthesia, but does not guarantee muscle relaxation.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Multiple species with different anesthetic protocols","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide · Master Tables pp. 8–9","reviewStatus":"Clinical safety and physiology clarification 2026-11-23; verify current product label.","contraindications":"Requires anesthesia monitoring and appropriate combinations; consider cardiomyopathy, increased sympathetic load and recovery agitation.","veterinaryRelevance":"Established veterinary use","veterinaryContext":"NMDA antagonist/dissociative anesthetic; also included in selected multimodal analgesia protocols.","lectureProtocol":["COURSE + CLINICAL CHECK: Dissociative NMDA-receptor antagonist for selected anesthesia, multimodal analgesia and specialist refractory seizure management; not used alone to manage ongoing status epilepticus."],"studyGuidePages":"19–20:121,140"},{"id":"phenytoin","name":"Phenytoin","class":"Hydantoin anticonvulsant • voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker","lecture":"Lecture 19","moa":"Use-dependent voltage-gated Na+ channel inactivation reduces repetitive neuronal firing.","uses":"Human epilepsy drug covered for comparison; not routine canine or feline seizure maintenance because of unsuitable pharmacokinetics/efficacy and adverse effects.","effects":"Drug interactions and adverse effects; historical equine myotonia context noted in course.","species":"Human comparison; specialist/historical equine context","veterinaryRelevance":"Human-medicine comparison / not routine dogs-cats","lectureProtocol":["COURSE pp. 122, 128: phenytoin is a human antiseizure-drug comparison; the lecturer specifically says not to choose it for maintenance epilepsy treatment in dogs or cats.","Drug class is hydantoin sodium-channel blocker, NOT 'unclassified'. Do not confuse with the partial GABA-A agonist imepitoin, which has distinct pharmacology."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE pp. 122, 128: phenytoin is a human antiseizure-drug comparison; the lecturer specifically says not to choose it for maintenance epilepsy treatment in dogs or cats. Drug class is hydantoin sodium-channel blocker, NOT 'unclassified'. Do not confuse with the partial GABA-A agonist imepitoin, which has distinct pharmacology.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"pp. 122, 128","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"mozavaptan","name":"Mozavaptan","class":"Vasopressin V2-receptor antagonist • aquaretic","lecture":"Lecture 16","moa":"Antagonizes collecting-duct V2 receptor signaling, reducing aquaporin-2 recruitment and free water reabsorption.","uses":"Human-medicine hyponatremia comparison in the lecture; not a routine veterinary diuretic.","effects":"Can cause rapid free-water loss and hypernatremia if inappropriately used.","species":"Human/comparative lecture drug","veterinaryRelevance":"Human comparison; not routine veterinary use","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 65: aquaretic distinct from natriuretic loop/thiazide diuretics—reduces water reabsorption via vasopressin V2, not sodium transporter blockade.","Not established as routine veterinary management of hyponatremia; monitor sodium carefully if discussed as specialist therapy."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE p. 65: aquaretic distinct from natriuretic loop/thiazide diuretics—reduces water reabsorption via vasopressin V2, not sodium transporter blockade. Not established as routine veterinary management of hyponatremia; monitor sodium carefully if discussed as specialist therapy.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"p. 65","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"isoproterenol","name":"Isoproterenol (isoprenaline)","class":"Nonselective beta-1/beta-2 adrenergic agonist","lecture":"Lecture 15","moa":"Activates beta-1 and beta-2 receptors, increasing heart rate/contractility while relaxing bronchial smooth muscle.","uses":"Older nonselective bronchodilator/chronotropic comparator; less selective than albuterol or terbutaline for bronchospasm.","effects":"Marked tachycardia and arrhythmia risk.","species":"Comparative/rare specialist veterinary use","veterinaryRelevance":"Historical / limited use","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 58: grouped with older nonselective beta agonists; beta-1 effects make it less attractive when selective beta-2 airway drugs are available.","Differentiation: albuterol is a more selective short-acting beta-2 bronchodilator; isoproterenol has substantial beta-1 cardiac stimulation."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE p. 58: grouped with older nonselective beta agonists; beta-1 effects make it less attractive when selective beta-2 airway drugs are available. Differentiation: albuterol is a more selective short-acting beta-2 bronchodilator; isoproterenol has substantial beta-1 cardiac stimulation.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"p. 58","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"propentofylline","name":"Propentofylline","class":"Xanthine derivative • neurovascular / phosphodiesterase modulator","lecture":"Lecture 18","moa":"Xanthine-family drug affecting purinergic/vascular signaling; mechanisms include phosphodiesterase and adenosine pathways.","uses":"Cognitive dysfunction support in some European veterinary contexts; not routinely US-approved/standard canine CCD drug.","effects":"Evidence and local approval limits; avoid substituting for a complete cognitive dysfunction evaluation.","species":"Dogs; country-dependent","veterinaryRelevance":"Non-US / limited evidence","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 119: discussed in supplemental comparison for canine cognitive decline; some European licensing but evidence for benefit is limited.","Compare with selegiline, which has a distinct MAO-B inhibitor mechanism and canine cognitive dysfunction indication."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE p. 119: discussed in supplemental comparison for canine cognitive decline; some European licensing but evidence for benefit is limited. Compare with selegiline, which has a distinct MAO-B inhibitor mechanism and canine cognitive dysfunction indication.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"p. 119","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"nicergoline","name":"Nicergoline","class":"Ergoline derivative • alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist","lecture":"Lecture 18","moa":"Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonism with effects on vascular/neurotransmitter signaling.","uses":"Cognitive/cerebrovascular veterinary comparison in some European jurisdictions; not routine US canine CCD therapy.","effects":"Hypotension and region-specific evidence/availability caveats.","species":"Dogs; regional historical use","veterinaryRelevance":"Non-US / comparison","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 119: mention relates to European canine cognitive decline drugs and does not establish a standard US recommendation.","Classify with ergoline alpha-adrenergic agents, not as an MAOI or SSRI."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE p. 119: mention relates to European canine cognitive decline drugs and does not establish a standard US recommendation. Classify with ergoline alpha-adrenergic agents, not as an MAOI or SSRI.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"p. 119","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"granisetron","name":"Granisetron","class":"Selective serotonin 5-HT3 receptor antagonist • antiemetic","lecture":"Lecture 17","moa":"Blocks 5-HT3 receptors at vagal afferents and the chemoreceptor trigger zone.","uses":"Selected chemotherapy-associated nausea/vomiting; human medicine comparator and specialized veterinary extra-label situations.","effects":"QT/arrhythmia considerations, constipation and interaction review.","species":"Human comparison; selected veterinary specialist use","veterinaryRelevance":"Human / selected extra-label","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 68: listed with ondansetron/dolasetron as 5-HT3 antiemetics; distinguish from NK1 antagonist maropitant.","Does not directly reverse vestibular motion sickness or diagnose the cause of emesis."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE p. 68: listed with ondansetron/dolasetron as 5-HT3 antiemetics; distinguish from NK1 antagonist maropitant. Does not directly reverse vestibular motion sickness or diagnose the cause of emesis.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"p. 68","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"dolasetron","name":"Dolasetron","class":"Selective serotonin 5-HT3 receptor antagonist • antiemetic","lecture":"Lecture 17","moa":"Blocks 5-HT3 receptors in vagal and emetic circuits.","uses":"Specialized/limited antiemetic comparison, including chemotherapy-associated vomiting; not a routine substitute for all emesis drugs.","effects":"QT prolongation and cardiac-conduction interactions may matter.","species":"Human/specialist veterinary","veterinaryRelevance":"Limited veterinary use / comparative","lectureProtocol":["COURSE p. 68: listed among the 5-HT3 blockers (ondansetron, granisetron, dolasetron).","Classify as 5-HT3 antagonist; compare NK1 and D2 targets and select based on etiology."],"route":"Source comparison; no routine veterinary dosing","why":"COURSE p. 68: listed among the 5-HT3 blockers (ondansetron, granisetron, dolasetron). Classify as 5-HT3 antagonist; compare NK1 and D2 targets and select based on etiology.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide lecture cited in protocol","studyGuidePages":"p. 68","reviewStatus":"Source-listed comparison drug; independent prescribing/approval validation required","contraindications":"Not a routine first-line veterinary medication; check contemporary region-specific availability and restrictions"},{"id":"tulathromycin","name":"Tulathromycin","brand":"","class":"Macrolide · 50S ribosomal inhibitor","route":"Injectable, product/species-specific; do not extrapolate route or withdrawal to dairy/veal cattle.","uses":"Cattle/swine BRD: preventive/metaphylaxis or treatment only within the applicable approved indication and clinical risk evaluation. CLINICAL TARGET: Mainly Mannheimia haemolytica, Pasteurella multocida and Histophilus somni in cattle BRD; targets differ by species and label.","moa":"Tria-milide macrolide binds bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit and impairs protein synthesis.","effects":"Injection-site reactions, GI/feeding changes and resistance selection; product-specific restrictions on dairy animals, age, species and withdrawals.","why":"Distribution into respiratory tissues supports labeled BRD/SRD targets but does not make a macrolide appropriate for every cough.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Cattle/swine labels depending specific product.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"Confirm labeled cattle or swine indication and production class. Do not assume safe equine use or blanket approval for lactating dairy; observe product-specific meat and milk rules.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Cattle/swine BRD: preventive/metaphylaxis or treatment only within the applicable approved indication and clinical risk evaluation.","Mainly Mannheimia haemolytica, Pasteurella multocida and Histophilus somni in cattle BRD; targets differ by species and label.","Not a universal respiratory drug: food-animal restrictions, dairy/age limits, macrolide interactions and resistance stewardship."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Cattle/swine BRD: preventive/metaphylaxis or treatment only within the applicable approved indication and clinical risk evaluation.","why":"Macrolide 50S binding inhibits bacterial protein synthesis; long tissue residence informs herd treatment choices but does not guarantee susceptibility.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"gamithromycin","name":"Gamithromycin","brand":"","class":"Macrolide · 50S ribosomal inhibitor","route":"Injectable cattle product; check age/class and route on current label.","uses":"Bovine respiratory disease control/treatment for at-risk cattle under product-specific indications. CLINICAL TARGET: Mannheimia haemolytica, Pasteurella multocida and Histophilus somni where label and susceptibility support.","moa":"Azalide macrolide binds bacterial 50S ribosome and inhibits protein synthesis.","effects":"Injection-site reactions and possible hypersensitivity; food-animal residue and antimicrobial resistance risks.","why":"Targeted respiratory tissue distribution aids BRD treatment but inappropriate antimicrobial exposure promotes resistance.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"U.S. Zactran labeling: beef and non-lactating dairy cattle; read species-specific label.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"Zactran label: do not use in female dairy cattle 20 months or older because milk discard time is not established; follow labeled age, class and meat withdrawal requirements. Do not generalize to goats or horses.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Bovine respiratory disease control/treatment for at-risk cattle under product-specific indications.","Mannheimia haemolytica, Pasteurella multocida and Histophilus somni where label and susceptibility support.","Food-animal use restrictions and withdrawal requirements; avoid substituting for every macrolide or assuming use in horses/cats."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Bovine respiratory disease control/treatment for at-risk cattle under product-specific indications.","why":"Azalide-related macrolide action at bacterial 50S; livestock pathogen coverage and label—not just class—determine appropriateness.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"},"clinicalRefs":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=4ad34f81-65e1-4858-8928-50af8bcb3db9"]},{"id":"tildipirosin","name":"Tildipirosin","brand":"","class":"Macrolide · 50S ribosomal inhibitor","route":"Injectable livestock formulation with label-defined route.","uses":"BRD treatment/control in labeled cattle groups, not general-purpose small-animal respiratory care. CLINICAL TARGET: Major BRD organisms in labeled cattle use, including Mannheimia, Pasteurella and Histophilus spp. depending product indications.","moa":"Macrolide binds bacterial 50S ribosome and inhibits protein synthesis, with prolonged tissue distribution.","effects":"Injection-site effects, hypersensitivity and antimicrobial resistance/residue concerns.","why":"Long lung exposure supports labeled BRD treatment, not indiscriminate respiratory antimicrobial use.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Species/production-class limitations are product-specific.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"Use only in labeled food-animal species and classes; check dairy, age, pregnancy and withdrawal limits on current product label. Do not substitute product routes or species.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["BRD treatment/control in labeled cattle groups, not general-purpose small-animal respiratory care.","Major BRD organisms in labeled cattle use, including Mannheimia, Pasteurella and Histophilus spp. depending product indications.","Age/production class restrictions, resistance and food-animal withdrawals; do not infer approved dairy use."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"BRD treatment/control in labeled cattle groups, not general-purpose small-animal respiratory care.","why":"50S ribosomal inhibition plus formulation distribution supports BRD use when the particular product covers the organism.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"tilmicosin","name":"Tilmicosin","brand":"Micotil 300 (formulation-specific label)","class":"Macrolide · 50S ribosomal inhibitor","route":"Micotil 300: SUBCUTANEOUS injection in cattle and sheep only using the specified tube-fed safety syringe; never IV or IM. Other products must be checked separately.","uses":"Specific labeled bacterial respiratory infections in cattle and sheep, including susceptible Mannheimia/Pasteurella under their species-specific product indications.","moa":"Macrolide antibiotic binds the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit and inhibits protein synthesis; its lethal accidental-injection cardiotoxicity is a SAFETY WARNING, not its antimicrobial mechanism.","effects":"Severe cardiotoxicity from accidental human injection; IV injection in cattle/sheep may be fatal. Injection-site reaction, collapse and anaphylactoid reactions reported. Fatal exposures reported in multiple non-target species.","why":"Lung tissue distribution supports respiratory indications, but treatment selection depends on the labeled species and organism. Human injection exposure is a medical emergency; stringent device and handling precautions are essential.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Micotil 300 labeled cattle and sheep only; do not generalize across tilmicosin formulations.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Drug-safety audit: primary US label reviewed 2026-11-23; check current label before prescribing.","contraindications":"Micotil 300 must NOT be used IV, in pigs, horses, goats, pets or other nonlabeled animals. Fatal accidental human injection risk; only trained personnel using label-specified safety equipment. Comply with food-animal withdrawal times.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Labeled cattle respiratory disease applications; students must recognize exceptionally serious human injection hazard.","Cattle BRD bacterial pathogens such as Mannheimia and Pasteurella when labeled.","ACCIDENTAL HUMAN INJECTION CAN BE FATAL. Do not use in equids; species/route restrictions and withdrawal rules are critical."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Labeled cattle respiratory disease applications; students must recognize exceptionally serious human injection hazard.","why":"Macrolide protein synthesis inhibition is not the unique danger: tilmicosin can cause severe cardiotoxicity in accidentally injected humans and susceptible species.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"},"clinicalRefs":["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=08f1aecd-33c2-457b-8a45-c38fafb3a1c7"]},{"id":"florfenicol","name":"Florfenicol","brand":"","class":"Amphenicol · 50S ribosomal inhibitor","route":"Injectable livestock formulations; route and withdrawal vary by country/product/species.","uses":"Veterinary treatment of susceptible BRD and labeled swine respiratory disease, sometimes combined with anti-inflammatory symptom treatment. CLINICAL TARGET: Mannheimia/Pasteurella/Histophilus in BRD; Actinobacillus pleuropneumoniae and Pasteurella multocida in relevant swine disease.","moa":"Amphenicol antibacterial binds the 50S ribosomal subunit, inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis.","effects":"Injection-site swelling, decreased feed intake and GI effects; potential hematologic effects, residues and resistance selection.","why":"Respiratory pathogens can be susceptible, but exposure and residue requirements depend on species, route and production class.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Cattle/swine labels by product; avoid generalizing to lactating dairy.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"Follow exact product indications and meat withdrawals; dairy age/milk restrictions differ by formulation. Do not assume a swine label covers cattle, or vice versa.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Veterinary treatment of susceptible BRD and labeled swine respiratory disease, sometimes combined with anti-inflammatory symptom treatment.","Mannheimia/Pasteurella/Histophilus in BRD; Actinobacillus pleuropneumoniae and Pasteurella multocida in relevant swine disease.","Do not generalize use to lactating dairy animals; consider antimicrobial susceptibility, residues and injection-site concerns."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Veterinary treatment of susceptible BRD and labeled swine respiratory disease, sometimes combined with anti-inflammatory symptom treatment.","why":"Blocks bacterial protein synthesis at 50S; paired flunixin addresses inflammation, not bacterial killing.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"ceftiofur","name":"Ceftiofur (including Naxcel)","brand":"","class":"Third-generation cephalosporin · beta-lactam","route":"Parenteral ceftiofur sodium/hydrochloride/long-acting formulations are NOT interchangeable; verify product route and interval.","uses":"Species/indication-labeled respiratory bacterial infection in cattle, swine and selected horses; compare formulations. CLINICAL TARGET: Cattle BRD Pasteurella/Mannheimia/Histophilus, swine bacterial respiratory agents and selected equine streptococcal disease where indicated.","moa":"Third-generation cephalosporin binds bacterial penicillin-binding proteins and prevents peptidoglycan cross-linking.","effects":"Hypersensitivity, GI/rumen flora disruption, injection-site reactions and antimicrobial resistance selection.","why":"Cell-wall inhibition treats susceptible bacteria, but route/formulation switches may violate regulatory restrictions.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Food-animal and equine indications vary by formulation; check exact product.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"U.S. cephalosporin rules are NOT an absolute ban on all extra-label indications: in cattle/swine/chickens/turkeys certain deviations in dose, frequency, duration, route, species/class and disease prevention are prohibited; extra-label disease indications at approved dose/frequency/duration/route for approved species/class may be allowed. Verify specific current formulation and withdrawal rules.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Species/indication-labeled respiratory bacterial infection in cattle, swine and selected horses; compare formulations.","Cattle BRD Pasteurella/Mannheimia/Histophilus, swine bacterial respiratory agents and selected equine streptococcal disease where indicated.","U.S. food-animal extra-label cephalosporin restrictions are stringent; do not invent nebulized/extra-label treatment protocols."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Species/indication-labeled respiratory bacterial infection in cattle, swine and selected horses; compare formulations.","why":"Binds bacterial PBPs to impair cell-wall cross-linking; formulation and species restrictions change what can legally be selected.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/antimicrobial-resistance/cephalosporin-order-prohibition-questions-and-answers"]},{"id":"enrofloxacin","name":"Enrofloxacin","brand":"","class":"Fluoroquinolone · DNA gyrase/topoisomerase inhibitor","route":"Oral/injectable formulations depending species; check labeled dosing/route, particularly cat formulation risks.","uses":"Respiratory Gram-negative infection only when bacterial indication and species label support; other uses require stewardship. CLINICAL TARGET: Mannheimia/Pasteurella and other susceptible bacterial pathogens depending species; not a reflex choice for any cough.","moa":"Concentration-dependent bacterial DNA replication inhibition","effects":"Extra-label use of fluoroquinolones in U.S. food-producing animals is prohibited; feline retinal toxicity with excessive exposure and juvenile cartilage concerns.","why":"Concentration-dependent bacterial DNA replication inhibition; susceptibility and local restrictions govern treatment.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Primarily livestock; inspect individual species label","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Course-listed; current species/legal label not independently revalidated in this pass","contraindications":"Extra-label use of fluoroquinolones in U.S. food-producing animals is prohibited; feline retinal toxicity with excessive exposure and juvenile cartilage concerns.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Respiratory Gram-negative infection only when bacterial indication and species label support; other uses require stewardship.","Mannheimia/Pasteurella and other susceptible bacterial pathogens depending species; not a reflex choice for any cough.","Extra-label use of fluoroquinolones in U.S. food-producing animals is prohibited; feline retinal toxicity with excessive exposure and juvenile cartilage concerns."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Respiratory Gram-negative infection only when bacterial indication and species label support; other uses require stewardship.","why":"Concentration-dependent bacterial DNA replication inhibition; susceptibility and local restrictions govern treatment.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"danofloxacin","name":"Danofloxacin","brand":"","class":"Fluoroquinolone · DNA gyrase/topoisomerase inhibitor","route":"Cattle-specific injectable product; verify route and age/production class.","uses":"Labeled bovine BRD pathogen treatment, not a generic interchangeable canine or feline prescription. CLINICAL TARGET: Bovine Mannheimia haemolytica/Pasteurella multocida/Histophilus somni as appropriate for current formulation.","moa":"Fluoroquinolone inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, preventing DNA replication.","effects":"GI/injection-site signs, potential cartilage effects in growing animals and selection for fluoroquinolone resistance.","why":"Bactericidal DNA synthesis blockade makes stewardship and strict food-animal restrictions critical.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"U.S. cattle label where indicated; not a food-animal extra-label option.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"FDA prohibits extra-label fluoroquinolone use in food-producing animals. Use only labeled species, dose, route, duration and production class; check milk/meat withdrawals.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Labeled bovine BRD pathogen treatment, not a generic interchangeable canine or feline prescription.","Bovine Mannheimia haemolytica/Pasteurella multocida/Histophilus somni as appropriate for current formulation.","U.S. prohibited extra-label fluoroquinolone use in food-producing species; follow labeled withdrawal and exclusions."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Labeled bovine BRD pathogen treatment, not a generic interchangeable canine or feline prescription.","why":"Fluoroquinolone DNA replication inhibition; product labeling and species context—not class alone—matter.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/antimicrobial-resistance/extralabel-use-and-antimicrobials"]},{"id":"pradofloxacin","name":"Pradofloxacin (Veraflox)","brand":"","class":"Fluoroquinolone · bacterial DNA gyrase/topoisomerase inhibitor","route":"Oral feline formulation in the U.S.; confirm current label and jurisdiction.","uses":"Source BRD list includes pradofloxacin, but U.S. clinical indication is feline bacterial infections under its product label, not routine cattle BRD use. CLINICAL TARGET: Susceptible bacterial organisms within the product's labeled feline indications; do not equate with approved cattle BRD therapy.","moa":"Fluoroquinolone inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV.","effects":"GI upset, rare neurologic hypersensitivity reactions and potential class-related cartilage/retinal concerns; use product-specific feline safety labeling.","why":"Shared target with cattle fluoroquinolones does not imply identical licensed species or indications.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Cats under specific U.S. label; not cattle BRD.","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Exam 2 targeted clinical/regulatory audit 2026-11-23; cross-check current product and jurisdiction before clinical use.","contraindications":"U.S. Veraflox is a feline product; do NOT teach it as FDA-approved cattle BRD treatment. All extra-label fluoroquinolone use in U.S. food-producing animals is prohibited.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Source BRD list includes pradofloxacin, but U.S. clinical indication is feline bacterial infections under its product label, not routine cattle BRD use.","Susceptible bacterial organisms within the product's labeled feline indications; do not equate with approved cattle BRD therapy.","Flag SOURCE-VS-CLINIC mismatch: do NOT teach pradofloxacin as a U.S. cattle BRD-approved product; extra-label fluoroquinolones in food animals are prohibited."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Source BRD list includes pradofloxacin, but U.S. clinical indication is feline bacterial infections under its product label, not routine cattle BRD use.","why":"Fluoroquinolone mechanism shared with enrofloxacin/danofloxacin, while licensed species and clinical targets differ.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"},"clinicalRefs":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/antimicrobial-resistance/extralabel-use-and-antimicrobials"]},{"id":"flunixin-meglumine","name":"Flunixin meglumine","brand":"","class":"NSAID · cyclooxygenase inhibitor","route":"Parenteral livestock formulations; route requirements and food-animal withdrawals depend on species/product.","uses":"Adjunct for fever, inflammation and endotoxin-associated discomfort in selected livestock respiratory disease; it does not replace antimicrobial selection. CLINICAL TARGET: Host inflammatory prostaglandin response, NOT a bacterial species or antimicrobial target.","moa":"COX inhibition lowers prostaglandin-mediated fever/pain","effects":"GI ulceration, renal hypoperfusion in dehydrated patients, NSAID stacking and food-animal regulatory restrictions.","why":"COX inhibition lowers prostaglandin-mediated fever/pain; treating inflammation alone does not clear BRD bacteria.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Primarily livestock; inspect individual species label","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Course-listed; current species/legal label not independently revalidated in this pass","contraindications":"GI ulceration, renal hypoperfusion in dehydrated patients, NSAID stacking and food-animal regulatory restrictions.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Adjunct for fever, inflammation and endotoxin-associated discomfort in selected livestock respiratory disease; it does not replace antimicrobial selection.","Host inflammatory prostaglandin response, NOT a bacterial species or antimicrobial target.","GI ulceration, renal hypoperfusion in dehydrated patients, NSAID stacking and food-animal regulatory restrictions."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Adjunct for fever, inflammation and endotoxin-associated discomfort in selected livestock respiratory disease; it does not replace antimicrobial selection.","why":"COX inhibition lowers prostaglandin-mediated fever/pain; treating inflammation alone does not clear BRD bacteria.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"ketoprofen","name":"Ketoprofen","brand":"","class":"NSAID · cyclooxygenase inhibitor","route":"Species/product-specific oral or injectable routes; restrictions differ by jurisdiction.","uses":"Course lists tulathromycin plus ketoprofen; treat it as antimicrobial plus anti-inflammatory, not a new antibiotic or universal combination. CLINICAL TARGET: Mammalian COX/prostaglandin pathways, not microbial ribosome or bacterial target.","moa":"Reduces inflammation/fever through prostaglandin pathways but has no primary antibacterial action.","effects":"Avoid NSAID combinations and use cautiously with dehydration, kidney disease, GI ulceration; assess food-animal approvals.","why":"Reduces inflammation/fever through prostaglandin pathways but has no primary antibacterial action.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Primarily livestock; inspect individual species label","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Course-listed; current species/legal label not independently revalidated in this pass","contraindications":"Avoid NSAID combinations and use cautiously with dehydration, kidney disease, GI ulceration; assess food-animal approvals.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Course lists tulathromycin plus ketoprofen; treat it as antimicrobial plus anti-inflammatory, not a new antibiotic or universal combination.","Mammalian COX/prostaglandin pathways, not microbial ribosome or bacterial target.","Avoid NSAID combinations and use cautiously with dehydration, kidney disease, GI ulceration; assess food-animal approvals."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Course lists tulathromycin plus ketoprofen; treat it as antimicrobial plus anti-inflammatory, not a new antibiotic or universal combination.","why":"Reduces inflammation/fever through prostaglandin pathways but has no primary antibacterial action.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"amoxicillin-clavulanate","name":"Amoxicillin–clavulanate","brand":"","class":"Aminopenicillin + beta-lactamase inhibitor","route":"Oral dog/cat formulations; exact preparation and frequency vary.","uses":"Selected canine/feline susceptible respiratory bacterial infections after evaluation; not every cough requires antibiotics. CLINICAL TARGET: Susceptible Pasteurella, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus and other organisms when susceptibility supports; does not treat viral/primary inflammatory asthma.","moa":"Amoxicillin inhibits PBPs/cell-wall synthesis","effects":"Avoid penicillin hypersensitivity; GI upset and unnecessary broad-spectrum use; bacterial culture may be needed for recurrent/complicated disease.","why":"Amoxicillin inhibits PBPs/cell-wall synthesis; clavulanate inhibits some beta-lactamases but does not overcome every resistance mechanism.","lecture":"Lecture 15","species":"Dogs/cats","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.63, Respiratory antibiotics ★; clinical restrictions require current label verification","reviewStatus":"Course-listed; current species/legal label not independently revalidated in this pass","contraindications":"Avoid penicillin hypersensitivity; GI upset and unnecessary broad-spectrum use; bacterial culture may be needed for recurrent/complicated disease.","studyGuidePages":"63","lectureProtocol":["Selected canine/feline susceptible respiratory bacterial infections after evaluation; not every cough requires antibiotics.","Susceptible Pasteurella, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus and other organisms when susceptibility supports; does not treat viral/primary inflammatory asthma.","Avoid penicillin hypersensitivity; GI upset and unnecessary broad-spectrum use; bacterial culture may be needed for recurrent/complicated disease."],"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Respiratory patient with clinical evidence suggesting bacterial infection; decide whether this class and species label fit.","decision":"Selected canine/feline susceptible respiratory bacterial infections after evaluation; not every cough requires antibiotics.","why":"Amoxicillin inhibits PBPs/cell-wall synthesis; clavulanate inhibits some beta-lactamases but does not overcome every resistance mechanism.","whatChanges":"Check infectious-agent testing, species authorization, production class, and antimicrobial stewardship before prescribing.","sourceType":"Course-specific discussion, not an executable drug order"}},{"id":"sertraline","name":"Sertraline","brand":"","class":"SSRI · serotonin transporter inhibitor","route":"Oral; verify species-specific off-label use and formulation.","uses":"Source-mentioned SSRI comparator; may be considered for selected off-label behavior disorders with an appropriate behavior plan.","moa":"Inhibits serotonin transporter (SERT), increasing synaptic serotonin over time.","effects":"GI adverse effects, agitation, serotonin interactions.","why":"Delayed neuroadaptation makes this unsuitable as a one-dose rescue tranquilizer; do not confuse behavioral treatment with immediate sedation.","lecture":"Lecture 18","species":"Dogs/cats (off-label comparison)","source":"Main Exam 2 Study Guide p.90, SSRI comparator","reviewStatus":"Source-mentioned, clinical use needs source check","contraindications":"Avoid with selegiline/other MAO inhibitors and review serotonergic combinations and washout.","studyGuidePages":"90","lectureProtocol":["Source-mentioned SSRI comparator; may be considered for selected off-label behavior disorders with an appropriate behavior plan.","Delayed neuroadaptation makes this unsuitable as a one-dose rescue tranquilizer; do not confuse behavioral treatment with immediate sedation.","Avoid with selegiline/other MAO inhibitors and review serotonergic combinations and washout."]},{"id":"medetomidine","name":"Medetomidine","brand":"","class":"Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist","route":"Injectable formulation; verify product species indication.","uses":"Sedation and anesthesia adjunct in appropriate patients; source contrasts racemate with active dexmedetomidine.","moa":"Presynaptic alpha-2 activation reduces norepinephrine release; also produces peripheral vascular effects.","effects":"Bradycardia, decreased cardiac output, emesis and respiratory compromise.","why":"Central sympatholysis produces sedation and analgesia, but peripheral vasoconstriction and reduced cardiac output can coexist.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Dogs in lecture comparison","source":"Main Exam 2 Study Guide p.131","reviewStatus":"Source-mentioned, requires product review","contraindications":"Avoid indiscriminate use in patients with significant circulatory disease or shock; label and monitoring apply.","studyGuidePages":"131","lectureProtocol":["Sedation and anesthesia adjunct in appropriate patients; source contrasts racemate with active dexmedetomidine.","Central sympatholysis produces sedation and analgesia, but peripheral vasoconstriction and reduced cardiac output can coexist.","Avoid indiscriminate use in patients with significant circulatory disease or shock; label and monitoring apply."]},{"id":"medetomidine-vatinoxan","name":"Medetomidine + vatinoxan","brand":"","class":"Alpha-2 agonist + peripheral alpha-2 antagonist combination","route":"Labeled injectable combination where marketed; verify species and administration instructions.","uses":"Source-highlighted combined sedation with peripheral alpha-2 adverse-effect mitigation; species and regional product status vary.","moa":"Medetomidine provides central alpha-2 sedation; vatinoxan reduces peripheral alpha-2-mediated vascular effects.","effects":"Sedation, emesis, cardiorespiratory effects and interaction risks may persist.","why":"Separating central sedation from peripheral vasoconstriction can improve hemodynamic profile but does not make anesthesia risk-free.","lecture":"Lecture 20","species":"Dogs per lecture combination context","source":"Main Exam 2 Study Guide p.133 ★","reviewStatus":"Source-highlighted; verify regulatory/label details","contraindications":"Peripheral antagonist mitigates some medetomidine vascular effects but does not guarantee normal cardiac output, ventilation or recovery; continue monitoring.","studyGuidePages":"133","lectureProtocol":["Source-highlighted combined sedation with peripheral alpha-2 adverse-effect mitigation; species and regional product status vary.","Separating central sedation from peripheral vasoconstriction can improve hemodynamic profile but does not make anesthesia risk-free.","Do not assume normal hemodynamics or use as substitute for monitoring; verify approved species and product label."],"coursePriority":{"level":"directly-starred-drug","guidePages":"133","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide • lecture tables (not opening master tables)","rationale":"Explicit ★ appears with this medication or its exact highlighted combination in the lecture table","verifyBold":"PDF text extraction does not establish bold/yellow typography"},"teachingDepth":{"clinicalDecision":"Use only when a particular dog patient and approved combination product fit the planned procedural sedation and staffing.","physiologyWhy":"Medetomidine supplies centrally mediated alpha-2 sedation; peripherally restricted vatinoxan antagonizes some vascular alpha-2 effects to lessen hemodynamic compromise.","compareAndContrast":"Different from giving medetomidine alone or fully reversing central sedation with atipamezole; peripheral antagonism is NOT complete anesthetic reversal.","monitorAndAvoid":"Monitor BP, pulse, oxygenation, ventilation, emesis and duration; verify formulation/label, concurrent medications and reversal plan.","provenance":"Main Exam 2 lecture table ★, course-specific clinical synthesis; prescribing requires current source verification"},"clinicalMicrocase":{"presentation":"Patient scenario: Use only when a particular dog patient and approved combination product fit the planned procedural sedation and staffing.","decision":"Use only when a particular dog patient and approved combination product fit the planned procedural sedation and staffing.","why":"Medetomidine supplies centrally mediated alpha-2 sedation; peripherally restricted vatinoxan antagonizes some vascular alpha-2 effects to lessen hemodynamic compromise.","whatChanges":"Monitor BP, pulse, oxygenation, ventilation, emesis and duration; verify formulation/label, concurrent medications and reversal plan.","sourceType":"Starred-lecture-priority teaching case"}},{"id":"calcium-lactate","name":"Calcium lactate","class":"Oral calcium salt · mineral replacement","lecture":"Lecture 12","uses":"Source-listed oral calcium replacement for selected chronic hypocalcemia/hypoparathyroidism, generally alongside appropriate vitamin D therapy and monitoring.","moa":"Supplies dietary calcium available for GI absorption; calcitriol increases intestinal calcium absorption when needed.","why":"Gradual oral calcium replacement differs from monitored IV calcium borogluconate for acute parturient hypocalcemia.","effects":"Hypercalcemia, constipation and calcium-phosphorus imbalance with excessive therapy.","route":"Oral calcium supplement; elemental calcium varies by salt/product.","contraindications":"Use caution with hypercalcemia, renal disease and concurrent calcitriol; monitor ionized calcium and phosphorus.","species":"Dogs/cats per course, product-specific","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Guide p.2 treatment table; chronic hypoparathyroidism","studyGuidePages":"2","brand":"","reviewStatus":"Course-mentioned, NOT professor-starred; clinically investigate before recommending"},{"id":"octreotide","name":"Octreotide (acromegaly comparator)","class":"Somatostatin analog","lecture":"Lecture 11","uses":"Source-mentioned investigational/selected specialist treatment of excess growth hormone in feline hypersomatotropism; not a standard first-line substitute for diabetic control.","moa":"Somatostatin-receptor agonism can reduce GH secretion; response depends on tumor receptor expression.","why":"Targets pituitary hormone secretion rather than replacing insulin or simply increasing insulin sensitivity.","effects":"GI effects and glycemic changes; clinical effect in cats is variable and formulation dependent.","route":"Specialist-selected formulations/administration; not a routine labeled feline diabetes treatment.","contraindications":"Do not promise remission or standardized efficacy in feline acromegaly; specialist endocrine monitoring.","species":"Cat (lecture comparison), specialist use","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.38, acromegaly paragraph; not identified as ★ drug","studyGuidePages":"38","brand":"","reviewStatus":"Course-mentioned, NOT professor-starred; clinically investigate before recommending"},{"id":"pegvisomant","name":"Pegvisomant (acromegaly comparator)","class":"Growth hormone receptor antagonist","lecture":"Lecture 11","uses":"Lecture-mentioned human comparator explored for GH excess; no routine established feline acromegaly treatment claim.","moa":"Competitive antagonism at growth hormone receptors reduces downstream IGF-1 signaling without directly replacing insulin.","why":"Blocks GH actions at peripheral targets, which is different from suppressing GH release using a somatostatin analog.","effects":"Potential glucose and hepatic effects; veterinary efficacy and safety remain uncertain.","route":"Human specialist injectable product; not a standard licensed feline regimen.","contraindications":"Treat as source comparison only; do not instruct owner use or claim routine feline authorization.","species":"Cat course comparison; human drug","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p.38, acromegaly paragraph; not identified as ★ drug","studyGuidePages":"38","brand":"","reviewStatus":"Course-mentioned, NOT professor-starred; clinically investigate before recommending"}],"questions":[{"id":"pharm-exam2-001","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 10","q":"A beta-cell experiences increased glucose uptake and ATP production after a meal. Which sequence most directly leads to insulin granule exocytosis?","why":"Glucose-driven ATP closes ATP-sensitive K channels, allowing membrane depolarization, Ca influx and insulin exocytosis. Sulfonylureas exploit this pathway, so viable responsive beta cells are required.","options":["ATP closes KATP channels, depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca channels, and calcium triggers granule release","ATP opens KATP channels, hyperpolarization opens calcium channels, and insulin exits by osmosis","ATP inhibits glucokinase, causing alpha-cell glucagon to package insulin","ATP directly activates insulin receptors on neighboring beta cells without membrane excitation"],"answer":0,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why does insulin enter the bloodstream after beta-cell glucose metabolism?","options":["Glucose metabolism raises ATP and closes KATP channels","Glucose opens potassium channels to hyperpolarize beta cells","Insulin is released from alpha cells directly by cortisol","Insulin is secreted only when sodium channels open"],"answer":0,"why":"ATP closes KATP channels → depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels → insulin granule exocytosis."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-002","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 10","q":"A newly diagnosed diabetic dog has persistent hyperglycemia, glucosuria, high urine output and compensatory thirst. Which renal mechanism connects these findings?","why":"Once filtered glucose exceeds proximal tubular reabsorptive capacity, glucose remains in tubular fluid and exerts an osmotic effect, causing polyuria and compensatory polydipsia.","options":["Hyperglycemia suppresses ADH at the hypothalamus and produces free-water diuresis only","Filtered glucose exceeds tubular reclamation; retained luminal solute reduces water reabsorption","Insulin binds apical aquaporins in collecting ducts and prevents urine concentration","Glucosuria raises glomerular oncotic pressure and halts urine output"],"answer":1,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"What best explains PU/PD in an untreated diabetic dog?","options":["ADH production stops completely","Glucosuria draws water osmotically into renal tubules","Insulin directly opens renal water channels","Glucose blocks the glomerulus"],"answer":1,"why":"Filtered glucose exceeds renal reabsorptive capacity, causing osmotic diuresis."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-003","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 10","q":"A dog is prescribed 6 units of U-40 insulin. An owner measures to the '6' mark on a U-100 syringe. What is the likely administered dose?","why":"A U-100 syringe's 6-unit mark is 0.06 mL; multiplied by 40 U/mL gives 2.4 U, an underdose. Device-concentration errors can cause under- or overdosing depending on the pairing.","options":["6 units, because insulin syringe graduations are interchangeable","15 units, because U-40 is 2.5 times more concentrated","2.4 units, because the U-100 mark holds 0.06 mL and U-40 contains 40 units/mL","0.6 units, because U-100 is 100 times more concentrated"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-004","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 10","q":"Two insulin products bind the same insulin receptor but one has a prolonged duration after subcutaneous administration. Which formulation change best explains this difference?","why":"Zinc and protamine alter solubility, aggregation and release from SQ depots. They affect pharmacokinetics, not insulin-receptor identity.","options":["Protamines permanently strengthen insulin-receptor binding affinity","Zinc and protamine change depot dissolution/absorption rather than creating a new receptor target","Zinc blocks renal filtration of all injected insulin throughout the entire dosing interval","Protamine induces new pancreatic beta cells, allowing endogenous insulin release"],"answer":1,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why can zinc/protamine prolong insulin action?","options":["They inhibit insulin receptors","They prevent insulin degradation by kidneys completely","They delay uptake from the injection site","They induce permanent beta-cell proliferation"],"answer":2,"why":"They slow absorption from the subcutaneous depot, spreading exposure over time."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-005","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 11","q":"A DKA dog has K 3.2 mmol/L after initial fluid resuscitation and a plan for short-acting insulin. Why might serum K fall rapidly after insulin begins?","why":"Insulin activation of cellular Na/K ATPase shifts potassium intracellularly; osmotic diuresis and vomiting may have already depleted total-body potassium. DKA protocols require repeated K monitoring and correction.","options":["Insulin inhibits Na/K ATPase and shifts potassium out of cells","Insulin increases urinary potassium excretion without influencing transcellular shifts","Insulin stimulates cellular potassium entry while total body potassium may already be low","Insulin removes potassium through the lungs as respiratory CO2 falls"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"What is the key risk when insulin is started in DKA with major potassium depletion?","options":["Potassium necessarily increases","Insulin suppresses renal glucose completely","Potassium immediately enters red blood cells only","Extracellular potassium can fall dangerously"],"answer":3,"why":"Insulin shifts K+ into cells and can worsen hypokalemia."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17","referenceIntervals":"Canine serum potassium: 4.1–5.4 mEq/L (mmol/L equivalent). Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-006","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 11","q":"A cat taking glargine has an elevated fructosamine concentration but an owner reports trembling episodes overnight. Which limitation should change the next diagnostic step?","why":"Fructosamine estimates integrated glycemic exposure over prior weeks, not individual nadirs. Episodic hypoglycemia can coexist with poor average control.","options":["Fructosamine reliably excludes all short nocturnal hypoglycemic episodes","Fructosamine only represents the preceding 60 minutes, so it cannot indicate any longer glycemic trends","An elevated fructosamine can coexist with brief dangerous lows; a glucose curve or CGM can evaluate them","Fructosamine is a direct beta-hydroxybutyrate assay, so it distinguishes DKA from hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"What does fructosamine fail to identify reliably?","options":["The average glycemic exposure across previous weeks","Brief low-glucose events","Long-term trend in glucose exposure","Poor regulation over weeks"],"answer":1,"why":"Short episodes of hypoglycemia; it reflects preceding average glycemia."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-007","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 11","q":"A newly diagnosed diabetic cat taking bexagliflozin becomes anorexic and tachypneic. Glucose is 175 mg/dL; beta-hydroxybutyrate is elevated and bicarbonate is low. Which interpretation best guides triage?","why":"SGLT2 inhibition causes glucosuria without resolving insulin deficiency. Significant ketonemia, acidemia and illness require emergency evaluation; glucose alone can miss euglycemic DKA.","options":["A low glucose excludes ketoacidosis and favors simple dietary intolerance","Euglycemic DKA remains possible despite the modest glucose","SGLT2 inhibitors can raise urinary ketones but never cause an acid–base disorder","Stress hyperglycemia is the primary explanation whenever glucose exceeds the laboratory RI"],"answer":1,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","difficulty":"clinical-application","referenceIntervals":"Feline serum glucose: 71–182 mg/dL. Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas SGLT2-associated euglycemic DKA is possible despite glucose within this interval."},{"id":"pharm-exam2-008","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 11","q":"Which cat is an appropriate SGLT2 inhibitor candidate under US labeling?","why":"A newly diagnosed, well-screened, otherwise healthy diabetic cat never previously treated with insulin; contraindications and monitoring still matter.","options":["A cat in active DKA","A cat previously treated with insulin","An otherwise healthy, screened newly diagnosed cat without previous insulin","A cat with anorexia and dehydration"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-009","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 12","q":"A cat newly started on methimazole for hyperthyroidism has a decreasing T4 but remains tachycardic at an early recheck. Which step does methimazole inhibit, and why may the heart rate remain elevated despite treatment?","why":"Methimazole inhibits thyroid peroxidase (TPO), blocking iodide organification and coupling reactions required to synthesize new T3/T4; it does not block release of previously synthesized hormone or destroy thyroid tissue. At an early recheck, circulating/stored hormone and persisting cardiovascular effects may explain tachycardia. If tachycardia remains after adequate thyroid control, assess other causes such as cardiac disease, hypertension, pain or stress rather than simply increasing antithyroid treatment. The question tests the synthetic enzyme target, not whether heart rate should normalize immediately.","options":["Thyroperoxidase-mediated iodination and coupling needed for new thyroid hormone synthesis","Release of stored T4 via complete destruction of thyroid follicular tissue","Binding of T4 to the nuclear thyroid hormone receptor throughout the body","Conversion of peripheral T3 back into T4 as the major synthetic pathway"],"answer":0,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"How does methimazole reduce thyroid hormone production?","options":["Blocks thyroid peroxidase","Directly destroys thyroid tissue via radiation","Replaces T4","Blocks thyroid hormone transport into blood only"],"answer":0,"why":"It inhibits thyroid peroxidase iodination/coupling of thyroglobulin precursors, reducing NEW hormone synthesis."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-010","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 12","q":"An older greyhound has lethargy, low total T4 and an active systemic inflammatory illness. What is the strongest next interpretation before prescribing lifelong replacement?","why":"Total T4 is sensitive but nonspecific. Breed, illness and drug effects can lower results; correlate with clinical signs and additional thyroid testing when appropriate.","options":["A single low total T4 in a symptomatic dog is enough to diagnose primary hypothyroidism","Measure total T3 alone, because T3 is universally more reliable during illness","Nonthyroidal illness, breed effects and medications may suppress total T4; evaluate clinically and with appropriate additional testing","Normal TSH in every dog definitively excludes hypothyroidism"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-011","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 12","q":"A hyperthyroid cat has controlled clinical signs on methimazole but develops severe facial pruritus. The owner wants I-131. Which assessment best determines whether radioiodine can proceed?","why":"Methimazole can help assess tolerance before permanent treatment, but serious adverse effects and patient stability alter preparation. The course's 10–14-day withdrawal and post-treatment precautions are exam notes; actual nuclear-medicine guidance governs.","options":["Methimazole must remain in place during isolation even if a serious adverse effect develops","Medical stability, renal and cardiovascular evaluation, plus the radiation center's medication-withdrawal and isolation plan","All methimazole-treated cats are permanently ineligible for I-131","A fixed three-day hospitalization is required by every center without regard to radiation measurements"],"answer":1,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Is methimazole pretreatment universally required before feline I-131 therapy?","options":["Yes, in every patient regardless of health","No; it depends on patient assessment and facility protocols","Never; methimazole is contraindicated before I-131","Only if the cat is hypothyroid"],"answer":1,"why":"No. It can help evaluate clinical/renal tolerance in selected cats; I-131 protocols and case selection vary."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-012","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 12","q":"What do calcium and calcitriol accomplish in chronic hypoparathyroidism?","why":"Calcium supplies substrate; active vitamin D enhances intestinal absorption, supporting extracellular calcium concentration.","options":["Raise PTH secretion in all patients","Abolish calcium excretion immediately","Support serum calcium by replacement and absorption","Treat excess thyroid hormone directly"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-013","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 13","q":"A spayed dog has urethral sphincter mechanism incompetence, but another animal has urinary retention due to urethral resistance. Which autonomic action could increase outlet closure in the incontinent dog?","why":"Sympathetic alpha-1 activation increases urethral outlet tone, relevant to phenylpropanolamine. A dog with obstructive urine retention requires an entirely different plan.","options":["Alpha-1 receptor blockade, which decreases urethral smooth-muscle tone","Muscarinic M3 stimulation of the detrusor, which directly increases urethral sphincter pressure","Alpha-1 adrenergic stimulation, which increases urethral smooth-muscle closure tone","Beta-2 stimulation, which produces consistent urethral sphincter contraction"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which receptor effect supports urethral sphincter tone and continence?","options":["Muscarinic agonism","Alpha-1 adrenergic activation","Beta-2 blockade","Alpha-1 antagonism"],"answer":1,"why":"Alpha-1 stimulation contracts urethral smooth muscle; phenylpropanolamine increases closure pressure."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-014","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 13","q":"A dog has a large residual bladder volume, detrusor hypocontractility and suspected unresolved mechanical outflow obstruction. Why defer bethanechol?","why":"Bethanechol stimulates muscarinic detrusor contraction. Confirm outlet patency before treating functional detrusor atony; obstruction must be addressed first.","options":["Muscarinic stimulation chiefly relaxes the external urethral sphincter","Bethanechol blocks alpha-1 receptors and may conceal urethral obstruction","Increasing detrusor pressure against an obstructed outlet can worsen injury without restoring flow","Bethanechol is a beta-2 agonist and only changes ureteric motility"],"answer":2,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-015","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 13","q":"An ophthalmologist needs a temporary dilated pupil for fundic assessment while avoiding the prolonged cycloplegia associated with atropine. Which option best matches that purpose?","why":"Tropicamide is a relatively short-acting ophthalmic antimuscarinic. Drug choice also depends on glaucoma and intraocular pressure risk.","options":["Phenylephrine because alpha-1 mydriasis always provides identical cycloplegia to tropicamide","Tropicamide because its relatively short-acting muscarinic antagonism relaxes the iris sphincter","Pilocarpine because muscarinic activation contracts the radial dilator","Bethanechol because systemic M3 stimulation causes reliable brief mydriasis"],"answer":1,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which drug provides short-acting mydriasis for routine ocular examination?","options":["Atropine as a short-acting alternative","Bethanechol","Tropicamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":2,"why":"Tropicamide blocks muscarinic signaling in the iris sphincter, dilating the pupil."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-016","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"CVM731B · Lecture 13","q":"An equine uveitis patient gets atropine drops. What additional risk should be considered?","why":"Systemic antimuscarinic absorption can reduce gastrointestinal motility and raise colic risk, especially with repeated ophthalmic dosing.","options":["Excess GI motility","Improved tear production in every patient","Guaranteed reversal of glaucoma","Reduced GI motility and colic risk"],"answer":3,"source":"CVM731B slides & recorded comments; core clinical guidance checked","exam":"Exam 2 preliminary; scope pending"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-001","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"An owner uses a U-100 syringe's 10-unit marking for a U-40 insulin vial. Which statement correctly identifies the potential problem?","options":["It delivers 10 U because all insulin syringes use the same volume markings","It delivers 4 U of U-40 insulin; syringe markings are calibrated for a different concentration","It delivers 25 U of U-40 insulin because the vial is less concentrated","It delivers 10 U but changes insulin pharmacokinetics"],"answer":1,"why":"A U-100 syringe is calibrated for 100 units/mL: its 10-unit mark draws 0.1 mL. At 40 units/mL (U-40 insulin), that same volume contains only 4 units. Why it matters: syringe markings are volume-based, not universal across insulin concentrations. Match the device to the exact prescribed product.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-002","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog requires an insulin with relatively prolonged basal action through reversible binding to albumin. Which formulation fits that mechanism?","options":["Insulin detemir","Insulin glargine, which primarily forms subcutaneous microprecipitates","Porcine lente insulin, a zinc suspension","PZI insulin, which contains protamine and zinc"],"answer":0,"why":"Detemir has a fatty acid side chain promoting albumin binding; glargine and zinc/protamine formulations prolong absorption differently.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-003","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A DKA cat initially has K 4.1 mEq/L, is dehydrated, and is about to begin insulin. What is the key physiologic reason to continue frequent K assessment?","options":["Insulin promotes potassium entry into cells while total-body potassium may already be depleted","Serum potassium invariably rises as glucose falls on insulin","A normal admission potassium proves normal intracellular potassium stores","Potassium changes only after ketones normalize"],"answer":0,"why":"DKA involves osmotic potassium loss and altered transcellular shifts; insulin can lower serum potassium precipitously even when the baseline looks normal.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application","referenceIntervals":"Feline serum potassium: 3.8–5.5 mEq/L (mmol/L equivalent). Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-004","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A newly diagnosed cat with diabetes has poor appetite and elevated blood beta-hydroxybutyrate. What most limits use of feline SGLT2 therapy in this case?","options":["The agent prevents all urinary glucose loss","SGLT2 therapy is only contraindicated when glucose exceeds 500 mg/dL","Insufficient insulin/ketosis can produce ketoacidosis despite improved glucose readings","SGLT2 inhibitors work by direct replacement of pancreatic beta cells"],"answer":2,"why":"SGLT2 inhibitors promote glucosuria; they do not replace insulin's anti-ketogenic effects. Poor intake/ketones are major danger signs.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-005","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A hyperthyroid cat starts methimazole. Which molecular event is directly reduced, distinguishing this therapy from radioiodine?","options":["Release of stored colloid T4 via direct radioablation","Peripheral binding of T3 to its nuclear receptor","Thyroid peroxidase-dependent iodination and coupling reactions","Conversion of T4 to T3 in every peripheral tissue"],"answer":2,"why":"Methimazole inhibits thyroid peroxidase-dependent hormone synthesis but does not directly remove thyroid tissue or immediately exhaust stored hormone.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-006","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog has confirmed primary hypothyroidism. Which intervention replaces thyroid hormone rather than inhibiting thyroid synthesis or conversion?","options":["Methimazole","Levothyroxine (T4)","Carbimazole","A restricted-iodine diet as the same pharmacologic action"],"answer":1,"why":"Levothyroxine is synthetic T4 replacement. Methimazole/carbimazole inhibit hormone synthesis.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-007","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A male cat has urethral spasm after relief of obstruction. Which pharmacologic effect explains a potential role of tamsulosin, while acknowledging that mechanical obstruction still requires correction?","options":["Alpha-1 antagonism relaxes urethral smooth muscle","Beta-2 agonism directly relaxes the detrusor","Muscarinic agonism increases sphincter contraction","Alpha-1 agonism increases urethral outlet resistance"],"answer":0,"why":"Tamsulosin antagonizes alpha-1 receptors; an obstruction must be addressed, and evidence for post-obstructive adjunct use varies.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-008","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A horse with uveitis receives atropine ophthalmic drops. Which receptor action produces mydriasis and cycloplegia and also explains systemic anticholinergic risks?","options":["Muscarinic receptor antagonism","Muscarinic receptor agonism","Acetylcholinesterase reactivation","Nicotinic neuromuscular receptor blockade"],"answer":0,"why":"Atropine antagonizes muscarinic receptors. Potential systemic absorption may reduce GI motility, warranting monitoring in horses.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-009","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog receiving digoxin and furosemide has K 2.9 mmol/L and new ventricular ectopy. Which drug interaction is most relevant?","options":["The low potassium displaces digoxin from albumin but does not change its receptor binding","Hypokalemia increases susceptibility to digoxin-associated arrhythmias at the Na+/K+-ATPase target","Furosemide blocks digoxin absorption in every dog, explaining treatment failure","Digoxin promotes renal potassium retention that reliably corrects the measured deficit"],"answer":1,"why":"K competes with digoxin for interaction with Na+/K+-ATPase; depletion predisposes to toxicity. Evaluate renal function, dose, ECG and drug concentration when indicated.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application","referenceIntervals":"Canine serum potassium: 4.1–5.4 mEq/L (mmol/L equivalent). Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-010","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with preclinical/clinical heart disease is prescribed pimobendan. Which pair describes its pharmacologic effects more accurately than those of digoxin or amlodipine?","options":["PDE5 inhibition and pulmonary vasodilation","Calcium sensitization and PDE3 inhibition","Na+/K+-ATPase blockade and increased intracellular calcium","L-type calcium-channel blockade with reduced systemic blood pressure"],"answer":1,"why":"Pimobendan combines positive inotropy from calcium sensitization with PDE3 inhibition and vasodilatory effects.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-011","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"An adult dog has sustained ventricular tachycardia with compromised perfusion. Which statement correctly distinguishes the usual acute role of lidocaine from oral maintenance agents?","options":["Oral mexiletine is preferred for every unstable emergency because onset is instantaneous","IV lidocaine is a class Ib sodium-channel blocker used for selected acute ventricular arrhythmias","Amlodipine is a class Ib drug that directly treats ventricular tachycardia","Sotalol is an IV local anesthetic with no proarrhythmia risk"],"answer":1,"why":"Lidocaine has a recognized IV acute ventricular antiarrhythmic role, but management depends on rhythm, stability and monitoring.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-012","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A cat with acute bronchospasm improves after terbutaline. What best distinguishes the mechanism from inhaled ipratropium?","options":["Terbutaline blocks muscarinic M3 receptors","Terbutaline directly antagonizes leukotriene receptors","Terbutaline stimulates beta-2 receptors to relax bronchial smooth muscle","Terbutaline is a methylxanthine that inhibits phosphodiesterase"],"answer":2,"why":"Terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist; ipratropium is an antimuscarinic and methylxanthines have additional PDE/adenosine actions.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-013","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with bacterial pneumonia has a moist, productive cough. Which rationale most strongly argues against indiscriminate opioid antitussive use?","options":["The cough aids mucus clearance and suppressing it can impair secretion removal","All opioid antitussives worsen airway infection by directly raising bacterial MIC","Productive coughs indicate reduced airway secretions and need suppression","Cough suppression is equivalent to treating the underlying infection"],"answer":0,"why":"Suppressing a productive cough can reduce protective airway clearance; treat underlying disease and assess whether suppression is justified.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-014","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with pulmonary edema improves on furosemide but develops hypokalemia. Which nephron mechanism best explains both effects?","options":["Inhibition of ENaC in the collecting duct increases potassium excretion","Inhibition of NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb increases sodium delivery distally and promotes potassium loss","Blockade of NCC in the distal convoluted tubule directly defines a loop diuretic","Aldosterone antagonism in the collecting duct directly increases urinary potassium secretion"],"answer":1,"why":"Loop diuretics reduce NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb; increased distal delivery/flow can increase K secretion.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-015","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with CHF receives spironolactone and an ACE inhibitor and later develops weakness with potassium above RI. Which mechanism makes this combination clinically relevant?","options":["Both agents inhibit NKCC2 and cause potassium wasting","Aldosterone antagonism and reduced RAAS activity can increase potassium retention","Spironolactone is a thiazide and increases distal sodium delivery","The combination selectively blocks calcium channels in ventricular myocardium"],"answer":1,"why":"Spironolactone blocks aldosterone receptors and ACE inhibitors suppress angiotensin II/aldosterone signaling, increasing risk of hyperkalemia.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-016","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with vomiting receives maropitant after obstruction is evaluated. Which receptor is targeted at emetic pathways, distinct from ondansetron and metoclopramide?","options":["Neurokinin-1 (substance P) receptor","5-HT3 receptor","D2 dopamine receptor","5-HT4 receptor"],"answer":0,"why":"Maropitant antagonizes NK1 receptors; ondansetron targets 5-HT3, and metoclopramide is predominantly a D2 antagonist with prokinetic effects.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-017","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A vomiting dog has suspected gastric outflow obstruction on imaging. Which pharmacologic reasoning most strongly argues against starting a prokinetic before further workup?","options":["Metoclopramide is always a contraindicated antiemetic when nausea is severe","Increasing GI contraction against mechanical obstruction can exacerbate pressure and injury","Cisapride's serotonin action directly dissolves obstructing tissue","All antiemetics increase gastric motility to the same extent"],"answer":1,"why":"A prokinetic raises motility; structural obstruction changes the risk–benefit assessment. Distinguish prokinetic activity from purely antiemetic effects.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-018","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with hepatic encephalopathy receives lactulose. Which colonic effect helps reduce ammonia absorption?","options":["Increasing pH promotes uncharged ammonia diffusion","Acidification shifts NH3 toward NH4+, which is less readily absorbed","Direct inhibition of hepatic urea synthesis removes circulating ammonia","Competitive blockade of intestinal glutamine transport is the primary mechanism"],"answer":1,"why":"Colonic acidification favors NH4+ trapping and limits ammonia absorption; catharsis and microbial changes can also contribute.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-019","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"An older dog on selegiline develops anxiety. Which pharmacologic consideration is most important before adding an SSRI?","options":["An SSRI can be added because MAO-B selectivity guarantees no interaction at veterinary doses","MAO inhibition combined with serotonergic reuptake blockade can cause serotonin toxicity; check washout/interaction guidance","Selegiline competitively antagonizes serotonin receptors and neutralizes SSRIs","The relevant adverse effect is exclusively increased thyroid hormone production"],"answer":1,"why":"Selegiline's MAO inhibition creates potentially serious serotonergic interactions. Treat source-specific claims about all serotonin drugs as requiring individual verification.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-020","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog receiving fluoxetine is prescribed trazodone for travel anxiety. Which clinical approach best accounts for the pharmacologic risk without implying the combination is always contraindicated?","options":["Use without monitoring because trazodone has no serotonin effects","Assess indication, doses and serotonergic toxicity signs; combinations may be used with veterinary oversight","Stop fluoxetine and begin any MAOI the same day","Increase both doses at once because shared serotonergic mechanisms prevent toxicity"],"answer":1,"why":"Combined serotonergic effects require individual risk assessment; toxicity presents with autonomic, neuromuscular and mental-status changes.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-021","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A cat with chronic disease-associated inappetence needs an approved transdermal appetite-stimulation option. Which drug is the formulation-specific choice?","options":["Mirtazapine transdermal","Cyproheptadine as the same approved transdermal formulation","Selegiline as an appetite gel","Fluoxetine as a direct appetite-stimulating receptor agonist"],"answer":0,"why":"Mirtazapine transdermal is a feline appetite stimulant product; other drug classes may have unrelated behavioral and appetite effects.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-022","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog with epilepsy continues seizures despite an appropriate phenobarbital regimen. Which proposed add-on is distinguished by binding SV2A rather than directly enhancing GABA-A activity?","options":["Levetiracetam","Imepitoin","Diazepam","Phenobarbital"],"answer":0,"why":"Levetiracetam binds SV2A and modulates synaptic neurotransmitter release; its mechanism differs from benzodiazepines, barbiturates and imepitoin.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-023","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A stable dog receiving potassium bromide changes to a high-chloride prescription diet. Which change is most likely without adjusting therapy?","options":["Chloride competitively reduces renal bromide clearance, increasing levels","Bromide is converted chemically to chloride, causing acute toxicity","Higher chloride intake can increase renal bromide clearance and decrease bromide concentrations","Dietary chloride changes have no effect once steady state is achieved"],"answer":2,"why":"Bromide and chloride compete in renal handling. Changing dietary chloride can lower/raise bromide concentrations, risking breakthrough seizures or toxicity.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-024","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A dog has been seizure-free for months on chronic phenobarbital. An owner asks whether it can be stopped tonight. Which response best reflects anticonvulsant pharmacology?","options":["Sudden cessation is appropriate if the last measured trough was therapeutic","Abrupt withdrawal can precipitate seizures; any change should be planned and tapered with clinical oversight","Only levetiracetam has clinically important withdrawal risk","A therapeutic drug level proves underlying epilepsy has resolved"],"answer":1,"why":"Phenobarbital discontinuation should be guided by a veterinarian; abrupt cessation risks rebound/breakthrough seizures.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-025","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A horse becomes sedated and bradycardic after detomidine. Which presynaptic change most directly contributes?","options":["Reduced norepinephrine release after alpha-2 autoreceptor activation","Increased norepinephrine vesicle exocytosis through alpha-1 stimulation","Blocked dopamine D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone","Reduced acetylcholine hydrolysis at autonomic ganglia"],"answer":0,"why":"Alpha-2 agonists decrease presynaptic norepinephrine release and sympathetic outflow, contributing to sedation and bradycardia.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-026","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"A sedated dog received dexmedetomidine and a mu opioid. Respiratory effort is poor. Which statement best differentiates available reversal agents?","options":["Naloxone reverses the opioid component; atipamezole targets the alpha-2 agonist component","Atipamezole reverses opioid respiratory depression; naloxone reverses alpha-2 sedation","Flumazenil reverses both opioid and alpha-2 agonist effects if administered together","Naltrexone exclusively reverses benzodiazepine-related ventilatory depression"],"answer":0,"why":"Reversal agents target particular receptor classes. Airway, monitoring and return of pain/sedation matter; the opioid may outlast naloxone.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-20261114-027","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Updated guide","q":"An equine patient with visceral pain receives butorphanol. Which receptor profile differentiates it from full mu agonists and buprenorphine?","options":["Full mu agonism without kappa activity","Mixed kappa agonism and mu antagonism/partial agonism","Pure mu antagonism with no analgesic activity","Selective delta agonism without any mu interaction"],"answer":1,"why":"Butorphanol is a mixed opioid, commonly taught as kappa agonist/mu antagonist; buprenorphine is a partial mu agonist.","source":"Updated Exam 2 study guide + teaching-level clinical interpretation; simulated questions, not verbatim Dr. Carlson prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-001","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A dog has polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia and weight loss. Which mechanism best explains PU/PD?","options":["Glucose in the renal filtrate creates osmotic diuresis","Insulin increases urine concentration","A glomerular immune reaction is obligatory","Only cortisol causes the water loss"],"answer":0,"why":"Hyperglycemia exceeds renal glucose reclamation, so urinary glucose retains water. Match the signs to solute-driven diuresis.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-002","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A beta-cell receives a large glucose signal. What sequence promotes insulin release?","options":["KATP opens then calcium leaves","ATP closes KATP, depolarization opens Ca2+ channels","Glucagon directly opens GLUT4","Alpha-2 receptor activation stimulates granule release"],"answer":1,"why":"Glucose metabolism increases ATP, shutting ATP-sensitive potassium channels; depolarization opens Ca2+ channels and drives insulin exocytosis.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-003","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A vial is U-40 and an owner uses a U-100-marked syringe. What is the immediate concern?","options":["No difference if volume matches a mark","Only a slower onset","Wrong delivered insulin units","Improved bioavailability"],"answer":2,"why":"Syringe graduations are concentration specific. A mismatched insulin syringe can cause dangerous dosing errors.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-004","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A newly diagnosed diabetic cat has obesity and some residual pancreatic beta-cell function. After appropriate insulin treatment and nutritional management, its insulin requirement declines. Which mechanism makes remission possible?","options":["Reduced glucotoxicity and insulin resistance may allow remaining beta cells to sustain adequate endogenous insulin","Increased insulin demand always destroys all beta cells permanently before diagnosis","Remission reflects renal glucose loss independent of beta-cell function in every cat","Long-acting insulin converts hepatic glycogen into permanent pancreatic insulin secretion"],"answer":0,"why":"Feline diabetic remission is possible if beta-cell function recovers as glucotoxicity and insulin resistance are addressed. It is not guaranteed and therapy must avoid hypoglycemia.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which reason best explains why feline diabetes sometimes remits?","options":["All feline diabetes is transient","Cats never lose beta cells","All cats can safely stop insulin within a week","Residual beta-cell function may recover with improved glycemic control"],"answer":3,"why":"Insulin resistance and glucotoxicity are potentially reversible in some cats, but remission depends on remaining function and comorbidities."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-005","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A diabetic cat receives insulin glargine. An owner proposes diluting it with saline and mixing it with another insulin to simplify injections. What is the pharmacokinetic problem?","options":["Glargine works only when bound to oral protamine, so injection is inappropriate","Glargine is a pH-dependent SQ precipitating formulation; mixing or diluting can alter depot formation and release","Glargine is specifically a crystallized zinc suspension that must be shaken vigorously","Diluting a U-100 solution with saline has no effect on dose or absorption kinetics"],"answer":1,"why":"Glargine's acidic formulation precipitates in neutral SQ tissue. Altering pH or mixing insulins can change depot release and dosing consistency.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why must a glargine formulation not be mixed or diluted casually?","options":["Altering pH can disturb its subcutaneous precipitation depot","It is an oral medication","Every insulin vial requires vigorous shaking","Glargine must always be IV"],"answer":0,"why":"Acidic formulation and tissue-pH precipitation drive its longer absorption profile."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-006","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A poorly controlled cat on SGLT2 therapy has vomiting and lethargy but modest glucose. What is important?","options":["Ignore ketones if glucose is low","Evaluate for euglycemic DKA","Assume insulinoma","Use glucose alone to assess safety"],"answer":1,"why":"SGLT2 drugs produce glucosuria, so glucose may appear deceptively lower while ketoacidosis develops.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-007","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"A stressed cat has glucose 260 mg/dL during a clinic visit, but a home glucose curve is lower. Which additional observation best helps distinguish transient stress hyperglycemia from persistent diabetes?","options":["Immediately diagnose diabetes and start SGLT2 therapy from a single visit glucose","Interpret repeated/home glucose data with compatible signs and fructosamine; do not rely on one stressed value","Use a single urinary glucose measurement as proof of insulin deficiency regardless of stress","Assume any fructosamine value excludes short hypoglycemic events"],"answer":1,"why":"Stress-related catecholamine release can transiently raise glucose in cats. Fructosamine reflects a longer-term average and must be interpreted with patient context.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application","referenceIntervals":"Feline serum glucose: 71–182 mg/dL. Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas SGLT2-associated euglycemic DKA is possible despite glucose within this interval."},{"id":"pharm-deep-diabetes-008","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Deep pathway clinical checks","q":"Which physiologic disturbance must be considered before starting insulin in DKA?","options":["Low MCV alone","Only systemic hypertension","Isolated hypercholesterolemia","Volume/electrolyte depletion including potassium"],"answer":3,"why":"Insulin can shift potassium into cells, potentially worsening hypokalemia. DKA care requires inpatient perfusion and electrolyte monitoring.","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 27–40, learning checks synthesized from lecture pathways (not verbatim instructor prompts)","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-001","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"A feline thyroid follicular cell is exposed to methimazole. Which reaction is directly inhibited?","options":["Thyroglobulin iodination/coupling via thyroid peroxidase","NIS transport across the basolateral membrane","Nuclear receptor binding by T3","Renal sodium reclamation"],"answer":0,"why":"Methimazole targets TPO-dependent organification and coupling, not the NIS iodide transporter.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-002","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"A sick greyhound has a low total T4 on screening. What should be considered first?","options":["Immediate lifelong levothyroxine for every low result","Breed effects, nonthyroidal illness and concurrent medications","Unavoidable feline hyperthyroidism","Routine parathyroidectomy"],"answer":1,"why":"Low total T4 is not equivalent to proven hypothyroidism, especially with illness, medication effects and breed-related ranges.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-003","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"A dog has weight gain, lethargy, dermatologic changes and appropriately confirmed primary hypothyroidism. Which drug replaces deficient thyroid hormone without directly suppressing thyroid peroxidase?","options":["Methimazole, a TPO inhibitor","Restricted iodine diet to lower hormone synthesis","Levothyroxine, a T4 preparation converted into active T3 in peripheral tissues","Phenoxybenzamine, an irreversible alpha blocker"],"answer":2,"why":"Levothyroxine provides thyroid hormone replacement for documented hypothyroidism. Methimazole and iodine restriction would further reduce thyroid hormone supply.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which treatment supplies thyroid hormone to a dog with confirmed hypothyroidism?","options":["Methimazole","Iodine restriction","Levothyroxine","Phenoxybenzamine"],"answer":2,"why":"Levothyroxine provides T4, which can be converted to active T3 as needed."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-004","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"A cat becomes hypocalcemic after thyroidectomy. What complication is most consistent?","options":["Persistent adrenergic stimulation","TPO overactivation","GI vagus injury as sole cause","Parathyroid injury and reduced PTH action"],"answer":3,"why":"Adjacent parathyroid tissue may be damaged during thyroid surgery, limiting PTH-mediated calcium regulation.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-005","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"A dog has detrusor atony and a patent urinary outlet. Which pharmacologic target improves bladder contraction?","options":["Muscarinic activation by bethanechol","α1 receptor stimulation at the sphincter","Beta-2 stimulation in airways","Nicotinic neuromuscular blockade"],"answer":0,"why":"Bethanechol stimulates muscarinic receptors to enhance detrusor contraction; first rule out outlet obstruction.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-006","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"Which comparison of tropicamide and phenylephrine is accurate?","options":["Both directly inhibit acetylcholinesterase","Tropicamide blocks muscarinic receptors; phenylephrine stimulates α1","Both are nicotinic agonists","Phenylephrine directly destroys thyroid tissue"],"answer":1,"why":"Both produce mydriasis, but via different receptors. Tropicamide blocks muscarinic-mediated constriction of the iris sphincter; phenylephrine stimulates alpha-1 receptors of the radial iris dilator. The mechanism helps predict associated physiologic effects and patient-specific contraindications.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-007","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"Why is succinylcholine not sufficient for humane painful procedures?","options":["It blocks thyroid hormone release","It prevents seizures permanently","It paralyzes motor endplates without providing anesthesia or analgesia","It is a long-acting anxiolytic"],"answer":2,"why":"Neuromuscular paralysis does not prevent awareness or pain. General anesthesia and analgesia are distinct.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-thyroid-autonomic-008","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Detailed mechanisms and clinical reasoning","q":"An OP-exposed patient has salivation and muscle weakness. What distinguishes atropine from pralidoxime?","options":["Both only block α1 receptors","Atropine directly restores AChE before aging","Pralidoxime is a beta blocker","Atropine blocks muscarinic effects; pralidoxime can reactivate inhibited AChE"],"answer":3,"why":"Atropine reduces muscarinic manifestations but cannot reverse nicotinic weakness; oxime treatment depends on exposure/aging and must accompany supportive care.","source":"User's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 40–50; original practice adapted from the lecture pathways, not verbatim instructor questions","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-001","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"Why is a dog with hypokalemia more susceptible to digoxin toxicity?","options":["Low K+ increases toxic Na+/K+ ATPase effects","Low K+ blocks all digoxin absorption","Hypokalemia prevents arrhythmias","Potassium makes digoxin inactive"],"answer":0,"why":"Potassium competes with digoxin at the Na+/K+ ATPase; low K+ raises susceptibility to adverse cardiac effects.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-002","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"A dog with an appropriate indication for an inodilator is prescribed pimobendan. Which combined pharmacologic action explains its contractility and vascular effects?","options":["Na/K ATPase inhibition plus vagal stimulation, as with digoxin","PDE5 inhibition plus selective pulmonary vasodilation, as with sildenafil","PDE3 inhibition with calcium sensitization, improving contraction and promoting vasodilation","L-type calcium-channel blockade predominantly suppressing sinoatrial rate"],"answer":2,"why":"Pimobendan is an inodilator: increased myocardial sensitivity to calcium and PDE3-related effects support inotropy with vasodilation; it is not simply another loop diuretic.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which inotrope combines PDE3 inhibition and calcium sensitization?","options":["Digoxin","Pimobendan","Lidocaine","Telmisartan"],"answer":1,"why":"Pimobendan is an inodilator; it also relaxes vascular smooth muscle."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-003","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"A monitored dog develops a hemodynamically important wide-complex ventricular tachyarrhythmia. Which drug from the Class Ib group is a typical acute IV consideration rather than oral chronic therapy?","options":["Mexiletine","Lidocaine","Amlodipine","Diltiazem"],"answer":1,"why":"Lidocaine is commonly administered IV for selected serious ventricular arrhythmias, while the related Class Ib agent mexiletine is used orally for longer-term management. Assessment of rhythm and stability is essential.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which agent is highlighted for acute IV ventricular arrhythmias in the lecture?","options":["Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Lidocaine","Pilocarpine"],"answer":2,"why":"Lidocaine is a class Ib Na+ channel blocker useful in selected ventricular rhythms."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-004","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"A hypertensive cat with proteinuria is prescribed a drug blocking the action of angiotensin II rather than inhibiting its synthesis. Which medication fits?","options":["Benazepril, an ACE inhibitor that reduces angiotensin II production","Telmisartan, an angiotensin AT1 receptor antagonist","Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker","Sildenafil, a PDE5 inhibitor affecting cGMP"],"answer":1,"why":"Telmisartan antagonizes AT1 receptors, whereas enalapril/benazepril inhibit ACE upstream. Amlodipine primarily acts at arterial L-type calcium channels.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which medication blocks angiotensin II at AT1?","options":["Amlodipine","Benazepril","Sildenafil","Telmisartan"],"answer":3,"why":"Telmisartan is an ARB, unlike ACE inhibitors acting upstream."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-005","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"An older cat has repeated systolic blood pressures above 180 mmHg with retinal changes and no evidence of shock. Which drug is most appropriate for targeted systemic blood-pressure reduction?","options":["Pimobendan","Benazepril alone as an automatic substitute for antihypertensive therapy","Amlodipine","Doxapram"],"answer":2,"why":"Amlodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker commonly used for feline systemic hypertension; assess target-organ damage and monitor pressure response.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application","referenceIntervals":"Feline systolic BP: <150 mmHg low target-organ-damage risk; ≥180 mmHg severe hypertension/high risk, not a lab reference interval. ACVIM consensus (2018). https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jvim.15331"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-006","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"A dog with pulmonary hypertension receives sildenafil while another is started on enalapril for RAAS modulation. What distinguishes sildenafil's mechanism?","options":["Inhibition of PDE5-mediated cGMP degradation in vascular smooth muscle","Inhibition of ACE-mediated conversion of angiotensin I to II","Competitive blockade of aldosterone at the mineralocorticoid receptor","Direct activation of the AT1 receptor to lower vascular resistance"],"answer":0,"why":"PDE5 inhibition retains NO-related cGMP signaling and supports pulmonary vascular relaxation; ACE inhibitors reduce RAAS-driven angiotensin II formation.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why is sildenafil distinguished from ACE inhibitors?","options":["It stimulates nicotinic receptors","It inhibits PDE5 and prolongs pulmonary vascular cGMP","It inhibits TPO","It blocks NKCC2"],"answer":1,"why":"PDE5 blockade helps pulmonary vascular relaxation, not the same pathway as RAAS blockade."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-007","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"A cat in respiratory distress improves shortly after inhaled albuterol but returns with chronic cough after repeated rescue treatments. Which receptor-to-disease distinction is correct?","options":["Beta-2 agonism inhibits all airway inflammatory leukocyte recruitment and is an adequate controller alone","M3 agonism increases cAMP and relaxes bronchial smooth muscle","Beta-2 stimulation raises smooth-muscle cAMP to bronchodilate, but inflammation needs separate management","Alpha-1 antagonism directly replaces inhaled glucocorticoids in chronic asthma"],"answer":2,"why":"Albuterol rapidly stimulates bronchial beta-2 receptors to increase cAMP and reduce bronchoconstriction. Chronic feline asthma requires disease control beyond a rescue bronchodilator.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which receptor produces albuterol-associated bronchodilation?","options":["M3 stimulation","Alpha-1 blockade","Beta-2 stimulation","Nicotinic stimulation"],"answer":2,"why":"Beta-2 activation promotes cAMP-mediated bronchial relaxation."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-008","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"Which inhaled drug reduces M3 bronchoconstriction?","options":["Theophylline","Clenbuterol","Doxapram","Ipratropium"],"answer":3,"why":"Ipratropium blocks muscarinic signaling in airways and is relatively poorly absorbed systemically.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-009","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"Why may clenbuterol cause tremor and tachycardia in horses?","options":["Systemic beta-receptor activity","Exclusive M3 inhibition","Thyroperoxidase inhibition","Opioid-receptor agonism"],"answer":0,"why":"The beta-agonist effect is not confined to bronchial muscle; monitor systemic adverse signs.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-010","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"A dog with pneumonia has productive cough and retained secretions. A caretaker asks for hydrocodone to completely stop coughing. Which physiologic issue is most important?","options":["An opioid antitussive reliably improves mucociliary clearance in productive pneumonia","All cough must be suppressed to prevent pulmonary inflammation","Hydrocodone bronchodilates by beta-2 receptor activation and therefore improves clearance","A productive cough can aid clearance; suppressing it may be inappropriate while treating underlying disease and monitoring respiratory effort"],"answer":3,"why":"Cough can clear airway secretions; opioid antitussives may be inappropriate when expectoration remains useful. Assess cough type, cause and respiratory status.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why assess the type of cough before suppressing it?","options":["All cough requires antibiotics","A productive cough can clear secretions","Every cough is an epileptic sign","Antitussives improve mucociliary clearance"],"answer":1,"why":"Cough is protective; treating an excessive dry cough differs from impairing airway clearance."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-011","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"How do methylxanthines differ from beta-2 agonists?","options":["They activate thyroid receptors","They exclusively block M3","PDE and adenosine-related mechanisms contribute","They act only as antibiotics"],"answer":2,"why":"Methylxanthines influence PDE and adenosine pathways and may cause notable CNS/GI/CV toxicity.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-cardioresp-012","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Cardiorespiratory mechanism and patient decisions","q":"What limits doxapram in a patient with ineffective ventilation?","options":["It reverses every respiratory disorder","It replaces an oxygen source","It eliminates mucus plugs","Respiratory stimulation does not ensure adequate ventilation"],"answer":3,"why":"Doxapram can increase the neural drive to breathe, but drive alone cannot reverse a blocked airway, severe mechanical impairment, or inadequate gas exchange. When ventilation is ineffective, securing the airway and supporting oxygenation/ventilation takes priority over relying on a respiratory stimulant.","source":"Original learning check based on uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 51–63; not an instructor-authored question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-001","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A dog on a loop diuretic develops greater urine flow and reduced urinary concentrating ability. Which renal transport target explains both effects?","options":["NCC in the distal convoluted tubule; this creates the medullary gradient","NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb; inhibition reduces NaCl uptake and medullary hypertonicity","ENaC in the collecting duct; inhibition always increases medullary tonicity","Aquaporin-2 receptor channels directly activated by furosemide"],"answer":1,"why":"Furosemide inhibits NKCC2 in the water-impermeable thick ascending limb. Reduced salt reclamation weakens the medullary concentrating gradient as it increases NaCl excretion.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which renal target is inhibited by furosemide?","options":["NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","Na+/Cl− symporter in the distal convoluted tubule","ENaC in the collecting duct","Carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule"],"answer":0,"why":"Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb; decreasing salt reabsorption also weakens the medullary concentrating gradient."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-002","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A dog with CHF improves respiratory rate after escalating furosemide, but develops poor appetite and rising creatinine. What should the veterinary team evaluate before labeling this intrinsic renal injury?","options":["Degree of remaining congestion, circulating volume, kidney perfusion and electrolytes","Assume furosemide directly destroyed glomeruli because creatinine rose","Increase diuresis until creatinine normalizes regardless of blood pressure","Add another nephron blocker without assessing hydration because sequential blockade prevents azotemia"],"answer":0,"why":"Loop diuresis can reduce effective circulating volume and renal perfusion, causing prerenal azotemia. Evaluate both residual fluid overload and dehydration before adjustment.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why might furosemide increase serum creatinine in a patient with aggressive diuresis?","options":["It always destroys glomeruli directly","Volume contraction can reduce renal perfusion","It blocks EPO receptor signaling","It permanently closes the urethra"],"answer":1,"why":"Excess volume removal can contribute to prerenal azotemia; assess patient congestion and perfusion together."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-003","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"Which paired drug action has the greatest risk of potassium retention?","options":["Loop plus thiazide","Acetazolamide alone","ACE inhibition combined with spironolactone","Mannitol followed by water"],"answer":2,"why":"Aldosterone signaling and RAAS suppression can reduce potassium excretion, so potassium monitoring is important.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-004","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"How does molidustat differ from darbepoetin for selected feline CKD anemia?","options":["Molidustat replaces thyroid hormone","Both directly activate the same receptor in identical ways","Darbepoetin inhibits NKCC2","Molidustat stabilizes HIF pathways; darbepoetin stimulates EPO receptors"],"answer":3,"why":"HIF-PH inhibition can promote endogenous EPO pathways, whereas darbepoetin is an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-005","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A patient receiving loop diuretics develops hypokalemia. Why?","options":["Increased downstream distal Na delivery favors K secretion","Loop drugs directly stimulate EPO receptors","The thick ascending limb secretes aldosterone","Potassium cannot be filtered by kidneys"],"answer":0,"why":"Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, increasing delivery of sodium and fluid downstream. Distal sodium reabsorption can promote potassium secretion, while volume contraction activates RAAS/aldosterone and can further increase renal potassium loss. Monitor electrolytes and volume status rather than assuming that more urine always means better perfusion.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-006","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A hospitalized dog with persistent vomiting is prescribed an NK1 receptor antagonist. Which pharmacologic distinction identifies the selected antiemetic?","options":["Ondansetron blocks NK1, while maropitant is a 5-HT3 blocker","Metoclopramide is a selective NK1 antagonist without prokinetic effects","Maropitant blocks substance P signaling at NK1 rather than serotonin 5-HT3 receptors","Ropinirole suppresses emesis through D2 antagonism"],"answer":2,"why":"Maropitant is an NK1 antagonist; ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist, while metoclopramide has dopaminergic antiemetic and prokinetic effects.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which medication blocks NK1-mediated emesis?","options":["Cisapride","Maropitant","Ropinirole","Lactulose"],"answer":1,"why":"Maropitant prevents substance P signaling at NK1 receptors, a different target from D2 and 5-HT3."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-007","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A dog has suspected mechanical GI obstruction. Which class deserves particular caution?","options":["Proton-pump inhibitors","Thyroid replacement","GI prokinetics","Cobalamin"],"answer":2,"why":"Enhancing intestinal contractions against a physical blockage may worsen injury; first evaluate for obstruction.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-008","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A pregnant dog develops NSAID-associated GI ulceration. Which concern makes misoprostol a particularly problematic alternative?","options":["It has the same H2 receptor antagonist mechanism as famotidine","Its PGE1 activity blocks all gastric bicarbonate secretion","As a prostaglandin analog it may stimulate uterine smooth muscle and jeopardize pregnancy","Its protective effect relies exclusively on irreversibly inhibiting gastric H+/K+-ATPase"],"answer":2,"why":"Misoprostol is a PGE1 analog and can induce uterine contractions; pregnancy risk distinguishes it from other ulcer therapies.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-009","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"Why does lactulose help some patients with hepatic encephalopathy?","options":["Colonic acidification favors NH4+ trapping and reduced ammonia absorption","It activates nicotinic muscle receptors","It destroys circulating bile acids directly","It prevents all portal blood flow"],"answer":0,"why":"Colonic bacteria metabolize lactulose to acids, lowering luminal pH. More ammonia (NH3) becomes ammonium (NH4+), which is less readily absorbed. The osmotic laxative effect also helps clear nitrogen-containing intestinal contents; it does not directly restore lost liver function.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-010","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A dog with pancreatitis is receiving fluids, antiemetics and analgesia. A clinician discusses a drug that reduces neutrophil adhesion and migration. Which is the correct agent and limitation?","options":["Fuzapladib inhibits LFA-1-mediated leukocyte adhesion but does not replace supportive management","Maropitant blocks LFA-1 while fuzapladib treats all causes of nausea","Fuzapladib stimulates neutrophil integrins and therefore replaces IV fluids","Furosemide inhibits LFA-1 in pancreatic tissue and is the first-line targeted anti-inflammatory"],"answer":0,"why":"Fuzapladib targets LFA-1-associated neutrophil activation/recruitment; labeled indications and conditions must be checked, and standard pancreatitis supportive care remains essential.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which drug directly targets LFA-1 in the lecture's pancreatitis section?","options":["Phenobarbital","Fuzapladib","Furosemide","Levothyroxine"],"answer":1,"why":"Fuzapladib is an LFA-1 inhibitor, not an NGF or anti-inflammatory monoclonal antibody."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-011","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"Which pair has different but complementary copper-related mechanisms?","options":["Ondansetron and hydrocodone","Maropitant and ropinirole","D-penicillamine and zinc","Furosemide and atropine"],"answer":2,"why":"D-penicillamine binds copper so it can be eliminated. Oral zinc instead reduces intestinal copper absorption, in part by inducing enterocyte metallothionein. Both address copper burden by different routes; the chosen protocol and monitoring depend on the patient and diagnosis.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-renal-gi-012","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Renal/GI mechanisms and patient decision","q":"A dog with chronic small-intestinal enteropathy continues losing weight despite improved diarrhea. Cobalamin is low and the owner asks whether an additional antibiotic is the answer. What is most relevant?","options":["Intestinal/pancreatic disease can impair cobalamin absorption, so assess deficiency and supplement if indicated while managing the cause","Low cobalamin necessarily proves bacterial enteritis that requires empiric metronidazole","Cobalamin acts as an antiemetic D2 antagonist, so it replaces nausea control","Cobalamin supplementation directly opens ileal transporters to cure all chronic enteropathies"],"answer":0,"why":"Ileal disease and altered pancreatic contribution to absorption may cause cobalamin deficiency; correcting it supports metabolic health but does not replace treating the underlying enteropathy.","source":"Original clinical checks synthesized from user's Study Guide 2 pp. 64–81; not verbatim instructor prompts","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Why might a patient with chronic enteropathy benefit from cobalamin assessment?","options":["Cobalamin always increases gastric acid","Every diarrhea case is bacterial","It is an alternative to treating a foreign body","Malabsorption and intestinal/pancreatic disease may reduce B12 availability"],"answer":3,"why":"Some chronic enteropathy, pancreatic and intestinal diseases impair cobalamin status; supplementation addresses deficiency."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-001","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A 13-year-old dog with cognitive dysfunction has taken selegiline for 3 weeks. The owner asks about starting fluoxetine for anxiety. Which mechanism is the main reason to reconcile the medication list before prescribing?","options":["Selegiline reduces monoamine metabolism through MAO-B inhibition; combined serotonergic effects can be dangerous","Selegiline increases renal clearance of fluoxetine so the SSRI may be ineffective","Selegiline competitively blocks 5-HT1A receptors, canceling fluoxetine's action","Selegiline irreversibly blocks opioid receptors, causing withdrawal"],"answer":0,"why":"Selegiline is an MAO-B inhibitor; serotonergic combinations and washout requirements are clinically important. Analyze the actual drug and timing rather than assuming MAO-B selectivity eliminates interaction.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical application","reviewedFor":"Plausible pharmacology distractors and clinical decision context"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-002","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog with separation-related distress is being considered for clomipramine rather than fluoxetine. Which counseling point most accurately distinguishes their pharmacology and risks?","options":["Clomipramine is a selective serotonin transporter inhibitor without relevant cardiovascular effects","Both agents increase serotonin only by MAO-B inhibition and can be interchanged overnight","Clomipramine inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake and has TCA-related anticholinergic/cardiovascular considerations; fluoxetine is an SSRI","Fluoxetine works immediately as a GABA-A agonist whereas clomipramine is an opioid"],"answer":2,"why":"Clomipramine is a TCA with SERT/NET effects and additional receptor actions; fluoxetine is an SSRI. Drug choice depends on patient comorbidities, interactions and behavior therapy—not class labels alone.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical application","reviewedFor":"Plausible pharmacology distractors and clinical decision context"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-003","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog on selegiline is prescribed another serotonergic drug. What should occur first?","options":["Ignore the medication list","Assume combinations are always safe","Review interaction risk, agent-specific precautions and appropriate washout","Give every drug at higher dose"],"answer":2,"why":"MAOI interactions can be serious. The source's caution is important, but drug-specific guidance must be verified.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-004","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"Why isn't sedation alone proof that a frightened dog feels less fearful?","options":["It reverses serotonin reuptake","It improves vision","It proves a compulsive disorder","Observable activity may fall without changing pain or emotional distress"],"answer":3,"why":"Behavioral assessment must consider fear, pain, environment and learning; tranquilization is not identical to anxiolysis.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-005","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog remains seizure-prone despite treatment, and the clinician chooses levetiracetam as an adjunct. Which mechanism distinguishes this agent from phenobarbital and sodium-channel blockers?","options":["It directly opens GABA-A chloride channels in the manner of barbiturates","It binds synaptic vesicle protein SV2A to modulate neurotransmitter release","It competes with chloride to delay bromide excretion","It blocks postsynaptic alpha-2 receptors to increase norepinephrine release"],"answer":1,"why":"Levetiracetam targets SV2A, a synaptic vesicle protein, rather than acting primarily through GABA-A potentiation or sodium-channel blockade. It is not interchangeable with drugs from those mechanistic classes.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical application","reviewedFor":"Plausible pharmacology distractors and clinical decision context"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-006","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog receiving KBr changes to a high-chloride diet. What may happen?","options":["Bromide concentration necessarily increases","Renal bromide clearance may increase and serum levels may fall","Chloride eliminates all seizures","Potassium becomes irrelevant"],"answer":1,"why":"Bromide and chloride compete in renal handling, so dietary chloride changes can change bromide exposure.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-007","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog receiving phenobarbital for chronic epilepsy is stable clinically but begins a new medication metabolized hepatically. Which concern supports therapeutic monitoring?","options":["Phenobarbital causes no metabolic interactions once steady state is reached","Phenobarbital can induce hepatic enzymes and alter concurrent medication exposure; hepatic adverse effects also warrant assessment","Phenobarbital is cleared only by the lungs and cannot affect drug levels","Phenobarbital permanently removes the need for serum drug concentration monitoring"],"answer":1,"why":"Phenobarbital is an enzyme inducer with clinically important pharmacokinetic interactions and potential hepatic adverse effects. Drug levels, clinical seizure control, and laboratory trends should be considered together.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"Which finding makes phenobarbital monitoring particularly relevant?","options":["No effects on hepatic pathways","Permanent absence of side effects","Enzyme induction, altered drug exposure and potential hepatic adverse effects","Elimination exclusively through lungs"],"answer":2,"why":"Monitoring drug concentration and clinical/lab response helps evaluate treatment and adverse effects."},"qualityReview":"Rewritten to case-based mechanism/application with original text retained; 2026-11-17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-008","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog has continuous convulsions. What is the safest interpretation of the lecture's emergency drug table?","options":["Use it as a home medication schedule","Treat each row as interchangeable","Wait for spontaneous resolution","Recognize status epilepticus and seek immediate monitored emergency care"],"answer":3,"why":"Continuous convulsions are an emergency because sustained neuronal excitation increases oxygen demand, heat production and secondary injury risk. Airway, oxygenation and rapid seizure control must be prioritized under an emergency protocol. A lecture drug table explains pharmacology but must not be used as a standalone dosing algorithm.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-009","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A horse given an alpha-2 agonist develops bradycardia and sedation. Which receptor-level explanation best accounts for decreased sympathetic outflow?","options":["Postsynaptic alpha-1 stimulation directly blocks norepinephrine synthesis","Presynaptic alpha-2 autoreceptor activation reduces norepinephrine release","Beta-2 receptor blockade increases central norepinephrine release","Muscarinic M3 stimulation decreases synaptic acetylcholinesterase activity"],"answer":1,"why":"Alpha-2 presynaptic autoreceptors suppress norepinephrine release, contributing to sedation and bradycardia alongside peripheral actions.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-010","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A fearful, hypotensive dog must undergo a painful diagnostic procedure. Acepromazine is proposed as the sole medication. What is the most important objection?","options":["Acepromazine is an opioid antagonist and prevents all rescue analgesia","Acepromazine produces strong visceral analgesia but no sedation","Acepromazine can tranquilize without adequate analgesia and may worsen hypotension through alpha-1 blockade","Acepromazine is a short-acting alpha-2 agonist and requires atipamezole"],"answer":2,"why":"Phenothiazine tranquilization is not equivalent to analgesia or resolution of fear. Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonism can lower vascular tone; a hypotensive patient needs an individualized, monitored alternative.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical application","reviewedFor":"Plausible pharmacology distractors and clinical decision context"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-011","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"An equine colic patient receives butorphanol for analgesia. Which receptor profile best distinguishes it from a full mu agonist?","options":["Full mu agonism with no kappa effect","Predominant kappa agonism with mu antagonism/partial agonism depending on context","Irreversible antagonism at mu, kappa and delta opioid receptors","Selective delta agonism with no measurable mu interaction"],"answer":1,"why":"Butorphanol has mixed opioid activity, often described as kappa agonist and mu antagonist. Its analgesic efficacy is context-dependent.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical-application"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-012","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog that received a long-acting opioid improves in ventilation after naloxone, then becomes progressively sedated again. Which explanation best guides continued care?","options":["Naloxone causes permanent opioid receptor loss, so recurring depression must be a new disease","Naloxone has reversed alpha-2 agonism rather than opioid effects","A short antagonist duration relative to the opioid can permit renarcotization, requiring reassessment and monitoring","The improvement proves analgesia will persist after all opioid action has ended"],"answer":2,"why":"Naloxone can wear off before an opioid does. Recurrence of opioid effects is possible and requires monitored clinical reassessment; antagonism can also reverse analgesia.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","difficulty":"clinical application","reviewedFor":"Plausible pharmacology distractors and clinical decision context"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-013","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"A dog undergoing surgery receives no preemptive analgesia and develops disproportionate postoperative pain despite healing tissue. Which mechanism best explains this transition?","options":["Repeated nociceptive input promotes spinal dorsal horn and NMDA-linked sensitization, increasing responses to later stimuli","Neuromuscular blocking agents inevitably prevent central sensitization because paralysis eliminates nociceptive traffic","Any opioid administration after surgery irreversibly reverses all sensitization","The pain must arise only from peripheral muscle spasm because CNS plasticity cannot occur"],"answer":0,"why":"Wind-up and central sensitization are maladaptive increases in dorsal horn responsiveness after repeated nociception. Timely multimodal analgesia targets both peripheral input and central processing.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2","sourceOriginal":{"q":"What best explains wind-up during ongoing nociceptive input?","options":["Repeated signaling increases central spinal responsiveness, including NMDA-related sensitization","Pain occurs only in peripheral muscles","Neuromuscular paralysis blocks pain perception","Every opioid prevents central sensitization completely"],"answer":0,"why":"Repeated nociceptive traffic can heighten dorsal-horn excitability; multimodal analgesia targets several points in the pathway."},"qualityReview":"Improved plausible distractors/clinical transfer Nov 17"},{"id":"pharm-deep-neuro-014","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Neuropharmacology mechanisms and patient care","q":"Why is loperamide particularly concerning in dogs with some ABCB1 variants?","options":["It inhibits thyroid synthesis","Reduced P-glycoprotein efflux can allow greater CNS opioid exposure","It becomes an NK1 antagonist","It stimulates a GABA-A receptor directly"],"answer":1,"why":"ABCB1 transporter dysfunction can allow loperamide to enter the CNS and cause opioid neurotoxicity.","source":"Original case check derived from user's Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 82–140; not a verbatim instructor question","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-iodine-20261114-01","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"I-131 pretreatment · lecture vs clinic","q":"A 13-year-old cat is hyperthyroid, tachycardic and azotemic on initial bloodwork. The owner requests immediate I-131. Which plan best follows the Lecture 12 preparation framework?","options":["Administer I-131 immediately because thyroid ablation corrects every cause of azotemia","Begin medically supervised stabilization when appropriate, reassess renal/cardiovascular/metabolic status, then determine whether a radiation-isolation period is safe","Stop all treatment immediately and assess only total T4 three months later","Treat with I-131 while scheduling daily hands-on intravenous care during isolation"],"answer":1,"why":"The lecture emphasizes medical stability and renal/cardiovascular/metabolic reassessment before I-131 because safe hands-on access is restricted during radiation isolation. Azotemia is a clinical consideration rather than an absolute universal ban.","difficulty":"Clinical decision","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 44–45; UT College of Veterinary Medicine I-131 FAQ","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-iodine-20261114-02","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"I-131 handling · source fidelity","q":"A student recalls that Lecture 12 says to stop methimazole and y/d before I-131 and avoid touching the cat after treatment. Which statement correctly separates the EXAM note from the actual treatment-center protocol?","options":["The lecture states a 10–14-day pre-I-131 withdrawal and 3-day no-touch rule; the specialist center determines whether/when withdrawal is safe and actual isolation/release rules","Every radiation center discharges all cats exactly 3 days after I-131, regardless of radiation measurements","Methimazole must always be withdrawn for exactly 3 days and then restarted during radiation therapy","Radiation precautions cease as soon as the cat eats after injection"],"answer":0,"why":"Exam recall: methimazole and y/d stopped 10–14 days before treatment; do not touch for 3 days, then gloves for 2 weeks, per the lecture. Real centers can require longer isolation and will individualize stabilization and medication withdrawal.","difficulty":"Clinical source comparison","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide pp. 44–45; UT College of Veterinary Medicine I-131 clinical guidance","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-sglt2-20261114-01","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"SGLT2 insulin contraindication","q":"A cat was diagnosed with diabetes 8 days ago and has received glargine since diagnosis. The owner requests oral Bexacat instead because injections are difficult. What is the safest next action?","options":["Add Bexacat to glargine to enhance glucosuria and reduce the insulin requirement","Stop glargine and start Bexacat the next morning without additional testing","Do not initiate Bexacat: prior or concurrent insulin use is a labeled contraindication; reassess an appropriate diabetes plan","Start Senvelgo instead because its product label permits cats previously treated with insulin"],"answer":2,"why":"Both US-labeled feline SGLT2 products are contraindicated in previously insulin-treated cats, concurrent insulin treatment, or insulin-dependent diabetes. Insulin must not simply be withdrawn to permit SGLT2 therapy because of potentially fatal DKA/euglycemic DKA.","difficulty":"Clinical decision","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p. 37; FDA Bexacat and Senvelgo warnings","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-sglt2-20261114-02","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"SGLT2 euglycemic DKA","q":"A cat taking Senvelgo develops anorexia, vomiting and lethargy. Glucose is 168 mg/dL, and blood ketones are elevated. Which interpretation is MOST important?","options":["The glucose value excludes ketoacidosis, so continue Senvelgo and reassess in two weeks","The cat may have euglycemic DKA; stop the SGLT2 inhibitor and pursue immediate emergency evaluation and insulin-based management if indicated","Glucosuria itself shows the cat has sufficient endogenous insulin to prevent ketosis","Add oral Bexacat to increase renal glucose loss while awaiting a ketone recheck"],"referenceIntervals":"Feline serum glucose: 71–182 mg/dL. Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas SGLT2-associated euglycemic DKA is possible despite glucose within this interval.","answer":1,"why":"SGLT2 blockade can normalize or lower serum glucose while inadequate insulin permits lipolysis and ketogenesis. FDA warnings emphasize euglycemic DKA; emergency treatment may include insulin AFTER stopping the SGLT2 drug.","difficulty":"Clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide p. 37; FDA Senvelgo boxed warning","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-01","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"An older male cat develops stranguria after relief of a mechanical urethral obstruction. Which drug improves urethral outflow by blocking alpha-1 receptors rather than contracting the detrusor?","options":["Bethanechol","Prazosin","Oxybutynin","Phenylpropanolamine"],"answer":1,"why":"Prazosin relaxes smooth-muscle outflow resistance via alpha-1 antagonism. Bethanechol contracts detrusor and is inappropriate if outflow remains obstructed.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-02","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"A dog receiving furosemide and digoxin develops anorexia, weakness, ventricular premature complexes and K+ 2.6 mmol/L (ref 3.5–5.5). Which mechanism makes this combination concerning?","options":["Hypokalemia increases susceptibility to digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia","Furosemide blocks every effect of digoxin","Digoxin raises extracellular potassium to normalize diuresis","The two agents have identical renal tubular targets"],"answer":0,"why":"Loop diuresis can cause hypokalemia, increasing digoxin toxicity risk. Check rhythm, renal function, electrolytes and digoxin exposure.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-03","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"An asthmatic cat with acute bronchospasm receives inhaled albuterol. Why must maintenance anti-inflammatory therapy still be addressed?","options":["Beta-2 agonism suppresses the entire eosinophilic inflammatory pathway permanently","Albuterol is a muscarinic antagonist that cannot relax the airway","Beta-2 agonism relaxes bronchial smooth muscle but does not adequately control the underlying airway inflammation","Selective beta-2 agonists should be used only for bacterial pneumonia"],"answer":2,"why":"Albuterol rapidly bronchodilates via beta-2 receptors but is not a complete anti-inflammatory treatment for feline asthma.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-04","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"A dog with CHF already takes furosemide and enalapril. Spironolactone is added. What additional adverse effect deserves particular monitoring?","options":["Permanent hypercalcemia from NKCC2 activation","Hyperkalemia because aldosterone receptor blockade reduces potassium excretion, amplified by RAAS blockade","Severe hypokalemia from ENaC stimulation","Rapid-onset thyroid hormone suppression"],"answer":1,"why":"Spironolactone blocks mineralocorticoid receptors, conserving potassium; combination with ACE inhibition increases hyperkalemia risk. Assess potassium, renal function and blood pressure.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-05","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"A dog with suspected mechanical intestinal obstruction has vomiting and abdominal pain. Why is metoclopramide as a prokinetic a poor empiric choice before confirming patency?","options":["It causes permanent blockade of all NK1 receptors","Increased GI contractions against an obstruction can worsen complications","It is a mu-opioid agonist that causes respiratory depression","It neutralizes all gastric acid"],"answer":1,"why":"Prokinetic stimulation against a mechanical blockage may worsen injury; evaluate for obstruction before using motility-promoting agents.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-06","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"A dog on selegiline for cognitive dysfunction is referred for new anxiety. Which drug decision needs an interaction and washout review before initiation?","options":["Topical ocular lubricant","Fluoxetine because it blocks serotonin reuptake and may produce serotonin toxicity with MAO inhibition","A balanced maintenance diet","A nonmedicated enrichment puzzle"],"answer":1,"why":"MAO-B inhibitor selegiline plus an SSRI can create dangerous serotonergic interactions; assess a proper washout and an alternative treatment plan.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-07","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"A dog with stable bromide serum levels changes to a high-chloride therapeutic diet. Which trend could occur weeks later?","options":["Decreased bromide levels and breakthrough seizures due to greater renal bromide clearance","Increased levels because chloride irreversibly binds bromide","No change because bromide is metabolized exclusively in the liver","Immediate complete reversal of phenobarbital's effects"],"answer":0,"why":"Chloride competes with renal bromide handling; increased dietary chloride can increase bromide clearance and lower concentrations, so diet changes require monitoring.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-audit-clinical-20261114-08","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Lecture clinical parameters","q":"A dog receives dexmedetomidine plus hydromorphone and becomes hypoventilatory. Which reversal strategy identifies the actual receptor targets?","options":["Naloxone reverses alpha-2 activity and atipamezole reverses mu agonism","Flumazenil reverses both","Atipamezole reverses the alpha-2 component; naloxone reverses the opioid component, but monitor for loss of analgesia and recurrent effects","Yohimbine reverses all drug classes without monitoring"],"answer":2,"why":"The drugs act at distinct receptors. Selective reversal does not eliminate the effects of other coadministered sedatives, and opioid antagonism can remove analgesia.","difficulty":"NAVLE-style clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide, corresponding lecture; clinical cautions cross-referenced where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-01","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A 12-kg diabetic dog receives a U-40 insulin prescription, but an owner draws to the 6-unit line on a U-100 insulin syringe. Relative to the intended 6 units, what is actually administered?","options":["About 2.4 units because the U-100-marked volume is only 0.06 mL","About 6 units because the insulin concentration is independent of syringe volume","About 15 units because the U-100 calibration overestimates a U-40 insulin dose","About 4 units because the calibration difference is a fixed two-unit adjustment"],"answer":0,"why":"U-100 syringe mark 6 corresponds to 0.06 mL; U-40 insulin has 40 units/mL, yielding 2.4 units. The mismatch risks underdosing and cannot be fixed by assuming markings are universal.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-02","lecture":"Lecture 10","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A cat receiving glargine has erratic glucose readings. The owner routinely vigorously mixes the clear insulin with NPH before injection. Which mechanism best explains the problem?","options":["Vigorous agitation may damage any protein product but glargine and NPH still have compatible release properties","Glargine's acidic formulation is altered by mixing and its subcutaneous precipitation profile becomes unreliable","Glargine should be mixed only when the NPH concentration is also U-100","The main issue is that the NPH will convert all glargine into a short-acting insulin"],"answer":1,"why":"Glargine forms a microprecipitate depot after SQ injection; mixing with other insulins changes pH and release characteristics. Do not mix formulations unless specifically supported.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-03","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A cat just started on velagliflozin has lethargy, anorexia and vomiting. Blood glucose is 142 mg/dL, beta-hydroxybutyrate is high and metabolic acidosis is present. What is the most important interpretation?","options":["DKA is unlikely until serum glucose exceeds 300 mg/dL despite increased ketones","Because SGLT2 agents lower glucose, a concurrent insulin dose should be added while keeping velagliflozin","SGLT2-associated euglycemic DKA remains possible and requires urgent treatment","Isolated starvation ketosis explains the findings; acid-base disturbance does not change urgency"],"answer":2,"why":"Glucosuria lowers glucose independently of insulin, while insufficient insulin fails to restrain lipolysis and ketogenesis. Stop the SGLT2 inhibitor and initiate emergency evaluation and treatment; concurrent insulin is not an appropriate outpatient combination.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2","referenceIntervals":"Feline serum glucose: 71–182 mg/dL. Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas SGLT2-associated euglycemic DKA is possible despite glucose within this interval."},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-04","lecture":"Lecture 11","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A cat with insulin-treated diabetes asks to switch to bexagliflozin because the owner cannot inject. Which is the key patient-selection issue?","options":["A trial is reasonable if glargine is stopped for 24 hours before bexagliflozin begins","Bexagliflozin is preferred after prior insulin because pancreatic beta cells have been rested","A short interval without insulin plus a negative urine glucose strip removes the DKA risk","Prior insulin treatment is a labeled contraindication to starting bexagliflozin; do not withdraw insulin to make the cat eligible"],"answer":3,"why":"Bexacat is not indicated for cats previously treated with insulin or those needing insulin. SGLT2 inhibition does not replace insulin's anti-ketogenic actions.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-05","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A hyperthyroid cat has tachycardia, weight loss and newly detected azotemia. The owner wants I-131 tomorrow. Which approach best addresses the physiologic reason for pretreatment?","options":["Stabilize and assess cardiovascular, renal and metabolic status before a period in which routine handling may be restricted","Proceed with I-131 despite tachycardia because stable isolation minimizes medication interactions","Exclude I-131 permanently because azotemia always proves irreversible renal failure","Focus on radioactive handling instructions alone; physiologic stability can be evaluated after discharge"],"answer":0,"why":"Restoring euthyroidism may unmask reduced renal function; radiation isolation limits close monitoring and hands-on care. The lecture gives a 10–14-day methimazole/y/d withdrawal and 3-day no-touch rule as exam notes, while the center sets actual policies.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-06","lecture":"Lecture 12","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A hyperthyroid cat on methimazole develops facial excoriations and thrombocytopenia. Which response best links drug effect to a safety decision?","options":["Reduce methimazole slightly and recheck only total T4 after one month","Evaluate for serious methimazole adverse effects and reconsider the treatment plan with CBC and clinical assessment","Attribute the excoriations to hyperthyroidism and continue methimazole unless neutropenia also appears","Switch to propylthiouracil without checking CBC because it lacks hematologic toxicity"],"answer":1,"why":"Methimazole can cause adverse effects including facial pruritus/excoriations and hematologic abnormalities. A patient with concerning signs needs drug assessment and alternative management.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-07","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"An anesthetized dog develops hypotension after an alpha-1-blocking medication. Which receptor effect explains why a beta-1 agonist is not an equivalent replacement for restoring arterial tone?","options":["Beta-1 stimulation improves inotropy but cannot directly replace loss of alpha-1 vascular resistance in every patient","Beta-1 stimulation primarily blocks norepinephrine release from nerve endings","Alpha-1 blockade reduces arteriolar constriction; beta-1 stimulation mainly increases heart rate/contractility","Alpha-1 blockade increases preload by constricting peripheral veins"],"answer":2,"why":"Alpha-1 receptors mediate vascular smooth-muscle contraction; beta-1 acts mainly in myocardium. Distinguish vascular resistance from cardiac output in blood-pressure physiology.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-08","lecture":"Lecture 13","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A blocked male cat has a distended bladder. A technician suggests bethanechol before confirming urethral patency. Which reasoning is most appropriate?","options":["Use bethanechol because abdominal palpation confirms detrusor atony rather than obstruction","Use bethanechol and prazosin together before checking urethral patency to balance contractility and resistance","Use bethanechol solely to improve urine production by the kidneys while leaving the outflow problem unaddressed","Do not stimulate detrusor against obstruction; address outflow first and reassess whether any motility drug is warranted"],"answer":3,"why":"Bethanechol is a muscarinic agonist promoting detrusor contraction. Outflow obstruction must be addressed rather than generating pressure against a closed urethra.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-09","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A dog with CHF receives furosemide and digoxin. It becomes anorexic and develops ventricular ectopy; potassium is 2.7 mmol/L and creatinine is rising. Which combination of mechanisms is most concerning?","options":["Loop-mediated potassium loss increases digoxin toxicity vulnerability; altered renal clearance also requires evaluation","Hypokalemia protects against digoxin-associated arrhythmias, so isolated ectopy suggests only myocarditis","Furosemide chiefly causes hyperkalemia, which explains decreased digoxin efficacy","Digoxin accumulation is independent of kidney function, so only serum potassium matters"],"answer":0,"why":"Hypokalemia increases digoxin-related arrhythmogenicity, and renal impairment can increase exposure. Evaluate electrolytes, ECG, perfusion, renal function and the treatment plan.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2","referenceIntervals":"Canine serum potassium: 4.1–5.4 mEq/L (mmol/L equivalent). Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-10","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A cat with systemic hypertension and HCM is prescribed amlodipine. What distinguishes its primary mechanism from diltiazem's clinically important cardiac actions?","options":["Amlodipine has preferential nodal effects whereas diltiazem acts only on peripheral arteries","Amlodipine predominantly relaxes arterial smooth muscle via L-type calcium-channel blockade; diltiazem also affects cardiac nodal conduction","Both agents are angiotensin receptor antagonists with different durations","Diltiazem primarily blocks sodium channels in ventricular myocytes, unlike amlodipine"],"answer":1,"why":"Dihydropyridines favor vascular smooth muscle while non-dihydropyridines such as diltiazem have more significant rate/conduction effects. Choice depends on the target problem.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-11","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"An asthmatic cat appears better after inhaled albuterol but develops recurrent coughing over the next several weeks. What is missing from a rescue-bronchodilator-only plan?","options":["Albuterol has anti-inflammatory activity, but the dose must be increased to prevent airway remodeling","A beta-1 blocker would reduce cough while preserving rescue bronchodilation","Long-term control of airway inflammation, commonly using an appropriate corticosteroid regimen","Long-term cough shows that bronchospasm was never present, so rescue medication has no role"],"answer":2,"why":"Albuterol relaxes airway smooth muscle but does not adequately control the underlying chronic inflammation. Rescue and controller therapy have distinct goals.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-12","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A severely hypoventilating anesthetized patient receives doxapram but oxygenation remains inadequate. Which clinical reasoning is best?","options":["Increase doxapram until ventilation normalizes regardless of obstruction or anesthetic depth","Add albuterol because stimulating beta-2 receptors reliably corrects hypoventilation","A normal respiratory rate by itself proves adequate tidal volume and CO2 clearance","A respiratory stimulant cannot substitute for airway management and assisted ventilation when ventilation remains ineffective"],"answer":3,"why":"Central respiratory drive is not the same as effective tidal volume and gas exchange. Address airway, anesthetic depth and mechanical ventilation as required.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-13","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A CHF dog on furosemide is also prescribed enalapril and spironolactone. Potassium rises from 4.4 to 6.2 mmol/L. Which pathway combination predicts this?","options":["ACE inhibition reduces angiotensin II–aldosterone signaling while spironolactone blocks remaining mineralocorticoid receptors","Furosemide prevents potassium excretion while ACE inhibition increases aldosterone synthesis","Spironolactone increases distal Na reabsorption and thus increases K secretion","Enalapril increases renal filtration and necessarily makes serum K fall"],"answer":0,"why":"Reduced aldosterone effects at the distal nephron diminish renal potassium excretion. Monitor potassium, renal indices and clinical status when these agents are combined.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2","referenceIntervals":"Canine serum potassium: 4.1–5.4 mEq/L (mmol/L equivalent). Cornell University AHDC Cobas 501 serum reference intervals (2017); performing laboratory's method/specimen intervals take precedence. https://www.vet.cornell.edu/animal-health-diagnostic-center/laboratories/clinical-pathology/reference-intervals/chemistry-cobas"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-14","lecture":"Lecture 16","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A diuretic is added to inhibit the Na-Cl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule rather than NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. Which difference follows?","options":["Distal NCC inhibition is usually more potent than NKCC2 inhibition because the DCT handles the majority of filtered Na","A thiazide acts at NCC in a lower-capacity segment and generally has less potent standalone natriuresis than a loop drug","NCC inhibition is equivalent to mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism in the collecting duct","Thiazides increase osmotic water retention at the proximal tubule without promoting natriuresis"],"answer":1,"why":"Thiazides inhibit distal NCC; loops inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, a major NaCl-reabsorbing segment. Target site explains effect and electrolyte risks.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-15","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A dog with vomiting, abdominal pain and radiographic evidence of small intestinal obstruction is offered metoclopramide for persistent vomiting. What is the key concern?","options":["Metoclopramide provides analgesia that can mask signs of strangulation while remaining safe for motility","Metoclopramide should replace fluid resuscitation because D2 blockade normalizes perfusion","Its prokinetic action is inappropriate when a mechanical blockage has not been relieved","The drug enhances colonic bacterial gas formation and therefore confirms obstruction"],"answer":2,"why":"Metoclopramide enhances upper GI motility via dopaminergic/serotonergic actions. Stimulating motility against an obstruction can worsen the clinical situation.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-16","lecture":"Lecture 17","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A dog with suspected pancreatitis has vomiting and dehydration. Which distinction best compares maropitant and ondansetron?","options":["Maropitant and ondansetron both block D2 receptors, but differ in duration","Ondansetron is a prokinetic that reliably resolves ileus whereas maropitant has no central antiemetic activity","Maropitant acts at 5-HT3 receptors while ondansetron blocks NK1 receptors","Maropitant is an NK1 substance-P antagonist; ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist, and neither replaces fluid/perfusion management"],"answer":3,"why":"Antiemetics target different emetic signaling systems. Choice is based on patient factors; vomiting suppression does not correct dehydration or underlying disease.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-17","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A severely noise-fearful dog is immobile after acepromazine but continues to show dilated pupils, rigid posture and distress. What does the drug effect fail to establish?","options":["Tranquilization does not prove fear relief or analgesia; assess behavior and use an appropriate anxiety-management strategy","Persistent physiologic fear responses may occur despite immobility; acepromazine produces reliable anxiolysis but no pain relief","Acepromazine's alpha-1 antagonism confirms anxiety is resolved even when posture suggests fear","The dose was necessarily inadequate because a tranquilized dog must lose consciousness"],"answer":0,"why":"Phenothiazines sedate through central dopamine antagonism but do not reliably provide anxiolysis or analgesia. Behavioral treatment should target the actual emotional state.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-18","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A dog on selegiline for cognitive dysfunction is prescribed fluoxetine for separation-related distress. What mechanism creates a safety concern?","options":["Concurrent treatment is safe if both agents are given at half dose without a washout","MAO inhibition combined with serotonergic reuptake inhibition increases risk of serotonin toxicity; plan an appropriate washout/alternative","Serotonin toxicity occurs only with combinations of two SSRIs, not MAO inhibitors","Selegiline increases monoamine breakdown, so fluoxetine is needed to prevent withdrawal"],"answer":1,"why":"Selegiline inhibits MAO-B, while fluoxetine increases synaptic serotonin. Drug interactions and prolonged elimination/washout matter.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-19","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A seizure-controlled dog on potassium bromide is changed to a high-chloride diet and develops breakthrough seizures weeks later. Which monitoring explanation is strongest?","options":["Dietary chloride changes bromide elimination but concentrations should rise when chloride intake increases","High chloride changes bromide GABA receptor affinity rather than changing systemic exposure","Higher chloride intake can increase renal bromide clearance and lower serum levels","The dietary effect is immediate and needs no follow-up concentration monitoring"],"answer":2,"why":"Bromide and chloride compete in renal handling, so stable dietary chloride is important for predictable bromide concentrations and seizure control.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-20","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A dog with status epilepticus receives diazepam and later phenobarbital. Which comparison best identifies their major GABA-A effects?","options":["Diazepam enhances GABA-A chloride channel open duration; phenobarbital increases opening frequency","Diazepam is a GABA-B agonist while phenobarbital is a glutamate receptor blocker","Both drugs inhibit presynaptic SV2A with the same binding mechanism","Benzodiazepines facilitate frequency of channel opening; barbiturates prolong opening duration at GABA-A receptors"],"answer":3,"why":"Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators at GABA-A receptors increasing opening frequency; barbiturates increase open duration, with broader CNS depression risk.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-21","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A dog received dexmedetomidine plus hydromorphone. Bradycardia and hypoventilation persist, but its surgical wound is painful. Which reversal logic is pharmacologically sound?","options":["Atipamezole targets alpha-2 sedation; naloxone targets opioid effects, and reversing opioid action may remove analgesia","Atipamezole antagonizes opioid receptors and therefore restores ventilation without affecting sedation","Naloxone restores opioid analgesia while reducing respiratory depression","Flumazenil reverses both agents because all anesthetic agents converge on benzodiazepine receptors"],"answer":0,"why":"Reversal targets are receptor-specific. Examine perfusion/ventilation and the remaining need for pain control; monitor for resedation or renarcotization.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-clinical-20261117-22","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Exam 2 · Applied clinical decisions","q":"A horse given acepromazine has hypotension despite appearing deeply sedated. Which explanation is most relevant?","options":["Acepromazine typically increases systemic vascular resistance through alpha-1 agonism","Alpha-1 blockade can reduce vascular resistance; sedation is not analgesia and does not assure perfusion","An apparently sedated horse is necessarily analgesic and hemodynamically stable","Phenothiazines selectively reduce heart rate by beta-1 blockade without changing blood pressure"],"answer":1,"why":"Phenothiazine alpha-1 antagonism contributes to hypotension. Neurologic appearance must not be mistaken for hemodynamic stability or adequate pain management.","difficulty":"Advanced clinical application","source":"Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide (course-aligned interpretation); consult drug-specific labeling for patient care","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-01","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A cattle feedlot experiences rapidly spreading BRD soon after arrival. The veterinarian is considering a macrolide for a high-risk group, with likely Mannheimia haemolytica. Which distinction matters MOST?","options":["BRD metaphylaxis is risk-based group treatment using an agent and species/production class explicitly supported by its label","All macrolides are interchangeable between cattle and cats if the 50S target is the same","A low-risk group must receive the same antibiotic as every clinically ill animal","Any evidence of viral exposure proves that all cattle have susceptible bacterial pneumonia"],"answer":0,"why":"Macrolides including tulathromycin/gamithromycin/tildipirosin have different approved indications and restrictions; BRD metaphylaxis is based on group risk, bacterial susceptibility and labeled use.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-02","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A technician is preparing injectable tilmicosin on a cattle operation and asks why handling precautions differ from other BRD antibiotics. Which response is most important?","options":["The major hazard is mild self-limited nausea, unlike fluoroquinolones","Accidental human injection can produce fatal cardiotoxicity; use strict product-specific handling and secure restraint","Tilmicosin can safely be administered IV in cattle to prevent accidental subcutaneous exposure","The main distinction is that tilmicosin blocks bacterial DNA gyrase instead of ribosomes"],"answer":1,"why":"FDA product labeling highlights potentially fatal cardiac toxicity after accidental human injection; the veterinary route/species restrictions are likewise critical. It is a macrolide 50S inhibitor.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-03","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A producer asks whether pradofloxacin from a lecture BRD table can replace danofloxacin in U.S. cattle without considering the label. What is the correct distinction?","options":["They differ only in trade name, so interchangeability is automatic","Pradofloxacin is an injectable U.S. cattle BRD product, while danofloxacin is limited to cats","Pradofloxacin's U.S. labeled use is feline, whereas danofloxacin has cattle BRD indications; extralabel fluoroquinolone use in food animals is prohibited","Any fluoroquinolone can legally be used extra-label in cattle if the organism is susceptible"],"answer":2,"why":"Both inhibit bacterial topoisomerases, but their U.S. labeled species differ. Food-animal extra-label fluoroquinolone use is legally prohibited. The lecture list requires this clinical distinction.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-04","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A calf with bacterial BRD receives florfenicol plus flunixin. Which mechanism-based explanation correctly distinguishes their purposes?","options":["Flunixin blocks ribosomes while florfenicol restores airway ciliary motion","Both agents kill bacteria, but only one binds penicillin-binding proteins","Florfenicol is an NSAID treating endotoxemia while flunixin prevents microbial protein synthesis","Florfenicol inhibits bacterial 50S protein synthesis; flunixin inhibits host COX-mediated inflammation and cannot replace antibiotic therapy"],"answer":3,"why":"The antibiotic targets bacterial protein synthesis; the NSAID targets host prostaglandin synthesis, relieving fever/pain but not curing infection.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-05","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A veterinarian is considering ceftiofur to treat cattle respiratory infection. Which concern separates it from using amoxicillin–clavulanate in a dog?","options":["Ceftiofur use in major food species has specific extra-label cephalosporin restrictions and product-dependent residues/production-class requirements","Amoxicillin-clavulanate is never a beta-lactam and therefore has no resistance issues","Ceftiofur acts mainly as an NSAID, not on bacterial cell-wall synthesis","Any veterinarian can make a ceftiofur nebulized formulation for food animals without examining extra-label rules"],"answer":0,"why":"Ceftiofur is a third-generation cephalosporin acting on PBPs; U.S. extra-label restrictions in cattle/swine/chickens/turkeys are specific and not equivalent to routine companion-animal prescribing.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-06","lecture":"Lecture 14","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"An older dog with reduced renal function has a recurrent ventricular tachyarrhythmia and is considered for oral procainamide. Which adjustment to clinical reasoning is most justified?","options":["Procainamide has no effect on conduction, so severe AV block is irrelevant","Consider accumulation/conduction effects and monitor ECG plus renal function, rather than copying an empiric dose for every patient","Its main clinical effect is selective beta-2 bronchodilation","The drug is identical to IV lidocaine in its channel-binding kinetics and renal clearance"],"answer":1,"why":"Procainamide is a Class Ia sodium-channel blocker with conduction and renal considerations. It is not an automatic replacement for acute IV lidocaine or a one-size-fits-all chronic dose.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-07","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A dog with episodic noise panic is already taking a daily SSRI. The owner asks for a rapidly acting rescue medication. Which comparison best guides an initial discussion?","options":["Fluoxetine can immediately terminate panic 20 minutes after the first dose","Acepromazine guarantees anxiolysis when the dog is immobile","A carefully evaluated situational benzodiazepine such as alprazolam may act sooner than an SSRI, but paradoxical disinhibition and interaction risks must be discussed","Buspirone provides surgical analgesia through mu opioid receptors and is the preferred emergency rescue"],"answer":2,"why":"Benzodiazepines potentiate GABA-A and can produce more rapid anxiolytic effects than SSRIs, which require longer adaptation. Appropriate use requires history, patient monitoring and behavioral planning.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-08","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"An anxious horse needs standing sedation but has borderline perfusion and bradycardia. Which statement about selecting romifidine over another alpha-2 drug is most defensible?","options":["Romifidine causes no cardiovascular changes because it only acts in the spinal cord","All alpha-2 drugs increase cardiac output at clinically sedating doses","Romifidine cannot be used in horses because its active receptor is beta-1","Its duration/clinical profile may differ, but alpha-2 effects can still cause vasoconstriction, reduced output and bradycardia, so monitoring and patient selection remain essential"],"answer":3,"why":"An alpha-2 drug can cause peripheral vasoconstriction plus reflex bradycardia, despite central sympatholysis; no alpha-2 agonist is universally safe in a hemodynamically compromised horse.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-09","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A dog appears immobile after acepromazine but remains fearful of nail trimming. What is the critical pharmacologic interpretation?","options":["D2-mediated tranquilization does not demonstrate relief of fear or pain; consider low-stress handling and a true anxiolytic plan","Acepromazine directly agonizes GABA-A to provide reliable anxiolysis and analgesia","The patient's immobility demonstrates that NMDA-driven pain is fully controlled","Acepromazine is the preferred opioid antagonist for fear-induced immobility"],"answer":0,"why":"Acepromazine is a phenothiazine tranquilizer without dependable analgesia or fear relief. Monitor cardiovascular effects, especially alpha-1 blockade and hypotension.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-10","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A dog receives a medetomidine–vatinoxan combination for a procedure. Which inference about hemodynamic safety is appropriate?","options":["Vatinoxan completely abolishes central sedation, so the dose must automatically be doubled","Peripheral alpha-2 antagonism can blunt some vascular effects while central sedation remains, but monitoring remains necessary","Both ingredients are mu opioid receptor antagonists and remove surgical pain","The combination prevents all possible respiratory and cardiovascular adverse events"],"answer":1,"why":"Medetomidine provides central alpha-2 actions; vatinoxan is peripherally acting and can lessen some adverse vascular effects without being a universal antidote.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-11","lecture":"Lecture 20","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A cat has significant acute postoperative pain and receives buprenorphine. Which interpretation of its receptor behavior is the best?","options":["It is a full mu agonist with no ceiling-related considerations","It is a pure kappa agonist with absent affinity for mu receptors","It is a high-affinity partial mu agonist; useful clinical analgesia can coexist with a lower maximal receptor efficacy than some full agonists","Its high potency guarantees higher maximal analgesic efficacy than morphine in every painful patient"],"answer":2,"why":"Buprenorphine has high mu affinity and partial intrinsic efficacy. Potency, affinity and maximal efficacy differ; route/formulation and severity of pain govern use.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-12","lecture":"Lecture 15","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A veterinarian is reviewing a coughing dog and a BRD-affected calf. Which antibiotic statement best preserves species-specific clinical targets?","options":["Because both have coughs, tulathromycin is first line for both without bacterial diagnosis","Ceftiofur and amoxicillin-clavulanate differ only by molecular weight and have identical food-animal restrictions","Every cough is bacterial, so delaying antimicrobial use causes resistance","An amoxicillin–clavulanate decision in a dog and a labeled cattle BRD drug choice require separate organism, disease and species assessments"],"answer":3,"why":"Antimicrobial class alone cannot define indication. Canine cough may be nonbacterial; BRD requires livestock-specific pathogen and label assessment.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-13","lecture":"Lecture 19","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A chronically seizure-controlled dog receiving phenobarbital develops recurrent seizures after a new medication is started. Which pharmacologic assessment belongs alongside adherence and drug levels?","options":["Phenobarbital enzyme induction may alter exposure to some concurrent drugs; also reassess patient status and any interactions","Phenobarbital does not undergo any hepatic biotransformation and cannot affect other drugs","Adding two drugs with any similar mechanism is always illegal","No medication interactions occur when the phenobarbital concentration is within range"],"answer":0,"why":"Phenobarbital induces metabolic enzymes, and concurrent drugs may alter or be altered by its PK. Mechanisms alone do not define whether combination therapy is appropriate.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"},{"id":"pharm-exam2-gap-20261117-14","lecture":"Lecture 18","topic":"Starred-drug inventory · clinical decisions","q":"A patient on selegiline for cognitive dysfunction is prescribed another serotonergic medication. Which planning principle prevents a clinically serious error?","options":["Start the SSRI immediately at a low dose because MAO-B inhibition blocks serotonin toxicity","Review MAOI interaction/washout requirements and alternative drugs; potentially dangerous serotonergic effects can occur even when both are used for different problems","Treat agitation prophylactically with additional serotonergic supplements","Assume selegiline lacks monoamine interactions because it is labeled for dogs"],"answer":1,"why":"Selegiline's MAO-B activity can interact with serotonergic drugs, especially SSRIs/TCAs. Timing, washout and product labeling matter.","difficulty":"Exam 2 advanced species/safety comparison","source":"Main Pharmacology Exam 2 Study Guide ★ lecture tables; FDA critical restrictions reviewed where noted","exam":"Exam 2"}]};
